window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"blind-lemon-jefferson-see-that-my-grave-is-kept-clean":["A man arranging his own funeral in advance. The verses ask for two white horses, count the church bells, and describe the coffin sound as the body goes down; each one is answered by a guitar that stops keeping time and plays a slow tolling figure on the bass strings. Jefferson's voice is high and conversational, almost light, which makes the subject stranger. The record is short, plain, and closer to a hymn's pacing than to a dance blues.","Recorded for Paramount in Chicago in 1927. Jefferson had been the label's biggest seller for two years, the first Black male country blues singer to move records in quantity, and Paramount had him cutting sides at speed. He had also recorded religious material under a pseudonym, and this song sits deliberately between the two catalogues: not a spiritual, but shaped like one. He died in Chicago in the winter of 1929, the circumstances never firmly established.","The guitar imitates two things in turn — the church bell, struck on a single low string, and the hollow knock of earth on a coffin lid. Once you hear the imitation you cannot unhear it. The song also moves in and out of a twelve-bar shape; Jefferson simply stops counting when the bell figure needs longer, a freedom that the accompanying-band blues of the next decade lost.","Remembered as much through its descendants as itself: Bob Dylan recorded it on his 1962 debut, and it has been covered continually since. Folklorists have argued over whether it is Jefferson's composition or his arrangement of a much older Southern funeral song; the honest answer is probably both. Its influence on the sung death-lament in American music is hard to overstate.","Listen for the bell — a single repeated low note between verses — and count how many times it strikes. Then hear Dylan's 1962 version, and Dowland's 'Flow My Tears' from three centuries earlier, where a lute does something not unlike what this guitar does between the lines."],"blind-lemon-jefferson-black-snake-moan":["A comic, filthy, entirely deadpan blues about a black snake that has got into the singer's room, crawled in his bed, and will not be found. Jefferson delivers it slowly and innocently while the guitar does the opposite: long single-string runs slither up and down between the vocal lines, refusing to settle into a strum. The joke is carried by the instrument, not the voice, and the pauses are as long as the phrases.","One of Jefferson's first big sellers for Paramount, cut in Chicago around 1926, when the label was still working out that a blind street singer from Dallas could outsell its jazz bands. The double entendre was standard commercial practice in the race-record market, and the censors of the day had little grip on records sold mostly by mail order and in Southern furniture stores. Jefferson's fee per side was small; Paramount's profit was not.","Listen to how the guitar fills. Jefferson never plays a steady accompaniment; he stops dead at the end of each sung line and then improvises a run of unpredictable length, so the record has no fixed metre at all. That freedom — voice and guitar taking turns rather than sharing a pulse — is the single most copied feature of his style, audible later in T-Bone Walker and B.B. King.","Long treated as a novelty beside 'See That My Grave Is Kept Clean', it has been reassessed as the clearest surviving demonstration of Jefferson's guitar technique, which no contemporary matched for speed or unpredictability. Its title has had a long second life in film and song. Disputes persist about the recording date, since Paramount's files are incomplete.","Follow only the guitar for one listen; the runs are the reason musicians studied this record. Then note how far the accompaniment is from the strummed backing you may expect of 1920s blues. Pair it with Charley Patton's 'Pony Blues' from three years later for the difference between Texas and the Delta."],"mississippi-john-hurt-avalon-blues":["A homesick song written on a trip north, and one of the sweetest-sounding records in the country blues. Hurt picks a steady alternating bass with his thumb while two fingers lay a melody over it, so the guitar is playing a duet with itself; nothing is hurried and nothing is loud. The voice is soft, slightly behind the beat, and never pushes. New York is a good town, he says, but it is not Avalon — and Avalon is a hamlet in the Mississippi hills of perhaps a few dozen people.","Recorded for OKeh in New York in December 1928, on the second of two trips the label paid for after a local fiddler recommended him. Hurt was a farmhand in his mid-thirties who played at country dances for Black and white neighbours alike; he was not a professional and did not become one. OKeh's race-record series collapsed with the Depression, the sides sold poorly, and Hurt went home and farmed for thirty-five more years.","The song named the singer. In 1963 the collector Tom Hoskins took the line 'Avalon, my home town' literally, found Avalon on an old atlas, drove there and knocked on doors until he found Hurt alive. Very few records have ever retrieved their own maker. Listen too for the thumb: it never varies, which is what lets the melody float so freely above it.","Almost unheard until Harry Smith included Hurt on the 1952 Anthology of American Folk Music, which put him in front of the revival generation. Since the rediscovery, critics have argued over whether his gentleness makes him marginal to the blues or shows how narrow the standard definition is; the second view has largely won, and 'songster' is now the usual word.","Listen with headphones for the separation between thumb and fingers — it is easy to miss how hard this is to play. Then hear the 1966 recordings of the same songs by a man in his seventies, and Dvořák's New World Largo, another outsider's song about a home that is somewhere else."],"mississippi-john-hurt-stack-o-lee-blues":["The American murder ballad, told by the mildest voice imaginable. Stack O' Lee shoots Billy Lyons over a Stetson hat, is arrested, tried and hanged; Hurt narrates all of it in the same unhurried, almost apologetic tone he uses for love songs, over a rolling three-finger guitar pattern that never changes pace. The gap between the violence and the delivery is the record. The refrain — that bad man, cruel Stack O' Lee — arrives each time like a shrug.","Recorded for OKeh in New York in December 1928. The song descends from a real killing in St. Louis in 1895 and had been travelling orally for thirty years by the time Hurt cut it; dozens of versions existed, from ragtime piano to prison work chants. Hurt's is a ballad version — verses telling a story in order — rather than a blues, which is one reason he is best described as a songster rather than a bluesman.","Notice that the guitar never dramatises anything. Where a blues singer would break the pattern at the gunshot, Hurt keeps picking exactly as before, and the steadiness makes the story colder. Listen also for how the accompaniment implies a bass line, a chord and a melody at once with three fingers — the technique young guitarists in the 1960s spent years transcribing.","Harry Smith placed it on the Anthology of American Folk Music in 1952, where it became one of the revival's foundational tracks and a standard for every folk guitarist who followed. Folklorists have used the song's many versions as a case study in how an American ballad mutates. Hurt's remains the most widely learned, largely because it is the most playable.","Follow the story first — it is unusually clear — then listen again only to the guitar and try to separate the three parts. Compare Copland's Billy the Kid, which turns another American badman into a concert score, and note how differently the two handle a killing."],"mississippi-john-hurt-today":["A man in his seventies, recorded properly for the first time, playing the repertoire he had carried since before the First World War: blues, ballads, spirituals, rags and one or two new songs. The guitar is the same alternating-thumb pattern as in 1928, a little slower and considerably clearer; the voice has thinned into something almost spoken. Because it is a studio album rather than a set of three-minute sides, the songs breathe, and you hear the room, the fingers and the occasional chuckle.","Made for Vanguard in 1966, three years after Hurt was found in Avalon, Mississippi and two Newport Folk Festival appearances later. The folk revival had created an audience — largely young, largely white, largely urban — for exactly this, and labels were recording rediscovered Southern musicians as fast as they could be located. Hurt died in November 1966, months after the sessions; it was the last record he completed.","Compare any song here with its 1928 OKeh original and the tempos are nearly identical. Thirty-eight years of not performing professionally changed almost nothing, which tells you the playing was habit rather than performance. Listen also for the quiet between phrases — modern tape catches the sound of his fingers leaving the strings, which shellac never could.","The rediscovery albums are sometimes dismissed as folk-revival souvenirs; this one resists the charge, because the fidelity finally matches the delicacy. Critics debate whether Hurt's late work was shaped by what his new audience wanted to hear, particularly in his choice of gentler material. His playing, at least, shows no sign of adjustment.","Begin with the familiar songs — 'Stagolee', 'Candy Man', 'Louis Collins' — then let the record run. Good headphones repay it: the point is the detail of the picking. Afterwards, hear Berio's Folk Songs for the opposite approach, a folk repertoire deliberately recomposed rather than preserved."],"skip-james-devil-got-my-woman":["Perhaps the eeriest record in the country blues. James tunes his guitar to an open minor chord, so every strum sounds hollow and unresolved, and picks a pattern that keeps circling without ever cadencing. Above it he sings in a light, unstrained falsetto — not a shout, not a growl, but a floating, almost disembodied voice, unlike anyone else on record in 1931. The words are simple jealousy: he would rather be the devil than be that woman's man, because his best friend took her.","Recorded in February 1931 at Paramount's Grafton, Wisconsin studio, at a single session that produced eighteen sides. The Depression had destroyed the race-record market; almost nothing sold, and James gave up music for the church, later becoming a minister. The tuning and the minor cast come from the area around Bentonia, Mississippi, where a small local tradition favoured that sound; James learned it from an older local player rather than inventing it.","The guitar is tuned so that an open strum gives a minor chord — an unusual choice that does most of the emotional work before a word is sung. Robert Johnson plainly knew this record: 'Hellhound on My Trail' takes its melody and shape from it, which means one of the most obscure discs of 1931 fed directly into the most famous blues of 1937.","Original copies are among the rarest 78s in existence, which shaped the record's mystique as much as its sound. Its standing rose steeply after James was rediscovered in 1964 and it reached a wider audience through the revival. Scholars now debate how much of the Bentonia sound was a genuine local school and how much was two or three men who knew each other.","Listen first for the tuning: strum-shaped chords that sound sad before anything happens. Then for the falsetto, which sits oddly high above the guitar. Follow it with Robert Johnson's 'Hellhound on My Trail' to hear the inheritance, and Dowland's 'Semper Dowland, semper dolens' for another musician who made a career out of one shade of misery."],"skip-james-hard-time-killing-floor-blues":["A Depression song made from almost nothing: the same hollow minor tuning, a guitar figure that repeats and never arrives anywhere, and that thin high voice reporting that hard times are here and everywhere you go, that people are drifting from door to door and cannot find a heaven. There is no anger in the delivery and no self-pity. James sings it slowly and evenly, as though describing weather, which is exactly what makes it hard to listen to twice in a row.","From the same February 1931 Grafton session. The timing is almost unbearable: James recorded a song about mass destitution into a market that destitution was destroying, for a label that would be out of business within a year. Cotton had collapsed, Mississippi tenant farmers were being turned off the land, and the Southern migration north was accelerating. Paramount pressed few copies and sold fewer.","There is no chorus and no refrain — each verse simply follows the last, so the song has no shape that promises an end. Listen for the guitar's refusal to resolve: the figure returns to the same unfinished place every time, which is the technical reason the record feels like a closing door. The phrase 'killing floor', from the slaughterhouse, was ordinary industrial language before the blues made it a metaphor.","Barely heard for seventy years, it reached a mass audience in 2000 when it appeared on the soundtrack of the Coen brothers' O Brother, Where Art Thou?. That exposure has divided opinion: some hear a Depression document finally given its due, others a period mood cue. Most critics now rank it with 'Devil Got My Woman' as James's finest surviving side.","Play it in a quiet room; it does not survive being background. Listen for how few notes the guitar actually uses. Then compare Son House's 'Death Letter Blues', which meets desolation with volume, and Dowland's 'In Darkness Let Me Dwell', which meets it with the same stillness three hundred years earlier."],"skip-james-im-so-glad":["The one fast record in the 1931 session, and a display piece. James picks a rapid, rippling figure that runs continuously under the vocal, never pausing for breath, while he sings a lyric of about a dozen words repeated in shifting order: glad, tired, weary, don't know what to do. The effect is of someone spinning in place. It is not a twelve-bar blues at all but a short circling form, closer to a rag, played at a speed that most guitarists still find awkward.","Recorded at Grafton in February 1931. James built the song on a sentimental popular tune of the 1920s, reharmonised and driven far beyond its original speed — the sort of transformation that shows how much commercial sheet music a Mississippi musician had absorbed. Like everything from the session, it sold almost nothing, and James was working as a labourer and later as a minister within a couple of years.","The words barely change, which throws all the attention onto the right hand. Listen for the moment the picking pattern shifts register and climbs; James does it without altering tempo, which is the difficult part. The lyric's circular repetition — the same four states shuffled — is a formal device, not a shortage of material.","Cream recorded it on their 1966 debut and again live, and the royalties reportedly helped pay James's medical bills in the last years of his life — one of the few cases where the revival's borrowing returned money to its source. Critics disagree about whether Cream's version has anything to do with the original beyond the tune. James's take remains the technical benchmark.","Count nothing; just follow the right hand and notice that it never rests. Then hear Cream's version for the distance a rock trio travels from a solo guitar. Pair it with Mississippi John Hurt's picking for the gentler use of the same three-finger technique."],"blind-willie-johnson-dark-was-the-night-cold-was-the-ground":["Three minutes with no words at all. Johnson hums and moans a melody while a bottleneck slide answers each phrase, sliding up to its notes from below and never quite arriving cleanly; guitar and voice trade lines like two people in the same grief. The underlying tune is an eighteenth-century English hymn about Christ's agony in Gethsemane, but nothing here is sung — the text is assumed, and its absence is the point. The dynamic barely changes from beginning to end.","Recorded in Dallas in December 1927, at Johnson's first session for Columbia. He was a street evangelist, not a nightclub musician, and sang on sidewalks with a tin cup wired to his guitar; the record company came to Texas rather than the other way around. Wordless moaning had a place in Southern Black worship — the congregation's sound when language runs out — so the record documents a practice, not an experiment.","The slide is played with a pocket knife rather than a bottleneck, which is why the tone scrapes. Listen for how Johnson tunes his humming slightly against the guitar so that voice and slide are never quite in unison — the beating between them is the record's whole texture. In 1977 the track was placed on the Voyager Golden Record and is now travelling out of the solar system.","Ry Cooder called it the most soulful piece on any American record and built his 1984 Paris, Texas score on its sound, which has made it the standard shorthand for desolate American landscape in film. Musicologists point out that it is technically a hymn arrangement, and argue over whether calling it 'the blues' at all is a category error. Nobody argues about its effect.","Listen in the dark, once, without doing anything else. Notice that you cannot tell where the voice stops and the guitar begins. Then hear Ives's The Unanswered Question, another American piece built from one line calling into silence, and Dowland's 'In Darkness Let Me Dwell'."],"blind-willie-johnson-john-the-revelator":["Call and response, stripped to two people. Johnson roars the question — who's that writing? — in a manufactured bass growl, and a woman's clear voice answers, John the Revelator, over a hard chopped guitar rhythm. The verses run through the Book of Revelation and the seven seals in fragments; there is no narrative, only the alternation, repeated until it becomes physical. The guitar keeps a blunt, almost percussive strum and stays out of the way.","Recorded in Atlanta in April 1930, at the last of Johnson's Columbia sessions. The second voice is probably his wife, Willie B. Harris, though the attribution has never been settled from the paperwork. The form — one leader, one responder, no choir — is the smallest possible unit of the congregational singing Johnson had grown up inside. Within months the Depression ended his recording career; he went on preaching on Texas streets until his death in 1945.","The growl is not his speaking voice. Johnson used a false bass for performance, and on the few sides where he sings in his natural register the difference is startling — the roar is a costume, adopted to carry over street noise. Listen also to the woman's part: she is not harmonising but answering, which keeps the record a conversation rather than a duet.","Endlessly covered, from gospel quartets to Depeche Mode and the White Stripes, usually with the call-and-response intact because nothing else will carry the song. Scholarship on Johnson has largely been a matter of recovering basic facts — his birth date, his wife's identity, the manner of his death — from almost no documents. The music, unusually, has never needed rehabilitation.","Listen for the two voices separately, then for the gap between them; the record lives in that gap. Play it after Son House's 'Preachin' the Blues' for the sacred answer to the same question. Tippett's A Child of Our Time, which set spirituals inside an oratorio, shows a third route out of the same tradition."],"lead-belly-goodnight-irene":["A waltz, which is the first surprise: three beats to the bar, gently rocked on a twelve-string guitar tuned low, with a chorus so easy that anyone can join it after one hearing. The second surprise is the verses, which are about a broken marriage, a woman's family, laudanum, and the singer's plan to jump in the river and drown. Lead Belly sings all of it in the same warm, declamatory voice, so the sweetness and the despair arrive together and neither cancels the other.","First recorded in 1933 by John and Alan Lomax on portable equipment at Louisiana's Angola prison, where Ledbetter was serving a sentence; the Lomaxes were collecting for the Library of Congress and believed prisons preserved older songs. Lead Belly did not write it — versions circulated in the South well before — but he made it his, and carried it north when the Lomaxes brought him out as a living exhibit, a role he resented and used.","The verses vary between every recorded take he made; the chorus never does. That is how an oral repertoire actually behaves, and it is visible here across a decade of sessions. Listen too for the twelve-string's low tuning, several steps down, which gives the bass strings their loose boom and let one guitar fill a room without amplification.","The Weavers' smoothed-out 1950 version, with strings and no laudanum, was the biggest-selling record in America that year — six months after Lead Belly died, poor, of ALS. The episode is the standard case study in who profits from folk material. Critics now generally treat his own recordings as the real thing and the hit as a sanitised cover.","Hear a Lomax-era recording before the Weavers' version, and notice which verses disappear. Count the three beats and feel how unusual a waltz is in this repertoire. Then compare Copland's Appalachian Spring, made from the same impulse to fix American vernacular material in place."],"lead-belly-midnight-special":["A prison song driven hard. The twelve-string hammers a rolling bass figure that does not let up, and Lead Belly half-sings, half-shouts verses about Miss Rosie coming to the gate, the sheriff, the Houston jail — and the train whose headlight sweeps across the cell blocks at midnight. The chorus asks the light to shine on him, which folklore read as a wish for release. It is the most physically forceful thing in his repertoire and the clearest demonstration of what one twelve-string can do.","Recorded by John and Alan Lomax in Louisiana in 1934 during their prison-farm collecting trips. The song was old and communal — versions had been noted among Texas convicts years before — and Lead Belly, who had served time in both Texas and Louisiana, sang it from inside the tradition rather than about it. The Lomaxes' portable disc cutter, heavy and temperamental, is why any of this survives at all.","Listen to the guitar rather than the words: the bass strings play a repeating pattern that behaves exactly like a boogie-woogie pianist's left hand, which is where the barrelhouse and the country blues meet. The 'special' is a named train, not a metaphor; several Southern prisons lay close enough to a line for a passing headlight to reach the windows.","It became a standard twice over — first in the folk revival, then in 1969 when Creedence Clearwater Revival turned it into a rock hit, by which point almost no listener connected it to a Louisiana prison farm. Folklorists have argued for decades over the headlight's meaning, with no agreement. The Lomaxes' methods, and their control of the copyrights, remain contested.","Play it loud enough to hear the low strings buzz. Notice how he pushes ahead of the beat on the chorus. Then hear Creedence's version for what electricity adds and subtracts, and Copland's Billy the Kid for the concert-hall version of the same American outlaw material."],"lead-belly-where-did-you-sleep-last-night":["Also known as 'Black Girl' and, further back, as 'In the Pines'. A woman is asked where she slept; she answers, in the pines, where the sun never shines, shivering the whole night. Somewhere in the same song a man's head is found under a train wheel. The two halves are never connected, and Lead Belly does not connect them. He sings it slowly, with long gaps, the twelve-string ringing out between phrases, and holds the word 'shiver' until it breaks.","Recorded in New York in 1944, by which time Lead Belly had spent a decade as a professional performer in the city, sharing stages with Woody Guthrie and Pete Seeger and finding the folk left a more congenial audience than the concert societies that first booked him. The song itself is far older, an Appalachian ballad documented in the 1870s and shared across Black and white Southern traditions long before any recording.","The song is a fragment collection, not a narrative — the head under the train and the night in the pines come from different older songs and were fused by oral transmission. That is why it never resolves, and why it unsettles. Listen for Lead Belly's pauses: he lets the guitar ring into silence between lines, which almost no commercial record of 1944 would have permitted.","Nirvana's 1993 performance on MTV Unplugged introduced it to millions who had never heard of Lead Belly, and Kurt Cobain's closing scream is now, for many listeners, the song's defining sound. Folklorists have traced dozens of variants under three main titles. Debate continues over whether the modern rock reading imports a violence the older versions leave ambiguous.","Listen for the silences, which are as composed as the notes. Then play Nirvana's version back to back and hear how little had to change. Berio's Folk Songs offers the opposite treatment — the same kind of material deliberately rewritten rather than handed on."],"memphis-minnie-when-the-levee-breaks":["Two guitars locked together and two voices trading lines. Minnie and Kansas Joe McCoy play interlocking parts — one keeping a bright steady figure, the other answering in the gaps — with a precision that sounds rehearsed rather than jammed. The words are a flood evacuation from the inside: mean old levee, the singer's home washed down, packing to leave, the thought of going to Chicago and the knowledge that Chicago is nobody's home. No chorus, no rescue, six plain verses.","Recorded in 1929, two years after the Great Mississippi Flood, which had displaced hundreds of thousands and pushed the Great Migration into its second wave. Minnie had come from Walls, Mississippi to Memphis; she and McCoy, then married, were discovered playing in a barbershop and taken to New York to record. The record's subject was still live news, and its politics — Chicago as the only exit — were the actual choice facing its audience.","Listen to how the two guitars divide the labour: neither plays a complete part, and it is only together that the accompaniment makes sense. That interlocking is the country blues moving towards a band sound years before amplification made bands compulsory. Led Zeppelin's 1971 rewrite kept the words and the title and threw away everything else, including both guitar parts.","Overshadowed for decades by the Led Zeppelin version, which is the one most listeners know and which credits Minnie only after litigation-era corrections. Reassessment came with the broader recovery of women in the blues, and Minnie is now generally ranked among the finest guitarists of her generation, not merely the finest woman. Her authorship of the lyric is no longer questioned.","Try to follow one guitar at a time; they are easier to hear apart with headphones. Then play Charley Patton's 'High Water Everywhere' about the same flood, and the Led Zeppelin version, and notice which of the three still sounds like a report from the water's edge."],"memphis-minnie-me-and-my-chauffeur-blues":["Minnie's biggest seller, and a record you can hear the 1950s coming through. She sings about wanting her chauffeur to drive her and nobody else, in a flat, amused voice that leaves the double meaning to the listener, and plays a clean, fast electric lead over a small band's steady shuffle. The solo is the point: single-note lines, bent and phrased like speech, delivered with a confidence that most electric guitarists would not reach for another decade.","Recorded in Chicago in 1941 for OKeh, with her partner Ernest Lawlars. Minnie had been in Chicago for years and had adapted completely to the tight, bass-and-drums studio style the Bluebird and Columbia sessions had standardised. The electric guitar was barely five years old as a commercial instrument; she took it up early and without fuss. Within a decade this sound, amplified further, would be Chicago blues.","Listen to the phrasing of the guitar solo rather than the notes: she breathes between figures exactly as she does when singing, so the instrument answers the vocal in the old country-blues way even though the setting is a modern band. The chauffeur was also a straightforward image of status — a Black woman in 1941 singing about having a driver is making a claim as well as a joke.","A commercial hit in its day, later sidelined by a critical taste that preferred the acoustic country blues to the Chicago studio sound. That preference has been steadily dismantled, and Minnie's electric work is now heard as a direct bridge to Muddy Waters and Chuck Berry. Her standing as a guitarist, rather than a singer who happened to play, took even longer to establish.","Go straight to the guitar solo, then rewind and hear how the vocal phrases the same way. Compare Blind Lemon Jefferson's runs from fifteen years earlier to see what she inherited. Then hear Ellington's Harlem for another portrait of Black urban confidence in the same decade."],"bessie-smith-downhearted-blues":["Voice and piano, nothing else, and the voice does not need anything else. Smith takes a slow twelve-bar song about a woman who has been mistreated once too often and delivers it with enormous, unhurried tone, bending single syllables across two or three pitches before letting them land. The piano stays out of the way, filling the gaps at the end of each line. The last verse turns: she has got the world in a jug and the stopper in her hand, and the threat is entirely credible.","Recorded in February 1923 in New York, Smith's first issued side for Columbia after other labels had turned her down. The song was written by Alberta Hunter and Lovie Austin, two Black women already established in the business. It sold in numbers nobody had forecast and rescued Columbia's finances; within two years the race-record market was a major industry, and the demand it created is what sent scouts to Mississippi looking for Charley Patton.","Listen to the ends of lines. Smith habitually finishes a phrase early and leaves a beat of silence before the piano answers, so the record breathes in a way that band accompaniments would later smooth out. Note also the acoustic recording process: no microphone, everything sung into a horn, which is why the voice sounds close and the piano distant.","The commercial event that defined the classic blues era and, indirectly, funded the country blues recordings that followed. Later critics, chasing authenticity, downgraded the theatre blues as commercial and elevated the Delta singers; that hierarchy has been thoroughly reversed, and Smith's influence on jazz and later on soul singing is now taken as the more consequential line.","Listen to one verse, then sing the same line yourself and hear how much she moves inside a single note. The acoustic sound is thin — let your ear adjust rather than reaching for a later record. Then hear Gershwin's 'Summertime', written a dozen years on by a man who had listened hard to singers like her."],"bessie-smith-st-louis-blues":["The meeting of two traditions on one disc: Smith's voice and Louis Armstrong's cornet, with a reed organ droning underneath instead of a piano. Every line she sings, he answers — not accompanying but replying, sometimes agreeing, sometimes contradicting. W. C. Handy's 1914 song has a tango-flavoured middle strain, which the organ turns solemn and churchy. Nothing is hurried; the whole record is two soloists taking turns and listening to each other, which was not yet a common way to make a record.","Recorded in New York in January 1925 with the twenty-three-year-old Armstrong, who had just arrived from Chicago and was months from his own Hot Five sessions. Handy's song was already the most published blues in America, a Tin Pan Alley property performed by white dance bands. Putting it in the hands of the two greatest Black improvisers then on record was a corrective, and it was Columbia's newly adopted electrical recording era that let their tone survive.","The reed organ is the oddity. It gives the record a harmonium's wheeze — a parlour and chapel sound, not a nightclub one — and turns Handy's dance number into something closer to a lament. Listen also to how Armstrong never plays over her: he waits for the end of every line, which is call and response taken straight out of church and into the studio.","Generally regarded as the definitive recording of the most recorded blues song of all, and as the clearest demonstration of what blues singing gave jazz. Handy's own view of blues authorship — he wrote down and copyrighted material he collected — has been argued over ever since. The record itself has never been seriously challenged.","Follow the cornet alone for one pass and treat the voice as its accompaniment; the record works both ways round. Then compare a white dance-band version of the same song from the same decade. Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige, eighteen years later, is where this conversation goes next."],"bessie-smith-nobody-knows-you-when-youre-down-and-out":["A song about money and friendship, sung with no self-pity at all. Smith had it, spent it, lost it, and reports that the people who drank her liquor are nowhere to be found; she also warns, evenly, that she may be back on top one day. A small band with horns backs her in a slow, almost stately tempo. It is not a twelve-bar blues but a thirty-two-bar popular song, and she sings it like one, holding the long notes and letting the horns fill behind her.","Recorded in New York in May 1929, five months before the Wall Street crash, which gave the lyric a second and much larger life. Jimmy Cox had written it in 1923. Smith's own career was already sliding: the theatre circuits that had made her were closing, talking pictures were taking their audiences, and Columbia dropped her in 1931. She died in 1937 after a car crash in Mississippi, aged forty-three.","Notice that she never sounds beaten. The warning in the final verse — that fortune turns — is delivered with more weight than any of the complaint, which is the reverse of how most singers have since read the song. Listen too for the small band's restraint: the horns answer between phrases and stay silent while she sings, the same discipline Armstrong showed four years earlier.","The Depression made it a standard, and Eric Clapton's 1970 acoustic version made it a rock one; most later readings treat it as a lament, which Smith's does not. Her death, and John Hammond's widely repeated claim about a segregated hospital, became a story of its own — later research has substantially complicated it, and Edward Albee turned the legend into a play.","Listen for the last verse and how her tone changes; everything before it is setup. Compare Clapton's version for what the song loses when it becomes purely sad. Then hear Weill's Threepenny Opera, written the year before, for another performer's clear-eyed account of who has money and who does not."],"ma-rainey-see-see-rider-blues":["The song that fixed a floating folk blues in one shape. Rainey sings heavily and slightly behind the beat about a rider who has gone, and then — calmly, in the last verse — about buying a pistol and shooting him. Behind her sits an exceptional small band: a cornet answering each line, trombone and clarinet weaving around it, piano keeping the floor. The tempo is slow enough to feel like weight rather than pulse. Her voice is rougher and more countrified than the polished singers who followed her.","Recorded in New York in October 1924 for Paramount, with Louis Armstrong on cornet and Fletcher Henderson at the piano — the best jazz players available, put behind a singer who had spent twenty years in Southern tent shows. Rainey had been performing the blues since around 1902, long before anyone recorded it, and she brought the rural idiom into the theatre and then into the studio essentially intact.","Listen for how far behind the beat she sits. Rainey lets the band start the bar and arrives late almost every time, which creates the drag that makes the record feel heavy; it is a tent-show technique for holding a crowd's attention. Armstrong, meanwhile, never fills the same space twice — his answers get shorter as the record goes on.","The song became one of the most recorded in American music, under several spellings, travelling through jazz, western swing and rock. Rainey's own version was long treated as a historical curiosity because of Paramount's poor sound; critical opinion now places her above most of her successors for rhythmic authority. August Wilson's play about a later session has done a good deal to restore her name.","Set a steady beat with your hand and hear how consistently she lands after it. Then follow Armstrong's cornet for a second pass. Compare Bessie Smith's 'St. Louis Blues' from a few months later, with the same cornet player and a completely different singer's approach."],"ma-rainey-prove-it-on-me-blues":["A jug band backs her — washboard, kazoo, guitar, the sound of a street corner rather than a theatre — while Rainey sings about going out with a crowd of friends who must have been women, since she does not like men, and wearing a collar and a tie. The refrain is a dare: they say she does it, but nobody can prove it on her. She sings it lightly, almost teasingly, and the cheap rattling band makes the whole thing sound like gossip set to music.","Recorded for Paramount in 1928. Rainey had been arrested in Chicago some years earlier after a party involving women in her chorus, a story that circulated in the business. Paramount advertised the record with a drawing of a woman in a man's jacket courting two women while a policeman watches from across the street — one of the most openly queer pieces of commercial art in 1920s America, and one the label clearly thought would sell.","The jug-band accompaniment is a choice, not a budget. Rainey could command the best jazz players in Chicago; putting a washboard and kazoo behind a lyric this pointed frames it as folk talk rather than a theatrical number, which is exactly what lets it get away with the subject. Listen for how the kazoo mocks the vocal line at the end of each verse.","Ignored for half a century, then recovered by scholars of Black women's blues as one of the earliest first-person queer statements in recorded American music; Angela Davis's work on Rainey and Smith put it at the centre of the argument. Debate continues over how much is autobiography and how much is stage persona, and whether that distinction meant much to Rainey.","Read the lyric before you listen — the diction is old and the pressing is rough. Then listen for her tone, which is amused rather than defiant. Pair it with Weill's Threepenny Opera for another 1920s stage voice that says the unsayable and lets the audience decide what to do with it."],"muddy-waters-i-cant-be-satisfied":["Just two musicians: Muddy Waters singing and playing slide on an electric guitar, and Big Crawford slapping an upright bass behind him. The song is a Delta blues about a restless man ready to leave, but the sound is new. The bottleneck buzzes and rings through the amplifier with a bite no acoustic guitar could give, and the bass pushes the beat forward so the whole record swings like a dance tune rather than a porch lament.","Muddy had been recorded on Stovall Plantation in Mississippi by Alan Lomax and John Work for the Library of Congress in 1941 and 1942, and had moved to Chicago in 1943, driving a truck by day and playing house parties at night. Leonard and Phil Chess, Polish-Jewish immigrants who ran a South Side club, had started the Aristocrat label. The record was cut in 1948 and reportedly sold out its first pressing in Chicago within a day, to migrants who heard home in it.","This is a remake. Muddy had sung a version of the same song for Lomax on Stovall Plantation, played on an acoustic guitar in the old style. Comparing the two is like watching the Great Migration happen in six years: the melody and words barely change, but the electric slide cuts sharper, the tempo quickens, and the bass turns a solitary field song into something a city crowd can move to.","Leonard Chess reportedly doubted the record would sell, since it sounded so rural; its immediate success in Chicago's Black neighbourhoods changed his mind and set the direction of the label. Historians now treat it as the hinge between the Delta and the Chicago blues. What is still argued is how much of the new sound was invention and how much simply the necessity of being heard over a noisy tavern crowd.","Start by listening only to the slide: every sung line is answered by a sliding phrase, as if a second voice were agreeing. Then play Son House's 'My Black Mama' and hear where it came from. Seek a Chess anthology of Muddy's early sides; the 1941 Library of Congress version makes the perfect companion."],"muddy-waters-rollin-stone":["A single voice and a single electric guitar, playing one heavy, repeating riff that barely changes for three minutes. The song imagines the singer as a catfish and ranges over wandering, desire and a restless life. There is no chord change in the usual sense: the guitar hammers the same low figure, lets it ring, and answers each line with a short sting. The effect is closer to a trance than a tune, and the amplified guitar thickens every note.","Recorded in Chicago in 1950 for Chess, the new label the Chess brothers launched that year after Aristocrat, it was among the first records issued under the Chess name. The song is Muddy's version of a widely shared Mississippi piece usually called 'Catfish Blues', recorded by many Delta singers. By 1950 Muddy was already playing with a full band in clubs, but Chess still preferred to record him nearly alone, as on his first hits.","The name travelled further than the song. A young Brian Jones, needing a band name for a gig in London in 1962, is said to have looked at a Muddy Waters record and taken 'Rollin' Stone' from it; the magazine Rolling Stone took its name partly from the same source. Listen, too, for how Muddy slightly delays the riff's return, making the listener lean forward.","A modest regional seller on release, it has grown in reputation as the purest document of Muddy's early electric style before the band took over. Critics note that it is less an original composition than a local standard made definitive. Some later listeners find it monotonous; most hear the monotony as the point, the Mississippi drone that John Lee Hooker and Howlin' Wolf also built on.","Turn it up and let the riff settle into your body before listening to the words. Then hear John Lee Hooker's 'Boogie Chillen'', recorded two years earlier, for a different take on the one-chord trance. The Chess box sets and 'The Anthology' collection carry clean transfers."],"muddy-waters-hoochie-coochie-man":["The full Chicago band at its peak: Muddy singing, Little Walter's amplified harmonica, Otis Spann on piano, Jimmy Rogers on second guitar, Willie Dixon on bass and Fred Below on drums. The record is built on a stop-time riff: the band plays a short, heavy five-note figure, then falls silent, leaving Muddy's voice alone to make another boast about his birth, his charms and his power. Then the band crashes back in for the refrain.","Willie Dixon, Chess's house bassist and songwriter, wrote the song and reportedly taught it to Muddy in a club washroom during an intermission; Muddy tried it that same night and the crowd demanded it again. It was recorded in Chicago in January 1954 and became one of his biggest sellers. The hoodoo imagery, gypsy prophecy, black cat bone, mojo, was drawn from Southern folk magic well known to his migrant audience.","That stop-time riff is the whole architecture of later hard rock: a band hitting together and then leaving a hole. Listen to Little Walter's harmonica fills between the vocal lines; he is not simply decorating but answering, like a second singer. And notice that the song's supernatural swagger is played half as comedy; Muddy smiles through the boasts, and live audiences laughed along.","It made Dixon Chess's key writer and Muddy the undisputed king of the South Side. The riff and the boast format spawned direct answers within a year: Bo Diddley's 'I'm a Man', then Muddy's own 'Mannish Boy'. British bands of the 1960s covered it constantly, and it is now read as a founding text of the rock persona. Some scholars caution against treating its hoodoo as authentic folk religion rather than theatre.","Count the silences: every time the band stops, feel how long Muddy holds the space. Then listen for Otis Spann's piano rippling quietly underneath. Afterwards play Mannish Boy to hear the answer-song. The Chess single remaster in 'The Anthology' is ideal; avoid the thinner reprocessed stereo versions."],"muddy-waters-mannish-boy":["A single stop-time riff repeated for the whole song, with no chord changes at all. Muddy declares, over and over, that he is a grown man, spelling the word out letter by letter while voices in the studio whoop and shout agreement. There is almost no melody; the song is rhythm, voice and attitude, a call-and-response between the singer and a crowd, with harmonica wailing in the gaps.","This was the third move in a Chicago conversation. Willie Dixon's 'Hoochie Coochie Man' (1954) inspired Bo Diddley's 'I'm a Man' (1955) on Chess's Checker label, and Muddy answered Bo within months with this, recorded in May 1955. The spelled-out word echoes the everyday insult of Black men being called 'boy' in the Jim Crow South; the claim to manhood carried a charge beyond sexual boast.","The best-known version is not this one. In 1977 Muddy recut the song for the album Hard Again, produced by the Texas guitarist Johnny Winter, whose excited shouts are all over it; that take was later used in films and advertisements, so many listeners have never heard the tighter 1955 original. Compare the two to hear the same song as a young man's challenge and an old man's victory lap.","Seen at first as a novelty answer record, it has become one of Muddy's defining songs, largely through the 1977 remake and through its use in film. Critics debate whether its power lies in the riff or in the social meaning of the word being spelled out. Its credit, shared between Muddy, Mel London and Bo Diddley, reflects how freely Chicago musicians borrowed from one another.","Listen first for the shouts behind the voice; they turn the record into a sermon with a congregation. Then play the 1977 Hard Again version and hear Muddy in full command at sixty-two. A double bill with Hoochie Coochie Man shows how a riff can be stripped down until only its bones remain."],"muddy-waters-at-newport-1960":["A live set by Muddy's touring band at the Newport Jazz Festival, with Otis Spann on piano and James Cotton on harmonica. The record opens with a fast, driving band sound and peaks with 'Got My Mojo Working', played once, then again at a crowd's insistence, faster and wilder, with Muddy visibly dancing. It closes with Spann singing a blues farewell to the festival. Short, loud, joyful: a club band playing to a lawn of white jazz fans for the first time.","Recorded on 3 July 1960 in Newport, Rhode Island. The festival, a genteel jazz event, had just been shaken by rioting crowds, and the city ended the event early; Muddy's set was among the last. The closing 'Goodbye Newport Blues' had words written on the spot by the poet Langston Hughes. The album came out as the folk revival was starting to look for 'authentic' Black music among college audiences.","'Got My Mojo Working' was not Muddy's song; it had been recorded first in 1956 by the singer Ann Cole, and Muddy reworked it. The second, encore performance is the one to listen to: the band accelerates, Cotton's harmonica duels with Muddy, and you can hear the moment the crowd stops being polite. Spann, whose piano holds the whole set together, is barely credited on the original sleeve.","It was among the first live blues albums and one of the first to reach a mainly white audience, and it circulated in Britain among the young players who would form the Rolling Stones and the Yardbirds. Critics have argued over whether Newport marked Muddy's discovery or his packaging for outsiders. Few argue about the performance itself, which remains the best document of his band on stage.","Go straight to the two versions of 'Got My Mojo Working' back to back and hear what an audience does to a band. Then return to the start and follow Spann's piano rather than the singer. Newer reissues add material and correct the sound; seek the expanded edition if possible."],"muddy-waters-folk-singer":["Muddy unplugs. Nine songs played on acoustic guitar, with a young Buddy Guy on second guitar, Willie Dixon on bass and a whisper of drums. The tempos are slow, the room is quiet, and every detail of the voice and fingers is audible: the rasp of the slide on the strings, the thump of a thumb on the bass notes. It sounds like a return to Mississippi, made with Chicago's precision.","Recorded at Chess studios in September 1963 and released in 1964, at the height of the folk revival, when white college audiences prized acoustic blues as authentic and electric blues as commercial. Chess, never shy of marketing, packaged Muddy accordingly. The engineer Ron Malo captured the small group with unusual clarity, and the record was designed as much for hi-fi listeners as for jukeboxes.","Buddy Guy, who would become one of the loudest guitarists in Chicago, plays here with extraordinary restraint, answering Muddy in soft, precise figures. Listen for the way the two guitars never double each other: one keeps time, the other comments. And listen to the silences between phrases, far longer than any Muddy single; the album is built from space.","Some listeners in the 1960s saw it as a cynical costume change for the folk market, and there is truth in the packaging. But the performances have outlasted the marketing, and the album is now widely regarded as one of the finest acoustic blues records of its decade, prized for recorded sound as much as for singing. It rewards comparison with his 1941 plantation recordings.","Listen in a quiet room, ideally on good speakers or headphones; the sound itself is half the pleasure. Start with the long, slow songs and notice how little happens and how much that little carries. Then play Son House's 1965 'Death Letter Blues' for the Delta original of this bare style."],"howlin-wolf-moanin-at-midnight":["The record opens with Wolf humming a low, wordless moan, deep in his chest, before the band enters on a single droning riff. Willie Johnson's guitar is loud and distorted, the drums thud, and Wolf alternates between singing and blowing raw harmonica. The song, about a restless man woken in the night by trouble, is almost all atmosphere: one chord, a menacing pulse, and a voice like gravel in a bucket.","Recorded in Memphis in 1951 at Sam Phillips's Memphis Recording Service, three years before Phillips recorded Elvis Presley. Phillips leased the masters to Chess in Chicago; meanwhile Ike Turner took Wolf to record for the rival Modern/RPM label, and the two companies fought over him until Chess won and Wolf moved north in 1952. Wolf was then a radio personality in West Memphis, Arkansas, and over forty.","Listen to the guitar tone: Willie Johnson's overdriven sound, probably from a small amplifier pushed past its limit, is one of the earliest heavily distorted guitars on record. Its B-side, 'How Many More Years', gave Led Zeppelin the frame for 'How Many More Times' in 1969. Sam Phillips later said that finding Howlin' Wolf mattered more to him than any other discovery, Elvis included.","It was a hit on the rhythm-and-blues market and launched Wolf's career. Today it is admired as a missing link between the Delta drone of Charley Patton, from whom Wolf learned, and the heavy riffs of the 1960s. Its crudeness, once a reason for dismissal, is now heard as a raw power the more polished Chicago studios would partly tame.","Start with the opening hum; it tells you everything. Then follow the guitar and notice how it growls rather than rings. Play Charley Patton's 'Pony Blues' next to hear where Wolf's voice came from. Collections of the Memphis Sun recordings give the rawest sound."],"howlin-wolf-smokestack-lightnin":["One chord, one circling guitar riff from Hubert Sumlin, and a groove that rocks like a train on a long straight track. Wolf sings loosely connected images of a train at night and a lover gone, and between lines he throws back his head and lets out a falsetto howl, the sound that gave him his name. There is no conventional blues progression at all; the song simply rolls forward until it stops.","Recorded in Chicago in 1956 for Chess, after Wolf had built a steady band there. He had been singing versions of the song since the 1930s in Mississippi, and it grew from the Delta style of Charley Patton and Tommy Johnson, whose falsetto yodel Wolf adapted into his howl. Sumlin, a young guitarist Wolf treated almost as a son, gave the band its angular, unpredictable sound.","The howl is borrowed and transformed: Tommy Johnson's 1920s falsetto and even Jimmie Rodgers's country yodel lie behind it; Wolf said he wanted to yodel like Rodgers but it came out as a howl. Listen to how Sumlin's riff subtly changes each time round, never quite repeating, so the drone never goes dead.","A rhythm-and-blues hit in 1956, it became one of the most covered blues songs of the 1960s rock boom, recorded by the Yardbirds, the Animals and many more. Critics treat it as proof that the modal, one-chord Delta blues could survive in the city. Wolf's own performances remain unrivalled; most covers sound polite beside them.","Listen for the howl and count how many times he uses it; he rations it. Then focus on Sumlin's guitar and hear the small variations. Play a Charley Patton record straight after, then a Yardbirds version, for the full journey. Any good Chess compilation will do."],"howlin-wolf-howlin-wolf-rocking-chair":["A dozen singles from 1959 to 1962, most written by Willie Dixon, gathered on an LP whose cover shows only an acoustic guitar leaning on a rocking chair. It holds 'Spoonful', 'Back Door Man', 'Little Red Rooster', 'Wang Dang Doodle', 'Shake for Me' and 'Down in the Bottom'. The band is tight and heavy, Hubert Sumlin's guitar darts and stabs, and Wolf's voice ranges from a purr to a roar, sometimes in one line.","By the late 1950s Wolf and Muddy Waters were bitter rivals at Chess, and Dixon, who wrote for both, fed Wolf some of his strongest material. Chess assembled the album from singles for the growing LP market in 1962, the year before British bands began to discover these songs. Hubert Sumlin, now mature, had become the most original guitarist in Chicago.","Wolf reportedly disliked several of these Dixon songs, 'Wang Dang Doodle' especially, calling it old-fashioned; you can hear him slightly ironic in the delivery. The one-chord 'Spoonful' is almost entirely riff and voice, the song as ritual. Notice too Sumlin's solo on 'Shake for Me', squeezed and quirky, which young guitarists copied note by note.","The album became a bible for British bands: the Rolling Stones took 'Little Red Rooster' to the top of the British chart in 1964, Cream turned 'Spoonful' into long improvisations, and the Doors recorded 'Back Door Man'. It regularly appears in lists of the greatest albums of any kind. Debate continues over how fairly Dixon and Chess credited and paid the performers who made these songs famous.","Start with 'Spoonful' and let the riff hypnotise you, then play 'Shake for Me' for Sumlin. Afterwards hear Cream's live 'Spoonful' to see how far the rock bands stretched it. Any remaster of the original Chess LP; the mono mixes hit hardest."],"howlin-wolf-killing-floor":["A fast, bouncing riff from Hubert Sumlin, doubled by bass and horns, drives this song about a man who regrets not leaving a bad relationship sooner. Unlike Wolf's hypnotic one-chord pieces, this is taut and quick, almost a soul record in its rhythm, with the band snapping on every accent. Wolf sings with controlled fury, and Sumlin's jagged fills cut across the beat.","Recorded in Chicago in 1964, when soul music was reshaping Black radio and Chess was pushing its blues artists toward sharper, more danceable arrangements. Wolf wrote the song himself. The phrase 'killing floor', meaning the slaughterhouse floor in the Chicago stockyards, was also a Delta figure of speech for rock bottom, used in Skip James's 1931 'Hard Time Killing Floor Blues'.","Jimi Hendrix opened his famous Monterey Pop Festival set in 1967 with this song, played at double speed, and Led Zeppelin reworked it as 'The Lemon Song' in 1969 without credit; a settlement later added Wolf's name. Listen for how Sumlin's riff stays the same while the chords move beneath it, a trick rock bands would lean on for decades.","Less celebrated at first than Wolf's 1950s classics, it gained stature through the rock covers, especially Hendrix's. Critics now hear it as proof that Wolf and Sumlin could modernise without dilution. The Led Zeppelin episode is often cited in the long argument about white rock bands profiting from Black blues writers.","Listen for the riff and try humming along; it is simple and restless. Then hear Hendrix's Monterey version and Zeppelin's 'The Lemon Song' to see how far it travelled. Skip James's 1931 record completes the lineage of the phrase."],"willie-dixon-i-am-the-blues":["The songwriter sings his own songbook. Willie Dixon, with a small Chicago band, performs nine of the songs he wrote for others: 'Back Door Man', 'I Can't Quit You Baby', 'The Seventh Son', 'Spoonful', 'I Ain't Superstitious', 'You Shook Me', 'Hoochie Coochie Man', 'The Little Red Rooster' and 'The Same Thing'. His voice is warm, rounded and knowing, the voice of a storyteller more than a shouter, and the arrangements are relaxed.","By 1970 the Chess era was ending and Dixon's songs were earning money for British and American rock bands: Led Zeppelin had recorded 'You Shook Me' and 'I Can't Quit You Baby' on their 1969 debut. Columbia, a major label, invited Dixon to record his own versions. He was also touring Europe with the Chicago Blues All-Stars and beginning his long campaign for songwriters' rights.","The album's title is a claim of authorship in a business that had often denied it. Listen to how Dixon sings 'Hoochie Coochie Man' not as a boast but almost as a smiling folk tale; the performer's swagger was always a role he wrote for someone else. His delivery reveals the gospel quartet training of his youth in every phrase.","Dixon was never regarded as a great singer, and some critics find these versions pale beside Muddy's and Wolf's. But the album is valued as a composer's statement, and its title has become shorthand for his place in history. In 1985 Dixon settled with Led Zeppelin over 'Whole Lotta Love', which borrowed from his 'You Need Love'; the album sits at the start of that reckoning.","Play each song beside the famous version: Muddy for 'Hoochie Coochie Man', Wolf for 'Spoonful' and 'Back Door Man', Otis Rush for 'I Can't Quit You Baby'. The contrast shows what a writer hears in his own song. Start with 'The Seventh Son'."],"little-walter-juke":["An instrumental for amplified harmonica and Muddy Waters's band, fast and swinging. Walter plays the harmonica like a jazz saxophone: riffing, bending notes, sliding into long, fat, distorted tones that seem too big for such a small instrument. The guitars chop a boogie rhythm underneath, and the drums shuffle. There is no singing and no need for it; the harmonica does all the talking.","Recorded in Chicago in May 1952 at the end of a Muddy Waters session, using Muddy's band, it was issued on Chess's new Checker label and went to the top of the rhythm-and-blues chart, a rare feat for a harmonica instrumental. Walter left Muddy's band almost at once to lead his own group. The record was a theme he had used to open the band's club sets.","The sound is the invention. Walter cupped the harmonica tightly against a cheap microphone meant for public-address systems and ran it into a guitar amplifier, pushing it into distortion; the cupping lets him shape the tone like a trumpet mute. Listen to the long held notes near the end, bent and swelling, which no acoustic harmonica could sustain.","'Juke' made Walter a star and changed the harmonica for good; almost every later blues and rock harmonica player, from James Cotton to Paul Butterfield, followed his amplified route. Critics sometimes rank him among the most original instrumentalists in any postwar American music, jazz included. The record's success also showed Chess that instrumentals and side musicians could sell.","Listen once for the harmonica tone alone; it sounds less like a mouth organ than a horn section. Then notice how Walter phrases in short riffs like a swing saxophonist. Follow with 'My Babe' for his singing, and with Junior Wells for the next generation."],"little-walter-my-babe":["A light, bouncing song about a woman who will not stand for any nonsense, sung by Little Walter over a stop-and-go band groove, with his harmonica answering each line. It is short, cheerful and catchy, closer to rhythm and blues than to the heavy Chicago sound, and it shows Walter as a relaxed, likable singer as well as a harmonica innovator.","Willie Dixon wrote it, and it gave Walter a number-one rhythm-and-blues hit in 1955. The tune is taken from the gospel song 'This Train (Is Bound for Glory)', which Sister Rosetta Tharpe had recorded, and turning sacred songs into secular ones was standard practice in the 1950s, as Ray Charles was doing at the same moment.","Once you know the gospel original, you cannot unhear it: the melody of the sacred train song has simply been given new words. Listen for the stop-time breaks, where the band cuts out and Walter's voice or harmonica fills the silence, the same device Dixon used in 'Hoochie Coochie Man'. Walter reportedly resisted recording it; Dixon persisted.","It was Walter's biggest vocal hit and was widely covered, by Ricky Nelson, Elvis Presley in concert and many British groups. Blues purists sometimes rank it below his instrumentals as too pop. It is now valued as an example of the sacred-to-secular exchange at the root of rhythm and blues and soul.","Listen to Sister Rosetta Tharpe's 'This Train' first, then this. Then notice how Walter's harmonica acts as a second vocal line. Pair with 'Juke' to hear both sides of his talent."],"elmore-james-dust-my-broom":["It opens with the most famous slide-guitar figure in the blues: a rolling, triplet run up the neck, hammered out on an electric guitar at full volume. Elmore James sings about leaving a lover and heading out at dawn, his voice strained and urgent, and repeats the slide riff at the start of each verse. The harmonica of Sonny Boy Williamson II wails behind him. The whole record sounds like it is about to boil over.","Recorded in August 1951 in Jackson, Mississippi, for Trumpet Records, a small label run by Lillian McMurry. James had long played Robert Johnson's 1936 'I Believe I'll Dust My Broom' in juke joints. It became a hit in 1952, and he soon moved to Chicago, recording the riff in many variants for different labels for the rest of his career.","The riff is Robert Johnson's, but the sound is new. Elmore, a radio repairman, modified his amplifiers and pickups to make them louder and dirtier, and the resulting distortion is part of the composition. Listen for the pounding rhythm on beat four, and for how the slide notes buzz and break up.","The record made James a star in the blues market and fixed the riff for generations: Brian Jones, Duane Allman and Jeremy Spencer of Fleetwood Mac all learned it; Spencer built a stage persona around it. Some critics find James repetitive, since he rerecorded the figure so often. Most hear it as a signature as distinct as any in music.","Play Robert Johnson's 1936 version first, then this; hear an acoustic idea turn electric. Next, listen to the slide opening again and notice how much volume and grit change its emotional meaning. Compilations of his Trumpet and Chicago sides are easy to find."],"elmore-james-the-sky-is-crying":["A slow blues in which rain streaming down a street becomes the image of a broken heart. Elmore James's slide guitar weeps rather than rolls here, with long, bent notes swelling behind the voice, and his singing is at its most anguished. A saxophone section breathes softly beneath. It is short and simple, and it proves he was far more than a one-riff player.","Recorded in Chicago in November 1959 for Bobby Robinson's Fire label, based in New York, and released in 1960. James was in failing health from heart disease and moving between Chicago, Mississippi and New York sessions. The song reportedly came from an actual downpour during the session. He died in 1963, aged forty-five.","The slide tone here is warmer and more vocal than in 'Dust My Broom'; listen for how he drags the slide slowly into each note rather than striking it. The simple words carry real grief when you know how ill James was when he sang them. The song's structure is the standard twelve-bar blues, used with no decoration at all.","It was a rhythm-and-blues hit and has become a standard, recorded by Albert King, Stevie Ray Vaughan, George Harrison and Eric Clapton among others. Critics regard it as James's finest slow blues. Discussions of his late work often mourn how little recording time he had with good bands before his early death.","Listen for the first slide phrase and the way it seems to weep. Then hear Stevie Ray Vaughan's version for a rock guitarist's approach. Play it after 'Dust My Broom' to hear both sides of Elmore: the whirlwind and the storm cloud."],"bb-king-three-oclock-blues":["A slow blues about a sleepless man at three in the morning, wondering where his woman has gone. B.B. King's guitar opens with a short, clear, single-note phrase and answers each vocal line with another: not chords but melody, sung on the strings. A small horn section and piano provide a soft bed. His voice is young and pleading, full of gospel inflection.","The song was written and first recorded by the West Coast bluesman Lowell Fulson in 1948. King, then a disc jockey on WDIA, the Memphis radio station programmed for a Black audience, recorded it in Memphis in 1951 for the Bihari brothers' RPM label, reportedly in a makeshift studio at a YMCA. It went to the top of the rhythm-and-blues chart in early 1952 and launched his touring career.","Listen for King's left-hand vibrato, still a little shy here: the note trembles as he shakes the string. It was his attempt to imitate the slide-guitar sound of his cousin Bukka White, which he could never master, so he made his fingers do what the slide did. That substitution became the most imitated sound in blues guitar.","It made King a national figure in the rhythm-and-blues market; for the next decade he toured hundreds of nights a year. Critics now see it as the foundation of his style: single-string, vocal, sparing. The debt to T-Bone Walker and to jazz guitar is widely acknowledged, as is the Delta influence through Bukka White.","Listen to how few notes King plays and how long he lets each hang. Then hear Lowell Fulson's original for contrast. Move on to Live at the Regal to hear what this young man became."],"bb-king-live-at-the-regal":["B.B. King and his touring band on a single night before a Black Chicago audience. The set moves without pause from fast shuffles to slow blues, King's guitar Lucille darting between vocal lines, the horns punching accents, and the crowd shrieking, laughing and answering throughout. Songs include 'Every Day I Have the Blues', 'Sweet Little Angel', 'It's My Own Fault' and 'How Blue Can You Get?', whose punchline sets off the loudest roar on the record.","Recorded on 21 November 1964 at the Regal Theater on Chicago's South Side, a grand venue on the Black entertainment circuit, and released by ABC in 1965. King was then playing chitlin' circuit dates almost nightly for Black audiences and had not yet been embraced by white rock fans. The album caught him in his natural element, before the Fillmore and Las Vegas years.","Listen to the audience as an instrument. King times his pauses to their reactions, holding a guitar phrase until the screaming peaks, then sliding into the next line. On 'How Blue Can You Get?' he builds a list of grievances to a comic final line, and the band holds a silence just long enough for it to land. It is showmanship as precise as any stand-up comedian's.","Sales were modest at first, but guitarists passed it around: Eric Clapton, Mike Bloomfield and others have named it essential study. It is now routinely listed among the greatest live albums in any genre. Some critics prefer the rougher 1971 Live in Cook County Jail, but Regal remains the reference for King at his peak.","Listen straight through, as a concert, with the volume up. Pay attention to how short King's guitar solos are and how the crowd responds to single notes. Then play Muddy at Newport for a contrasting live audience."],"bb-king-the-thrill-is-gone":["A slow minor-key blues about the calm, final end of a love affair, set not in a nightclub but in a cool, spacious studio sound with a string section. King's guitar is at its most expressive: short phrases, long bends, that trembling vibrato. The strings enter quietly and swell. It is the most elegant record of his career, as close to pop balladry as to the blues.","Written and first recorded in 1951 by Roy Hawkins and Rick Darnell, the song was recut by King in 1969 for the album Completely Well, produced by Bill Szymczyk, with strings arranged by Bert de Coteaux. King was now crossing to white rock audiences through Fillmore concerts, and the record crossed to the pop charts and won a Grammy.","The strings were added after the band was recorded, and King reportedly did not expect them. Listen to how they enter only in the later part, doubling the melancholy rather than sweetening it. The minor key is unusual for King; it gives the song a coolness quite unlike his usual gospel warmth.","It became King's signature song and his biggest crossover hit. Purists grumbled about the strings, but the record brought him to a mass audience that sustained him for four decades. It is now considered among the finest blues recordings of the rock era, and a model of how orchestral colour can serve the blues.","Listen for the moment the strings come in; notice how they change the song's colour without changing its shape. Then listen to the original 1951 Roy Hawkins version. The album cut, over five minutes, is preferable to the edited single."],"albert-king-born-under-a-bad-sign":["Albert King's guitar over the Stax house band: Booker T. and the MG's with the Memphis Horns. The title song rides a sinuous bass-and-horns riff, and King tells of a lifetime of bad luck. His guitar enters with enormous bends, a note pushed up until it screams, and his soft voice contrasts with the ferocity of his playing. The album also includes 'Crosscut Saw', 'The Hunter', 'Personal Manager' and 'As the Years Go Passing By'.","Recorded at Stax in Memphis in 1966 and 1967 and assembled as an album in 1967. Stax, an integrated soul label, set King's blues over tight Southern soul arrangements, a new sound for the blues. The title song was written by Booker T. Jones and William Bell. King was then in his forties, a heavy-set guitarist who played a Flying V left-handed, upside down.","King's strings were set for a right-handed player, but he held the guitar left-handed, so his thinnest string sat on top. This lets him pull strings down rather than push up, bending them farther than anyone else. Listen for bends that go past the note you expect. Eric Clapton's solo in Cream's 'Strange Brew' (1967) copies King's phrasing almost note for note.","Cream recorded 'Born Under a Bad Sign' in 1968, and the album spread through rock guitar circles. It is now regarded as one of the most important electric blues albums, and as a model of blues meeting soul. Critics note that the arrangement's tightness, sometimes seen as confining, is what gives King's big bends their shock.","Start with the title song and focus on the first guitar bend. Then hear Cream's version, and then Clapton's 'Strange Brew' solo, to recognise King's phrases in rock. Follow with Stevie Ray Vaughan to hear the next generation."],"albert-king-live-wire-blues-power":["Albert King in concert, stretching out. Long instrumentals and slow blues give his guitar space to develop ideas across many choruses. On the title piece he talks to the audience between phrases, explaining what the blues is and why it matters, and the guitar answers. The band is smaller and looser than on the Stax studio records, and the atmosphere is intimate despite the volume.","Recorded in June 1968 at the Fillmore Auditorium in San Francisco, booked by the promoter Bill Graham, who put blues artists on bills alongside psychedelic rock bands. The audience was largely young and white; King was introduced to them as a master, and he responded with extended solos suited to the rock-concert setting. Released by Stax later that year.","Listen to how King builds a solo in the long instrumentals: he repeats a short phrase, bending it a little further each time, until tension becomes almost unbearable. Rock guitarists of the Fillmore era learned that approach here. Also notice his quiet talking between phrases; he is teaching the room.","A strong seller for Stax, it confirmed King's standing with rock audiences. Some critics find the long solos indulgent compared with his tight studio sides, while guitar players tend to prize them for exactly that reason. It is now seen as a key document of the moment blues met psychedelia on its own terms.","Begin with 'Blues Power' and follow the spoken asides as well as the guitar. Then try one of the long instrumentals and count how many times a phrase is repeated before it changes. Compare with B.B. King's Live at the Regal for a very different audience."],"freddie-king-hide-away":["A bright, bouncing guitar instrumental in a shuffle rhythm, built from several catchy sections strung together like a medley: a stop-time opening, a walking-bass boogie, and snatches of other tunes. Freddie King's guitar rings hard and clear, attacked with picks on thumb and finger. It is short, jaunty and made to dance to, with not a vocal in sight.","Recorded in Cincinnati in 1960 for Federal, part of Syd Nathan's King Records, and released in 1961. King adapted it from a tune played by the Chicago slide guitarist Hound Dog Taylor and named it after Mel's Hide Away Lounge on the West Side. Instrumentals were in vogue, and the record crossed over to the pop chart.","Listen for the quoted snippets: part of the tune borrows the riff of Henry Mancini's television theme 'Peter Gunn', turned into a blues. The stop-time opening became a test piece for young guitarists; Eric Clapton recorded it as 'Hideaway' with John Mayall's Bluesbreakers in 1966, his guitar sound on that album widely regarded as a turning point for British rock guitar.","A hit in its day, it became a standard of every bar band and blues jam. Critics see it as the start of a line of blues-rock guitar instrumentals, and as proof that King deserves a place beside B.B. and Albert King as one of the 'three Kings' of electric blues guitar. It is sometimes dismissed as a novelty, but guitarists never have.","Listen for each new section and try to hear where one tune ends and the next begins. Then play Clapton's 1966 version with Mayall to hear how a British player absorbed it. Follow with 'Have You Ever Loved a Woman' for King's singing."],"freddie-king-have-you-ever-loved-a-woman":["A slow blues about loving a woman who belongs to one's best friend, sung with gospel force. Freddie King's voice rises and breaks, and his guitar fills each space with stinging, ringing notes. The arrangement is spare: piano, bass and drums hold the slow pulse, and the guitar provides all the drama. It is short and intense, with an emotional climax that arrives far sooner than a listener expects.","Written by Billy Myles and recorded in 1960 for Federal in Cincinnati, it was among King's first singles. He was then working in Chicago clubs, where Chess had declined to sign him, reportedly finding his guitar too close to B.B. King's. Federal gave him the chance to record both instrumentals and vocal blues.","The song's situation, loving your best friend's wife, is exactly the plot Eric Clapton was living in 1970, and he recorded it with Derek and the Dominos on the album Layla, alongside songs written for Pattie Boyd. Knowing that, listen to how King's version feels less like confession and more like story; the personal meaning was added later.","Less famous than 'Hide Away', it is now considered one of King's finest vocal performances. Clapton's recording kept it alive, and critics note it as an example of British rockers transforming a Chicago blues into autobiography. King's own later live versions, stretched out and louder, are also prized.","Listen first for the singing: the break in King's voice is as powerful as any guitar. Then play Clapton's version on Layla for a rock treatment. King's live versions from the 1970s show how he himself grew the song."],"buddy-guy-first-time-i-met-the-blues":["A slow blues in which the blues itself is personified as a figure who comes walking through the woods toward the singer. Buddy Guy's voice rises to a piercing scream, and his guitar snaps and stings between lines, loud and nervy, with notes that jump out of the mix. The band plays quietly behind him, making his outbursts even more startling.","The song was written by the pianist Little Brother Montgomery and recorded by him in 1936. Guy, newly arrived in Chicago from Louisiana, cut it for Chess in 1960, early in a period when the label used him mostly as a session guitarist. The West Side of Chicago was then producing a younger, more aggressive blues style than Muddy Waters's South Side sound.","Listen to the extreme dynamics: Guy shifts from a whisper to a shriek with no warning, both in voice and guitar. This was his stage style too, which Chess found hard to capture. Hendrix, who saw Guy play in the 1960s, adopted much of that theatricality. The personification of the blues as a stranger walking up is old Delta imagery, from Montgomery's generation.","It sold modestly but became a touchstone among guitarists. Critics now see it as an early glimpse of the intensity that British and American rock players would copy. Guy has said that Chess wanted him to play quietly; this record shows what happened when he did not.","Turn it up and listen for the sudden scream in the voice and the answering sting of the guitar. Then play Little Brother Montgomery's 1936 version for the piano original. Guy's later live versions go even further."],"buddy-guy-damn-right-ive-got-the-blues":["A big, polished blues-rock album, with Buddy Guy's guitar and voice front and centre and guest appearances by Eric Clapton, Jeff Beck and Mark Knopfler. The title song is a declaration of identity; other tracks range from slow blues to rock-edged shuffles. Guy's guitar is louder and wilder than ever, full of screaming bends and sudden leaps, and his voice has aged into a rough, commanding instrument.","By the late 1980s Guy was running his own club in Chicago but had not made a major-label album in years. The British label Silvertone signed him, and producer John Porter built the album around his reputation among rock guitarists. It won a Grammy and began a late-career revival that lasted three decades, making Guy the public face of the Chicago blues after Muddy's death.","Listen to how the guests play differently from Guy: Clapton cleaner and more controlled, Beck more abstract, Knopfler cool and precise. Guy is the one who takes the most risks. The album's opening track, the title song, quotes the attitude of his early Chess records, but now he is the elder, not the upstart.","Commercially his biggest success, it was welcomed as long-overdue recognition. Some blues critics found the rock production too glossy; others argued it was the only way to reach a new audience. Guy's revival is now credited with keeping Chicago blues visible into the twenty-first century.","Start with the title track, then play one of the guest duets and listen to how guitarists of different generations speak. Then return to 'First Time I Met the Blues' from 1960 to hear the same voice three decades younger."],"junior-wells-hoodoo-man-blues":["Junior Wells's quartet, with Buddy Guy on guitar, playing the kind of set they played nightly in Chicago clubs. Harmonica and voice lead; Guy's guitar is supple and inventive; bass and drums groove with a loose, funky feel influenced by James Brown. Songs include 'Snatch It Back and Hold It', 'Hoodoo Man Blues' and 'Good Morning Little Schoolgirl'. The sound is warm and immediate, as if you were at a table near the stage.","Recorded in September 1965 for Delmark, a small Chicago label run by Bob Koester that aimed at blues and jazz fans rather than jukeboxes. It was among the first Chicago blues albums made as albums, capturing a working club band rather than assembling singles. Guy, under contract to Chess, was credited under a pseudonym, 'Friendly Chap'.","Listen to Guy's guitar playing rhythm figures that sound more like soul music than blues, a sign of the times. Also notice how Wells's harmonica often sounds amplified only faintly: the album's engineers aimed for the room sound rather than the hard distortion of Little Walter's records. The pseudonym on the sleeve fooled nobody.","It became Delmark's best-selling album and a model for later blues records, and critics regard it as one of the finest Chicago albums of its decade. Its informal, club-set approach influenced how blues would be recorded for the new album-buying audience.","Start with 'Snatch It Back and Hold It' for the groove, then follow Guy's guitar through a slow number. Compare with Little Walter's 'Juke' to hear how the harmonica's role shifted in thirteen years."],"etta-james-at-last":["Etta James's first album mixes lush, string-backed versions of pop standards with blues and rhythm-and-blues numbers. The title song, a 1940s ballad, floats on sweeping strings while her voice moves from husky warmth to a controlled cry. Other tracks include 'Stormy Weather', 'A Sunday Kind of Love', Willie Dixon's 'I Just Want to Make Love to You' and the blues ballad 'All I Could Do Was Cry'. The contrasts are the point: a blues singer in evening dress.","Leonard Chess signed James in 1960 and placed her on the Argo label, pairing her with the arranger Riley Hampton's string charts to reach both Black and white audiences. The title song, by Harry Warren and Mack Gordon, came from the 1942 film Orchestra Wives. The album was released in late 1960 and yielded several singles.","Listen to how James phrases the ballads like a blues singer: she lags behind the beat, bends notes and adds small growls, even over the most polished strings. On the Dixon song, she transforms a swaggering male boast into a woman's frank declaration, changing its meaning without changing a word.","Received as a strong debut, it gained status over the decades, and the title song became a wedding standard and her signature. Critics argue about whether the strings sweeten her too much; most now hear the tension between the raw voice and the smooth arrangement as the record's secret.","Start with the title song, then jump to the Dixon number for a completely different Etta. Listen to how the voice cuts through the strings. Then play 'I'd Rather Go Blind' to hear where she went next."],"etta-james-id-rather-go-blind":["A slow Southern soul ballad about watching a lover walk away with someone else. The band plays a gentle, rocking six-beat feel with warm organ and a horn section; the guitar picks out quiet phrases. Etta James sings with extraordinary restraint at first, building to a raw, pleading climax. It is soul in form and blues in feeling.","Recorded in 1967 at FAME Studios in Muscle Shoals, Alabama, with Rick Hall producing and the studio's house musicians. It appeared as the B-side of 'Tell Mama' on Chess's Cadet label. Credited to Ellington Jordan and Billy Foster, it was, James later said, partly her own work, but she gave her share of credit to Foster.","Listen for how little James does in the first verse: almost no vibrato, very soft. Then notice the moment near the end where she lets the voice crack wide open. The Muscle Shoals rhythm section plays almost nothing, which leaves her space. The song was a B-side; the A-side was the hit, but the B-side outlived it.","It became one of the most admired soul ballads of the 1960s, covered by Chicken Shack with Christine Perfect (later McVie) in 1969, by Rod Stewart and many others. Critics consider it one of James's finest performances. The authorship story has become part of the song's legend.","Listen for the build from whisper to cry. Then hear Chicken Shack's British version from 1969. Compare with 'At Last' to hear James without strings, standing on a soul band's bare floor."],"koko-taylor-wang-dang-doodle":["A rowdy party song listing a crowd of colourful characters invited to an all-night gathering. Koko Taylor sings with a rough, powerful growl, pushing the band. The groove is a steady stomp; harmonica and guitar punctuate each line. It is loud, funny and irresistible, with Taylor's voice commanding it all.","Willie Dixon wrote it and first had Howlin' Wolf record it in 1960; Wolf reportedly disliked it. Dixon, producing Taylor at Chess in 1966, gave it to her, and it became a rhythm-and-blues hit and her signature. Taylor had been cleaning houses and singing in South Side clubs. Few women had led a hard Chicago band on record.","The characters named in the song are drawn from old Southern party lore, and Dixon reportedly based the idea on a traditional song about a wild all-night gathering. Listen to how Taylor's version turns a mild male performance into a commanding female one; she sounds like the host, not a guest.","It made Taylor the leading female voice of Chicago blues, a title she kept for four decades. Critics see it as a key document of women claiming the loudest blues style. Some compare it with Wolf's version and find hers more convincing, which rarely happens with Wolf covers.","Play Howlin' Wolf's 1960 version first, from the 'Rocking Chair' album, then Taylor's 1966 Checker single, and listen to how she takes command where Wolf sounds half-amused. Notice the harmonica jabbing between her lines and the band's steady, stomping beat. Then move to her Alligator Records albums of the 1970s and 1980s, where the same growl leads her own touring band at full power."],"john-lee-hooker-boogie-chillen":["Just John Lee Hooker, an amplified guitar and his stomping foot. The guitar plays a single, relentless boogie figure on one chord, and Hooker half-talks, half-sings about arriving in Detroit, walking down Hastings Street and hearing the music in the clubs. The rhythm is loose and irregular, bars stretched and shortened as he pleases, yet the groove never loses its grip.","Recorded in Detroit in 1948 by the producer Bernard Besman and released by Modern Records in Los Angeles, it became a major rhythm-and-blues hit. Hooker had come north from Mississippi, working in the car factories. Hastings Street was the heart of Detroit's Black entertainment district, later demolished for a freeway.","The boogie figure comes from Hooker's stepfather, Will Moore, a Mississippi guitarist; Hooker said he learned the style from him. Listen to the foot: it is recorded almost as loud as the guitar, part drum, part percussion, and it holds the time more steadily than the guitar does. The song's irregular bars would baffle any band trying to follow him.","It made Hooker a star and inspired countless imitations. Canned Heat and ZZ Top built songs on its groove. Critics now see it as a root of rock and roll's riff-based style, and as proof that Delta modal blues could flourish in the industrial North.","Listen to the foot first; then the guitar; then the voice. Notice how each is slightly independent. Then play Muddy's 'Rollin' Stone' for a Chicago counterpart, and a Canned Heat boogie for the rock inheritance."],"john-lee-hooker-boom-boom":["A tight, danceable stop-time song in which Hooker says he wants to take someone home. The band hits short accents together, then stops, leaving Hooker's voice and guitar alone. There is a sharp, bright horn section, a steady beat and a catchy hook. It is one of Hooker's most structured recordings, closer to rhythm and blues than to his solo boogies.","Recorded in Chicago in 1961 and released by Vee-Jay in 1962. Vee-Jay was a Black-owned Chicago label, home to Jimmy Reed, and soon to issue the Beatles' first American records. Hooker had spent the 1950s recording solo or with small groups for many labels; here he fronted a full band with horns, reportedly including members of Motown's house band, the Funk Brothers. The single crossed over to pop radio and reached white teenage listeners at the start of the blues revival.","Listen for the stop-time breaks and how Hooker times his lines against the silences. Also notice that the song stays in regular bars, unlike his earlier records; the band clearly insisted. The British group the Animals recorded it in 1964, and their version, with its heavy organ, introduced it to many listeners.","It became one of Hooker's best-known songs and has been used in film and advertising. Critics sometimes find it lightweight beside his solo boogies, but it shows how he could adapt to the band format. Its crossover success helped establish him with British bands, who toured and recorded with him.","Listen first to the stop-time structure and count the gaps where the band falls silent and Hooker's guitar answers alone. Then play the Animals' 1964 version, with Alan Price's organ, to hear how a British band heard it. Finally go back to 'Boogie Chillen'' and notice how differently Hooker breathes when a disciplined band holds him to regular bars. The original Vee-Jay mono single is the version to seek."],"spiritual-go-down-moses":["A slow, heavy call to Pharaoh: the leader tells the story of Moses in Egypt, and every verse closes on the command ‘Let my people go’, sung by all. The tune stays low and narrow, circling a minor-sounding scale, and rises only on that refrain, so the demand lands like a blow. There is no chorus in the hymn-book sense — just the same four words hammered home verse after verse, often twenty verses in a traditional telling.","It first reached print in 1861 as ‘The Song of the Contrabands’, taken down by the chaplain Lewis Lockwood from escaped slaves sheltering behind Union lines at Fort Monroe, Virginia — the first spiritual published with its music. For enslaved people the Exodus was not an allegory at a distance: Egypt was the South, Pharaoh the master, and Harriet Tubman was called ‘Moses’ by those she led north. The Fisk Jubilee Singers made it a concert staple after 1871.","The earliest sheet music dressed it in a genteel piano arrangement with a major-key feel; the minor, modal colour everyone now hears came later, from singers rather than editors. Listen to how the refrain falls a whole step at its end rather than resolving upward — a cadence closer to a field holler than a hymn. That falling close is also why the tune sits so naturally in a blues singer's mouth.","White collectors in the 1860s debated whether songs like this were ‘music’ at all or crude imitations of Methodist hymns; Black scholars from W. E. B. Du Bois, who called the spirituals ‘sorrow songs’, onward answered by treating them as the nation's great folk art. Paul Robeson made it his signature in the 1920s and 30s, and Louis Armstrong's 1958 recording with a choir brought it to jazz audiences.","Start with Paul Robeson's bass, where the refrain sounds like an order rather than a plea; then hear Tippett's choral setting in A Child of Our Time, which keeps the tune plain inside modern harmony. Notice how few notes the melody uses — the power is in the repetition. Then hear Armstrong's version, which swings it without softening it."],"spiritual-steal-away":["The quietest of the great spirituals. A gentle refrain — ‘Steal away, steal away, steal away to Jesus’ — alternates with verses in which ‘my Lord calls me, he calls me by the thunder’ and ‘the trumpet sounds within my soul’. The melody moves in small steps and long held notes and is almost always sung softly, as if overheard. It lasts barely three minutes, but its hush makes it one of the most recognisable songs of the American nineteenth century.","It appeared in the first songbook published for the Fisk Jubilee Singers in 1872 and was one of their most requested pieces on tours of the North and of Britain. A tradition, impossible to prove, links it to Nat Turner's revolt of 1831 or to secret gatherings in the woods; another credits it, with ‘Swing Low’, to Wallace Willis, a freedman in the Choctaw Nation of Indian Territory.","The double meaning is the point: ‘steal away’ is both to die and go to God and to slip out of the quarters to a hidden prayer meeting, or north. Listen for the thunder and trumpet in the verses — images of the Last Judgment that are, sung softly, a promise of rescue rather than a threat. The contrast between loud words and a whispered tune is the song's secret.","Nineteenth-century listeners praised its ‘plaintive’ tenderness and missed its irony; twentieth-century scholars, from Sterling Brown to Lawrence Levine, read it as a key text for the coded meanings of the spirituals, while more cautious historians warn that most ‘escape code’ stories were attached later. Tippett's choice of it to end Part One of A Child of Our Time made it known to European concert audiences.","Hear it first sung very quietly by a small ensemble or a single voice, and notice how the refrain's third ‘steal away’ reaches upward like a door opening. Then compare Tippett's version, where a soprano floats over the choir. Mahalia Jackson's slow reading shows how a gospel singer stretches it."],"spiritual-nobody-knows-the-trouble-ive-seen":["A lament that turns on itself: the refrain sings ‘Nobody knows the trouble I've seen, nobody knows but Jesus’, then ends — astonishingly — ‘Glory, hallelujah!’ The tune is built on a five-note scale, rocking gently in a slow walking rhythm, and the verses (‘Sometimes I'm up, sometimes I'm down’) are short and personal. It is the spiritual as a private confession, and the sudden hallelujah is the moment a first-time listener remembers.","A version, ‘Nobody Knows the Trouble I've Had’, was printed in Slave Songs of the United States (1867), the first collection of the genre, gathered by Northern teachers among freedpeople on the Sea Islands of South Carolina during the Civil War. The Fisk Jubilee Singers carried it to Europe, and by the early twentieth century it was sung in recital, in vaudeville and by church choirs across the country.","The ‘Glory, hallelujah’ at the end of a song about suffering is not a happy ending tacked on: it is the characteristic double motion of the spirituals, grief and praise in one breath. Listen for the five-note scale — the same pentatonic shape Dvořák heard in these songs and used in his ‘American’ Quartet and New World Symphony.","It became one of the most arranged spirituals, sometimes smothered in sentimental harmonies. Marian Anderson's plain recordings restored its dignity; Louis Armstrong's 1938 recording with a choir and his later versions made it a jazz standard. Tippett placed it at the heart of A Child of Our Time, and it remains a test of whether a singer can keep it simple.","Listen for the turn to ‘Glory, hallelujah’ and ask whether the singer makes it triumphant or weary — the best versions make it both. Marian Anderson's recording is the classic; Armstrong's shows the melody's swing. Then hear Tippett's choral setting and notice how he leaves the tune almost untouched."],"spiritual-sometimes-i-feel-like-a-motherless-child":["Three lines and a sigh: ‘Sometimes I feel like a motherless child, a long ways from home’, repeated, then ‘a long ways from home’ once more, falling. Later verses — ‘Sometimes I feel like I'm almost gone’ — sink further. The melody is modal and bare, with long notes on ‘motherless’ and ‘home’ that invite the singer to bend and moan. It is the darkest of the well-known spirituals, and it offers no hallelujah.","Its image comes straight from slavery, in which children were routinely sold away from their mothers. The Fisk Jubilee Singers sang it on their tours in the 1870s, and it entered published collections in the following decades. It became a recital piece in Harry T. Burleigh's generation, a protest song in the civil-rights years, and a staple of folk revival singers such as Odetta.","Many versions end with ‘true believer’ or a turn to heaven, but the oldest-sounding performances simply stop on ‘a long ways from home’, unresolved. Richie Havens's opening set at Woodstock in 1969 grew, famously improvised, into ‘Freedom’ on top of this tune — a whole generation heard the spiritual there without knowing its name.","Du Bois singled out the ‘sorrow songs’ as the truest American music, and this is the text most often quoted to show why. Some twentieth-century arrangers rendered it lush and slow; singers from Paul Robeson to Mahalia Jackson and Odetta preferred it stark. It is now one of the spirituals most often heard in films and at memorials.","Seek an unaccompanied or near-unaccompanied version first — Paul Robeson, Odetta or a folk singer — and listen for the moan on ‘long’. Then hear Richie Havens's ‘Freedom’ to hear it turned into a frantic guitar chant. Compare with ‘Nobody Knows’, which escapes into praise; this one does not."],"spiritual-were-you-there":["A Passion meditation in four questions: ‘Were you there when they crucified my Lord? … when they nailed him to the tree? … when they pierced him in the side? … when they laid him in the tomb?’ Each verse halts on a drawn-out ‘Oh!’ followed by ‘Sometimes it causes me to tremble, tremble, tremble.’ The trembling is sung — the word is stretched and shaken on repeated notes. The song asks the listener to stand at the cross as a witness.","Sung in the nineteenth century, it reached print in 1899 in a collection of plantation hymns, and in 1940 it entered the hymnal of the Episcopal Church, often said to be the first spiritual in a major white denominational hymnal. It is now sung in Good Friday services across denominations worldwide and was a favourite of Marian Anderson and Johnny Cash.","The question ‘Were you there?’ collapses time: the enslaved singer claims to have been present at the crucifixion, identifying her suffering with Christ's. Listen for the long ‘Oh!’ that breaks each verse — a written-down trace of the congregational moan, the wordless groan of the Black church that later reappears in blues and gospel singing.","Its adoption by white churches has been praised as recognition and questioned as appropriation that softened its meaning; recent scholarship reads it alongside lynching, when the ‘tree’ was not metaphorical. Musically it is admired for its structure — question, cry, tremble — as much as for its melody.","Hear Marian Anderson's recording, then a congregation singing it in a Good Friday service, and notice how the ‘tremble’ is made physical. Compare it with Bach's St Matthew Passion, whose chorales also make the listener a witness to the crucifixion, by very different means."],"spiritual-wade-in-the-water":["A baptism song with a rolling, insistent beat: ‘Wade in the water, wade in the water, children, God's gonna trouble the water.’ The verses call up the Red Sea and the pool of Bethesda, where an angel ‘troubled’ the water and the first to step in was healed. The tune is minor-tinged and repetitive, made for moving bodies — a procession down to the river — and it swings naturally.","It was printed in 1901 in New Jubilee Songs, edited at Fisk University by John Wesley Work II and his brother Frederick. Its setting is the outdoor river baptism of the rural South. In the twentieth century it became a gospel quartet number, a civil-rights anthem, and the centrepiece of Alvin Ailey's dance Revelations (1960).","A popular story makes it Harriet Tubman's coded instruction to escaping slaves to take to the water and lose the dogs; historians find no nineteenth-century evidence for this, and the legend is itself a twentieth-century creation. The ‘troubled water’ is from the Gospel of John — and it is the phrase Paul Simon echoed, via gospel quartets, in ‘Bridge over Troubled Water’.","Few spirituals have crossed genres so freely: the Ramsey Lewis Trio's jazz version was a hit in 1966, and funk, soul and film soundtracks have borrowed its groove. Some purists regret the loss of its baptismal meaning; others see its mobility as proof of its vitality.","Start with a church or quartet version and clap on the offbeat. Then watch or hear the ‘Wade in the Water’ section of Ailey's Revelations, and compare Ramsey Lewis's jazz trio, where the tune becomes a vamp. Listen for how the ‘trouble the water’ line rises."],"spiritual-joshua-fit-the-battle-of-jericho":["The most exuberant of the spirituals: ‘Joshua fit the battle of Jericho, and the walls came tumbling down.’ The melody runs in a minor key but at a quick, driving tempo, the words tumble in syncopation, and the verses retell the siege — the rams' horns, the marching, the shout. It is a narrative song for a crowd, meant to be sung with clapping and to end in a roar.","Sung during slavery and collected after emancipation, it was among the songs that jubilee choirs and later quartets made showpieces. For singers who could not fight openly, the story of an army bringing down walls by marching, trumpeting and shouting had obvious force. The Golden Gate Quartet and Mahalia Jackson both recorded it, and it was a staple of school choirs by mid-century.","‘Fit’ is the old Southern past tense of ‘fight’, and editors who ‘corrected’ it to ‘fought’ killed the rhythm. Listen for the shout itself: in many versions the walls fall on a sudden unison cry or a dropped beat, reproducing in sound the moment the Israelites shouted. It is a spiritual built on noise as a weapon.","Its minor-key swing made it a favourite for jazz and folk groups; some mid-century arrangements turned it into a novelty. Scholars link its triumphant tone to the ‘ring shout’, the circle dance of West African origin that survived in Black worship.","Hear Mahalia Jackson or a quartet version, then a big choir, and listen for where the ‘tumbling down’ falls in the bar. Compare it with ‘Motherless Child’: the same people made both, and the range between them is the range of the spirituals."],"fisk-jubilee-singers-swing-low-sweet-chariot":["Four men singing a hymn-like harmony, unhurried and exactly blended: ‘Swing low, sweet chariot, coming for to carry me home.’ The refrain falls and rises in a gentle arc built on the pentatonic scale, and the verses — ‘I looked over Jordan and what did I see’ — are sung by a lead voice answered by the group. The recording's surface hiss and the singers' formal diction preserve the concert style the Fisk singers invented in the 1870s.","The song is usually credited to Wallace Willis, a freedman in the Choctaw Nation of Indian Territory, whose songs reached the Fisk Jubilee Singers through a missionary around 1871. The first Fisk troupe sang it across the North, in Britain and before Queen Victoria, raising money to build Jubilee Hall. The Fisk University Jubilee Quartet, led by John Wesley Work II, recorded it for Victor in the first years of acoustic recording, among the earliest recordings of African American sacred music.","Dvořák, who heard spirituals from Harry T. Burleigh in New York, gave the solo flute in the first movement of the New World Symphony a theme that almost quotes this tune. Listen also to the recording's blend: the quartet sings as a choir in miniature, with little of the swing or improvised ornament of later gospel quartets — the price and the achievement of making folk song ‘respectable’.","The Fisk approach was debated from the start: some Black listeners felt concert harmonisation betrayed the congregational style, and Zora Neale Hurston later attacked ‘glee-club’ spirituals as a false front. Yet the Fisk recordings are now treasured as documents, and the song itself has become a national anthem of sorts, sung at funerals and even, in England, on rugby terraces.","Listen past the surface noise to the balance of the four voices and the evenness of the tempo. Then hear the Golden Gate Quartet's swinging jubilee sound and hear what changed in thirty years. Dvořák's first-movement flute tune makes the perfect companion."],"burleigh-deep-river":["A spiritual turned art song. The voice sings the old melody — ‘Deep river, my home is over Jordan; deep river, Lord, I want to cross over into camp-ground’ — in long, arching phrases that leap an octave on ‘deep’, while the piano supplies rich, slow-moving harmony with chromatic inner voices, like a Brahms or Dvořák song. It lasts about three minutes and is sung slowly, with a hush in the middle verse about the ‘gospel feast’.","Burleigh, the Erie-born baritone who had sung spirituals to Dvořák in the 1890s, published this setting in 1916. It was the first spiritual arranged as a concert song for solo voice and piano to become widely popular, and it opened the recital stage to the repertory: Roland Hayes, Marian Anderson and Paul Robeson built programmes around such settings. Samuel Coleridge-Taylor had already arranged the tune for piano and for violin.","Burleigh's first version was for chorus; he revised it several times, and the solo version's slower, more luxuriant harmony is what made it famous. Listen for how the piano holds back on the octave leap and then answers it — Burleigh keeps the tune exactly as sung and puts all his art into the accompaniment. Tippett closed A Child of Our Time with this spiritual.","Welcomed by white critics as proof that spirituals belonged in the recital hall, it was later criticised — by Zora Neale Hurston among others — for Europeanising a congregational music. Today it is seen as a founding document of a distinctive Black art-song tradition, which continued through Hall Johnson, Florence Price and Margaret Bonds.","Hear Marian Anderson or Paul Robeson for the classic reading, then a modern recital singer such as Jessye Norman. Listen to the piano rather than the voice for once, and notice how it prepares each high note. Compare with the unaccompanied spirituals in this collection to hear what was gained and lost."],"dorsey-its-tight-like-that":["Two men having fun: Tampa Red's bright slide guitar and Georgia Tom Dorsey's rolling piano over a bouncing medium tempo, with a sly verse-and-refrain lyric — ‘It's tight like that, beedle um bum’ — full of double meanings. It is short, repetitive and infectious, closer to a party record than to the Delta blues, and it launched a craze for good-humoured, suggestive ‘hokum’ blues.","Thomas A. Dorsey had been Ma Rainey's pianist and bandleader in Chicago. He and the guitarist Tampa Red recorded this for Vocalion in 1928, and it sold heavily, spawning sequels and imitators. Its royalties arrived at the same moment as Dorsey's religious crisis and turn to church music — the man who would father gospel was, that year, the hottest blues writer in Chicago.","The piano rhythm and blues harmony you hear here are exactly what Dorsey carried into his gospel songs a few years later; only the words changed. That is why church elders initially barred his gospel music as ‘the devil's music’. Listen also for Tampa Red's slide, clean and precise rather than raw — the ‘Guitar Wizard’ who shaped Chicago electric blues.","Hokum was long dismissed by blues historians as trivial novelty next to the Delta masters; later writers recognised its commercial importance and its role in shaping urban blues and early rhythm and blues. Dorsey himself later preferred not to discuss his blues career.","Listen once for the jokes, then again for the piano: that walking, rocking left hand is the heartbeat of Dorsey's gospel. Put it beside ‘Take My Hand, Precious Lord’ from four years later. For contrast, hear Blind Willie Johnson, who sang sacred songs with blues guitar from the other direction."],"dorsey-take-my-hand-precious-lord":["A slow gospel song in three verses: ‘Precious Lord, take my hand, lead me on, let me stand; I am tired, I am weak, I am worn.’ The melody is simple and hymn-like, but Dorsey wrote it for gospel's slow, stretched delivery, where a singer can linger on ‘tired’ and ‘worn’, bend notes blue and build to the plea ‘lead me home’. Over a rolling piano it can take five minutes or fifteen, depending on how far the singer travels.","Dorsey wrote it in August 1932 after his wife Nettie died in childbirth while he was away singing at a revival in St Louis; their newborn son died soon after. He was by then music director at Pilgrim Baptist Church in Chicago and selling his songs as sheet music door to door. It became gospel's most famous song, the favourite of Martin Luther King, sung by Mahalia Jackson at his funeral in 1968 and by Aretha Franklin at Mahalia's in 1972.","The tune is adapted from a nineteenth-century hymn, George N. Allen's ‘Maitland’ (sung to ‘Must Jesus Bear the Cross Alone’). Dorsey's contribution is the rhythm and the gaps: the melody leaves room between phrases for the pianist's blues fills and the singer's moans — the space, not the notes, is the gospel.","It was translated into many languages and recorded by Elvis Presley and by countless soloists and choirs. Some church musicians object to the blues-laden, stretched performances that became standard; others hold that the song only lives in them. It is regarded as the foundational text of gospel song.","Start with Mahalia Jackson, whose slow tempo and vast breath set the pattern; then Aretha Franklin's teenage recording from Songs of Faith. Listen for the word ‘tired’ — every great singer makes it a small drama. Compare with ‘Were You There’ for the older congregational moan behind it."],"golden-gate-quartet-golden-gate-gospel-train":["Four voices become a steam train. The bass chugs a rhythm, the tenors whistle and moan like the engine, and the lead calls out the stations of salvation as the ‘gospel train’ pulls in. There are no instruments at all, yet it swings like a small swing band. The song is an old train-to-glory spiritual; the performance is the jubilee quartet style at its most brilliant, a three-minute showpiece of vocal mimicry and rhythm.","The quartet, from Norfolk, Virginia, recorded it in their first session for RCA's Bluebird label in 1937, and their records sold across the South. John Hammond booked them for his ‘From Spirituals to Swing’ concert at Carnegie Hall in December 1938, where they shared a bill with Count Basie and Sister Rosetta Tharpe, and they went on to radio, film and the White House.","The ‘train’ is not only a sound effect: the train as the road north and the road to heaven is one of the oldest images in Black music, shared with blues harmonica ‘train’ pieces and later with soul (‘People Get Ready’). Listen to the bass singer: he is doing the job of a string bass and a drummer at once, a technique later doo-wop groups inherited.","Jubilee quartets were sometimes dismissed by gospel purists as entertainment and by jazz critics as novelties. Later historians, notably Anthony Heilbut and Doug Seroff, placed them at the centre of Black popular music between the wars, linking barbershop, jazz and doo-wop.","Listen for each voice doing a different job: bass as rhythm section, tenors as whistle and steam, lead as preacher. Then hear the Fisk quartet's ‘Swing Low’ of 1909 to hear how much swing arrived in thirty years, and Sam Cooke's Soul Stirrers to hear where the quartet went next."],"rosetta-tharpe-rock-me":["A young woman alone with her guitar, picking a swinging, bluesy accompaniment and singing a prayer — ‘Rock me in the cradle of thy love’ — with a joy that makes it sound like a dance record. Her guitar answers every vocal line with bent notes and quick runs, and the rhythm is pure swing. Three minutes that sound, heard now, like the dawn of rock and roll.","‘Rock Me’ is Thomas A. Dorsey's gospel song ‘Hide Me in Thy Bosom’, retitled. Tharpe, raised in the Pentecostal Church of God in Christ, recorded it in her first Decca session in 1938, the year she also appeared at the Cotton Club and at John Hammond's ‘From Spirituals to Swing’ concert at Carnegie Hall. She soon recorded it again with Lucky Millinder's big band. The church was scandalised; the record-buying public was delighted.","The word ‘rock’ in the title meant being rocked in God's arms — but it already carried the other meaning in Black secular slang, and the double sense is audible in her delivery. Listen for the guitar's fills between lines: that call-and-response between voice and guitar is exactly what Chuck Berry built a career on.","Gospel purists condemned her nightclub appearances; jazz critics treated her as a novelty. Her reputation fell with the rise of rock and roll, whose male stars took her style, and she was largely forgotten until biographers and the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame (2018) restored her as a founding figure.","Listen to the guitar alone first — the swing, the bends, the confidence. Then compare the big-band version with Lucky Millinder to hear her hold her own against a whole orchestra. Put it beside Dorsey's slow gospel style to hear how she sped his songs up."],"rosetta-tharpe-strange-things-happening-every-day":["A shuffling, upbeat gospel song with a boogie-woogie piano, a walking bass and drums, over which Tharpe sings a sly sermon about hypocrites — ‘There are strange things happening every day’ — and plays a stinging electric guitar solo. It swings hard, the solo is loud and bluesy, and the whole thing sounds like rhythm and blues in everything but its words.","Recorded in 1944 in New York with the pianist Sammy Price's trio, it became a hit on the ‘race’ charts in 1945 — one of the first gospel records to cross over to a secular Black audience. Wartime America was buying jump blues and boogie-woogie; Tharpe simply played gospel in that idiom. Johnny Cash, Little Richard and Jerry Lee Lewis heard her as teenagers.","It is often proposed as the first rock-and-roll record, years ahead of the usual candidates; the claim is unprovable, but the guitar solo, the backbeat and the shuffle are all there. Listen to the lyric too: it is a piece of social commentary, mocking the ‘church folks’ who condemn others while they sin — sung by a woman her own church was criticising.","Rock historians long overlooked it; since the 1990s it has become the standard exhibit for Tharpe's influence and for gospel's role in rock and roll's birth. Some scholars caution against ‘first rock-and-roll record’ contests, preferring to see a broad field of jump blues and gospel from which rock emerged.","Wait for the guitar solo and listen to its tone and attack — then play any early Chuck Berry record. Notice the piano's boogie-woogie left hand, which links it to Dorsey's blues years. Then hear her ‘Up Above My Head’ with Marie Knight for the duet side of her art."],"rosetta-tharpe-up-above-my-head":["A joyous duet: Tharpe and Marie Knight trade lines — ‘Up above my head, I hear music in the air’ — one calling, the other answering, then joining in harmony, over a bouncing rhythm section. The tune is a simple traditional spiritual refrain, but the call-and-response between the two women builds excitement verse by verse, and Tharpe's guitar punctuates it all.","Tharpe met Marie Knight, a contralto from Florida, in 1946, and the two toured and recorded together for several years; this was one of their most popular records, released by Decca in the late 1940s. Their duets filled auditoriums and ballparks; Tharpe's wedding in 1951, staged at a Washington baseball stadium before a paying crowd, was the peak of her fame.","Listen to how the two voices differ: Knight's is darker and more restrained, Tharpe's bright and rough, and the tension between them is the drama. The call-and-response is the oldest structure in Black sacred music — the leader and congregation of the spirituals — shrunk to two singers and a band.","In its day it was a popular record on the Black charts and a staple of the duo's packed tours, though church critics grumbled that Tharpe and Knight had turned worship into showbusiness. After Knight left for secular music and Tharpe's star faded, it was remembered mainly by gospel collectors. Biographies, documentaries and her Rock and Roll Hall of Fame induction revived it; historians now debate how far such duets shaped rhythm and blues vocal groups.","Follow the two voices as if they were a conversation, noticing who leads when. Compare with ‘Strange Things’ for Tharpe's solo swagger. Then hear the Staple Singers, where a family's voices do the same call and response over a blues guitar."],"mahalia-jackson-move-on-up-a-little-higher":["A rousing song about heaven, where the singer will ‘move on up a little higher’, meet the saints and ‘drink from the fountain that never runs dry’. Issued in two parts across both sides of a 78 because it was too long for one, it builds from a steady, rocking tempo into ecstatic repetition, with Jackson's huge voice swooping over piano and organ, bending notes and riding the beat like a jazz singer.","The song is by the Memphis minister W. Herbert Brewster, gospel's greatest songwriter after Dorsey. Jackson recorded it for the small Apollo label in New York in 1947; it sold enormously, reportedly in the millions, and made her the first gospel superstar. It led to radio, a Columbia contract, a Carnegie Hall concert in 1950 and national television.","Listen for the join between the two sides: part two picks up in mid-flight, and the momentum carries over as if nothing happened. Jackson's phrasing — delaying a word, then catching up with a rush — is the rhythmic freedom she learned partly from Bessie Smith records in New Orleans, though she refused ever to sing the blues.","Its sales startled a record industry that had treated gospel as a niche, and it is universally named as the record that launched gospel's ‘golden age’. Some church musicians regretted the commercial success and the star system it created,, and traditionalists disliked her blues-tinged delivery. Jazz critics heard in Jackson a singer of Bessie Smith's stature; the argument now is less about her greatness than about how much her style owed to the blues she refused to sing.","Listen to both parts in one go, and notice how the tempo seems to rise though it hardly changes — the excitement comes from her rhythmic placement. Then hear her ‘Come Sunday’ with Duke Ellington on Black, Brown and Beige, the most unexpected setting she ever sang in."],"clara-ward-surely-god-is-able":["A slow, swaying song in a lilting triple-feel rhythm, with the Ward Singers' close, high harmony and lead singers trading phrases of rising intensity: ‘Surely, surely, he's able to carry you through.’ Piano holds everything together. The effect is of a gradual climb, each lead singer pushing higher than the last until the group is almost shouting.","Another song by W. Herbert Brewster, recorded by the Ward Singers for Savoy around 1950, it became one of the best-selling gospel records of its time. The Philadelphia group, run by Gertrude Ward with her daughter Clara as pianist and arranger, featured the extraordinary Marion Williams, whose soaring leads were a key part of the sound.","The rhythm is the secret: the swaying, rocking triple lilt, unusual in gospel then, became a staple of later gospel and soul ballads. Listen too for the alternation of leads — Clara Ward's warm voice and Marion Williams's higher, wilder one — a method that trained the young Aretha Franklin, who heard these singers in her father's church.","The Ward Singers were acclaimed for their musicianship and later criticised for glamour, gowns and wigs when they moved into nightclubs and Las Vegas. This record remains the one everyone agrees on; Marion Williams's later solo career has raised her reputation among critics above Ward's own.","Listen for the lilt and let it sway you; then pick out each lead singer as she takes over. Compare Mahalia's ‘Move On Up’ for the solo star style, and Aretha's Amazing Grace, where she sings Clara Ward's ‘How I Got Over’ with Ward in the congregation."],"soul-stirrers-jesus-gave-me-water":["A brisk, rhythmic quartet number telling the story of the woman at the well from the Gospel of John. The group sets up a steady rocking pulse and harmony; over it the young Sam Cooke floats a light, clear lead, sliding between notes and adding the little ‘whoa-oh-oh’ yodel that would become his trademark. It is sweet rather than rough, and it swings.","Recorded in Los Angeles for Art Rupe's Specialty label in 1951, it was among Cooke's first records after he replaced R. H. Harris as the Soul Stirrers' lead singer at nineteen. His good looks and ease made the group idols of young church audiences, especially young women, on the gospel circuit. Six years later he left for pop and invented soul with the same voice.","Listen for the yodel: that brief break in the voice, carried almost unchanged into ‘You Send Me’ in 1957, is the single most direct thread from church to soul music. Behind Cooke, the quartet's two-lead system, invented under Harris, lets a second voice take over when the first has built the excitement.","Gospel historians rank Cooke's Soul Stirrers records among the best quartet singing on record; some elders at the time thought his style too smooth and too flirtatious for church. His move to pop in 1957 was seen by many gospel fans as betrayal.","Focus on Cooke's voice and count the ways he decorates a phrase without straining. Then hear his later soul records, especially ‘A Change Is Gonna Come’, and hear the same voice. For the harder quartet style, compare the Five Blind Boys of Mississippi."],"five-blind-boys-of-mississippi-our-father":["The Lord's Prayer as a slow, heavy moan. The quartet hums a deep, sustained harmony; over it Archie Brownlee sings the prayer's words, stretching them, then suddenly erupts into screams and falsetto cries that crack open the calm. The contrast between the hushed background and the raw lead is the whole drama.","The Five Blind Boys of Mississippi, formed at the Piney Woods school, recorded for Don Robey's Peacock label in Houston, which also made Big Mama Thornton's ‘Hound Dog’. ‘Our Father’ became one of the best-selling gospel records of its day and a jukebox favourite beyond church audiences. This was the era of ‘hard gospel’, when quartets competed to see who could bring a congregation to its feet.","Listen to Brownlee's scream: it is controlled, not wild, placed exactly where the text peaks. Ray Charles, who listened closely to gospel quartets, used the same device in soul; James Brown took it further. What sounds like abandon is technique.","It sold strongly well beyond church audiences, turning up on jukeboxes beside rhythm and blues, and its crossover showed labels a market for gospel sounds that they soon exploited in soul. Some ministers found Brownlee's screaming theatrical rather than devout. Gospel historians such as Anthony Heilbut now rank him among the greatest quartet leads, though his death in 1960 cut short his fame, and the debt soul singers owe him is still less widely acknowledged than it should be.","Listen first to the humming quartet, then notice where Brownlee breaks free. Compare the smooth Sam Cooke with the Soul Stirrers, the opposite pole of quartet singing, and then early Ray Charles for the inheritance."],"cl-franklin-the-eagle-stirreth-her-nest":["A sermon that turns into music. Rev. C. L. Franklin begins by speaking calmly about a verse in Deuteronomy — the eagle that stirs up her nest to teach her young to fly — and gradually his speech tightens into rhythm and pitch, the ‘whooping’ chant of the Black preacher, answered by the organ and by shouts from the congregation. By the end he is singing phrases on a few pitches in a rhythmic chant as the church rises with him.","Recorded at New Bethel Baptist Church in Detroit, and issued on records distributed by Chess, this was the most famous of dozens of Franklin sermons sold nationally in the 1950s. Radio broadcasts and records made him a star preacher; he toured with gospel singers and drew huge crowds. His daughter Aretha grew up hearing him every Sunday.","The ‘whoop’ is a musical form: it moves from speech to a chanted, pitched delivery with a rhythmic pulse, often landing on repeated tones answered by the organ. Listen for the moment the organ first answers him — that is when speech becomes music. Steve Reich's speech-melody pieces begin from the same observation.","It is counted among the classic recordings of African American preaching and has been studied by folklorists and musicologists as an oral art with its own forms. Franklin's reputation was clouded by personal scandal and he was criticised in his lifetime for the commercial side of his fame, but his recorded sermons are now central to understanding how Black church music and speech feed each other.","Listen for the moment talking turns to chanting, and for the organ's first response. Then hear Aretha's Amazing Grace, where her father speaks to the congregation between songs, and listen for his rise and fall in her phrasing. Steve Reich's Different Trains makes a fascinating, secular companion."],"aretha-franklin-songs-of-faith":["A fourteen-year-old girl at the piano in her father's church, singing gospel standards live — ‘Never Grow Old’, ‘There Is a Fountain Filled with Blood’, and a long ‘Precious Lord’ in two parts. The voice is already enormous, full of bends and melisma, and the congregation responds audibly. The sound is rough, recorded on simple equipment, but the singer is unmistakably Aretha.","Recorded at New Bethel Baptist Church in Detroit in 1956, it was issued first by the small JVB label and then by Chess's Checker imprint, the outlet for her father's sermons. Aretha had been touring with her father's gospel caravan and learning from Clara Ward, Mahalia Jackson and James Cleveland, all family friends. Her mother had left and later died; the church was her world.","Listen to how she handles ‘Precious Lord’: the long, stretched phrases and sudden bursts are Clara Ward's and Mahalia's, but the pianistic sense of harmony is her own. The later reissue title, ‘The Gospel Soul of Aretha Franklin’, points at what the record reveals — soul was already present in 1956.","On release it circulated mainly among gospel listeners who already knew her father's records, and it attracted little notice when Columbia signed her in 1960 as a jazz-leaning pop singer. After her Atlantic breakthrough it was reissued and reappraised, first as a curiosity and then as an essential document of her roots. Critics hear the range and control of the later soul records already in a teenager; some find the rough church recording a barrier, others its chief virtue.","Start with ‘Precious Lord’ and listen to how far she departs from the written tune. Then jump to Amazing Grace sixteen years later, and hear how much and how little changed."],"staple-singers-uncloudy-day":["A shimmering electric guitar with heavy tremolo introduces a family singing about heaven, ‘O they tell me of an uncloudy day’. Mavis Staples, still a teenager, sings in a deep contralto, her family harmonising around her, while Pops Staples's guitar supplies a shimmering, bluesy accompaniment that sounds like nothing else in gospel. The song is an old white hymn, but the sound is Mississippi.","Recorded for Chicago's Vee-Jay label in 1956, it was the Staples' breakthrough. Pops Staples had grown up near Dockery plantation, home of Charley Patton, and brought the Delta guitar style into church music. Bob Dylan, hearing it on the radio as a boy, later described it as the most mysterious thing he had ever heard.","The hymn was written in the 1880s by Josiah K. Alwood, a white Methodist; the Staples turned it into Delta gospel. Listen to the guitar's tremolo, which makes it sound like it is under water — Pops used an amplifier effect that was then new and rare in church music.","It was a hit on the gospel circuit and made the family's name, though some church listeners found Pops's amplified guitar too close to the juke joint. Later critics came to regard it as one of the great gospel records of the 1950s and a bridge between Delta blues and gospel. Dylan's testimony drew folk and rock audiences to it; scholars still weigh how much of the Staples' sound came from the Delta and how much from Chicago's electric blues.","Listen to the guitar first, then the voices. Notice Mavis's deep voice, unusual for a teenage girl. Compare with the Delta blues of Robert Johnson and Son House to hear where the guitar came from."],"mahalia-jackson-live-at-newport-1958":["Gospel's greatest singer in front of a jazz audience in the open air, with only piano and organ behind her. The set runs from stately hymns to rocking up-tempo numbers such as ‘Didn't It Rain’, whose Noah's-flood refrain she drives like a big band, and closes with a slow, enormous ‘The Lord's Prayer’. You hear the crowd go from polite attention to shouting along — a church service improvised on a festival stage.","By 1958 Jackson was a Columbia recording artist, a television presence and a national figure; earlier that year she had recorded Black, Brown and Beige with Duke Ellington, singing his ‘Come Sunday’ and a setting of the Twenty-third Psalm. The Newport Jazz Festival was the most prestigious in the country, and it booked her on its Sunday programme. The festival was filmed for Bert Stern's documentary Jazz on a Summer's Day, where her ‘Lord's Prayer’ closes the film.","Listen to her pianist Mildred Falls, the unsung partner in every Jackson record: she follows the voice's sudden stretches and accelerations without a cue, and fills every gap with gospel's rolling left hand. And notice that Jackson, who refused ever to sing the blues or jazz standards, makes no concession to the jazz crowd — she brings the church to them.","It is counted among her finest live recordings, the best record of the physical power that studio sessions tamed. Some church people disliked seeing her at a jazz festival; jazz critics, who had long compared her to Bessie Smith, took the performance as proof that gospel belonged in any discussion of great American singing.","Start with ‘Didn't It Rain’ and listen to how she pushes the tempo against the piano; then the closing ‘Lord's Prayer’ for the opposite, pure breadth of tone. Follow with her ‘Come Sunday’ on Black, Brown and Beige, and with Aretha's Amazing Grace, which takes this live-church sound to its limit."],"swan-silvertones-mary-dont-you-weep":["A spiritual from slavery sung as a late-1950s quartet showpiece: ‘O Mary, don't you weep, don't you mourn — Pharaoh's army got drownded.’ Over a light guitar and the group's rocking harmony, the leads alternate — Claude Jeter's floating, silvery falsetto and a harder, preaching second voice — and the performance pushes past the written verses into improvised lines, each one raising the temperature. About three minutes, but it feels like a whole service.","The song itself fuses two Bible stories — Mary of Bethany weeping for her brother Lazarus, and the drowning of Pharaoh's army in the Red Sea — into one promise of deliverance, a characteristic spiritual move. The Swan Silvertones, founded among West Virginia coal miners, recorded it for Chicago's Vee-Jay label in 1959, at the height of the gospel-quartet circuit, when groups toured Black churches and auditoriums in fierce competition with one another.","Near the end comes an ad-libbed line — ‘I'll be your bridge over deep water if you trust in my name’ — which Paul Simon has acknowledged as the seed of ‘Bridge over Troubled Water’ (1970), itself later recorded by Aretha Franklin. Listen for it: a spontaneous church phrase that became one of the most-sung pop songs of its decade.","Among gospel collectors it has always been a classic of the quartet style, prized for Jeter's falsetto, which Al Green and the Temptations' Eddie Kendricks both learned from. The Simon connection has given it a second audience, and Aretha's performance of the song on Amazing Grace confirmed its place in the canon.","Listen for the handover between the two lead voices — sweet, then rough — and then wait for the ‘bridge’ line. Follow with Aretha's longer version on Amazing Grace and then Simon and Garfunkel's ‘Bridge over Troubled Water’, and you have heard a single phrase travel from church to pop."],"james-cleveland-peace-be-still":["The storm on the Sea of Galilee, told by a preacher and a hundred-voice choir. Cleveland's gravelly baritone narrates — the wind, the frightened disciples, the sleeping Christ — and the choir answers with the words ‘Peace, be still’, at first softly, then in waves that grow until the whole church is roaring, with piano and organ swelling underneath. It is slow, patient and cumulative: the storm is in the build, the calm in the choir's last held chord.","Cleveland recorded it with the Angelic Choir of the First Baptist Church of Nutley, New Jersey, directed by Lawrence Roberts, and Savoy issued it in 1963. It became one of the best-selling gospel records of the decade and made the mass choir, rather than the quartet or the solo star, the centre of gospel. Cleveland had sung as a boy in Dorsey's choir at Pilgrim Baptist and coached the young Aretha Franklin in Detroit.","Listen to how Cleveland uses the choir like an orchestra — whole sections sustain while others punch short answers, and the dynamics rise in steps rather than a smooth crescendo. His rough, almost broken voice is deliberately set against the choir's smoothness: the preacher is human, the choir is the sea and then the peace.","Gospel historians treat it as the record that opened the choir era, the model for thousands of church and community choirs and for the Gospel Music Workshop of America, which Cleveland founded later in the decade. Some quartet fans felt the mass choir swamped individual artistry; its success settled the argument commercially.","Listen once for Cleveland's storytelling and once for the choir alone, noticing how each return of ‘Peace, be still’ is louder or richer. Then hear Handel's ‘Hallelujah’ chorus for another choir used as a wave of sound, and Aretha's Amazing Grace, where Cleveland directs a choir behind her."],"staple-singers-freedom-highway":["A live album made in a Chicago church, where hymns, spirituals and new freedom songs sit side by side as if they had always belonged together. The title track, by Pops Staples, is a march: a steady, stomping beat, Pops's tremolo guitar and the family's voices — ‘Marching up freedom highway, marching each and every day.’ Mavis leads with a low, rough power, Pops speaks between songs, and the congregation answers throughout. Church service and political rally are the same event.","Recorded in 1965 at New Nazareth Church on Chicago's South Side and issued by Epic, the album grew straight out of the civil-rights movement. Pops wrote the title song for the Selma-to-Montgomery march of March 1965, after the violence on the Edmund Pettus Bridge. The family had met Martin Luther King in 1963 and resolved, in Pops's words, that if he could preach it they could sing it.","Listen for Pops's spoken introductions: he frames each song as testimony, the way a preacher lines out a hymn, and that framing is what turns the concert into a service. Musically, the freedom songs use exactly the gospel toolkit — call-and-response, the marching backbeat, the guitar's shimmering blues — which is why the movement could sing them on the road without rehearsal.","Long overshadowed by the Staples' later Stax hits, it has come to be regarded as one of the essential records of the movement; an expanded edition later restored the whole church concert, and critics now prize its unpolished live sound above many studio records of the period.","Begin with the title track and let the march rhythm settle, then listen through for the congregation's voices. Compare the Staples' earlier ‘Uncloudy Day’ for the same guitar in a quieter mood, and Margaret Bonds's Montgomery Variations, a concert-hall response to the same struggle built on spirituals."],"edwin-hawkins-singers-oh-happy-day":["A youth choir, a relaxed groove and one refrain. Edwin Hawkins's piano and a soft rhythm section set up an easy mid-tempo beat, Dorothy Combs Morrison's lead voice sings ‘Oh happy day, when Jesus washed my sins away’, and the choir echoes each phrase back, adding handclaps and harmony as the song gathers force. It never shouts the way older choir gospel does; its joy is sunny and unhurried, and it lasts about five minutes.","The Northern California State Youth Choir recorded it in 1967 in a Pentecostal church in Berkeley for a small private album meant to raise travel money. A San Francisco disc jockey began playing it, a national label picked it up, and in 1969 it was an international hit and a Grammy winner; the choir was renamed after Hawkins. This was the Bay Area of the late 1960s, where church, soul radio and the counterculture overlapped.","The words are an eighteenth-century English hymn by the Congregationalist minister Philip Doddridge, ‘O happy day, that fixed my choice’, which had long been sung to a quite different tune. Hawkins kept only the refrain idea and built it on pop-soul harmony — listen to the piano's chords, which owe more to Motown than to Dorsey.","It was one of the very few gospel records to become a mainstream pop hit, and church traditionalists objected that its soft sound and radio play diluted the message. Others hailed it as the birth of contemporary gospel, the line that runs through Andraé Crouch to Kirk Franklin; that view has prevailed.","Listen to how little the song changes and how much the choir's growing confidence does. Then hear James Cleveland's ‘Peace Be Still’ for the heavier, older choir sound, and Handel's ‘Hallelujah’ for another choral hit that crossed from church to the wider world."],"aretha-franklin-amazing-grace":["Two nights of church, on four sides of vinyl. Aretha sings and sometimes plays piano in front of a full choir and a soul rhythm section, working through gospel standards — Clara Ward's ‘How I Got Over’, ‘Mary, Don't You Weep’, ‘Precious Memories’, ‘Never Grow Old’ — plus Marvin Gaye's ‘Wholy Holy’ and Carole King's ‘You've Got a Friend’ joined to ‘Precious Lord’. At its centre is an ‘Amazing Grace’ slowed almost to a standstill, each word stretched into long, climbing melismas while the church cries out.","Recorded on 13 and 14 January 1972 at New Temple Missionary Baptist Church in Watts, Los Angeles, with Rev. James Cleveland at the piano and in charge, and the Southern California Community Choir. Aretha was the biggest soul star in the world; Atlantic's Jerry Wexler agreed to let her go home to gospel. Her father C. L. Franklin and Clara Ward sat in the congregation on the second night. Sydney Pollack filmed the sessions, but the footage could not be synchronised with the sound, and the film waited until 2018.","Listen for Cleveland's voice during the title song: he is overcome and breaks off, and the choir carries on without him. On the second night her father rises to speak, recalling hearing her sing as a child — the whole family history of this collection in a few minutes of talk. The band is Atlantic's soul players, among them Bernard Purdie on drums and Chuck Rainey on bass: the grooves are Aretha's pop records, the words are the church.","It became the best-selling record of her career and is usually named the best-selling gospel album of all time. Critics at the time treated it as a curiosity beside her hits; it now stands with the greatest live recordings in any genre, and the 2018 film, which shows the congregation weeping and dancing, drew a new generation to it.","Give ‘Amazing Grace’ its full length without interruption and follow the voice as it rises in stages. Then hear ‘How I Got Over’ for the up-tempo side. Go back to her teenage Songs of Faith, and to Mahalia's ‘Move On Up’, to hear the lineage; then watch the film."],"staple-singers-ill-take-you-there":["A four-minute vamp on almost nothing. A loping bass line with a reggae lilt, light drums and a scratch of guitar set up a groove that never changes, and Mavis Staples sings of a place where ‘ain't nobody crying, ain't nobody worried, ain't no smiling faces lying to the races’. Then the song simply opens up: she calls on the band — ‘Daddy, now, play your guitar’ — and the musicians take turns while she urges them on. The destination is never named; it could be heaven or a better America.","By 1972 the Staples had moved from gospel labels to Stax in Memphis, where the executive Al Bell, who wrote and produced the song, steered them toward ‘message music’ for the pop charts. It was cut with the Muscle Shoals Rhythm Section in Alabama and went to number one. Black radio and the post-civil-rights mood made room for songs that spoke of freedom without naming the church.","The bass line and introduction are borrowed from a Jamaican instrumental, the Harry J. All Stars' ‘The Liquidator’ (1969) — early evidence of reggae feeding back into American soul. And listen to Mavis's calls to the players: they are a preacher's calls to the congregation, the call-and-response of the spirituals transferred to a studio band.","Gospel audiences were divided about the Staples' move into secular soul, though the family always insisted the message was unchanged. The record is now counted among the essential soul singles of the 1970s and a lasting demonstration that gospel's structures could top the pop charts intact.","Let the groove settle and follow Mavis's ad-libs rather than the words. Then hear ‘Uncloudy Day’, sixteen years earlier, and notice that Pops's guitar is the constant. Compare Aretha's Amazing Grace from the same year, which took the opposite road — soul back into church."],"blind-boys-of-alabama-spirit-of-the-century":["A quartet formed in 1939 sings spirituals and gospel songs alongside numbers by Tom Waits, the Rolling Stones and Ben Harper, over a spare, swampy band of acoustic and slide guitars, harmonica, bass and drums. The voices are old and weathered, the harmonies still tight, and Clarence Fountain's lead can still shout. The track everyone remembers is ‘Amazing Grace’ sung to the tune of ‘The House of the Rising Sun’ — a hymn wearing a brothel ballad's melody.","By 2001 the Blind Boys had spent six decades on the gospel circuit, with a revival in the 1980s through the stage work The Gospel at Colonus. Peter Gabriel's Real World label paired them with blues and roots musicians, the harmonica player Charlie Musselwhite among them, for a record aimed at a secular audience that loved Americana. It won the Grammy for traditional soul gospel album and began a late run of acclaimed records.","Their version of Tom Waits's ‘Way Down in the Hole’ from this album became the opening theme of the first season of the television series The Wire (2002), so millions heard the Blind Boys every week without knowing it. And the ‘House of the Rising Sun’ tune works for ‘Amazing Grace’ because both share the same common metre — an old hymn-singers' trick.","It was widely praised and sold far beyond the gospel market; some gospel listeners regretted the secular repertory and rock musicians, while the group, which had heard such complaints since the 1950s, answered that they sang only songs whose message they could stand behind.","Begin with ‘Amazing Grace’ and notice how the minor-key tune darkens the hymn. Then go back to Blind Willie Johnson's ‘John the Revelator’ for the older blues-gospel sound this record reaches toward, and to the Five Blind Boys of Mississippi for the hard-quartet style the group grew up in."],"woody-guthrie-dust-bowl-ballads":["Two albums of 78 rpm discs, one man with a flat-picked guitar and a harmonica on a neck rack. The songs follow the migration of Oklahoma and Texas farmers driven west by drought and dust: 'The Great Dust Storm', 'Talking Dust Bowl Blues', 'Do Re Mi', 'Dust Pneumonia Blues', 'I Ain't Got No Home', 'Vigilante Man'. The centrepiece is 'Tom Joad', a ballad in two parts that retells a whole novel in verse. The voice is dry, nasal and unhurried, closer to talking than singing.","Guthrie had ridden the same road himself, from Pampa, Texas, to California, where he sang on Los Angeles radio for Okie migrants. By early 1940 he was in New York, taken up by Alan Lomax and the left-wing theatre world. Victor, a major label, recorded him that spring, hoping to catch the audience for John Steinbeck's The Grapes of Wrath and John Ford's film of it. It is often called the first concept album: a set of records designed to tell one story.","'Tom Joad' borrows its tune from the outlaw ballad 'John Hardy', and Guthrie said he wrote it after seeing Ford's film rather than reading the book, which is why it follows the movie's plot. The 'talking blues' form he uses on several sides, half-spoken over a strummed pattern, came from Chris Bouchillon's 1920s records; Dylan would lift it wholesale twenty years later.","It sold modestly, and Victor did not follow it up. Its reputation grew backward, once the folk revival of the 1950s went looking for ancestors and found in Guthrie a model of the singer as witness. Critics now treat it as a founding document of American protest song and of the album as a unified statement, though some hear the songs as sharper on the page than on these rather hurried takes.","Start with 'Talking Dust Bowl Blues' for the deadpan humour, then play 'Tom Joad' straight through, all six minutes and more, and notice how little the guitar changes while the story moves. Compare Bruce Springsteen's 'The Ghost of Tom Joad' for the long afterlife. Any Buddha or Bluebird reissue will do; the sound is thin but clear."],"woody-guthrie-library-of-congress-recordings":["Several hours of Guthrie talking and singing into a microphone for Alan Lomax, who prompts him with questions. The songs are the Dust Bowl repertory and older ballads, but the talk is the point: stories of his Oklahoma boyhood, his mother's illness, oil-boom towns, hoboing and the camps of California, told in a slow, joking drawl. Songs grow out of the conversation and fall back into it, so the whole set plays like an oral autobiography with a guitar.","Lomax, then running the Archive of American Folk Song at the Library of Congress, brought Guthrie to Washington in March 1940, weeks after hearing him in New York, and recorded him for the national collection. The discs sat in the archive for more than two decades. Elektra issued them as a three-record set in 1964, when Guthrie lay in hospital with Huntington's disease and the young revivalists who revered him had never heard him speak at length.","Listen to Lomax as much as Guthrie. He steers the talk toward hardship and social comment, and Guthrie, a born performer, obliges; the recordings are as much a portrait of what the New Deal folklorists wanted as of the man himself. The rambling asides, where Guthrie loses the thread and finds another, show how his songs were built: talk first, tune second.","Revival audiences in 1964 received the set as scripture, and it helped fix Guthrie's image as the plain-spoken Okie prophet. Later biographers note how carefully he shaped that persona; he was well read, a cartoonist and a prolific writer. The recordings remain a primary source for historians of the Depression, and the Rounder reissue restored material cut from the Elektra LPs.","Do not try to hear it at one sitting. Take one side at a time as you would a radio programme, and let the stories carry you to the songs. The Rounder CD edition is the one to seek. Pair it with Lead Belly's Last Sessions, the other great document of a folk singer explaining himself."],"woody-guthrie-this-land-is-your-land":["A plain strophic song: a chorus that claims the whole country, coast to coast, for ordinary people, and verses that describe a walker crossing forests, deserts, wheat fields and highways. Guthrie sings it at an easy walking pace over a strummed guitar, with none of the swell of an anthem. The tune is so simple that a room of schoolchildren can pick it up after one chorus, which is exactly what has happened for eighty years.","Guthrie wrote it in February 1940 in a cheap New York hotel, irritated by hearing Kate Smith's recording of Irving Berlin's 'God Bless America' everywhere. His first draft ended each verse with a sarcastic twist on Berlin's title. He recorded it for Moses Asch in April 1944, during the war, and it slowly spread through schools, summer camps and the Popular Front songbooks before becoming a second national anthem in the 1960s.","The best-known versions leave out the verses that give it teeth: one mocks a private-property sign, and another watches hungry people queuing for relief and doubts the chorus's promise. The melody is borrowed almost note for note from the Carter Family's 'When the World's on Fire', itself drawn from an old Baptist hymn.","Its gentleness has made it adaptable to almost anyone, from grade-school pageants to presidential inaugurations, and many listeners never suspect it began as a rebuttal. Folk singers since Pete Seeger have made a point of restoring the missing verses. A 2004 parody dispute revealed that the song's copyright was probably weaker than its publishers claimed, a fitting footnote for a song about ownership.","Hear Guthrie's own 1944 take first, then look up a performance that includes the private-property verse, such as Pete Seeger and Bruce Springsteen at the Lincoln Memorial in 2009. Then play Kate Smith's 'God Bless America' and hear the argument in the difference of tone: one song kneels, the other walks."],"lead-belly-last-sessions":["Hours of Huddie Ledbetter at home, singing to his twelve-string guitar and talking between songs about where each one came from: field hollers, prison work songs, children's game songs, spirituals, cowboy songs, blues, and his own topical pieces. The twelve-string rings like a small piano, with a thumping bass line under chiming upper strings. Because he is relaxed and unrushed, the songs often run far longer than on his three-minute commercial sides.","The jazz writer Frederic Ramsey Jr. recorded him in New York in the autumn of 1948 on one of the new portable magnetic tape machines, which freed the session from the three-minute limit of the disc. Lead Belly was already weakened by the motor neurone disease that killed him in December 1949. Moses Asch's Folkways issued the tapes in 1953, just as young urban musicians were starting to learn his repertory.","The spoken introductions are a masterclass in how a folk singer thinks about sources: he credits people, places and prison farms, sometimes contradicting the stories he told the Lomaxes in the 1930s. Listen to how he stamps and slaps time, and to the way he half-speaks verses before singing them, a habit from years of performing for audiences who needed the words explained.","Issued posthumously, the set became a textbook for the revival, a library of songs later recorded by the Weavers, Odetta and many others. It is also valued as one of the first long-form tape documents of any American vernacular musician. Some listeners find the talk repetitive; historians find it indispensable.","Choose a single disc and listen as if you were in the room. Notice how his guitar walks a bass line under everything. Then play the Weavers' 'Goodnight, Irene' of 1950 and hear what polish added and what it took away. The Smithsonian Folkways CD reissue is the edition to find."],"the-weavers-goodnight-irene":["A slow waltz, sung in four-part harmony by a quartet of two men and two women, with Pete Seeger's banjo and Fred Hellerman's guitar nearly buried under Gordon Jenkins's strings and background chorus. Ronnie Gilbert's alto cuts through on the chorus. It sounds like a gentle, swaying singalong for a dance hall at closing time, which is why it filled jukeboxes across the country.","The Weavers had been singing Lead Belly's song at the Village Vanguard, and Decca's Gordon Jenkins, a big-band arranger, signed them and dressed it for the pop market in 1950. It became one of the biggest hits of the year, and a wave of cover versions followed. Lead Belly had died a few months earlier, too soon to see his song reach millions. Within two years the group was blacklisted for its members' left-wing politics, and Decca dropped them.","Compare the words with Lead Belly's and you will find the darker verses gone, including one about despair. The quartet's harmonies come from union halls and summer camps; Seeger's banjo, trained on old-time records, sits under the string section like a working man at a wedding. The B-side, 'Tzena, Tzena, Tzena', an Israeli song, was also a hit.","Purists complained that the arrangement smothered the song; the pop audience did not care. Historians now see it as the moment folk song first became mass-market pop, and as a case study in the blacklist, since a group at the top of the charts could be driven off radio and television within two years. The record paid royalties to Lead Belly's widow for decades.","Play Lead Belly's own 'Irene' first, with its steady twelve-string and blunt voice, then the Weavers: listen for the moment the full chorus enters, and for Gilbert's voice on top. Then hear the Weavers' Carnegie Hall concert of 1955, where the same group sings without the strings and sounds like itself."],"harry-smith-anthology-of-american-folk-music":["Eighty-four commercial 78s from the late 1920s and early 1930s, gathered into three two-record volumes: Ballads, Social Music and Songs. Murder ballads, sacred harp singing, Cajun two-steps, jug bands, Delta blues, Baptist preaching and mountain fiddle tunes sit side by side, with Black and white performers mixed together. The voices are strange and unpolished; the old shellac hiss makes it sound like a message from a lost country.","Harry Smith, a young painter, film-maker and obsessive collector from the Pacific Northwest, had bought thousands of old records that the labels had deleted when the Depression killed the rural market. Moses Asch of Folkways agreed to issue a selection in 1952, largely ignoring copyright. At a moment of McCarthyism and bland pop, the set showed a generation of urban students an America that was violent, comic, devout and utterly unlike the one on television.","Smith's booklet summarises each song in the style of a tabloid headline and hides a private cosmology: the three volumes were linked in his mind to three of the four alchemical elements, and a fourth volume was compiled but not issued until 2000. The cover reproduces an engraving of a celestial monochord from the seventeenth-century mystic Robert Fludd.","Its influence on the revival is hard to overstate: Dylan, Baez, the New Lost City Ramblers and Dave Van Ronk all learned songs from it, and several of its forgotten performers were tracked down and recorded again in the 1960s. Critics such as Greil Marcus later read it as a secret history of America. The 1997 Smithsonian reissue won Grammys and reintroduced it to a new audience.","Start with volume one, the ballads, and follow the stories: Clarence Ashley's 'The Coo Coo Bird', Buell Kazee, the Carter Family. Then turn to the Social Music volume for the sermons and sacred singing. Read Smith's notes as you go. Seek the 1997 Smithsonian Folkways box, which reproduces his booklet."],"jean-ritchie-singing-the-traditional-songs":["A single high, clear, unornamented voice, sometimes alone and sometimes over the drone of the Appalachian dulcimer, a long, narrow fretted zither laid across the lap and strummed with a quill. The songs are ballads, love songs, play-party and religious songs handed down in her family in the Cumberland Mountains. The dulcimer holds one or two notes under the tune like a bagpipe, giving the modal melodies a sound that is ancient and plain at once.","Jean Ritchie was the youngest of fourteen children in Viper, Perry County, Kentucky, a family that sang constantly. After college she moved to New York as a social worker and was soon singing for Alan Lomax and at the city's folk gatherings. The young Elektra label made this its second release. The same year she went to Britain and Ireland on a Fulbright to trace her family's songs back to their sources.","In 1917 the English collector Cecil Sharp visited the Ritchie family and noted ballads from Jean's older sisters, so some of these songs had already passed through the same notebooks that fed Vaughan Williams's folk-song settings. Listen for the 'lonesome' vocal tone with no vibrato, and for tunes that end on a note other than the one a hymn would lead you to expect.","Received quietly, the record became a cornerstone for revival singers looking for an authentic source that was not a scratchy 78. Ritchie did more than anyone to spread the mountain dulcimer. Her family song 'Nottamun Town' later surfaced as the tune of Dylan's 'Masters of War', which led to a quiet settlement.","Listen with the lights low. Notice the dulcimer's steady drone and how the voice floats free of any beat. Then play Vaughan Williams's English Folk Song Suite or his Five Variants of Dives and Lazarus to hear the same family of tunes dressed for orchestra, and Copland's Appalachian Spring for a composer's imagined mountains."],"alan-lomax-parchman-prison-songs":["Groups of men singing as they work: a leader calls a line and the gang answers, while axes and hoes fall on the beat, so the percussion is the work itself. Some songs are slow and heavy, timed to felling trees; others are quicker, for hoeing. The voices are rough, powerful and tightly together, bending notes and holding long, wavering tones. 'Rosie' and 'Early in the Mornin'' are among the pieces a listener will not forget.","Alan Lomax, who had first visited Parchman Farm with his father John in the 1930s, returned in 1947 and 1948 to record the prisoners, most of them Black men serving time under a system that worked much like slavery. The recordings were issued a decade later on a small New York folk label, Tradition, and reached a white student audience that had never imagined such music existed.","The songs have a job: they set a pace everyone can keep, so that no one is singled out by guards for falling behind, and they carry coded complaint about sentences, women and the 'captain'. Listen for the moment when the axe strokes land a fraction behind the downbeat of the leader's line; that tiny delay is how a gang of twenty stays together.","The record was admired by folklorists and blues scholars and influenced musicians from Odetta to later blues-rock bands. Today it is also discussed for the ethics of recording captive people who could not refuse, and for how Lomax's framing shaped what outsiders heard. Reissued by Rounder as Prison Songs, it remains one of the essential documents of African American music.","Play it loud and let the rhythm of the tools take over. Pick one song and follow only the answering chorus. Then play Lead Belly's 'Midnight Special', another prison song, and Johnny Cash's At Folsom Prison, which turned the prison concert into a stage. The Rounder CD sounds best."],"odetta-sings-ballads-and-blues":["One voice, huge and dark, with a guitar strummed so hard it sounds like a drum. Odetta sings work songs, spirituals, prison songs and blues: 'Water Boy', 'Muleskinner Blues', 'Another Man Done Gone', and a spiritual medley that includes 'Oh, Freedom'. She moves from a whisper to a full, trained contralto roar within a line, and her guitar pounds out a single rhythmic pattern that drives every song.","Odetta Holmes, born in Birmingham, Alabama, and raised in Los Angeles, trained as a classical singer and worked in musical theatre before turning to folk song in the San Francisco coffeehouses. This first solo album came out on Tradition, the small New York label run by the Clancy Brothers' circle, in the year of the Montgomery bus boycott. Her songs of forced labour and defiance were heard as civil rights statements.","Listen for her 'hammer' on the guitar: she strums on the beat with the force of a work gang, which is why 'Water Boy' sounds like a prison song even without axes. Her classical training shows in the breath control on the long notes. Bob Dylan said that hearing this record in a Minneapolis shop made him trade his electric guitar for an acoustic.","The album made her a star of the revival and was widely copied. Martin Luther King Jr. is reported to have called her the queen of American folk music. Later critics note how she turned field material into concert performance, the same shift that made the revival possible, and how her authority as a Black woman gave that shift its moral weight.","Start with 'Water Boy' and let the guitar's pounding pull you in, then 'Oh, Freedom', which she sang at the March on Washington in 1963. Compare the Parchman work-song recordings for the source and the Weavers for the gentle alternative. Any Tradition reissue is fine."],"the-weavers-at-carnegie-hall":["A live concert: four voices, Pete Seeger's banjo, Fred Hellerman's guitar and an audience that sings along. The set list ranges across the world, from 'Wimoweh' (the South African song later known as 'The Lion Sleeps Tonight') to 'Kisses Sweeter Than Wine', 'Darling Corey' and songs in Hebrew and Spanish. Without the studio strings, the harmonies are close and bright, and Lee Hays's deep bass anchors the chorus.","Blacklisted and dropped by Decca, the Weavers had broken up in 1952. Their manager Harold Leventhal booked Carnegie Hall for Christmas Eve 1955 for a reunion, and it sold out. The young Vanguard label released the recording in 1957. For a generation of students, this record, not the pop hits, was the Weavers: the group as political survivors singing to their own public.","Listen to the audience. The Weavers treat the crowd as a fifth singer, lining out words and pausing for response, a technique borrowed from union meetings and churches. Seeger's shrieking falsetto on 'Wimoweh' is his imitation of Solomon Linda's 1939 Zulu recording, whose composer received almost nothing for decades.","It sold steadily for years and inspired the Kingston Trio and dozens of college groups. Critics hear in it the purest statement of the Popular Front folk ideal: a sing-along world music. Later writers have questioned the Weavers' handling of songs like 'Wimoweh' that they borrowed from other cultures, while still treating the concert as a turning point.","Put it on as a concert and listen for the moment the crowd starts to join in. Compare their studio 'Goodnight, Irene' to hear the difference between pop gloss and the real thing. Then try Pete Seeger's We Shall Overcome for the same method put to the civil rights movement."],"elizabeth-cotten-folksongs-and-instrumentals-with-guitar":["Quiet, rolling guitar pieces and a few songs sung in a soft, unassuming voice. The playing is the thing: a steady alternating bass with the thumb and a melody picked above it, sweet and unhurried, as in 'Freight Train' and the parlour-style 'Vastopol'. The songs and rags sound like music made at home for the pleasure of it, never pushing for effect.","Elizabeth Cotten, from Chapel Hill, North Carolina, had played guitar as a girl, then given it up for work and church for decades. In Washington she met the Seeger family after returning young Peggy Seeger, lost in a department store, and went to work as their housekeeper. Years later the family found her playing again, and Mike Seeger recorded her at home. She was in her sixties when this album appeared.","Cotten was left-handed and played a right-handed guitar upside down, so her thumb picks the melody and her fingers play the bass, the reverse of the normal method. This gives her bass a lighter, more melodic lilt. She wrote 'Freight Train' as a girl, around 1906; a British skiffle version in 1957 carried other people's names until she won credit back.","The album spread 'Cotten picking' through the revival; Peggy Seeger, Joan Baez, the Grateful Dead and many others took up her songs. She began touring in her seventies and won a Grammy in 1985. Critics now value her as a composer in her own right rather than a folk curiosity.","Start with 'Freight Train' and listen only to the bass, the steady thumb, then only to the melody on top. Then hear the Anthology's rural guitarists and Mississippi John Hurt's Today! for kindred fingerpicking. The Smithsonian Folkways reissue is the one to have."],"the-kingston-trio-tom-dooley":["A spoken introduction about a love triangle, then a slow, minor-key ballad of a man awaiting execution, sung by three clean-cut young men over a strummed guitar and a plucked banjo. The chorus, telling the condemned man to bow his head, is sung in close harmony and repeated until it lodges in the brain. Short, smooth and catchy, it sounds like a campfire singalong with a gallows in it.","The Kingston Trio, three college students from the San Francisco Bay Area, recorded it on their first Capitol album; a disc jockey's airplay made it a surprise number one in late 1958. It sold in the millions and set off a craze for collegiate folk groups, banjos and striped shirts. With the Weavers blacklisted, this was a politically safe folk song for suburban America.","The song is about a real killing: Tom Dula was hanged in Statesville, North Carolina, in 1868 for the murder of Laura Foster. The trio's version came from the revival's collectors, who had learned it from the North Carolina singer Frank Proffitt; Proffitt's family had sung it for generations, and he later won a share of the royalties.","Purists scorned the trio's polish and lack of politics, but their record made folk commercially viable and opened doors for Baez, Dylan and Peter, Paul and Mary. It won a Grammy in the first year of the awards, oddly in a country category. Historians now see it as the start of the revival's mass phase.","Listen once for the tune, then read the story of Tom Dula's 1868 trial and listen again: the calm, pretty harmonies sit oddly against a hanging. Next find Doc Watson's version, which he learned in his own North Carolina family, for the mountain original with its harder edge. Finish with the Weavers at Carnegie Hall to hear the political sing-along the trio carefully avoided."],"the-new-lost-city-ramblers-the-new-lost-city-ramblers":["A string band of three young New Yorkers reproducing the sound of the rural records of the 1920s and 1930s: sawing fiddle, frailing banjo, a steady guitar and nasal, high-pitched voices. The songs are old-time dance tunes, comic songs and ballads. The performances are rough on purpose, with the rhythmic drive and loose tuning of the originals.","Mike Seeger, John Cohen and Tom Paley formed the group in 1958 to play the music they had found on old 78s and Harry Smith's Anthology. Against the smooth commercial folk of the Kingston Trio, they argued for faithfulness to the originals. Folkways released their debut, and they toured colleges and festivals, often bringing the older musicians they admired onto the same stage.","Mike Seeger was the son of Ruth Crawford Seeger, the modernist composer who spent her last years transcribing folk songs for the Lomaxes, and half-brother of Pete Seeger. Listen for how they swap instruments between songs to match the lineups of particular old records, a kind of historically informed performance before the term existed in folk music.","Critics praised their scholarship and some found them stiff. Their larger achievement was to point audiences back to the older musicians, several of whom were rediscovered and recorded partly because of them. The 'old-time' revival of later decades descends directly from their example.","Pick a tune and then find its source on the Anthology to hear how close they come. Listen to the fiddle's short, bouncing bow strokes. Then hear Old Time Music at Clarence Ashley's for the real thing recorded in the 1960s."],"alan-lomax-sounds-of-the-south":["A series of LPs recorded across the American South: Black and white church singing, fife-and-drum bands, mountain fiddlers, blues guitarists, prison singers, children's game songs and banjo players. The sound is unusually vivid for field recordings, with the fiddles and voices placed in stereo space. It works as a musical atlas of the region at the end of the 1950s, before much of it vanished.","Lomax had spent most of the 1950s in Britain and Europe, partly to escape the blacklist. Back home in 1959 he set out on a 'Southern Journey' with the English singer Shirley Collins and a stereo tape machine, recording in homes, churches and prisons. Atlantic, a rhythm and blues label, issued the results in 1960, bringing the music to a far larger audience than most field recordings reached.","This trip produced the first recordings of the Mississippi hill-country guitarist Fred McDowell, who became a revival star. Listen for the fife-and-drum pieces, a tradition of Black marching music from northern Mississippi that preserves military drumming alongside African rhythmic ideas. Lomax's archive later became a sampling source; Moby's Play (1999) built several hits on his field recordings.","Hailed as a landmark in audio documentation, the series influenced a generation of musicians and scholars. Lomax's romantic framing of 'vanishing' traditions has been debated, but the recordings themselves are treated as essential. Atlantic's 1993 CD reissue brought them back into print.","Choose one disc and let it play as an unfolding landscape. Try the fife-and-drum tracks first for a sound you will not hear elsewhere, then the church singing. Pair with the Anthology for the 1920s view of the same region and Bartók's Romanian recordings for another collector's journey."],"joan-baez-joan-baez":["A young soprano with a crystal-clear, fast vibrato and a steady fingerpicked guitar. The songs are old ballads and folk songs: 'Silver Dagger', 'House of the Rising Sun', 'Mary Hamilton', 'Henry Martin', 'East Virginia', 'Wildwood Flower', and the Mexican 'El Preso Número Nueve'. Each is sung simply, with no drama, the purity of the voice doing the emotional work.","Baez, nineteen, had built a following in the Boston and Cambridge coffeehouses and made a stir at the 1959 Newport Folk Festival. Vanguard recorded this album with minimal production. It sold steadily and made her the revival's first female star; within two years she was on the cover of Time. Her mother's Scottish roots and father's Mexican heritage are both reflected in the songs.","Several songs are Child ballads, numbered in Francis James Child's nineteenth-century collection of English and Scottish ballads: 'Mary Hamilton' is Child 173, 'Henry Martin' Child 250. Their tunes reached Baez partly through American mountain versions, via singers like Jean Ritchie. Listen to how she holds the last note of a verse longer than the beat, lingering like a church singer.","Critics raved about the voice, and the album stayed on the charts for well over a year, a rare feat for a record of traditional songs with one guitar. Some later listeners found the purity cool and distant beside Odetta or Jean Ritchie. Historians now see it as the moment a middle-class audience embraced old ballads as serious art, and as the template for the female folk singer that Judy Collins and others followed.","Start with 'Silver Dagger' and notice how little the guitar does. Then try 'Mary Hamilton' and follow the story of a queen's lady hanged for infanticide. Compare the Carter Family's 'Wildwood Flower' for the guitar style she borrows, and Vaughan Williams's folk settings for the ballads' English cousins."],"pete-seeger-where-have-all-the-flowers-gone":["A slow, gentle circle of questions and answers that follows flowers to the young women who pick them, the women to the men they marry, the men to war, the soldiers to graves, and the graves back to flowers. Seeger sings it quietly over a picked accompaniment. A rueful refrain asking whether people will ever learn turns a children's chain-song into an anti-war lament.","Seeger wrote the first verses in 1955 after reading a Cossack song quoted in Mikhail Sholokhov's novel And Quiet Flows the Don, during the years he was blacklisted and called before the House Un-American Activities Committee. Joe Hickerson added verses in 1960 that closed the circle. The Kingston Trio and Peter, Paul and Mary made it a hit, and Marlene Dietrich sang it in German.","The circular form is the whole meaning: the song ends where it began, with no resolution, because war repeats. The 'flowers' of the Sholokhov source are part of an old Russian song pattern, so the song is itself a folk process: a novel quoting a folk song, a folk singer quoting the novel.","It became one of the most translated protest songs of the century and was sung across the Cold War divide. Some found it sentimental; for others its simplicity was the point. The Kingston Trio's early credit of 'traditional' caused a rights dispute that Seeger resolved amicably.","Hear Seeger's own quiet version first and follow the chain from verse to verse until it closes on itself. Then play Marlene Dietrich's German recording, a weary cabaret lament from a woman who had sung for Allied troops. Compare the Kingston Trio's bright, strummed version for how much a tempo changes a song. Finally turn to Britten's War Requiem, finished two years later, for a composer's answer to the same question."],"clarence-ashley-old-time-music-at-clarence-ashleys":["A group of neighbours from the mountains of northeastern Tennessee and western North Carolina playing old-time songs and tunes: Clarence Ashley's clawhammer banjo, Doc Watson's flatpicked guitar, a fiddle, and several voices singing ballads and hymns. The music is dense and rhythmic, the voices high and hard. It sounds like a front-porch party, which it partly was.","Ashley had recorded in the late 1920s; his 'The Coo Coo Bird' was on Harry Smith's Anthology. The folklorist Ralph Rinzler and the collector Eugene Earle found him in 1960 and recorded him with local musicians, including a blind guitarist named Doc Watson who was then playing electric guitar in a dance band. The album brought both to the Newport and college festival circuit.","Listen for Doc Watson's guitar: he had been playing rockabilly, and Rinzler persuaded him to play acoustic, flatpicking fiddle tunes at high speed. That style changed the guitar's role in bluegrass and folk. Ashley's banjo is tuned to open chords that give his songs their eerie modal sound.","The record was praised for capturing a living tradition rather than a revival copy. Watson became one of the most admired guitarists in American music. The album's documentary method, recording older musicians at home, became a model for later folklorists.","Start with a banjo song led by Ashley and listen to the clawhammer stroke, a down-picking brush that makes the banjo sound like a drum and a fiddle at once. Then find a track where Doc Watson takes the lead and notice how cleanly and fast he picks single notes. Compare Ashley's 1929 'The Coo Coo Bird' on the Anthology to hear what thirty years did to his voice, and what they did not."],"pete-seeger-turn-turn-turn":["The words are almost entirely from the third chapter of Ecclesiastes, the passage that pairs a season for every opposite: birth and death, planting and harvest, war and peace. Seeger added only the three-word refrain of the title and a closing line pleading that peace is still possible. His own recording is plain, over a strummed twelve-string guitar, with a melody that rises gently on each 'turn'.","Seeger wrote it in the late 1950s and recorded it for Columbia around 1962, while still fighting a contempt-of-Congress conviction for refusing to name names. Judy Collins recorded it in 1963 with Roger McGuinn as arranger, and McGuinn's band, the Byrds, took it to number one in late 1965 with electric twelve-string guitar and harmonies, at the height of the Vietnam escalation.","The one line Seeger added, the plea for peace at the end, turns an ancient text about acceptance into a protest. The song is also a sly argument for folk process: Seeger always said he had only arranged the words of 'the original writer', and used the song to show that old texts could be made new by singing.","The Byrds' version is far better known and made the song a folk-rock landmark. Seeger's own recording is now valued as the source and a reminder of his craft as a songwriter. It is still one of the few number-one hits drawn from the Bible.","Listen to Seeger's version with its plain strum, then the Byrds' 1965 single, and notice how Roger McGuinn's chiming electric twelve-string and the stacked harmonies turn a sermon into a pop record. Judy Collins's 1963 recording sits between the two. Then read the third chapter of Ecclesiastes aloud and hear how little Seeger had to change to make it singable."],"peter-paul-and-mary-peter-paul-and-mary":["Two men and a woman with two guitars and close, polished harmonies. The album includes 'Lemon Tree', 'If I Had a Hammer', '500 Miles' and 'Where Have All the Flowers Gone?'. Mary Travers's strong voice leads and the men's voices wrap around it. The sound is clean, warm and precise, perfectly pitched for college dormitories and suburban living rooms.","The trio was assembled in Greenwich Village by the manager Albert Grossman, who also managed Dylan, as a commercial folk group with a political edge. Warner Bros. released this debut in 1962 and it became one of the best-selling albums of the decade's first half. 'If I Had a Hammer', written by Pete Seeger and Lee Hays of the Weavers, became a civil rights song in their hands.","The arrangements are more complex than they seem: listen for how the three voices trade melody and harmony line by line, and for the counter-melodies that the guitars and voices weave. 'If I Had a Hammer' had been written in 1949 for a left-wing cause and was a flop; the trio's version made it a hit and a movement song.","Purists called them commercial, but their success carried Dylan's songs to a mass audience and gave the civil rights movement a soundtrack on the radio. They sang at the March on Washington in 1963. The album won Grammys and remains a best-selling folk record.","Play 'If I Had a Hammer' and listen for how the voices divide the verse. Then hear the Weavers at Carnegie Hall to hear what the trio learned, and Dylan's own 'Blowin' in the Wind' to see how differently a song can sound."],"bob-dylan-blowin-in-the-wind":["Three verses, each a string of questions about how long war, injustice and indifference must go on before people see them. The answer is always the same, and always withheld: it is out there, carried on the air, for anyone willing to catch it. Dylan sings it plainly, strumming a guitar, with a harmonica break between verses. It is short and simple enough that anyone can sing it.","Dylan, twenty, wrote it in April 1962 and first sang it at Gerde's Folk City in Greenwich Village. It appeared on his second album in May 1963, but Peter, Paul and Mary's cover a month later made it a hit. The song became an anthem of the civil rights movement and was sung at the March on Washington. Sam Cooke was so moved that a Black songwriter had not written it that he wrote 'A Change Is Gonna Come' in response.","The melody is borrowed from the spiritual 'No More Auction Block', which Dylan had heard in the Village, perhaps in Odetta's version. The chord pattern repeats with minimal variation, so the song's force comes from the words and the unchanging refrain. Dylan later said it was not a protest song but a song about the mystery of indifference.","It made Dylan famous and the spokesman he did not want to be. Some critics find the lyrics vague; others argue that vagueness is what made it universal. It has been recorded hundreds of times and is taught in schools as a key text of the 1960s.","Hear Dylan's own version first, rough and quick, then Peter, Paul and Mary's, which sold far more and made the song an anthem: notice how their harmonies turn a question into a hymn. Then play Sam Cooke's 'A Change Is Gonna Come' and hear one song answering another. Finally seek out a recording of the spiritual 'No More Auction Block' to hear the tune's roots in slavery."],"bob-dylan-a-hard-rains-a-gonna-fall":["Seven minutes of apocalyptic visions, sung over a steady strum. Each verse opens with a parent questioning a son about where he has been and what he has seen, and the answer is a torrent of images of a wounded world: poisoned landscapes, empty roads, crowds who cannot speak or be heard. The refrain warns of a coming deluge. The lines pile up relentlessly until the final verse becomes the son's vow to go back out and bear witness.","Dylan wrote it in the autumn of 1962 and sang it first at a Carnegie Hall hootenanny that September, weeks before the Cuban missile crisis; many listeners later assumed it was written during the crisis. It appeared on The Freewheelin' Bob Dylan in 1963. Pete Seeger called it the song that would outlive all the others from those years.","The question-and-answer frame comes straight from the old British ballad 'Lord Randal', Child 12, in which a mother asks her son where he has been, and he answers that he has been poisoned. Dylan keeps the form and replaces the story with a vision. Listen for how each answer-line starts with the same words, a litany like a preacher's.","It is often called Dylan's first great poem and was cited when he won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 2016; Patti Smith sang it at the ceremony and stumbled on a verse. Critics still argue about whether it is about nuclear war or something broader.","Give it full attention once: seven minutes, no chorus to rest in, and a guitar that barely changes, so the images carry everything. Then look up Patti Smith's performance at the 2016 Nobel ceremony, where her nerves and recovery made the song new. Finally find a traditional singing of 'Lord Randal' and hear the old question-and-answer frame that Dylan borrowed and filled."],"bob-dylan-the-freewheelin-bob-dylan":["Thirteen songs, nearly all Dylan's own, sung to his guitar and harmonica. Protest anthems like 'Blowin' in the Wind' and 'Masters of War' sit beside love songs like 'Girl from the North Country' and 'Don't Think Twice, It's All Right', and comic talking blues like 'Talkin' World War III Blues'. The voice is nasal and rough, the guitar steady and simple, and the words are the main attraction.","Dylan's first album had sold poorly, and Columbia executives called him 'Hammond's folly' after John Hammond, who had signed him. This second album, recorded over a year and released in May 1963, made him a star. The cover shows Dylan and his girlfriend Suze Rotolo walking down a snowy Greenwich Village street. Her absence in Italy during the recording coloured the love songs.","Several melodies come from British folk songs Dylan learned from Martin Carthy in London in the winter of 1962–63: 'Girl from the North Country' borrows from 'Scarborough Fair', and 'Bob Dylan's Dream' from 'Lord Franklin'. 'Masters of War' uses Jean Ritchie's family song 'Nottamun Town'. Dylan had also heard an advance copy of Robert Johnson's King of the Delta Blues Singers from Hammond.","It was hailed at once and has stayed central to the singer-songwriter canon. Critics see it as the record that turned folk singers into writers of their own material. Some purists missed the traditional songs; many listeners felt a new kind of voice had arrived.","Take three songs in a row for the album's range: 'Girl from the North Country' for tenderness, 'Masters of War' for fury, 'Talkin' World War III Blues' for comedy. Then play Martin Carthy's 'Scarborough Fair' and Jean Ritchie's family songs to hear the tunes Dylan borrowed, and Guthrie's Dust Bowl Ballads for the talking-blues model. The mono mix is closest to what listeners heard in 1963."],"pete-seeger-we-shall-overcome":["A live concert recorded at Carnegie Hall in June 1963, with Seeger alone on stage with banjo and twelve-string guitar, leading an audience that becomes his choir. The programme is freedom songs and topical songs, climaxing with the title song, sung slowly by the whole hall. The effect is of a mass meeting as much as a concert, with Seeger lining out each verse before the crowd sings it back.","'We Shall Overcome' came from a hymn by the Black Methodist minister Charles Albert Tindley and was adapted by striking tobacco workers in the 1940s. Zilphia Horton taught it at the Highlander Folk School, where Seeger learned it and altered the words; Guy Carawan brought it to the student sit-in movement in 1960. The concert came months before the March on Washington, while Seeger was still banned from network television.","Seeger is credited with changing one word of the refrain, 'will' to 'shall', saying the open vowel sang better. Listen for how he slows the tempo until the song almost stops, which lets a thousand people sing together. He also sings songs by younger writers such as Malvina Reynolds and Bob Dylan, using his platform to spread them.","The album spread the song worldwide and helped make it the anthem of civil rights movements from South Africa to Northern Ireland. Some historians emphasise that Black activists, not Seeger, made the song what it became; Seeger always said the same.","Play the album in order and save the title track for last, as the concert intends: notice how the audience grows bolder with each song until the hall is singing on its own. Then hear Curtis Mayfield's 'People Get Ready' for the same hope in soul music, and Frederic Rzewski's North American Ballads, which turn protest songs of the same tradition into virtuoso concert piano pieces."],"bob-dylan-the-times-they-are-a-changin":["Ten songs, spare and dark, just voice, guitar and harmonica. The title song is a hymn-like warning to parents and politicians. 'The Lonesome Death of Hattie Carroll' tells of a Black barmaid killed by a rich white man's cane. 'Only a Pawn in Their Game' is about the murder of Medgar Evers. 'With God on Our Side' marches through American wars. The mood is grave, the voice harsh.","Dylan recorded it in the autumn of 1963, the season of the March on Washington and the Birmingham church bombing, and Columbia released it in January 1964. It is his most topical album, and his last before he turned away from protest. He had sung 'Only a Pawn in Their Game' at a voter-registration rally in Greenwood, Mississippi, that summer.","'With God on Our Side' takes its melody from Dominic Behan's 'The Patriot Game', itself from an Irish folk tune. 'Hattie Carroll' is built with a refrain that tells mourners to wait: only in the last verse, when the killer receives a six-month sentence, does Dylan tell them to weep. Listen for how he withholds the moral until then.","On release some critics found it preachy and grim after the wit of Freewheelin', and Dylan himself soon disowned the role of spokesman. Many now consider 'The Lonesome Death of Hattie Carroll' among his finest songs, a model of restraint in narrative. The title song has become a generational motto, often borrowed by the very politicians and advertisers it warned, which says something about how protest songs age.","Start with 'The Lonesome Death of Hattie Carroll' and hear the whole story before judging, noticing how the refrain shifts only in the last verse. Then the title song, sung like a hymn from a pulpit. Compare Phil Ochs's I Ain't Marching Anymore for another singing journalist, and Sam Cooke's 'A Change Is Gonna Come', released the same year, for the civil rights era heard from the other side."],"buffy-sainte-marie-its-my-way":["A startling voice, with a strong vibrato and sudden swoops into a high, piercing register, over a bold, driving guitar. The songs are her own: 'Now That the Buffalo's Gone', about the treatment of Native Americans; 'Cod'ine', about addiction; 'Universal Soldier', about who makes war. In 'Cripple Creek' she plays the mouth bow, a single-string instrument held against the mouth, which gives an eerie twang.","Sainte-Marie, raised in New England, had become a star of the Greenwich Village and Toronto coffeehouses while still in college. Vanguard released this debut in 1964. She presented herself as a Cree woman adopted in infancy, and her songs about Indigenous rights were among the first in popular music. In 2023 a CBC investigation produced evidence that she was born in Massachusetts to a white family, and her identity became the subject of intense controversy.","Listen to how she uses her voice as an instrument, with sudden changes of timbre and vibrato. 'Cod'ine' was drawn from her own experience of addiction after being prescribed the drug; it was covered by Quicksilver Messenger Service and others. The mouth bow on 'Cripple Creek' is an Appalachian and Indigenous instrument, the kind of detail she used to widen the revival's sound.","The album made her a name, and 'Universal Soldier' became an anti-war standard. Her songs about Indigenous people were celebrated as a breakthrough. The 2023 revelations complicated her legacy: some honours were withdrawn, while the songs remain in circulation and are still debated.","Start with 'Now That the Buffalo's Gone', then 'Cod'ine', and listen to how her voice changes colour, from a near-whisper to a sudden, piercing cry with a wide vibrato. Then play 'Cripple Creek' for the twang of the mouth bow. Compare Joan Baez's debut of 1960 to hear two very different female voices in the same revival, one pure and still, the other restless."],"buffy-sainte-marie-universal-soldier":["A short, sharp song over a steady guitar. The verses describe one soldier who is every soldier, of every height, age, faith and nation, fighting for whichever flag he is given. The argument is that he is the one who offers his body to war, and that without his consent the wars could not happen. The ending lays the blame on the soldier, not only on the leaders.","Sainte-Marie wrote it in the early 1960s, before the big escalation in Vietnam, and included it on her debut album. The Scottish singer Donovan made it a hit in 1965. She later said the song was kept off American radio in the Johnson years, though that is hard to document.","The song's force comes from its list form: each verse widens the soldier to include more kinds of people until he stands for everyone, and only then does the argument turn toward the listener. The guitar pattern never changes, which keeps attention on the words. Listen for how she presses slightly ahead of the beat as the list grows, building urgency without raising her voice.","Donovan's 1965 cover made it a hit in Britain and spread it across the anti-war movement. Some critics found the argument simplistic, since conscripts rarely chose to fight; others saw it as one of the sharpest songs of the era precisely because it refuses to let anyone off. It remains widely sung, though the 2023 controversy over its writer's identity now shadows discussion of her work.","Hear Sainte-Marie's own version, short and fierce, then Donovan's gentler, more wistful cover and notice how the same words shift in a softer voice. Compare Seeger's 'Where Have All the Flowers Gone?' for a lament that blames no one, and Phil Ochs's 'I Ain't Marching Anymore' for a soldier's refusal told in the first person."],"dave-van-ronk-house-of-the-rising-sun":["A gravelly, bluesy voice over a guitar that walks a slow descending bass line under ringing chords. The song is an old ballad of ruin in a New Orleans house of ill repute. Van Ronk sings it with a rough tenderness, stretching the lines and letting the guitar's minor-key progression carry the mood.","Van Ronk, the leading guitarist and teacher of the Greenwich Village folk scene, developed this arrangement around 1960, drawing on older recordings. Bob Dylan learned it from him and recorded it on his debut album in 1962 without permission; Van Ronk stopped playing it because audiences thought he was copying Dylan. The Animals then used Dylan's version for their 1964 electric hit.","The descending bass line in Van Ronk's arrangement, walking down the chords, is the core of the Animals' hit and of every rock band's version since. The song itself goes back at least to the 1930s, when Alan Lomax recorded it from Georgia Turner in Kentucky.","Van Ronk's own recording is far less known than Dylan's or the Animals', and for years he got little credit for the arrangement that made them famous. Folk historians now recognise it as the source of the song's modern form. The Coen brothers' film Inside Llewyn Davis (2013), loosely drawn from his memoir, brought new listeners to his records, and his reputation as the Village's great teacher has only grown.","Play Van Ronk, then Dylan's 1962 debut, then the Animals' 1964 single, and trace the same walking bass line through all three: acoustic, then acoustic and hurried, then electric with organ. Then listen to Joan Baez's version on her first album, which follows a different, older melody. Finally hear Van Ronk's blues records for the ragtime fingerpicking behind his reputation."],"phil-ochs-i-aint-marching-anymore":["A clear, earnest tenor over a strong strummed guitar, delivering songs that read like editorials. The title song marches through American wars from the Revolution to Hiroshima and refuses to go on. 'Draft Dodger Rag' is a comic list of excuses to avoid the draft. 'Here's to the State of Mississippi' is a scathing attack on segregation. 'That Was the President' mourns John F. Kennedy.","Ochs, a journalism student turned folk singer, called himself a 'singing journalist' and titled his first album All the News That's Fit to Sing. This second album came out on Elektra in 1965, as the Vietnam War escalated. He was more openly political than Dylan and never turned away from protest, which limited his commercial success.","The title song hides a drumbeat inside the guitar strum, a march rhythm for a song that refuses to march. Its verses move in order through American wars, from the Revolution to Hiroshima, so the song doubles as a history lesson told by one soldier who has fought them all. 'Draft Dodger Rag' works the other way, using comedy to deliver the same refusal.","Ochs never approached Dylan's fame, and critics sometimes found him too literal, more headline than poetry. That directness is now seen as his strength: his songs are still sung at protests because their meaning cannot be mistaken. His reputation has grown steadily since his death in 1976, helped by documentaries and by younger singer-songwriters who cite him.","Start with 'Draft Dodger Rag' for the humour and the rapid patter, then the title song, and notice the march hiding in the strum. 'That Was the President' shows his gentler side. Compare Dylan's The Times They Are a-Changin' for a similar approach with more poetry, and Buffy Sainte-Marie's 'Universal Soldier' for another song about who bears responsibility for war."],"bob-dylan-bringing-it-all-back-home":["Side one is electric: a band with electric guitars, bass and drums backs Dylan on 'Subterranean Homesick Blues', 'Maggie's Farm' and 'She Belongs to Me'. Side two is acoustic, with four long songs: 'Mr. Tambourine Man', 'Gates of Eden', 'It's Alright, Ma (I'm Only Bleeding)' and 'It's All Over Now, Baby Blue'. The lyrics are surreal and allusive, far from the protest songs of two years earlier.","Recorded in January 1965 and released in March, the album marked Dylan's break with the folk revival. Four months later he played electric at the Newport Folk Festival, provoking boos and a legend that Pete Seeger wanted to cut the cables with an axe. The Byrds' electric 'Mr. Tambourine Man' reached number one that summer and launched folk rock.","The acoustic side is the real farewell to folk: 'It's All Over Now, Baby Blue' reads as a goodbye to the movement. 'Maggie's Farm' is a refusal to work for someone else's cause. Listen for how the acoustic songs are longer and more complex than anything on his earlier albums.","Folk purists were dismayed by the electric side and by lyrics that no longer took sides; rock critics hailed it as a turning point. It reached the top ten in America and number one in Britain. It is now seen as the album where folk and rock merged and the modern singer-songwriter was born, with the acoustic 'It's Alright, Ma' often ranked among Dylan's greatest songs.","Play side two first: four long acoustic songs that are the true farewell to the revival. Then side one, and hear the band crash in with 'Subterranean Homesick Blues'. Compare the Byrds' electric 'Mr. Tambourine Man' of the same year, and Seeger's 'Turn! Turn! Turn!' for the folk-rock connection. The original mono mix has the most punch."],"joan-baez-diamonds-and-rust":["A reflective song over a gently rocking band: an unexpected phone call from an old lover stirs memories of a shabby New York hotel room and a winter long ago. The chorus, with its ringing high notes, weighs those memories as something both precious and corroded, the image of the title. Baez's voice is lower and warmer than in 1960.","By 1975 Baez had moved from traditional ballads to writing her own songs, and this is the best known of them. It was the title song of an album on A&M, produced by David Kershenbaum with Los Angeles session players, and her biggest commercial success. The lover it recalls is widely understood to be Bob Dylan, with whom she had a relationship in the early 1960s and who was about to invite her on his Rolling Thunder tour.","The song's images are specific and personal: the phone call, the hotel, the gift of cufflinks. Baez later said she was surprised Dylan did not recognise himself. The song compresses the folk revival's story, its idealism and its disillusion, into one relationship.","Critics praised it as her finest original song, and the album went gold. Judas Priest's heavy metal cover in 1977 showed how far the melody could travel from folk. It is now seen as a classic of the singer-songwriter era and a rare case of the revival looking back on itself with both affection and irony, from the woman who had been at its centre.","Listen first for the story, then for the chords shifting under the chorus and the high notes she holds there. Compare her 1960 debut to hear how her voice lowered and warmed in fifteen years. Then play Dylan's Freewheelin' for the young man she remembers, and Judas Priest's cover for a startling second life."],"child-ballad-56-dives-and-lazarus":["A short ballad retelling the parable from Luke: the rich man Dives feasts while the beggar Lazarus starves at his gate; both die, angels carry Lazarus to Abraham's bosom, and serpents carry Dives to hell, where he begs for a drop of water. The verses are plain and quick, with no refrain. The tune most often sung to it is a wide-ranging modal melody that rises through an octave and settles gravely, one of the most recognisable shapes in English folk song.","A ballad of the rich man and poor Lazarus was licensed to a London printer in 1557, making this one of the oldest English ballad subjects on record. Francis James Child included it as number 56 of his English and Scottish Popular Ballads (1882–1898), the Harvard professor's attempt to fix the canon of traditional narrative song. The tune now associated with it was collected in the early twentieth century by Vaughan Williams and others, in several regional variants.","The melody has had several lives under other names. Vaughan Williams set a version of it as the hymn tune Kingsfold in the English Hymnal of 1906, and the same family of tunes carries the Irish song The Star of the County Down. When you hear any of them you are hearing cousins of the one melody, bent a little by different singers and districts.","Child printed only one text of this ballad, from an eighteenth-century source, and some scholars have doubted how widely it circulated orally in Britain. Its fame now rests almost as much on the tune as on the words, largely because Vaughan Williams made it the backbone of an orchestral work in 1939. Singers argue whether the fierce moral ending should be softened; traditional versions never soften it.","Hear it first sung unaccompanied, so the tune's long arch stands clear. Then play Vaughan Williams's Five Variants of Dives and Lazarus for strings and harp and notice how he treats the melody as a hymn. Finish with a sung version of The Star of the County Down and see if you can hear the family resemblance."],"child-ballad-10-the-twa-sisters":["A jealous elder sister pushes the younger into a river and lets her drown rather than lose a suitor. The body drifts to a mill; a passing minstrel makes a harp from her breastbone and strings it with her golden hair, and at the wedding feast the harp sings on its own and names the murderess. The ballad moves in two-line verses, each followed by a nonsense or refrain line such as Binnorie, O Binnorie, which keeps coming back like water.","A broadside printed in 1656 as The Miller and the King's Daughter is the earliest English text, but the story of a singing bone that accuses its killer is found right across northern Europe, especially in Scandinavia. Child gathered more than twenty variants for number 10 of his collection. The ballad lived on in Scotland, England, Ireland and the Appalachians, where the harp motif often fell away and the miller who robs the corpse took over.","Watch the refrain. In many versions it is a Scottish place name, Binnorie, sung after every line, so the listener hears the scene of the crime again and again before the murder is even told. Pentangle's 1970 title track Cruel Sister uses a different refrain, 'lay the bent to the bonny broom', and the change makes it feel like a spell instead of a lament.","Folklorists have long used this ballad as a test case for how songs cross languages: is the English text a translation from Norse, or did the story travel by many routes at once? Child's heavily Scottish selection has also been questioned by later collectors who found vigorous English and American variants he did not print. The supernatural harp is the part singers argue over keeping.","Choose an unaccompanied Scottish version first and let the refrain lull you before the push. Then hear Pentangle's Cruel Sister for the same story in a chamber-folk setting, and an Appalachian version, often titled The Old Miller, to hear how American singers trimmed the magic away."],"child-ballad-2-the-elfin-knight":["A riddle ballad in the form of a contest. A man, in the oldest texts an elf, sets a woman impossible tasks: make a shirt without seam or needlework, wash it in a dry well. She answers with equally impossible tasks for him: plough an acre between salt water and the sea strand, reap it with a sickle of leather. The verses carry a refrain of herbs, most famously parsley, sage, rosemary and thyme, and whoever runs out of answers first loses.","The earliest printed text is a broadside of around 1670, 'A Proper New Ballad Entitled The Wind Hath Blown My Plaid Away', where the elf is a supernatural suitor to be outwitted. Over three centuries the song lost its elf and became a love-token exchange; one branch settled in the north of England as Scarborough Fair, sung with the herb refrain. Child placed it second in his collection, just after another riddle ballad.","The herb refrain is not decoration. Parsley, sage, rosemary and thyme were all plants associated with protection and fidelity, and in riddle ballads the woman's wit is her defence against being carried off. In the oldest version she wins by refusing to be trapped. Later versions that sound merely wistful have quietly dropped the danger.","Scarborough Fair became world-famous through Martin Carthy's arrangement of 1965 and then Simon and Garfunkel's recording of 1966, which borrowed Carthy's setting without credit and caused a long quarrel, patched up only decades later. Folklorists treat the whole family as one ballad; most listeners have never heard of the elf, and the song's supernatural origin is now its least known fact.","Listen for the structure of the contest: task, counter-task, refrain. Start with a traditional English Scarborough Fair, then find a text that keeps the elfin knight so you hear the menace return. Compare Martin Carthy's guitar setting with the duo's hit to hear how one arrangement travelled."],"child-ballad-12-lord-randal":["A mother questions her son, just home from hunting. Where have you been? What did you eat? What became of your hounds? Each answer ends 'mother, make my bed soon, for I'm weary wi' hunting and fain wad lie down', and bit by bit the truth comes out: his sweetheart fed him eels, the dogs that ate the leftovers died, and he is poisoned. The ballad ends with his will, leaving hell and fire to the girl.","Walter Scott printed a Scottish text in his Minstrelsy of the Scottish Border in 1803, though an Italian song on the same story, L'avvelenato, is documented earlier, and the question-and-answer form has relatives across Europe. Child gathered many versions as number 12, including English and American ones where the victim is a child poisoned by a stepmother. It remained among the most widely sung ballads in the Appalachians.","Everything is done by indirection: no one ever says 'poison' in some versions. The hounds dying is the moment the listener works it out, and a good singer lets that verse land without emphasis. The final bequest structure, gifts to mother, sister, brother, then curse to sweetheart, is a standard device called the testament, found in several other ballads.","Critics from Scott onward praised Lord Randal as the model of ballad economy. Its most famous descendant is Bob Dylan's A Hard Rain's A-Gonna Fall (1962), which borrows the question-and-answer opening, 'Oh, where have you been, my blue-eyed son?'. Scholars still argue whether the ballad originated in Italy or Scotland; most now assume a shared European tale.","Follow the questions one by one and notice when you understand. Then listen to Dylan's Hard Rain and hear the old frame carrying nuclear dread. An Appalachian version, often called Henry, My Son, shows the song stripped even barer. Notice how the refrain line never changes while the news gets worse."],"child-ballad-58-sir-patrick-spens":["The king of Scotland sits drinking in Dunfermline and wants a skipper to sail to Norway. A knight names Sir Patrick Spens; the king writes a letter; Spens reads it, laughs, then weeps, because it is the wrong season. He sails anyway. The new moon with the old moon in her arm warns of storm; the ship goes down; and the ballad closes on the ladies waiting on the shore with their fans and golden combs, and Spens lying fifty fathoms deep with the Scots lords at his feet.","Bishop Thomas Percy printed the ballad in his Reliques of Ancient English Poetry in 1765, the book that launched the eighteenth-century ballad craze and fed Romanticism. Scholars have tried to link it to real voyages, perhaps the bringing of Margaret, Maid of Norway, in 1290 or an earlier royal marriage, but no single event fits. Child printed more than a dozen versions as number 58.","The wreck is never described. The ballad jumps straight from the omen to the drowned lords' hats floating on the water, a cut as sharp as any film edit. Coleridge quoted the new-moon omen as the epigraph to his Dejection: An Ode of 1802, so a Scottish sea ballad sits at the front door of English Romantic poetry.","Percy's short text, perhaps the best-known, may have been touched up for print, and scholars debate how much of the ballad's famous compression is an editor's work. Poets have admired it without reservation: it is anthologised as literature as often as it is sung. Singers now use longer traditional texts from Scotland alongside Percy's.","Hear it sung by a Scottish traditional singer, and notice the jump from omen to aftermath. Compare the sea music of Britten's Peter Grimes, where a town also watches the water. Then read the words on the page, as the Romantics did."],"child-ballad-84-barbara-allen":["A young man lies dying for love of Barbara Allen, who comes slowly to his bedside and says only 'young man, I think you're dying'. He dies; on her way home she hears the death bell, and seized with remorse she asks her mother to make her bed, and dies too. From his grave grows a rose, from hers a briar, and they twine together in a true lovers' knot. The tunes vary endlessly, but all move at a slow, dignified walk.","Samuel Pepys heard the actress Elizabeth Knepp sing 'her little Scotch song of Barbary Allen' on 2 January 1666 and wrote in his diary that it delighted him. From London theatres and printed broadsides it spread into oral tradition across Britain, Ireland and North America, where collectors found it more often than any other ballad. Child printed it as number 84.","The rose-and-briar ending is a floating motif, shared with several other ballads, and many singers attach it only when they feel like it. Listen for which tune your singer uses: Cecil Sharp alone collected dozens in England and the Appalachians, and the American ones are often in older modes than the English.","Folklorists regard Barbara Allen as the most widely collected ballad in the English language, and it is a favourite test of the singer: to hold attention through many verses with no refrain and almost no action. Arguments continue over whether it began as a Scottish song or a London stage piece dressed as Scottish; Pepys's phrase allows either.","Pick an unaccompanied version and let the slowness work. Then hear an Appalachian singer for a different mode, and Shirley Collins for the English plainness at its purest. Purcell's Dido's Lament makes a good neighbour: the same decades, another woman who asks to lie down and die."],"ewan-maccoll-dirty-old-town":["Four short verses and no chorus, sung to a plain tune in a steady walk. The singer meets his girl by the gasworks wall, dreams a dream by the old canal, kisses her by the factory wall; then a smoky wind blows through the town and he resolves to take a sharp axe to it and cut it down like an old dead tree. The last verse turns the love song into a quiet threat against the place itself.","MacColl wrote it in 1949 to cover a scene change in Landscape with Chimneys, a Theatre Workshop play about his native Salford, the industrial town next to Manchester. It was a working playwright's quick fix, not a planned folk song. MacColl sang it in the clubs and recorded it in the 1950s, and it passed into the repertoire of singers who had no idea who wrote it.","Local councils once tried to stop Salford's streets being associated with the song, and early printings sometimes softened the line about the axe. The shift in the last verse from courting to demolition is the hinge: MacColl was a political songwriter, and the town he wants cut down is the one the mills built, not the one he grew up loving.","The Dubliners made it an Irish pub standard in the late 1960s, and The Pogues' 1985 recording made many listeners assume it was Irish and traditional. MacColl himself was ambivalent about its success. It is now among the most covered British songs of the century, and a textbook case of a new song becoming 'folk' within one lifetime.","Hear MacColl's own spare version first, then The Dubliners' and The Pogues' to see how a Salford song became Dublin's. Listen for how each singer handles the last verse: gentle, bitter or rousing. Then go back to MacColl and ask which version the words themselves seem to want, and why a song about one English town travelled so easily."],"ewan-maccoll-the-first-time-ever-i-saw-your-face":["Three verses, each one line longer in feeling than in words: the first time I saw your face, the first time I kissed your mouth, the first time I lay with you. The tune rises slowly and falls back, with long held notes that leave the singer exposed. There is no chorus and no instrumental break; the whole song is a single slow breath, and most singers take it slower than it looks on paper.","MacColl wrote it in 1957 for Peggy Seeger, the American singer and half-sister of Pete Seeger, with whom he had fallen in love while still married; the story goes that he taught it to her over a transatlantic telephone line when she was touring. Seeger sang it in the folk clubs. It was written in the idiom of a traditional love song by a man who usually wrote about work and politics.","Peggy Seeger's mother was the modernist composer Ruth Crawford Seeger, who in the 1930s and 1940s transcribed Library of Congress field recordings with minute care. The song's plainness came from a household that took traditional singing as seriously as any score. Bert Jansch recorded an early cover on Jack Orion in 1966, before anyone outside the folk clubs knew it.","Roberta Flack's slowed-down recording of 1969 became a transatlantic hit in 1972 after Clint Eastwood used it in Play Misty for Me, and won a Grammy. MacColl reportedly disliked nearly every cover, including hers, finding them over-sung. Critics still divide between the spare folk versions and Flack's six-minute soul reading.","Hear Peggy Seeger sing it unaccompanied first, then Flack, and notice what extra time does to the words. Bert Jansch's guitar version sits between them. Ruth Crawford Seeger's Three Songs are a surprising neighbour from the same family. In every version, listen for where the singer chooses to breathe."],"ewan-maccoll-the-ballad-of-john-axon":["An hour-long radio programme telling the death of a steam-engine driver through songs, instrumental interludes and the recorded voices of railwaymen, his workmates and his widow. There is no narrator. The story, a runaway goods train on a steep gradient and a driver who stayed at the controls, is told in snatches of speech that pass straight into newly written songs in ballad and work-song styles, with fiddle, harmonica, banjo and guitar, and the rhythm of the engine underneath.","On 9 February 1957 John Axon's engine lost its brakes above Chapel-en-le-Frith in Derbyshire; he stayed on the footplate, was killed, and was awarded the George Cross. The BBC producer Charles Parker commissioned MacColl and Peggy Seeger to make a programme, and the three spent days recording railwaymen on portable tape. It was broadcast on the BBC Home Service in July 1958, the first of eight Radio Ballads made until 1964.","The breakthrough was technological: light portable tape recorders let the team collect hours of working people's speech, and instead of rewriting it for actors they cut the actual voices into the programme. MacColl then wrote song lyrics from phrases the railwaymen used, so the songs and the speech share words. It is a documentary made of montage, closer to musique concrète than to a radio play.","Critics at the time hailed it as a new form, and it won the Italia Prize for radio. Some colleagues at the BBC found its politics and its working-class voices uncomfortable. Its reputation has grown with its influence on documentary radio, oral history and later folk-opera projects; the series was revived in tribute in 2006.","Listen straight through without doing anything else, as its first audience did. Notice how often you cannot tell where a speaking voice ends and a song begins. Afterwards hear Britten's Peter Grimes interludes for another tale of working men and hard nature, told in the concert hall."],"shirley-collins-folk-roots-new-routes":["An album-length duet between an English ballad singer and a guitarist steeped in blues, jazz and North African music. Collins sings old songs such as Nottamun Town, Reynardine and Pretty Saro in her unadorned Sussex voice, while Davy Graham plays behind her in open tunings, with drones, bent notes and modal runs that owe as much to Morocco and Monk as to England. Graham also takes solo instrumentals, including a version of Monk's Blue Monk.","Collins had returned in 1959 from the Alan Lomax field trip through the American South and knew traditional music as a world-wide family. Graham, the London guitarist who had recorded the instrumental Anji in 1962, had spent time in Tangier. Decca put the two together in 1964, when the British folk clubs were split between purists and people who wanted to add guitars. The record sold modestly.","Graham tuned his guitar to what became known as DADGAD for some of these tracks, a tuning he had worked out playing Moroccan music; Jansch, Page and hundreds of Celtic guitarists adopted it. Listen for the drone strings he leaves ringing under Collins's voice, which give an English ballad the sound of an oud.","Purists in the clubs complained the accompaniments distracted from the songs. Later critics have treated the album as the moment English folk opened to the wider world, a direct ancestor of Pentangle and of every folk guitarist who plays in DADGAD. It is now considered one of the essential British folk albums.","Start with Nottamun Town and listen to the guitar more than the voice. Then play Graham's solo tracks. Compare with Bert Jansch's first album a year later to hear what he took, and with Mississippi John Hurt's recordings for the American fingerpicking Graham also loved."],"shirley-collins-anthems-in-eden":["Shirley Collins with her sister Dolly, who plays a small portative flute organ, and a consort of early instruments: crumhorns, sackbuts, rebecs and recorders. The first side is 'A Song-Story', a sequence of traditional songs joined by instrumental links into a single suite about an English village before, during and after the First World War. The sound is reedy, buzzing and strange, more like a Tudor household band than a folk club.","Dolly Collins had studied composition and arranged her sister's songs on an instrument built for her, a portable pipe organ small enough to take on tour. The early-music consort came from the circle around David Munrow, then reviving medieval and Renaissance instruments in London. EMI released the album on its new progressive label Harvest, alongside psychedelic rock, in the year of Fairport's Liege & Lief.","The organ is voiced like a chamber organ of Purcell's time, so the harmony has no piano or guitar at all. Dolly's arrangements use the kind of held chords and simple counterpoint Vaughan Williams and Holst put under folk tunes, and the suite form is deliberately like a song cycle. The war story is never stated outright; the choice of songs tells it.","It was broadcast in full by the BBC and admired, but sold slowly. Its reputation climbed with the later interest in English 'wyrd folk' and pastoral psychedelia, and it is now often named, with Liege & Lief, as the other road British folk could have taken: toward early music rather than rock.","Play the whole of side one without a break, as a cycle. Listen for the crumhorns' buzz against Shirley's straight voice. Then hear Vaughan Williams's English Folk Song Suite and Butterworth's Shropshire Lad to hear the same village before the same war."],"fairport-convention-a-sailors-life":["Eleven minutes on one traditional song. Sandy Denny sings the ballad of a girl whose sailor lover has drowned, over a slow, open-ended groove; then the band lets go, and Richard Thompson's electric guitar and Dave Swarbrick's fiddle trade and build over drums and bass into a long, storming improvisation before subsiding. There is no chorus and very few chords; the drone and the modal tune carry everything.","Fairport were still largely a covers band playing American songwriters when they cut this for Unhalfbricking, released in 1969. Swarbrick came in as a session fiddler. Weeks later, in May 1969, the band's van crashed on the M1, killing the drummer Martin Lamble and Thompson's girlfriend Jeannie Franklyn; the survivors decided to make a wholly traditional record, and this track had shown them how.","It was reportedly recorded in essentially one live take, with Denny teaching the others the song in the studio. Listen for Lamble's drumming, which moves from brushes to full rock without ever settling into a standard beat. Swarbrick's playing here got him invited to join, and the improvisation is where British folk rock was really born.","Contemporary reviews noticed it as an oddity on an eclectic album. Later critics and the band themselves came to see it as the prototype of Liege & Lief, and it is often cited as the first true English folk-rock recording. Some purists still find the long jam self-indulgent; most listeners hear it as a breakthrough.","Listen for the moment around the middle when the guitar stops accompanying and starts arguing with the fiddle. Then play Liege & Lief, recorded months later, to hear the idea worked out. Compare with Coltrane-era modal jazz for the same drone-based freedom."],"fairport-convention-liege-and-lief":["Eight tracks, most of them traditional: the ballads Matty Groves and Tam Lin, Reynardine and The Deserter, a medley of fiddle tunes, and three new songs in traditional style. Sandy Denny sings, Richard Thompson and Simon Nicol play electric guitars, Ashley Hutchings bass, Dave Swarbrick fiddle and Dave Mattacks drums. The instantly recognisable sound is a Child ballad sung straight over a rock band, the fiddle doubling the guitar in the instrumental breaks.","After the M1 crash of May 1969 the band retreated to a rented house in Farley Chamberlayne, Hampshire, and rehearsed a programme of English traditional material, with Hutchings researching songs at Cecil Sharp House, the library of the English Folk Dance and Song Society. Joe Boyd produced for Island. It was released in December 1969, the year of Woodstock, as an explicit English answer to American rock.","Matty Groves (Child 81) is a murder ballad about adultery and a duel, and the band speeds up over the final instrumental until it becomes a kind of hoedown jig after the killing. Tam Lin (Child 39) is a fairy ballad. The original sleeve included notes and pictures on the history of English folk custom, making the album a small lesson in the tradition.","It was a success on release and has since been voted the most influential folk album of all time by BBC Radio 2 listeners. Folk purists at the time objected to drums and amplification. Both Denny and Hutchings left within weeks, in opposite directions: she toward songwriting, he toward stricter tradition in Steeleye Span.","Start with Matty Groves and follow the story, then listen for the instrumental that grows out of it. Tam Lin next. Then go back to the Child ballads it draws on, and on to Vaughan Williams's English Folk Song Suite for the older, orchestral way of electrifying the same tradition."],"sandy-denny-who-knows-where-the-time-goes":["A slow, rocking song about the birds leaving the sky, the tide turning, and the singer staying put while time passes. Denny's voice floats over a gentle band arrangement by Fairport Convention, with Richard Thompson's electric guitar making small, liquid comments between lines. The tune rises to the title question and resolves quietly each time, and the song ends without a crescendo, as if the singer simply stopped watching.","Denny wrote it around 1967, still in her teens, and demoed it while briefly singing with the Strawbs. Judy Collins recorded it first and used it as the title of her 1968 album, which brought Denny royalties before she was well known. Fairport's version, with Denny singing, appeared on Unhalfbricking in 1969, the album recorded just before the crash that reshaped the band.","The guitar tuning and the chord changes are more sophisticated than most folk-club songs of the time, owing something to jazz and to Joni Mitchell. Listen to how Denny breathes before the title line, delaying it slightly each time. Written by a nineteen-year-old, it sounds like the work of someone old, which is exactly the trick.","It became Denny's signature, sung at her memorials and covered by dozens of singers from Nina Simone to Eva Cassidy. After her early death in 1978 it took on the sound of an epitaph, which critics have argued overstates its sadness: it is a song about accepting time, not fearing it.","Hear the Fairport version first, then Judy Collins's for the prettier folk-pop reading. Listen for the pause before each chorus. Delius's On Hearing the First Cuckoo in Spring makes a good English neighbour: the same seasons, the same stillness. Notice how little the band plays behind her."],"sandy-denny-the-north-star-grassman-and-the-ravens":["Denny's first solo album mixes her own songs with two traditional pieces and covers of Dylan and Brenda Lee. She plays piano on many tracks, and the arrangements move between stripped guitar and bass, a full folk-rock band with Richard Thompson on lead guitar, and orchestral strings. Late November opens with a dream of drowning; John the Gun is a cold anti-war portrait; Next Time Around is an elusive song about the American folk singer Jackson C. Frank, her former boyfriend.","Denny had left Fairport at the end of 1969, formed Fotheringay, and broken it up after one album. Island released this in 1971, the same year she sang with Robert Plant on Led Zeppelin's The Battle of Evermore. She was voted top British female singer by Melody Maker that year and again in 1972, but her records never sold in proportion to her reputation.","Blackwaterside, the traditional song on side two, is the same one Bert Jansch recorded in 1966 and Jimmy Page adapted as Black Mountain Side; Denny's version reclaims it as a song. Her lyrics are full of water, birds and sea, and the title's grassman and ravens are images she never explained.","Reviews were warm but puzzled by the mix of styles. Critics have since placed it among the best British singer-songwriter albums of its period, though some find it uneven next to her later Like an Old Fashioned Waltz. Its songs, especially Late November, have become favourites of later folk singers.","Start with Late November and listen for the piano under the voice. Then compare her Blackwaterside with Jansch's on Jack Orion. Finish with John the Gun, and notice how much she leaves out. Last, hear Who Knows Where the Time Goes? again and notice how far her writing has travelled in two years, from guitar song to something closer to a piano ballad."],"nick-drake-five-leaves-left":["Ten songs for voice and intricately fingerpicked guitar, set in several tracks against string arrangements, and in others with Danny Thompson's double bass or conga. River Man, the album's centre, moves in a lilting five-beat metre with strings that seem to slow time. Way to Blue is voice and string ensemble alone. The recognisable sound is Drake's close, murmuring baritone just in front of a guitar in an unusual tuning.","Joe Boyd, who produced Fairport Convention, signed Drake to Island while he was studying English at Cambridge, and the album was recorded in London with engineer John Wood at Sound Techniques. A fellow student, Robert Kirby, wrote most of the string arrangements; River Man was arranged by Harry Robinson. Released in 1969, it sold poorly, and Drake's rare concerts were unhappy affairs.","The title comes from the warning printed in packets of Rizla cigarette papers, telling you only five sheets remain. The strings on River Man were recorded live with Drake in the room, and the arranger was reportedly told to aim for the atmosphere of Delius. Richard Thompson plays electric guitar on Time Has Told Me.","It received few reviews in 1969 and those were polite. Reappraisal began in the 1970s and 1980s among musicians, and since the late 1990s it has regularly appeared in lists of the best albums of all time. Some critics argue its posthumous romance has inflated its standing; others hear in it the most delicate arranging on any British folk record.","Start with River Man, then Way to Blue. Listen to the guitar as if it were a piano: bass, middle and melody all moving at once. Afterwards hear Delius's Brigg Fair and its sister pieces for the English string sound Kirby and Robinson were reaching for."],"nick-drake-bryter-layter":["Drake's most arranged record: songs with a full rhythm section, horns, strings, flute and even backing singers, framed by three instrumentals that open, name and nearly close the album. Hazey Jane I and II, Poor Boy and At the Chime of a City Clock bring jazz and soul colours to his guitar; Northern Sky, with John Cale on celesta, piano and organ, is the tenderest love song he wrote.","Recorded in 1970 after Drake had left Cambridge a term short of graduating, with Joe Boyd producing and John Wood engineering, and members of Fairport Convention in the rhythm section. Boyd hoped for a commercial breakthrough and gave the album a fuller, more polished sound. It sold even fewer copies than the first, and Boyd moved to the United States soon after, leaving Drake without his chief ally.","Drake wrote the instrumentals as feature pieces, but he never sings on them, so almost a third of the album is his music without his voice. Listen to the three together as a kind of suite; Robert Kirby's writing for flute and strings is close to English light music. John Cale arranged and played on his two tracks himself.","Drake was reportedly devastated by its failure, and critics of the time largely ignored it. Some later listeners prefer it to his other records for its warmth and colour; others find the arrangements dated beside the bare Pink Moon. Northern Sky is now one of his most loved songs, used widely on film and television.","Begin with Northern Sky, then Hazey Jane I. Listen for the bass and drums locking under the guitar. Play the instrumentals as a set to hear Kirby's arranging. Then play Pink Moon straight after and feel the difference. Notice how the voice never changes, whatever the arrangement does."],"nick-drake-pink-moon":["Eleven songs in twenty-eight minutes, Drake alone with his guitar and, on the title track only, a few bars of piano. No strings, no band, no backing voices. The songs are short, some under two minutes, built on hypnotic repeated guitar patterns in open tunings, and the words are compressed to near-aphorism: Place to Be, Road, Which Will, Things Behind the Sun, From the Morning. The effect is intimate and uneasy, as if the singer were in the room and slightly out of reach.","By 1971 Drake was isolated, depressed and barely performing. He approached John Wood and recorded the album in two late-night sessions at Sound Techniques in London, then, the story goes, left the tapes at Island's reception. Island released it in February 1972 with little promotion. Drake recorded only a few more songs before his death in November 1974, aged twenty-six.","The piano overdub on the title track is the only addition on the whole album, and it makes the first song sound bigger than everything that follows. Listen to the guitar on Road and Things Behind the Sun: Drake uses tunings where open strings ring as drones against moving bass lines, closer to Jansch's and Davy Graham's methods than to American folk strumming.","Island ran full-page advertisements but the album barely sold. A Volkswagen advertisement using the title track in 1999 brought it to a mass audience and sales soared. Critics now treat it as one of the purest solo acoustic records ever made, while some warn that its reception is bound up with the legend of the doomed artist.","Listen in one sitting, late at night, as it was recorded. Notice how much the songs repeat, and how small changes in the guitar pattern carry the feeling. Afterwards play Blind Willie Johnson's Dark Was the Night, another record of one guitar saying more than words."],"richard-thompson-i-want-to-see-the-bright-lights-tonight":["The first album credited to Richard and Linda Thompson: ten songs, all by Richard, split between his dry voice and Linda's clear, warm one. The title song is a Saturday-night dance number with a silver band; Withered and Died and The End of the Rainbow are among the bleakest songs in English; The Calvary Cross is a guitar-led nightmare. His electric guitar solos sound like bagpipes and Northumbrian pipes bent through an amplifier.","Thompson had left Fairport in 1971 and made one uncertain solo album. He married the singer Linda Peters in 1972 and recorded this in 1973 with members of the folk-rock scene; Island held it back and released it in 1974, into a market dominated by glam and progressive rock. It sold little. Shortly afterwards the couple converted to Sufi Islam and briefly lived in a commune.","The title track's brass band is deliberately the sound of English working-town bandstands, the same world Dirty Old Town describes. On The End of the Rainbow a father sings a lullaby telling his baby there is nothing at the end of the rainbow; its gentle tune is part of the joke. Listen for how Thompson's guitar answers Linda's vocal lines like a second singer.","Reviews were good but few. Its reputation rose through the 1980s and it appears in many lists of the greatest British albums; Rolling Stone named it among the best of the 1970s. Some listeners find its misery relentless; its defenders point to how funny and humane much of it is.","Start with the title track for the swing of it, then Withered and Died for Linda's voice, then The Calvary Cross for the guitar. Go back to Liege & Lief to hear where Thompson came from. Keep an ear on the lyrics: the jokes are easy to miss under the tunes."],"richard-thompson-shoot-out-the-lights":["Eight songs about love going wrong, sung alternately by Richard and Linda Thompson over a lean electric band. Don't Renege on Our Love, Walking on a Wire and Did She Jump or Was She Pushed? are pleas and accusations; the title track is a menacing guitar piece with some of Thompson's most famous soloing; Wall of Death closes on a fairground ride as the one place you feel alive. The sound is raw, largely live, almost without folk instruments.","The Thompsons recorded a first version with Gerry Rafferty as producer, abandoned it, and then re-recorded the album with Joe Boyd for his small label Hannibal in 1981. By the time it was released in 1982 their marriage had ended, and the tour that followed, with both on stage, became notorious for its tension. It was their last record together.","Most of the songs were written before the break-up, so the album is not the diary it seems; the events caught up with the songs. Listen for Linda's vocal on Walking on a Wire, recorded while pregnant, and for the tiny guitar figures under the verses of Wall of Death that imitate the fairground machinery.","It was the Thompsons' best-reviewed and best-selling record, especially in the United States, where it reached many critics' lists of the year's best. It is now routinely named among the greatest albums of the 1980s. The debate is less about quality than about how far to read it as autobiography.","Start with Walking on a Wire and Wall of Death. Then play the title track loud for the guitar. Go back to Bright Lights to hear how the same pair sounded eight years earlier and happier. Notice which of the two voices you believe more, song by song."],"pentangle-basket-of-light":["Nine tracks mixing traditional ballads with originals. Light Flight skips along in shifting metres of five, six and seven with scat vocals; House Carpenter is the Child ballad (243) of a woman lured away by a former lover, sung by Jacqui McShee over sitar and banjo; Once I Had a Sweetheart and The Cuckoo are English songs given jazz-trio accompaniments. Two steel-string guitars interlock, Danny Thompson's double bass is sometimes bowed, and Terry Cox plays drums and glockenspiel with a jazzman's lightness.","Pentangle had formed in 1967 and built a following in London clubs and concert halls. Light Flight was chosen as the theme for the BBC drama Take Three Girls, and the album, released on the independent Transatlantic label in 1969, reached the upper reaches of the British album chart, a rare feat for acoustic folk. It was their commercial peak.","Terry Cox and Danny Thompson were a jazz rhythm section, and the odd metres come from them as much as from the guitarists. Listen to the bass under House Carpenter: it holds long drones like a medieval instrument while the guitars move. The ballad texts come from Child and from Appalachian sources collected by Cecil Sharp.","Reviews praised the musicianship and some folk critics found the jazz elements precious. Its reputation settled as the best balance of Pentangle's many sources, and guitarists still study it. The shadow of Fairport's Liege & Lief, released the same year, has kept it slightly less celebrated than it deserves. Its hit single remains its best-known track.","Start with Light Flight and count along, then House Carpenter for the ballad. Listen for the bass under the guitars. Then play Liege & Lief and compare the acoustic and electric answers to the same question. Notice how rarely anyone plays loudly; the energy comes from rhythm, not volume."],"pentangle-cruel-sister":["Five tracks, all traditional. A Maid That's Deep in Love, When I Was in My Prime, Lord Franklin and Cruel Sister are sung ballads with delicate interplay; the second side is filled by an extended Jack Orion, about eighteen minutes long, that moves through ballad, instrumental improvisation and back. For the first time on a Pentangle album John Renbourn plays electric guitar.","Released in 1970 after the success of Basket of Light, this was a deliberate return to purely traditional material, with the band reacting against being cast as a pop act. Renbourn's interest in early music, which later produced his own medieval-leaning ensemble, shows in the arrangements. It sold far less than its predecessor. The sessions reportedly took longer than any of the band's earlier records, and the tension between the members began to show.","Cruel Sister is The Twa Sisters (Child 10), and Jack Orion is Glasgerion (Child 67), the ballad of a harper whose servant tricks his way into a princess's bed. Jansch had already recorded Jack Orion solo in 1966; here the band stretches it into a suite. Listen for the dulcimer, a folk instrument with a fixed drone, under the ballads.","It was received coolly at the time as a retreat from the band's jazz mix. Later critics have reassessed it as their most concentrated work on tradition, and the long Jack Orion has admirers and detractors in equal measure. It is a favourite of those who come to Pentangle from the ballads rather than from jazz.","Hear the title song first, next to an unaccompanied Twa Sisters. Then give the long Jack Orion full attention and notice when the instruments take over from the singer. Compare Fairport's A Sailor's Life for the electric equivalent. Listen with the texts to hand; the ballads are long and the words matter."],"bert-jansch-bert-jansch":["A voice and one borrowed acoustic guitar. Jansch's own songs, Strolling Down the Highway, Running from Home, Do You Hear Me Now and the drug song Needle of Death, sit beside instrumentals including his version of Davy Graham's Angie. The guitar is hard, fast and percussive, mixing blues bends, folk ornaments and jazz chords, with the thumb pounding the bass strings. The voice is quiet, flat and Scottish, often half-hidden behind the playing.","Recorded in 1964 by Bill Leader on a portable tape machine in his London flat, with Jansch playing a borrowed guitar, and sold to the small Transatlantic label for a flat fee of around a hundred pounds. Jansch had arrived in London from Edinburgh after drifting through Europe and was playing the Soho folk clubs. The album appeared in 1965 and reportedly sold well for such a modest release.","Needle of Death was written after a friend in Edinburgh died from a heroin overdose, and was one of the first songs in British popular music about drugs, unhip and unglamorous. Neil Young later adapted its tune for Ambulance Blues. Listen for the thumb on Angie, playing a walking bass line while the fingers play the melody, the technique that half of British guitarists then set out to learn.","It was admired at once in the clubs and by a generation of guitarists: Jimmy Page, Johnny Marr, Paul Simon and Neil Young have all cited Jansch. Critics now place it among the landmark British debut albums. Its lo-fi sound, a man in a room, is part of its appeal.","Start with Angie and just watch the guitar. Then Needle of Death, for the song. Then compare Davy Graham's playing on Shirley Collins's Folk Roots, New Routes, and hear what Nick Drake did with the same school of fingerpicking. Notice how little the voice pushes."],"bert-jansch-jack-orion":["Jansch's third album and his first given almost entirely to traditional songs: the long title ballad, Blackwaterside, Nottamun Town, The Waggoner's Lad, Henry Martin, Pretty Polly, and MacColl's The First Time Ever I Saw Your Face. John Renbourn plays second guitar on several tracks, and the two guitars weave independent lines in a style that would become Pentangle's. Jansch plays banjo on a couple of songs.","Recorded in 1966 for Transatlantic, as the London folk scene around Les Cousins club was absorbing the ballad repertoire and the collectors' archives. Jansch and Renbourn were sharing a flat and had just made a duet album. The ballads came from Child, from Cecil Sharp's English and Appalachian collections, and from other singers in the clubs. Bill Leader again made the recordings, simply and closely miked.","Jimmy Page listened closely to Blackwaterside and adapted its guitar arrangement as the instrumental Black Mountain Side on Led Zeppelin's first album in 1969, without credit; the matter was never settled in court. Listen to the guitar in Blackwaterside, which runs a drone in a modal tuning under the melody, the same method Davy Graham had used behind Shirley Collins.","It was well reviewed in the folk press, and later critics have placed it among his best. Its influence came through its musicians: Page, Sandy Denny, and Pentangle all drew on it. It is sometimes judged less personal than his first album, but it is where Jansch showed what a guitarist could do with the old ballads.","Start with Blackwaterside, then play Led Zeppelin's Black Mountain Side. Then the title ballad, all ten minutes of it, and hear Pentangle stretch it further on Cruel Sister. Compare his First Time Ever with Peggy Seeger's. Notice how the guitar keeps its drone even when the melody moves far away."],"planxty-planxty":["The band's debut, known from its dark sleeve as 'the black album'. Songs such as Raggle Taggle Gypsy, The Blacksmith and MacColl's Sweet Thames Flow Softly alternate with dance tunes and the harper Carolan's Sí Bheag, Sí Mhór on the pipes. Liam O'Flynn's uilleann pipes carry the tunes; Dónal Lunny's bouzouki and Andy Irvine's mandolin weave counter-melodies around them instead of strumming chords, and Christy Moore and Irvine share the singing.","The four came together in 1972 to back Christy Moore's solo album Prosperous, recorded in a country house in Co. Kildare, and liked the sound enough to stay. Their live shows in Dublin caused a sensation, and Polydor released the debut in 1973, when Irish traditional music was split between the céilí band and the ballad group. Planxty offered a third way.","The Greek bouzouki had come to Irish music only recently, brought back by Johnny Moynihan and adapted by Lunny and Irvine with a flat back and different stringing; this is its founding record. The name Planxty is a term from Carolan's harp music for a tune written in tribute to a patron. Listen for the counterpoint between bouzouki and mandolin under the pipes.","It was an immediate success in Ireland and is now considered one of the most influential Irish albums ever made, the source of a string-band texture copied by countless groups. Some older traditional players disliked the arrangements; within a few years they had become the norm. Christy Moore and Dónal Lunny went on to shape Irish popular music for decades, in Moving Hearts and beyond.","Start with Raggle Taggle Gypsy running into the tune that follows it and listen to the bouzouki. Then Sí Bheag, Sí Mhór for the pipes alone. Compare the Chieftains' first album, a decade earlier, and The Bothy Band two years later."],"planxty-the-well-below-the-valley":["Planxty's second album, released only months after the first, with the same line-up and the same interlocking strings under the pipes. Its heart is the title track, a strange, unresolving ballad sung by Christy Moore, in which a stranger at a well questions a woman about her children until her hidden crimes come out. Dance sets, Andy Irvine's songs and pipe airs complete the record.","Recorded in 1973 while the band was touring constantly across Ireland and Britain, it consolidated their success. Moore learned the title ballad from John Reilly, a singer from the Irish Travelling community recorded by collectors in the 1960s, whose repertoire held some of the rarest ballads in Ireland. The album also shows the band widening its repertoire, with Andy Irvine bringing songs learned on his travels and from older Irish singers into the arrangements.","The title song is a version of Child 21, The Maid and the Palmer, a ballad about a woman confronted with her sins, sometimes linked to the story of the Samaritan woman at the well in John's Gospel. Child had found only fragments; Reilly's version was one of very few complete ones from oral tradition, and Planxty's recording made it known.","Received as a worthy follow-up, it has sometimes been rated slightly behind the debut, though the title track is now among the band's best-known recordings. Its importance for ballad scholarship is its use of a Traveller source, part of a new respect for Travellers as tradition-bearers. The band split two years later, so the album also marks the end of its first phase.","Hear the title song first and notice that it never quite resolves, musically or morally. Then go back to Lord Randal for the same question-and-answer method. Finish with a dance set to hear the band at full speed. Keep the words in front of you the first time."],"the-chieftains-the-chieftains-1":["Instrumental Irish dance music and slow airs, arranged for a small ensemble: jigs, reels, slides and a hornpipe, with Paddy Moloney's uilleann pipes at the centre, tin whistle, wooden flute, fiddle and bodhrán around them. Instead of everyone playing the tune together in unison, as in a céilí band, the arrangements pass it from one instrument to another, add harmonies and drop players out, so each set has a shape like a small chamber piece.","Claddagh Records was founded in Dublin by Garech Browne, the Guinness heir, to record Irish traditional music and poetry, and this was one of its first releases. The players came from Seán Ó Riada's Ceoltóirí Chualann, which had shown in the early 1960s that traditional tunes could be arranged for concert performance. The band stayed part-time amateurs for over a decade.","The approach of passing a tune around the ensemble came directly from Ó Riada, a composer trained in European classical music, who treated a traditional dance set as a small concerto grosso. Listen for the moments where the pipes drop out and the whistle and flute carry the tune alone, then return with a drone underneath.","It made little noise on release but set a template later groups have followed ever since. The Chieftains' later career of celebrity collaborations has sometimes been criticised for diluting the music; their early Claddagh records remain the ones traditional players respect most. Collectors and players alike prize it for its unhurried tempos and for tunes that were then little known outside the regions they came from.","Pick one reel set and follow the tune as it moves between instruments. Then hear a slow air on the pipes and listen for the ornaments. Compare with Stanford's Irish Symphony, which uses tunes of the same kind in a Victorian orchestra."],"the-chieftains-women-of-ireland":["A slow air, played without words. The melody rises in long, arching phrases and is passed between uilleann pipes, fiddle and flute over a soft, sustained accompaniment, building gradually to a restrained climax with a steady drum pulse before falling away. It has the gravity of a lament, though the poem it was written for is a love song. Harp and fiddle trade the lead at points, and the pipes return for the last phrase.","The words are an eighteenth-century Irish poem by Peadar Ó Doirnín; the tune was written in the 1960s by Seán Ó Riada, the composer whose ensemble spawned the Chieftains. The Chieftains' instrumental version was recorded for their fourth album. Stanley Kubrick used it in Barry Lyndon (1975), whose soundtrack also features Handel and Schubert, and the film made it widely known.","Many listeners assume the melody is centuries old; it is a modern composition by a classically trained composer, written deliberately in the style of a traditional air. Its placing in Barry Lyndon, beside Handel's Sarabande, makes the point: Kubrick heard it as belonging with baroque music. The poem itself lists famous women of Irish legend and history.","It has become one of the best-known Irish melodies and has been recorded by singers such as Kate Bush and Sinéad O'Connor with the original Irish words. Traditional players sometimes object that its fame has overshadowed older airs; others hold it up as proof that the tradition can still grow new tunes.","Listen for the drum entering and the gradual build. Then hear a sung version with the Irish words. Finally watch the relevant scenes of Barry Lyndon, and compare with Bax's Tintagel, another composer's Celtic seascape. Notice how long the melody takes to breathe out."],"the-bothy-band-the-bothy-band":["The debut of a six-piece that played Irish reels and jigs faster and harder than anyone before. Matt Molloy's flute, Paddy Keenan's uilleann pipes and Tommy Peoples's fiddle play the tunes in tight unison over Dónal Lunny's bouzouki, Mícheál Ó Domhnaill's guitar and Tríona Ní Dhomhnaill's clavinet or harpsichord. Songs in English and Irish, sung by the Ó Domhnaill siblings in Donegal style, break up the dance sets.","Lunny formed the band in 1974 after leaving Planxty, drawing on the young musicians gathering at Dublin sessions. The debut appeared in 1975, when Irish traditional music was newly popular with a young audience that also listened to rock. They toured widely in Britain and Europe; the high tempos and volume made their concerts feel like rock shows. Their studio sound tried to catch that live energy.","Ní Dhomhnaill's clavinet, an electric keyboard associated with funk, gives the rhythm section a percussive snap. Listen for the way the three melody instruments play not quite identical versions of each tune, each ornamenting in its own style: the tiny differences are what makes the unison sound so thick. The songs, by contrast, are slow and unhurried.","Traditional musicians argued about the speed, which some felt robbed the tunes of their lift. The band's example nonetheless won out; their line-up and approach became the model for later groups such as Altan and Lúnasa. The album is regarded as one of the essential Irish traditional records. Their live album remains the favourite of many fans.","Start with any reel set and try to follow just one instrument. Then compare the same kind of tune on The Chieftains 1 to hear how much faster and fuller it has become. Play it loud. Then listen once more to a song by Tríona Ní Dhomhnaill, where the band pulls right back."],"the-bothy-band-old-hag-you-have-killed-me":["The band's second album, with Kevin Burke now on fiddle. The title comes from a jig; the record mixes long dance sets with songs, including the Scottish Gaelic mouth-music of Fionnghuala, sung by the Ó Domhnaill siblings as a rhythmic, wordplay-driven dance song without instruments. The ensemble sound is the same dense unison of pipes, flute and fiddle over bouzouki and keyboards, somewhat better recorded and more varied than the debut.","Released in 1976, the year the band was at its busiest on tour in Ireland, Britain and Europe. Burke, a London-born fiddler of Sligo parents, brought the smoother Sligo style in place of Tommy Peoples's Donegal attack. The band broke up in 1979, and Matt Molloy went on to join the Chieftains. Their sound became a pattern for touring Irish bands.","Fionnghuala is mouth music, puirt à beul, the Gaelic practice of singing dance tunes with syllables so people can dance without instruments. Hearing it between the instrumental sets shows the root of the whole style: the tunes are meant for bodies, and the voices here are doing the job of a fiddle.","Critics received it as confirming the band's standing; it is often named with the debut as their best. Some traditional commentators continued to find the tempos excessive. Fionnghuala became a favourite of later Celtic groups. The band recorded only two more albums before breaking up, making this the centre of its short career and, for many listeners, the easiest place to begin.","Start with Fionnghuala, then any reel set. Compare the fiddle with the first album to hear the different regional styles. Then hear Grainger's or Holst's folk-dance arrangements for the orchestral take on the same impulse. Keep the volume up: the density is the point."],"jimmie-rodgers-blue-yodel-no-1":["Under three minutes of one man and one guitar. Rodgers sings four loose twelve-bar blues stanzas about a woman who has left him and the pistol he intends to buy, strumming a plain bass-and-brush accompaniment with short runs between the lines. At the end of each verse he snaps up into falsetto for a three-note yodel that answers the sung phrase like a second singer. Nothing else is on the record: no band, no harmony, no reverb to hide in.","Rodgers had worked as a brakeman on Mississippi railroads until tuberculosis ended that life. He recorded for Victor during the company's rush into Southern vernacular music, made practical by the electrical microphone, which could finally catch a soft voice and a guitar without a recording horn. Victor was selling discs to rural households that had just bought their first phonograph. The record sold in the hundreds of thousands and turned a sick former railroad man into the first full-time star of the music.","Listen closer: the yodel is not Alpine. Rodgers grew up around Black railroad and levee workers, and this falsetto break belongs to the same world as the field holler, a leap into head voice used as punctuation rather than display. Notice too that he does not keep strict twelve-bar lengths. He adds and drops beats as the words need, and the guitar simply follows him. Every later country singer inherited that licence.","Victor's ledgers called it a blue yodel because nobody yet had a word for what it was. The disc effectively created the commercial category first sold as hillbilly and later as country. Scholars still argue about how much Rodgers took from the Black blues singers he heard on the railroads, and whether that is best described as theft, as exchange, or as one Southern vernacular that segregated marketing afterwards cut in two.","Play it twice: once for the story, once listening only to where the yodel lands against the guitar's beat. Then put on a Delta blues from the same decade and hear how nearly identical the twelve-bar frame is. Look for a transfer made from a clean original pressing; aggressive noise reduction thins the voice and files the edge off the yodel."],"jimmie-rodgers-blue-yodel-no-9":["The ninth in Rodgers's numbered blues series, and the one with company. Louis Armstrong plays trumpet behind him and Lil Hardin Armstrong plays piano, the three of them running through a slow twelve-bar about a man on a street corner, a woman, and a policeman who takes an interest. Armstrong does not solo so much as converse, filling every gap Rodgers leaves at the end of a line, and answering the yodels with bent, smeared phrases of his own.","The session took place in Los Angeles in 1930, arranged on short notice while all three were passing through. By then Rodgers was the best-selling country artist alive and Armstrong was remaking American singing and trumpet playing at once; the record companies filed them under different catalogues and sold them to different shops. The Depression was already closing in on the record business, and within three years Rodgers would be dead of tuberculosis at thirty-five.","Listen closer: Rodgers is the amateur here and it shows in the timing. He comes in early on at least one verse, and Armstrong simply moves, stretching a note to cover the gap. The trumpet is mixed close enough that you can hear breath in it. Armstrong went uncredited on the label, which is why the record spent decades being described as a curiosity rather than what it is, a meeting between the two most influential singers of the era.","For years the disc was filed as a novelty, an odd afternoon. It reads differently now, as evidence that the walls between hillbilly and race records were a marketing decision rather than a musical one. Some writers hear the session as unequal, Armstrong carrying a limited singer; others hear two men from the same Southern soundworld recognising each other. Both readings survive because the record supports both.","Follow the trumpet first and ignore the words entirely: Armstrong is writing a second song in the spaces. Then play it again for the voice and notice how Rodgers's yodel and Armstrong's bent notes do the same expressive job. Pair it with a Hot Five side from a few years earlier to hear how little Armstrong had to change to fit."],"carter-family-single-girl-married-girl":["Sara Carter sings alone, unhurried, over Maybelle Carter's guitar, alternating two verses that set the same woman's two possible lives against each other: the single girl going where she pleases in a fine new dress, the married girl rocking a cradle and crying. The tune barely moves, four phrases that fall and settle, and the accompaniment does nothing to comment. The flatness is the point. Sara delivers the bleakest lines without a flicker of emphasis.","Recorded at Ralph Peer's field sessions in Bristol, on the Tennessee-Virginia line, in the summer of 1927, the same fortnight that produced Jimmie Rodgers's first sides. Peer had brought portable electrical equipment south to find material he could copyright and sell. The Carters were a farming family from Poor Valley, Virginia, who had driven over a mountain road to get there. By some accounts A. P. Carter was out of the room when this one was cut, which is why only Sara sings.","Listen closer: Maybelle is barely playing. There is no picking pattern to admire, just chords and a few bass notes, and the restraint throws every word forward. Note also that the song offers no resolution and takes no position; it simply lays the two lives side by side and stops. Commercial country would spend the next fifty years learning to be that unsentimental about marriage, and mostly failing.","It was the Carters' first real seller and it financed everything that followed. Later folklorists treated their repertoire as a rescue operation on a dying oral tradition; the Carters themselves were making records for money, and A. P. copyrighted material he had collected from neighbours, including Black musicians such as Lesley Riddle who travelled with him. That double character, preservation and appropriation at once, is still the central argument about them.","Listen for what is absent: no chorus, no harmony, no moral. Then hear the same voice in the fuller family trio recordings and notice how much Sara's plainness survives the harmony. A good transfer matters here more than usual, because the whole effect lives in the grain of an unadorned voice close to a microphone."],"carter-family-wildwood-flower":["The most copied guitar part in American music. Maybelle Carter plays the melody with her thumb on the low strings while brushing chords across the high ones with her fingers, so a single guitar sounds like two instruments, lead and rhythm. Sara sings a garland of flower names and a woman abandoned by a faithless man. Between verses the guitar takes the tune alone, which is why generations of beginners learned this before anything else.","Cut in the year after Bristol, when Victor was recording the Carters steadily and their discs were reaching farms across the South. The song was not theirs: it descends from an 1860 American parlour ballad, printed with piano accompaniment for middle-class drawing rooms, which had drifted into oral circulation and come back up to them unrecognisable. The Carters recorded it as their own, in the ordinary practice of the day, and their arrangement replaced the original everywhere.","Listen closer: the words make almost no sense, and that is the evidence. The parlour original's botanical vocabulary, pale amaranthus and aronatus and the rest, was passed mouth to mouth through people who had never seen the printed page, and each singer reshaped what they misheard into something that sounded plausible. Sara sings the garbled version without hesitation. The song's own history is audible inside its lyric.","Guitarists argue about whether Maybelle invented the thumb-lead style or adapted what she had heard, and Lesley Riddle, the Black guitarist who travelled with A. P. collecting songs, is usually part of that conversation now. Nobody disputes the consequence. The Carter scratch became the default way to accompany a country song, and the tune became a standard test piece, played at every level from a first lesson to the concert stage.","Ignore the singing for one pass and follow only the thumb, then follow only the brushed chords, and hear two players emerge from one pair of hands. Compare a bluegrass flatpicked version afterwards and notice what speed costs the tune. Any decent reissue of the Victor sides will do; the guitar sits forward in the original balance."],"carter-family-can-the-circle-be-unbroken":["A hymn chorus wrapped around a funeral. The verses describe a hearse arriving, a mother carried out, a son walking behind and asking the undertaker to drive slow; the chorus lifts into three-part harmony and asks whether the family circle will be unbroken in a better home above. Maybelle's guitar keeps its steady thumb-and-brush pattern throughout, unmoved by any of it, and the harmony enters exactly on cue each time, like something rehearsed at a graveside.","A. P. Carter took a 1907 gospel hymn, kept its chorus and its question, and wrote new verses that put a specific corpse in a specific hearse. By 1935 the Carters were recording prolifically and would soon be broadcasting from a border radio station in Texas whose transmitter reached most of North America at night. The Depression had emptied the record shops but not the airwaves, and this song travelled further on radio than on shellac.","Listen closer: the verses are in the past tense and the chorus is in the future, and the song never lets the two meet. The narrator has already watched his mother go; the reunion stays hypothetical, phrased as a question in every single repetition. Country gospel usually answers its own questions. This one declines to, which is why it works equally well as consolation and as an expression of doubt.","It became the American folk revival's communal closing number, sung at festivals and funerals by people with no interest in the Carters, and its title ended up naming the 1972 album that introduced the old-time repertoire to a rock audience. Purists sometimes object that the mass-singing version smooths away the harshness of the verses. The complaint is fair and has changed nothing.","Listen to the verses alone first, with the chorus treated as an interruption, and the song turns bleak. Then listen for the moment the three voices lock; the shift from one voice to three is the whole emotional mechanism. Follow it with a Sacred Harp recording to hear the harmony tradition these singers were raised inside."],"hank-williams-lovesick-blues":["Williams enters alone on the word I, holds it, and breaks it in half with a yodel before the band has played a note. What follows is a fast, grinning complaint about a woman who has gone, driven by a shuffling bass and a steel guitar that answers each line. The vocal keeps cracking upward on purpose, sometimes twice in a phrase, so the song sounds simultaneously wretched and delighted with itself. It is over in well under three minutes.","Williams did not write it. It began as a 1922 Tin Pan Alley show song and had already passed through vaudeville and the recordings of the blackface yodeller Emmett Miller and the singer Rex Griffin, from whom Williams took his arrangement. His publisher Fred Rose thought the song was a mistake and old hat. It became the biggest hit of Williams's life and won him his Grand Ole Opry debut, where the audience reportedly refused to let him leave the stage.","Listen closer: the yodel break is a controlled crack between chest and head voice, and Williams places it on the stressed syllable of the most self-pitying words, turning misery into a joke at his own expense. Note also how little the band does. There is no solo of any length; everything is deference to the voice. The arrangement is essentially Griffin's, which makes Williams's originality a matter of delivery alone.","Rose's judgement was wrong and country's commercial history turned on it. Critics since have worried at the fact that the record that made the music's defining writer was a borrowed vaudeville number, filtered through the minstrel stage; that lineage is now discussed openly rather than tidied away. Nobody argues about the performance. It is still the standard demonstration of what a voice can do to a song it did not write.","Count the yodel breaks on a first hearing, then stop counting and just feel where they fall against the beat, always slightly ahead. Compare Rex Griffin's earlier version to see exactly what Williams added. Any well-mastered reissue of the MGM singles works; avoid the later overdubbed editions that bury the band under strings."],"hank-williams-i-saw-the-light":["Two minutes of pure uplift, taken at a brisk walking pace. Four short verses trace a sinner wandering in the night until the light finds him, each ending in a chorus so plainly built that a room full of strangers can join it on first hearing. The band plays it like a dance tune, with fiddle and steel trading brief fills, and the singer sounds untroubled throughout. No minor chord appears anywhere in the song.","Williams wrote it early, before the hits, and it became the number he closed shows with for the rest of his short life. Gospel was not a side venture in this music but its other half: the same bands played honky-tonks on Saturday and revival songs on Sunday radio, and audiences expected both from the same man. Williams took the melody from a Carter Family gospel side, which was ordinary practice, and put his own words on it.","Listen closer: the lyric is entirely in the past tense. Every claim of salvation is a report of something that happened earlier, and the song never says the light is still there. Williams, who spent his adult life in alcoholism and chronic pain and died at twenty-nine, reportedly told his mother more than once that there was no light for him. Knowing that changes the brightness of the record without altering a note of it.","It is sung in churches by people who would not otherwise own a Hank Williams record, and it has become the standard closing hymn at country funerals, including those of musicians whose lives it did not describe. Critics divide over whether to hear it straight or ironically. The most persuasive readings refuse to choose, treating the gap between the singer's life and the song's certainty as the thing the record is actually about.","Hear it immediately after one of his desolate songs; the pairing is the point, and Williams himself put the two sides of his work on consecutive nights of the same week. Listen for the fiddle, which never once darkens. Bluegrass and gospel quartet versions are everywhere, but start with the original, where the tempo is faster than most people remember."],"hank-williams-im-so-lonesome-i-could-cry":["Three verses, no chorus, almost no motion. Williams sings a series of images from a country night, a whippoorwill too blue to fly, a midnight train whining, a falling star, a silent moon behind clouds, and after each one states the plain fact of his loneliness. Don Helms's steel guitar sits very high above the voice and answers every line with two or three notes. The band plays as quietly as a band can while still being audible.","Recorded in Nashville in 1949, at the moment Williams turned from a regional draw into a national one. It was issued as the other side of a cheerful novelty number, which tells you what his label expected to sell. Country songs of the period were usually built on situations, a cheating wife, a lost job; this one has no situation at all, only weather and an interior state, which was not what the jukebox trade was ordering.","Listen closer: Williams never says why he is lonesome. There is no woman in the lyric, no departure, no cause of any kind. The images are borrowed from folk commonplaces and arranged so that each natural fact is also a person, the whippoorwill too blue to fly, the leaves that die. Helms tuned his steel unusually high for these sessions, and that thin, tight upper register is the sound most people mean when they say the word lonesome.","It is the Williams record that non-country listeners tend to know, and the one poets quote. It has been recorded by jazz singers, soul singers and rock bands without the slightest resistance from the material, which suggests how little of it is generic. Arguments today are mostly about the later MGM reissues that added orchestral overdubs to Williams's voice after his death, a practice now generally regarded as vandalism.","Take one image at a time and notice that the music does nothing to illustrate any of them. Then listen only to the steel guitar, which is effectively a second singer and often the more expressive of the two. Seek the undubbed original mix. Afterwards, try a song cycle about a solitary winter walk and hear how close the two traditions come."],"hank-williams-your-cheatin-heart":["A slow shuffle in which the singer promises an unfaithful lover a future of sleepless nights and useless tears. The melody climbs on the accusation and falls on the verdict, and Williams bends the last note of nearly every line downward, a small vocal catch that became one of the most imitated mannerisms in American singing. Steel guitar and fiddle alternate short answering phrases. The whole thing is structurally simple enough to teach in five minutes.","Recorded in Nashville in the autumn of 1952 at what proved to be one of his final sessions. By then Williams had been dismissed from the Grand Ole Opry for unreliability, was in constant pain from a spinal condition, and was drinking and taking morphine. He died in the back of a car on New Year's Day 1953, aged twenty-nine, and the single was released weeks afterwards into a country music that had just lost its centre.","Listen closer: the song is addressed entirely to someone who is not present, and the singer takes no action in it. He does not confront, leave or forgive; he simply predicts. Every verb about the other person is in the future tense, which turns the record from a complaint into something closer to a curse. Williams reportedly dictated the words to his second wife on a car journey, talking about his first.","Its posthumous release fixed it as the last word of a man who had just died, which no amount of later analysis can quite undo. It gave its title to a film, entered the standard repertoire of singers with no country connection at all, and is routinely named the single most important country song. The main scholarly argument now concerns the overdubbed reissues, and how much of Williams's recorded legacy reaches listeners in a form he would recognise.","Listen for the downward catch at the end of the lines; once you hear it you will hear it in every country singer since. Then compare a smooth pop version from the 1950s and notice how much the polish removes. The mono single mix, with the band dry and close, is the one to find."],"patsy-cline-i-fall-to-pieces":["A mid-tempo song about meeting an ex-lover in public and pretending to be fine. Cline sings almost conversationally in the verses and then opens her chest voice on the title phrase, so the collapse the words describe happens audibly in the tone. Behind her a male vocal quartet hums sustained chords where a steel guitar would once have been, a soft electric guitar plays a rocking two-note figure, and brushed drums keep everything horizontal.","Owen Bradley produced it in his Nashville studio as part of a deliberate attempt to keep country selling after rock and roll took its teenage audience. Fiddles and steel were dropped, backing choirs and light strings brought in, and the resulting style was marketed as the Nashville Sound. Cline reportedly disliked the song at first. It was climbing the charts in the summer of 1961 while she was in hospital after a severe car crash, and it made her a national name.","Listen closer: there is no drama in the arrangement at all, no crescendo, no key change, nothing to signal an emotional event. Everything is carried by the difference between how Cline sings the verse lines and how she sings the four words of the title. Notice also the vocal quartet: they never sing words, only vowels, which is what lets them function as an orchestra rather than as a gospel group.","Traditionalists heard the Nashville Sound as a surrender, country music trading its fiddles for a string section to chase pop money, and that argument has never entirely stopped. The counter-case is this record: the smoother the setting, the more exposed the voice. Cline's reputation grew steadily after her death in a plane crash in 1963, and she is now credited with proving that a woman could carry a country career without a male star attached.","Listen once for the two vocal registers and where exactly she crosses between them. Then play a honky-tonk record from ten years earlier and hear what was traded away and what was gained. Her studio recordings are best in mono; the later stereo remixes push the backing voices forward and make the arrangement sound more crowded than Bradley intended."],"patsy-cline-crazy":["A torch ballad taken slowly, with a melody that keeps climbing where you expect it to fall. Floyd Cramer's piano opens with a few unhurried chords; the vocal quartet breathes underneath; Cline enters low and stays conversational until the bridge, where she pushes into a full, wide tone and then pulls straight back. There is no instrumental solo. The record's whole architecture is one voice moving between restraint and release across two and a half minutes.","The song was written by Willie Nelson, then an unknown Nashville songwriter scraping by on demo fees, in a style closer to a jazz standard than to country. Cline initially disliked its wandering phrasing. She recorded it in Bradley's studio in the late summer of 1961, weeks after a car crash that had left her with broken ribs, which restricted how long she could hold a note; the vocal was completed once she had healed enough to sustain the lines.","Listen closer: the melody almost never lands where a country tune would. Nelson wrote long phrases that cross the bar lines and resolve late, which is why the song sits so comfortably in the repertoire of jazz singers. Notice too that Cline sings the title word differently every time it appears, four readings of one syllable. The Jordanaires' hum in the second verse quietly outlines a chord the piano has left out.","It became, by most reckonings, the most played song on American jukeboxes, and it is the record usually offered as the definitive answer to anyone who calls the Nashville Sound a dilution. Cline died eighteen months later at thirty. Debate now runs less about the arrangement than about the industry it typified, and about how many Nashville women of the 1960s were given the same production budget and the same freedom.","Start with the bridge, the one passage where she opens fully, then go back to the top and hear how carefully she has been holding back. Follow it with Willie Nelson's own version, which is almost spoken, to hear two completely different songs inside one set of words. The mono single is the intended balance."],"johnny-cash-i-walk-the-line":["Three instruments and a voice, locked into a dry chugging pulse that sounds like a freight train at moderate speed. Luther Perkins picks a bass-string figure with no vibrato and almost no decay; Marshall Grant slaps an upright bass; Cash strums and sings a vow of fidelity in a register so low it seems to be resisting the melody. Between each verse he hums a single pitch, unaccompanied, for a couple of seconds, and the whole song shifts into a new key.","Recorded at Sun Studio in Memphis for Sam Phillips, in the same building and the same year that Elvis Presley left for a major label. Cash was a former air force radio operator selling appliances door to door; he had written the song for his first wife while stationed overseas. Phillips wanted it faster than Cash intended, and the released version is the compromise. Sun sold it to country and pop markets at once, which almost nothing did in 1956.","Listen closer: the buzzing edge on the rhythm guitar is a piece of waxed paper woven through the strings, a trick Cash used to get a snare-like click from a band with no drummer. The humming between verses is not a stylistic flourish but a practical necessity; the song modulates five times, and he needed the pitch. That makes a record celebrated for its plainness one of the more harmonically restless singles of its decade.","It was Sun's biggest country seller and it fixed the boom-chicka-boom rhythm as Cash's signature for forty years. Critics have argued ever since about whether the song is sincere or anxious: a vow repeated that insistently, by a man who would soon wreck the marriage it was written for, can be heard either way. Cash encouraged both readings and never settled the question in interviews.","Try to hear the key changes on a first pass; most listeners never notice them, which is the trick. Then listen to the guitar alone for its complete lack of sustain, the sound of a cheap amplifier used deliberately. Play it against a train-rhythm piece from another tradition and hear how universal the pulse is. The Sun mono master is the one."],"johnny-cash-ring-of-fire":["The song opens not with a guitar but with two trumpets playing a bright mariachi fanfare in thirds, and they return between every verse. Underneath, the familiar chugging rhythm continues, so the record is Cash's Memphis pulse with a Mexican border band bolted on top. He sings about desire as a descending ring of fire, low and steady, and the trumpets answer each time with something almost festive, entirely at odds with the words.","June Carter, of the Carter Family and later Cash's wife, wrote it with Merle Kilgore about her feelings for him while both were married to other people. Anita Carter recorded it first, quietly. Cash said the trumpet idea came to him in a dream, and he cut his version for Columbia in Nashville in 1963, when he was deep in amphetamine use and his career was unsteady. It restored him commercially at once.","Listen closer: nothing in the arrangement is sad, and the lyric is about a destructive compulsion. That mismatch is the whole design. Notice also that the trumpets never play behind the voice, only between the lines, so the record alternates between two moods rather than blending them. The falling shape of the trumpet figure mirrors the falling shape of the phrase about going down.","Mariachi horns on a country single were startling in 1963, and the record widened what a Nashville arrangement was allowed to contain. Authorship has been the recurring argument: Cash's first wife claimed late in life that he had written it himself and credited June Carter, a claim the documented publishing does not support and most historians reject. The song's afterlife in adverts and films has worn its strangeness down.","Listen to the trumpets in isolation and notice they are playing a party. Then read the words as if the trumpets were not there. The gap between the two is the record. Follow it with a Copland score that uses brass the same way, as a bright frontier colour, and hear the shared American mythology."],"johnny-cash-at-folsom-prison":["A live album recorded in front of prisoners, opening with Cash announcing his own name and going straight into a song about shooting a man to watch him die. The set runs through murder ballads, jokes, a duet with June Carter and a long stretch of songs about confinement, with the band playing at one dynamic throughout and the audience audible all the way. It closes with a song written by an inmate who was sitting in the room while Cash sang it.","Cash had wanted to record inside a prison for a decade and Columbia had refused; a new producer, Bob Johnston, agreed. Two shows were played at the California state prison at Folsom in January 1968, and the album assembles the stronger one. Cash had just come through the worst of his amphetamine years. He had never served a prison sentence himself, only nights in county cells, a distinction the record quietly declines to make clear.","Listen closer: the whoop that follows the line about shooting a man in Reno is not what it appears. Prison officials had restricted how the men could react, and the crowd noise on the album was shaped in the mix, with applause moved and added. Listen also for the prison announcements cutting across the music; those were left in deliberately, to keep reminding you where you are standing.","It sold enormously, revived a stalled career and made Cash a figure for the counterculture as well as for country radio. It also set the terms of a long argument about authenticity, since the outlaw persona it cemented was largely constructed. Later scholarship has drawn attention to Glen Sherley, the inmate whose song closes the record, who was released partly through Cash's advocacy and whose life afterwards went badly.","Listen to the first four minutes without skipping: the announcement, the silence, the first chord and the first reaction together do more than any single song. Then attend to the crowd rather than the band. Seek the original single-disc album rather than the expanded edition, which restores both shows and dilutes the pacing that made the record work."],"johnny-cash-american-recordings":["One voice and one acoustic guitar for forty minutes, with nothing added. Cash, then in his sixties, sings thirteen songs: his own murder ballad about Delia, a nineteenth-century hymn, and pieces borrowed from a Canadian poet-songwriter, a Los Angeles barroom writer and a heavy metal frontman. The guitar playing is basic and occasionally uneven. The recording is so close that you hear the chair, the breath and the dry click of a plectrum against wound strings.","Cash had been dropped by his label and was playing dinner theatres when Rick Rubin, a producer known for hip-hop and metal, offered him a contract and a single condition: no band, no Nashville, just songs. Most of the album was cut in Rubin's living room and in Cash's Tennessee cabin, with two tracks taken from a small Los Angeles club. The idea was to record him the way a folklorist would record an old singer in a kitchen.","Listen closer: the song called Thirteen was written for him, at his request, by a metal singer whose band Cash had never heard, and it is built entirely from the imagery Cash had been singing since the 1950s. Note also what the silence does. With no drums to mark time, Cash's phrases stretch and shorten as he pleases, and several tracks are audibly slower at the end than the beginning.","The record won a Grammy in a folk category, sold to an audience that had never bought country, and began a sequence of albums that ran to his death in 2003. Some longtime admirers found the framing calculated, an old man styled as a prophet by a young producer with an ear for gravity. Others point out that the repertoire choices were Cash's and that the voice, worn and unhidden, does the persuading.","Play it at low volume in a quiet room; the recording was made for that scale and collapses through a car stereo. Start with the opening track and its slow-building dread. Then go back to a 1950s Sun single and hear the same man with forty years and no band between him and you. The debt to a solitary winter song cycle is not accidental."],"loretta-lynn-coal-miners-daughter":["A banjo figure opens it and keeps rolling underneath for three minutes while Lynn recites, in strict chronological order, the facts of her childhood in a Kentucky hollow: the shack, the father on the night shift, the mother washing on a scrubboard and reading the Bible by coal-oil light, eight children, no money, no self-pity. The melody is almost a chant, four repeated phrases. Nothing in the arrangement dramatises anything; the details do the work alone.","Lynn had married at fifteen and had four children before she was twenty, and she wrote her own material at a time when very few women in Nashville did. Owen Bradley produced it. The song appeared as country radio was reaching an audience of migrants from exactly these places, people who had left the coalfields for factory towns in Ohio and Michigan, and who heard their own family history read back to them in the third person plural.","Listen closer: there is no chorus. The song never returns to a hook, which is why it sounds like testimony rather than a single, and Lynn reportedly wrote many more verses than were recorded, with the rest cut purely for length. Notice that the poverty is described without a single abstract word. Everything is an object: the scrubboard, the coal oil, the hand-me-down shoes. The one general statement comes in the last line.","It gave its name to her memoir and to the 1980 film made from it, and it turned a singer into a representative figure for Appalachian working people, a role she accepted and occasionally resented. Critics debate whether its plainness is artless or extremely controlled; the manuscript evidence of heavy cutting supports the second reading. It is now routinely cited as the model for autobiographical songwriting in country music.","Count the concrete nouns on one hearing. Then listen for the banjo, which is doing the job a string section would do in a Nashville Sound production, and note how different the social claim sounds as a result. Pair it with an orchestral evocation of a Southern childhood and hear two classes remembering the same region."],"loretta-lynn-the-pill":["A fast, cheerful honky-tonk shuffle in which a farm wife informs her husband that the years of pregnancy are over and she is going out. The imagery is agricultural and unsparing: she has been the hen in the incubator while he was the rooster running loose, and now the maternity dresses are going in the bin. Lynn sings it grinning, at the top of her range, and the band plays it like a dance number. It takes under three minutes.","The oral contraceptive had been available in the United States for well over a decade, but access for married rural women had come slowly and the subject was unmentionable on country radio. Lynn recorded the song some years before her label would release it, and when it appeared in 1975 many stations refused to play it. It reached a large audience anyway, and rural doctors reportedly said it did more practical good than their leaflets.","Listen closer: the song never once uses a clinical word or makes a political claim. It is entirely domestic, addressed to one husband in one kitchen, and its argument is about fairness within a marriage rather than rights in the abstract. That is why it got past listeners who would have rejected a feminist statement, and it is also why the humour is so pointed. Note the bright major key throughout; nothing in the music admits to controversy.","The bans made it news and the news made it sell, a pattern Lynn had already seen with earlier songs about drinking husbands and other women. Critics then and now split over whether it is a feminist landmark or a comedy record that happened to touch a nerve; Lynn herself framed it as simple description of what women she knew were doing. It remains among the most banned singles in the format's history.","Listen for how much of the meaning sits in the delivery rather than the words, particularly the laugh she keeps almost suppressing. Then hear one of her earlier songs about a rival woman to see how consistently she wrote from inside a marriage rather than about one. The original single mix keeps the band bright and the voice forward."],"george-jones-the-grand-tour":["A man invites you into his house and shows you around. Room by room he points out the furniture his wife chose, the bed they shared, and finally a nursery with a child's things still in it; she has gone and taken the child. Jones sings in the polite, level voice of a guide, and only breaks it on the last word of each verse. Strings, steel guitar and a soft rhythm section keep the tour moving without ever raising their voices.","Billy Sherrill produced it for Epic in Nashville, working in the plush, string-heavy style the trade called countrypolitan, in which a session orchestra did the work that fiddles had once done. Jones's own marriage to Tammy Wynette was disintegrating in public at the time, and audiences heard the song against that; the writers had drafted it independently. Jones was drinking heavily and had begun missing sessions and concerts, a pattern that would define the next decade.","Listen closer: the conceit is a real estate viewing, and it never slips. Every emotional detail is presented as a feature of the property, which is why the record can describe a ruined family without a single direct statement of feeling. Notice where the strings enter, always on the move between rooms, never under the revelation itself. The nursery arrives in the final verse with no musical preparation at all.","It is the connoisseur's George Jones record rather than the famous one, and singers cite it as the hardest to bring off, because the restraint has to hold for three minutes before it pays. Countrypolitan production has been out of fashion and back again; Sherrill's strings, once dismissed as syrup, are now read as the deliberate cushion that lets a voice like this one whisper instead of cry.","Follow the floor plan. Hear it as a walk through a house and notice how the camera, so to speak, keeps moving. Then listen only to the last word of each verse, where the guide's composure fails for a syllable. Follow it with a Romantic song cycle about a man revisiting the places a lover has left; the method is the same."],"george-jones-he-stopped-loving-her-today":["A friend describes a man who kept a woman's letters and photograph for decades after she left, swearing he would love her until he died. The narrator gives the joke a turn in the title phrase, and then the bridge is spoken rather than sung: the man is dead, and she has come to see him one last time. A string section swells at the final chorus and Jones takes the song up a key, his voice thinning at the top.","Billy Sherrill spent a long time getting this record finished. Jones's addiction to alcohol and cocaine had reached the point where sessions collapsed and concerts were missed so often that promoters advertised him as No Show Jones; the vocal was assembled over many visits across more than a year. Jones reportedly disliked the melody and told Sherrill nobody would buy a song that morbid. It restored his career and is the record he sang every night afterwards.","Listen closer: the title works as a double meaning that the song withholds until the bridge, and the spoken passage exists because a sung line would have given it away too early. Notice the key change at the end, unusual in a ballad this restrained; it arrives exactly where the funeral does. The strings play a falling figure between verses that never resolves until the last bar.","It is routinely voted the greatest country song ever recorded, which has made it hard to hear freshly. Its defenders point to the construction, a punchline withheld for two and a half minutes; its sceptics call it a gimmick dressed in a string arrangement. The story of how it was extracted from a man in freefall has become part of the record's meaning, and some writers argue that biography is doing work the song cannot do alone.","Listen once knowing nothing and let the bridge land. Then listen again for the strings, and for how carefully Sherrill keeps them off the vocal until the last chorus. Afterwards try a song cycle that ends at a graveside; country and lieder solve the same problem, how to make a small voice carry a death, in almost the same way."],"tammy-wynette-stand-by-your-man":["Two short verses and a chorus that arrives twice, the second time a whole step higher. Wynette begins almost gently, advising that it is hard to be a woman giving all your love to one man, then pushes into a wide, cracking upper register on the title line, with a catch on the first word that sounds like a sob and is entirely deliberate. Strings and steel guitar rise underneath. The record is under three minutes and spends most of them climbing.","Wynette wrote it with her producer Billy Sherrill in his Nashville office, reportedly very quickly, near the end of a session that needed one more song. She had worked as a hairdresser in Alabama and had three children before her recording career began. The single appeared in 1968, into an America already arguing loudly about what women owed their marriages, and it became the best-selling record ever made by a woman in country music.","Listen closer: the verse is a warning and only the chorus is advice. Wynette spends the first half of the song saying that this arrangement is hard, that he will do things you cannot understand, and only then delivers the title. The modulation into the final chorus is what most people remember as emotion; it is in fact a mechanical device, and it works because her voice has to strain to reach the new key.","It was attacked by feminists on release and defended by Wynette as a song about love rather than submission. Hillary Clinton's dismissive reference to it in a 1992 television interview restarted the argument on a national scale and prompted an apology. Recent criticism has tended to read the record against Wynette's own life of five marriages and financial exploitation, hearing the crack in the voice as evidence against the words.","Listen to the verse and the chorus as two different songs, because they say different things. Then track the modulation and notice how audibly she is working at the top. Pair it with an opera in which a woman's self-sacrifice is presented as the noble choice, and the question the record raises turns out to be a very old one."],"tammy-wynette-d-i-v-o-r-c-e":["A mother and father are spelling out words so their four-year-old will not understand them. The song's hook is the spelling itself, letter by letter, and Wynette sings those letters in the same warm, careful tone she uses for everything else, which is what makes it unbearable rather than cute. The band plays a moderate shuffle with steel guitar fills. At the end the child's own future is spelled out as well, and the song stops without comment.","Written by the same pair who would later write He Stopped Loving Her Today, and produced by Billy Sherrill in the year of Stand by Your Man, so Wynette had two of the decade's defining country singles inside twelve months. She was building the public role that earned her the title First Lady of Country Music, singing about marriages under strain while her own were collapsing, and country radio in 1968 was still uneasy about the word divorce in a title.","Listen closer: the spelled words are not all sad ones. The device is introduced with ordinary domestic secrets before it is used for the real thing, so the listener learns the code in the first verse and is then made to decipher the second. Notice that the child never speaks and is never described, only referred to. The song's whole emotional weight rests on a person who is not in it.","Novelty spelling songs were a familiar country device and the writers used the convention against itself, which is why the record has outlasted the genre it came from. It has been parodied often enough that some listeners meet the joke before the song. Critics who dislike it hear a manipulative trick; those who defend it point out that the trick is doing something no direct statement could, which is to make the listener perform the concealment.","Notice that you decode the letters faster than the child would, and that the discomfort is exactly that gap. Then listen for how little Wynette changes her tone across the song. Pair it with a ballad in which a child is the unwitting centre of a catastrophe narrated by adults; the technique of withholding is identical."],"dolly-parton-coat-of-many-colors":["Parton tells a story from her childhood: her mother sewed her a coat out of a box of rags, stitching it while telling the Bible story of Joseph, and the children at school laughed at her for wearing rags. The arrangement is light, an acoustic guitar and a discreet steel, and Parton sings high and clear with almost no ornament. There is no chorus; the song moves straight through the incident from the sewing to the mockery to the last line.","Parton was recording for RCA under the wing of Porter Wagoner, whose television show had taken her from a Tennessee mountain family to a national audience. She reportedly wrote this on the back of a dry-cleaning receipt from one of Wagoner's flamboyant stage suits, a detail she has told often and which the receipt itself, now in a museum, supports. Country radio in 1971 rewarded songs about rural poverty, and she had lived the material.","Listen closer: the song makes a theological argument and never announces it. The mother's Bible story gives the coat its meaning, the schoolchildren refuse that meaning, and the child accepts her mother's reading over theirs, which is the only conflict in the piece. Notice also that Parton does not describe the coat's colours; she describes the box of rags and the sewing. The object is never actually shown to us.","Parton has repeatedly named it her favourite of her own songs, and it has become the anchor of a public persona built on the distance between where she started and where she arrived. Critics sometimes note how effectively that persona works as a commercial instrument, and how carefully the poverty is presented without bitterness. The song's afterlife in books and television films has made it more sentimental than the recording is.","Listen for what the arrangement declines to do: no strings, no swell at the humiliation, no key change at the end. Then hear her sing it forty years later and notice how little the reading has changed. Pair it with an orchestral setting of a remembered Southern childhood to hear the same landscape from a different class."],"dolly-parton-jolene":["An acoustic guitar figure circles a minor chord four times before the voice enters, and it keeps circling, unchanged, for the whole song. Parton addresses a rival by name and begs her not to take a man she could take without trying, cataloguing the woman's beauty in detail that sounds close to infatuation. The melody is built from a descending three-note phrase repeated obsessively. Parton doubles her own voice in places, so the plea seems to come from two people at once.","Recorded for RCA in Nashville while she was still appearing on Porter Wagoner's television show and preparing to leave it. She has said the name came from a young fan at an autograph table and the situation from a red-haired bank teller who flirted with her husband. The early 1970s were the point at which Nashville's women stopped being novelty acts on male-led shows, and this single, along with what came next, funded her independence.","Listen closer: the song has no bridge, no key change and effectively no chord progression, just the same short loop under everything. All the variation is in the voice. Notice that the narrator never speaks to her husband and never blames him; the entire drama is conducted between two women, one of whom is silent. The guitar's circling figure is also the reason the song survives transplanting into rock, metal and electronic settings without losing its shape.","It has become one of the most covered songs written in the last fifty years, taken up by artists with no connection to country at all, and it survives being slowed to a dirge or sped into a punk song. Critics have written a great deal about the desire audible in the narrator's description of her rival. Parton has always declined the reading, which has not slowed it down.","Listen to the guitar loop alone and notice it never develops; then listen to how many different emotions Parton puts over the identical four bars. Try a radically slowed cover afterwards to hear what the tempo was hiding. The original RCA single mix, with the voice close and the band dry, is still the best."],"dolly-parton-i-will-always-love-you":["A farewell sung at walking pace, with a spoken passage in the middle where Parton simply talks to the person she is leaving and wishes them well. The melody rises through the verse and settles on the title phrase without any display; she never belts, and the highest notes are the quietest. An acoustic guitar carries it, with light strings entering late. Parton's vibrato, fast and narrow, does most of the expressive work.","She wrote it as a goodbye to Porter Wagoner, whose television show and business partnership had made her career and had become a cage; she has said she played it to him in his office and that he wept and then agreed to let her go. Elvis Presley wanted to record it, but his manager required half the publishing, and she refused. That refusal became one of the most consequential business decisions in American popular music.","Listen closer: the song is a resignation letter, not a love song, and the words work as precisely for a professional parting as for a romantic one, which is why it reads differently once you know who it was written to. Notice the spoken bridge. Parton drops out of singing entirely at the emotional centre, so the most direct statement in the song is not sung at all.","Parton took it to the top of the country chart twice, in 1974 and again in 1982. Whitney Houston's 1992 recording, sung as a soul ballad with an unaccompanied opening and an enormous final chorus, became one of the best-selling singles ever made and for most listeners replaced the original. Critics comparing the two usually frame it as restraint against release; Parton has praised the cover while noting that she wrote it quiet.","Hear Parton's version first if you only know Houston's; the shock is how small it is. Listen for the spoken section and for the fact that nothing swells behind it. Then compare the two recordings back to back as two answers to the same question, whether a farewell should be whispered or sung at full voice."],"merle-haggard-mama-tried":["Two and a half minutes, fast, with a bright electric guitar figure that snaps rather than sustains. Haggard sings about a boy who would not be told, a widowed mother who did everything right, and a life sentence he is now serving. Roy Nichols plays a clipped Telecaster lead with a hard treble edge, and the rhythm section pushes slightly ahead of the beat throughout. The famous line about turning twenty-one in prison arrives in the first verse and never lets go.","Haggard had in fact served time in San Quentin as a young man for an attempted burglary, and had been in the audience when Johnny Cash played there. His records were made in Los Angeles rather than Nashville, with a band of West Coast players whose hard, trebly electric sound had been shaped in the dance halls of Bakersfield, a town full of families who had come west from Oklahoma in the Depression. That sound existed partly as a refusal of Nashville's strings.","Listen closer: the song is not strictly true and does not pretend to be. Haggard was never sentenced to life, and the exaggeration turns memoir into ballad, which is the form's old privilege. Notice that the mother is the only person described with any warmth, and that the narrator accepts full blame in every verse. Listen for the banjo buried in the introduction, a nod to the Appalachian music his Oklahoma parents brought west.","It gave its name to an album and became the centre of Haggard's prison songs, a body of work whose credibility rested on his having actually been inside. Later writers have examined how carefully that biography was managed, and the pardon he received in the 1970s. The Bakersfield sound is now heard as the first significant argument against Nashville's centralised production, and as the ancestor of every subsequent country rebellion.","Listen to the guitar tone rather than the words on a first pass; the bright, undecorated Telecaster is the whole regional argument in one sound. Then compare a countrypolitan ballad of the same year. Follow it with an orchestral outlaw ballad from a generation earlier and hear how old the American appetite for a sympathetic criminal is."],"merle-haggard-okie-from-muskogee":["A brisk, almost jaunty shuffle in which a man from a small Oklahoma town lists what they do not do there: smoke marijuana, burn draft cards, wear beads or let their hair grow long. Fiddle and steel guitar trade cheerful fills, the band swings, and Haggard sings it deadpan, with no audible irony and no audible anger either. It runs under three minutes and the chorus is designed to be shouted back by a crowd.","Written on a tour bus with his drummer while passing a road sign for Muskogee, in the autumn of 1969, with American cities and campuses in open conflict over the Vietnam War. Haggard's audience included working people whose sons were being drafted while students protested. The record sold enormously, was played at political rallies, and turned a Bakersfield bandleader into a national symbol in a fight he had not obviously enlisted in.","Listen closer: the song is written in the first person plural, and Haggard was not from Muskogee and did not live that life. He described it at different times as a joke on his own band, as a character study of his father's generation, and as something he meant. The deadpan delivery is what makes all three readings possible, and the band's cheerfulness refuses to settle the question either way.","It was received as a conservative anthem and has never quite escaped that. Haggard spent decades complicating it, later writing anti-war material and speaking with sympathy about the people the song mocked, which convinced some listeners and not others. Critics now tend to read it as the moment country music's political identity hardened in public, whatever its author intended, and as a case study in how a record's meaning stops belonging to the person who made it.","Listen for the absence of scorn in the voice, which is the strangest thing about the record and the reason the argument survives. Then hear his later songs about hard times for working people and decide how consistent a writer he was. Pair it with an American orchestral piece built on marching bands and hymn tunes to hear the same patriotism handled with more irony."],"willie-nelson-red-headed-stranger":["A narrative album about a preacher who kills his wife and her lover and rides west, told across short songs, fragments and instrumental passages that often last under two minutes. The sound is startlingly bare: Nelson's nylon-string guitar, his sister Bobbie's upright piano, a quiet bass, brushed drums, and long stretches of near-silence between phrases. Themes recur between tracks so the record plays as one continuous thing rather than a collection of songs.","Nelson had spent the 1960s in Nashville writing hits for other singers and failing as a performer, then moved back to Texas and grew his hair. A new contract with Columbia gave him complete creative control, which no Nashville artist of his standing had held. He recorded the album quickly and cheaply in a studio near Dallas. Executives who heard the tapes reportedly assumed they were demos and asked when the real sessions would begin.","Listen closer: almost nothing on the record is new. The title song is a cowboy number written for a children's radio show in the 1950s, and the album's biggest hit was a country ballad from the 1940s; Nelson wrote the connecting material and let the borrowed songs carry the plot. Notice the silences. He leaves gaps where a band would normally play, and those gaps are what make the thirty-three minutes feel like a landscape.","Columbia released it under protest and it became one of the best-selling country albums of the decade, which effectively ended the label system's argument that Nashville producers knew best. It is the record usually credited with proving that outlaw country was commercial rather than merely defiant. Critics still divide over whether the sparseness is an aesthetic choice or a budget, and Nelson has cheerfully agreed with both.","Play it whole and in order; individual tracks are fragments and make little sense alone. Listen for the guitar tone, a nylon-string classical instrument with a worn hole in its face, which sounds like nothing else in country music. Then hear a nineteenth-century song cycle about a man walking away from a ruined love and notice how similar the strategy is."],"willie-nelson-stardust":["Ten standards from the American songbook, sung by a country singer with his own road band and produced by a soul musician. Nelson phrases far behind the beat, arriving late on almost every line and catching up by the end of the phrase, which turns familiar melodies slightly unfamiliar. Mickey Raphael's harmonica takes the solos a saxophone would normally take. The arrangements are unhurried and small; nothing is orchestrated, and the whole record moves at one gentle tempo.","Booker T. Jones, the organist behind the Memphis soul records of the 1960s, was Nelson's neighbour in California and agreed to produce. Nelson chose songs he had known since childhood, including pieces by Gershwin and by Kurt Weill, and made the record at a point when his outlaw success had bought him the freedom to do something his label considered commercial suicide. Columbia expected it to fail. It stayed on the country chart for roughly a decade.","Listen closer: his phrasing is the whole record. Nelson sings almost nothing on the beat, and the band holds a steady pulse precisely so the delay is audible; it is the technique of a jazz singer applied to a country voice with no vibrato to hide behind. Notice too that several of these songs began in Broadway shows and film scores, so the album quietly reunites two branches of American song that the record industry had spent fifty years separating.","It was heard at the time as a gamble and is now read as the record that made the standards repertoire available again to singers outside pop, decades before that became a common late-career move. Some jazz listeners find the intonation approximate and the band plain. The usual defence is that a songbook this over-recorded needed a voice with no polish at all to sound like a person again.","Pick one song you know well in another version and follow only where Nelson places the words against the beat. Then listen to the harmonica solos as substitute saxophone. Pair it with a Gershwin recording to hear how far these melodies travel. The original album sequence, which opens slow and stays slow, is part of the effect."],"emmylou-harris-pieces-of-the-sky":["Harris's first album as a country singer, built around a clear, unforced soprano with a distinctive tremor at the top. The band is loose and warm, with pedal steel and electric guitar taking turns. The repertoire runs from a close-harmony duet in the Louvin Brothers manner to a Beatles song to a honky-tonk drinking number, and at its centre is her own song about a friend's death, which is the only piece here she wrote and the reason the record exists.","Harris had been a folk singer in Washington when Gram Parsons recruited her to sing harmony on his solo albums and taught her the country repertoire. Parsons died in 1973, and she assembled a group of Los Angeles session players, later known as the Hot Band, to make this. It arrived while country rock was already a commercial category, but Harris's interest ran backwards, towards the brother duets and honky-tonk records the Californian bands had only glanced at.","Listen closer: she sings the Louvin Brothers song as a duet with herself, overdubbing the harmony line, which is how a singer without a brother joins a brother-duet tradition. Her elegy for Parsons places its grief in one specific geographical image, a road between two cities, and never names him. The Hot Band's steel player and lead guitarist are audibly playing 1950s licks on 1970s equipment, which is the record's whole argument.","It established Harris as the most reliable interpreter in country music, a singer who could make any song sound like part of one tradition, and it began a run of albums that carried older repertoire to a rock audience. The recurring criticism is that she wrote very little, which she has answered by pointing at the results. Her curatorial instinct is now generally treated as a form of authorship.","Start with the self-harmonised duet to hear the technique, then the elegy, then work outwards. Listen for how the steel guitar is recorded, dry and close, unlike the reverb-washed country rock of the period. Follow it with a pastoral elegy from another tradition; the business of mourning a friend through borrowed material is very old."],"emmylou-harris-wrecking-ball":["Harris's voice, by then twenty years lower and rougher, set inside vast reverberant space. Daniel Lanois built the record out of tremolo guitars, deep drums recorded in a room rather than close, and washes of sustained sound, so that the songs seem to be arriving from the far end of a hall. The material is borrowed from rock and folk songwriters rather than country ones. There is almost no steel guitar and no fiddle anywhere on it.","Country radio had stopped playing Harris, and rather than chase the format she went to a producer known for atmospheric rock records. The album was made in New Orleans, and the songs were chosen from writers she admired, including a Canadian rock songwriter whose title track gives the record its name, a young Appalachian revivalist, and a Texas songwriter then at her peak. It was released into a Nashville market that had no idea what to do with it.","Listen closer: the reverb is not decoration but structure. Lanois records the drums far from the microphone so that the decay of each hit becomes part of the harmony, and Harris's voice sits inside that decay rather than in front of it, which is why the album sounds like weather. Notice that several songs end without a final chord, simply fading into the room tone that has been present all along.","It won a Grammy, was ignored by country radio, and is now generally credited with giving the Americana category its sound and much of its self-image. The complaint from traditionalists was that the production swamps the songs; the counter-argument is that Harris had spent two decades serving material faithfully and was entitled to a record that served her voice instead. It remains the model every atmospheric roots album is measured against.","Listen on headphones in the dark; the record is built on depth and collapses on small speakers. Start with the title track and attend to the drums rather than the voice. Then play her 1975 debut immediately afterwards to hear twenty years of wear as an instrument. An ambient piano piece from a century earlier makes a surprisingly good companion."],"bill-monroe-mule-skinner-blues":["A Jimmie Rodgers blue yodel taken at a gallop. Bill Monroe and His Blue Grass Boys drive the old tune far faster than Rodgers ever did, with a tight string band pushing hard on every beat and Monroe's voice sitting high and hard above it, leaping into falsetto yodels at the end of each verse. It lasts under three minutes, and the first thing you notice is speed: this is country music played with the urgency of a fiddle contest.","Monroe auditioned for the Grand Ole Opry in Nashville in October 1939 with this song, and he was hired on the spot; the Opry's George D. Hay reportedly told him that if he ever left, he would have to fire himself. He cut it for RCA's Bluebird label in 1940. Country music on the radio was then dominated by smooth western swing and cowboy singers; Monroe's Kentucky band sounded older and wilder at once.","Listen to how the band treats the song's blues shape. Rodgers sang it loose and conversational over one guitar; Monroe keeps the blues structure but hardens the rhythm into a relentless chop. The banjo, later the signature of bluegrass, is absent here: Monroe's band sound was still forming, and the recording shows the style half-built, with the high lonesome voice already in place and the instrumental engine still arriving.","It was an immediate Opry favourite and became Monroe's calling card for decades. Historians treat it as the moment the Blue Grass Boys' identity took shape, though most date full-fledged bluegrass to the 1945–46 band with Flatt and Scruggs. The song itself went on to a long life in other hands, from Dolly Parton's hit version in 1970 to rockabilly and punk covers.","Play Jimmie Rodgers's Blue Yodel No. 1 first to hear the relaxed model, then this. Notice how the tempo alone changes the meaning: the loneliness becomes restlessness. Then jump ahead to Monroe's 1946 recordings with Flatt and Scruggs to hear what the missing banjo would add."],"bill-monroe-blue-moon-of-kentucky":["A slow waltz of heartbreak, sung by Bill Monroe in his high, keening tenor over the classic Blue Grass Boys: Lester Flatt's guitar, Earl Scruggs's banjo, Chubby Wise's fiddle and a string bass. The fiddle sighs through the introduction, Monroe's voice soars over a steady three-beat sway, and the mandolin fills the gaps with bright tremolo. It is gentle and plaintive, a song about a moon that shines on a lover who has gone.","Monroe wrote it in the mid-1940s and recorded it for Columbia in 1946, with the band many consider the first true bluegrass line-up; it was issued in 1947. Postwar country audiences were moving to cities and factories, and Monroe's music carried the sound of rural Kentucky with it. He named his whole style after the Bluegrass State, and this song made the naming explicit.","The song's second life is the thing to know. In July 1954, at Sun Studio in Memphis, a young Elvis Presley and his trio sped it up into four-beat rockabilly for the B-side of his first single. Monroe, rather than objecting, re-recorded the song himself soon afterwards with a slow waltz opening that breaks into a fast section, answering Elvis in his own style.","It became the Kentucky state bluegrass song and one of the most recorded numbers in the genre. Critics point to it as the clearest example of how close early rock and roll stood to hillbilly music: the same song sits at the root of both. Arguments continue over whether Monroe's original or the Elvis version matters more historically; the fair answer is that each needs the other.","Hear Monroe's 1946 waltz, then Elvis's 1954 reshaping, then Monroe's later hybrid version, one after another. Listen for the fiddle in the original: Chubby Wise's long, sliding phrases are as much the voice of the song as Monroe's singing. Then notice what Elvis keeps, the melody, and what he throws away, the waltz."],"bill-monroe-uncle-pen":["A brisk, affectionate portrait of a fiddler. Monroe sings of his uncle and the old tunes he played, and the band answers each verse with fiddle breakdowns that are themselves the tribute. The rhythm is a fast two-beat, the fiddle leads rather than decorates, and the chorus lifts into Monroe's trademark high harmony. It is a song about music, and it proves its point by playing the kind of music it describes.","Pendleton Vandiver, Monroe's mother's brother, was a fiddler in Rosine, Kentucky, who took the orphaned teenage Monroe in and played him old-time tunes at country dances. Monroe recorded this memorial in 1950, by then signed to Decca and leading a band that trained generations of players. Uncle Pen had died years earlier; the song fixed him as the fountainhead of the whole bluegrass style.","Notice what the song claims about origins. Monroe consistently credited Uncle Pen's fiddle bowing and Black blues guitarist Arnold Shultz as his two great teachers. The fiddle part here imitates an old-time Kentucky style, with its rocking rhythm and drone strings, grafted onto the modern bluegrass band. You are hearing Monroe place himself on a family tree, deliberately.","It became a bluegrass standard and a staple of fiddle-contest repertoire. In 1984 Ricky Skaggs took it to number one on the country chart, confirming its reach decades later. Historians value it as Monroe's own account of where bluegrass came from, while noting that his story tends to underplay the African American threads he elsewhere acknowledged.","Listen to the fiddle breaks between verses as the real subject. Then compare with a field recording of an old-time Appalachian fiddler to hear the tradition Monroe is honouring. Ricky Skaggs's 1984 version shows how the song sat in a slicker Nashville setting."],"flatt-and-scruggs-foggy-mountain-breakdown":["An instrumental showcase built around Earl Scruggs's banjo. Three fingers pick in rolling patterns so fast that single notes blur into a cascade, while guitar, mandolin, fiddle and bass pass solos around in turn. The tune is simple, a few bars repeated and varied, but the sound is unmistakable: a bright, metallic, forward-leaning rush. Most people know it without knowing its name, as the soundtrack to a car chase.","Lester Flatt and Earl Scruggs left Bill Monroe's band in 1948 and formed the Foggy Mountain Boys. They recorded this for Mercury in late 1949. Scruggs had perfected a three-finger picking style in North Carolina that turned the banjo, a rhythm instrument in string bands, into a lead voice capable of long, flowing runs. Postwar audiences for country instrumentals were small; the record sold modestly at first.","Listen for the moment the chord sequence darkens partway through each pass, a turn to a minor-sounding chord that gives the tune its urgency. Also notice that nobody plays drums: the forward drive comes entirely from the mandolin's off-beat chop and the bass. In 1967 Arthur Penn used the old recording in Bonnie and Clyde, and a new 1968 recording won a Grammy, nearly twenty years after the original.","It became the most famous banjo tune in the world and is now a rite of passage for every bluegrass picker. The film revived Flatt and Scruggs's career and pulled bluegrass into the counterculture. Purists sometimes resent its ubiquity as a comic chase cue, but musicians still use it as the benchmark of Scruggs-style technique.","Put on the 1949 Mercury original and follow the banjo alone for one chorus, then switch attention to the mandolin's chop underneath. Then watch the chase scene in Bonnie and Clyde. For contrast, hear Doc Watson flatpicking a fiddle tune on guitar."],"stanley-brothers-man-of-constant-sorrow":["An old Appalachian lament recast by Carter and Ralph Stanley in their early bluegrass style. Ralph takes the lead in a piercing, grainy high voice, telling of a wanderer who has lost home and love and will die a stranger; the band answers with spare mandolin and fiddle fills. The song is slow and grave, closer to a ballad than a breakdown, and the sound is stark rather than showy.","The song was probably first published around 1913 by Dick Burnett, a blind Kentucky fiddler, and circulated in variants through the mountains. The Stanley Brothers, from Virginia's Clinch Mountains, recorded it for Columbia around 1950, when they were forging their own mountain-flavoured variant of Bill Monroe's style. Their version made the song central to the bluegrass repertoire.","The Stanleys' vocal sound is the lesson. Where Monroe's tenor was bright, Ralph's is raw and nasal, rooted in the unaccompanied Primitive Baptist singing of his childhood. Listen to how he bends and holds certain words, a mountain church habit. Bob Dylan put a very different version on his first album in 1962, and the 2000 film O Brother, Where Art Thou? made it a hit again for a new generation.","It is now among the best-known songs in American folk music. For decades, however, it lived mainly in the bluegrass and folk-revival circuits; the O Brother soundtrack's success brought it to mass audiences and a Grammy. Scholars still debate its origins, which may predate Burnett, and folklorists treat its many variants as a case study in oral tradition.","Start with the Stanleys' recording and listen only to Ralph's voice. Then hear the Soggy Bottom Boys' version on the O Brother soundtrack, sung by Dan Tyminski, to see how the song was polished for film. Dylan's 1962 reading shows what the folk revival heard in it."],"stanley-brothers-rank-stranger":["A gospel song of homecoming and estrangement. Carter Stanley sings the verses in his warm, plain baritone: a man returns to the home of his youth and finds every face unknown. At the chorus Ralph Stanley's high tenor enters above him, ghostly and piercing, and the song turns from sorrow to a hope of reunion beyond death. The accompaniment is light and slow, leaving the two voices exposed.","The song was written by the Missouri gospel composer Albert E. Brumley, author of I'll Fly Away, and published in the 1940s. The Stanley Brothers recorded it in 1960, in the middle of a period when rock and roll had cut deeply into bluegrass's audience and they were making a living on small labels and on the road. Gospel material was always a large part of their repertoire.","The song works by vocal handover. Carter's lead is intimate and earthly; Ralph's answering voice seems to come from somewhere else entirely, which is exactly what the words are about. Listen to the chorus as a dialogue between the living and the dead. After Carter's death in 1966, when Ralph sang it with other partners, audiences heard the song as a memorial to his brother.","It is widely considered the Stanleys' finest recording and one of the great bluegrass gospel performances. It rarely gets the pop attention of Man of Constant Sorrow, but musicians cite it constantly. Its eeriness has made it a touchstone for later Americana singers who draw on the gothic, sacred strain in mountain music.","Wait for the chorus: the entry of Ralph's voice is the whole point. Listen for how little the instruments do. Then hear the Carter Family's Can the Circle Be Unbroken for another song about home, death and reunion, and Gillian Welch's Revival for its modern descendants."],"doc-watson-doc-watson":["Doc Watson's first solo album: a North Carolina mountain man, mostly alone with a flat-top guitar, playing ballads, blues, hymns and fiddle tunes. His baritone is relaxed and warm, and his guitar is a revelation, picking fast fiddle melodies note by note with a flat pick, clean and unhurried at speeds that sound impossible. Pieces like Black Mountain Rag and Deep River Blues show the two sides: dazzling instrumental runs and easy, rolling fingerpicked blues.","Watson, blind since infancy, grew up in Deep Gap, North Carolina, and had played electric guitar in a local rockabilly and swing band. In 1960 the folklorist Ralph Rinzler met him while recording Clarence Ashley, and brought him to the urban folk revival, where he quickly became a star at Newport and in coffeehouses. Vanguard released this album in 1964 at the height of that revival.","Watson learned many fiddle tunes on guitar because his band had no fiddler and dance crowds wanted them: the style that amazed folk audiences was a practical workaround from playing square dances on an electric guitar. Listen for how his flatpicked lines copy fiddle bowing, with little slides and double notes. Much of the repertoire came from his family and from the Carter Family and Jimmie Rodgers records he heard as a boy.","It established Watson as the defining flatpicking guitarist, and the style it popularised became standard in bluegrass guitar, notably through Clarence White and Tony Rice. Folk revival audiences treated him as an authentic tradition-bearer; later writers stress how eclectic his tastes actually were, including jazz and rockabilly. The album remains a starting point for acoustic guitarists.","Start with Black Mountain Rag for the flatpicking, then Deep River Blues for the fingerpicked side. Listen to how calm his voice stays while the guitar races. Then hear Old Time Music at Clarence Ashley's to meet him in his home setting."],"nitty-gritty-dirt-band-will-the-circle-be-unbroken":["A sprawling triple album in which a California folk-rock band sits down in Nashville with the elders of country and bluegrass. Maybelle Carter, Roy Acuff, Earl Scruggs, Doc Watson, Merle Travis, Jimmy Martin and fiddler Vassar Clements lead songs and tunes, with the young band backing them. Between numbers the tape keeps rolling, catching studio chat and count-ins. It ends with the whole company singing the Carter Family hymn of the title.","In 1971 the Nitty Gritty Dirt Band, long-haired Californians, persuaded musicians from a generation and a political world apart to record with them. The Vietnam War divided country's audience from rock's, and many Nashville figures distrusted hippies. Recorded quickly in a Nashville studio, largely live, it was released by United Artists in 1972 and sold far beyond expectations.","Listen to the talk between tracks. The candid moments, such as Acuff sizing up the young players before a take, and old musicians recalling where they learned a tune, are part of the record's argument that tradition passes hand to hand. Maybelle Carter's autoharp and guitar on Wildwood Flower connect straight back to her 1928 recording.","It became a landmark of cross-generational collaboration and brought many listeners to bluegrass and old-time country. Critics praise its warmth and documentary value more than any single performance. Two sequels followed, in 1989 and 2002; the 1972 original is still regarded as the one that mattered.","Do not listen straight through the first time. Pick the Maybelle Carter tracks, the Doc Watson tracks and the closing title song, then fill in the rest over several sittings. Notice how the young band plays under the elders rather than over them. Compare the finale with the Carter Family's original Can the Circle Be Unbroken from the 1930s."],"emmylou-harris-roses-in-the-snow":["Emmylou Harris, then one of country's biggest stars, makes an all-acoustic bluegrass and old-time record. Her clear, high voice rides over dobro, fiddle, banjo and flatpicked guitar, singing mountain gospel, Carter Family and Stanley Brothers songs, the old hymn Wayfaring Stranger, and a bluegrass version of Simon and Garfunkel's The Boxer. The arrangements are spare and bright, and the harmony singing is close and old-fashioned.","Harris had built her career on country rock since Gram Parsons's death. In 1980, with bluegrass unfashionable in Nashville, she and her producer and husband Brian Ahern chose an acoustic, traditional sound, drawing on her band's young players, above all Ricky Skaggs, with guests including Tony Rice and Jerry Douglas and harmony from stars such as Dolly Parton, Linda Ronstadt and Johnny Cash. Warner Bros. released it, and it sold well.","Listen to Ricky Skaggs, who sings harmony and plays fiddle and mandolin throughout and did much to shape the arrangements; the record foreshadows his own run of hit albums that brought traditional sounds back to country radio in the 1980s. The Boxer, turned into a bluegrass song, argues quietly that the folk revival and mountain music were never far apart.","It was a surprise success and is widely credited with helping the new traditionalist movement that followed in 1980s country. Some bluegrass purists found Harris's voice too polished; most welcomed a major star lending her platform to the tradition. It is often ranked among her finest albums.","Start with Wayfaring Stranger, then The Boxer, then a Carter Family song. Listen for Skaggs's high harmony against Harris's lead, and for the dobro between verses. Then go back to Gram Parsons's Grievous Angel to hear where her country voice was formed, and forward to Gillian Welch's Revival for the tradition's next turn."],"flying-burrito-brothers-the-gilded-palace-of-sin":["Honky-tonk, Southern soul and Los Angeles rock rolled into one. Gram Parsons and Chris Hillman sing close brother-style harmonies over Sneaky Pete Kleinow's pedal steel, which is often run through a fuzz box until it snarls like an electric guitar. Songs like Sin City and Hot Burrito No. 1 sit beside two Memphis soul covers, Do Right Woman and The Dark End of the Street. It is sad, sly and strange.","After leaving the Byrds, Parsons and Hillman formed the Burritos in Los Angeles in 1968 and released this on A&M early in 1969. They wore custom Nudie suits embroidered with pills, marijuana leaves and flames, turning the rhinestone costume of Nashville into a counterculture joke that was also sincere. Parsons called his ideal Cosmic American Music: country, soul and rock as one Southern inheritance.","The two soul covers are the key. Do Right Woman had been recorded by Aretha Franklin two years earlier; Parsons sings the Southern soul songs straight, as country laments, arguing that the Black and white music of the South were one family. Listen also for Kleinow's steel imitating horns and electric guitars.","It sold poorly but became a cult touchstone, cherished by the Rolling Stones, Elvis Costello, and later the alt-country movement. Critics now rank it with Sweetheart of the Rodeo as a founding country-rock document, sometimes above it for its originality. Hillman's contribution has been reappraised as central to the songs.","Begin with Hot Burrito No. 1 for Parsons's aching vocal, then Sin City for the harmony. Then compare Do Right Woman with Aretha Franklin's version. The Rolling Stones' Let It Bleed shows the Burritos' influence on British rock."],"gram-parsons-grievous-angel":["Gram Parsons's second and last solo album, built on his duets with Emmylou Harris. Their voices twine through Love Hurts, the title song and In My Hour of Darkness, his thin, fragile tenor leaning on her clear high harmony. The band, including members of Elvis Presley's touring group, plays spare, elegant country; the mood is wistful and road-weary, shifting between honky-tonk ballads and brighter country rock.","Parsons recorded it in Los Angeles in summer 1973 and died that September, aged 26, at Joshua Tree in the California desert, after a drug overdose. Reprise released it in January 1974. Harris, a folk singer he had discovered in Washington, D.C., had been learning country harmony from him; the album made her name and launched her career.","The medley of Cash on the Barrelhead and Hickory Wind is presented as a live bar recording, but it was cut in the studio with crowd noise and chatter added, a mock-live frame for Parsons's favourite song. Listen for the laughing and clinking glasses. In My Hour of Darkness, written with Harris, mourns friends including Clarence White and sounds now like his own elegy.","Received respectfully, it grew into a cult classic and set the template for the male-female country duet in Americana. Critics praise the harmonies above almost anything else in his work. The myth of his death, including the theft and burning of his body by his road manager, has sometimes overshadowed the music.","Start with Love Hurts and listen only to how Harris shapes the harmony. Then hear the Everly Brothers' close-harmony style, which Parsons loved. After this, Emmylou Harris's Pieces of the Sky shows her carrying the torch forward."],"the-band-the-band":["Often called the brown album, after its sepia cover. Four Canadians and an Arkansan play songs that sound like old America: the Civil War, farms, travelling medicine shows, floods. Three lead singers, Levon Helm, Rick Danko and Richard Manuel, trade verses in rough, overlapping voices; Garth Hudson's organ and horns thicken the sound. The Night They Drove Old Dixie Down and Up on Cripple Creek are its best-known songs.","After backing Bob Dylan in his electric years and working with him in upstate New York, the Band recorded this in 1969 in a pool house in the Hollywood hills converted into a studio. Rock in 1969 was loud, psychedelic and political; this record went the other way, toward period dress and the past. Robbie Robertson wrote most of the songs; Capitol released it that autumn.","Listen to the instruments swapping roles. Danko sometimes plays fiddle, Manuel drums on some tracks, Hudson overdubs horns himself, and Helm's drumming sits behind the beat in a relaxed Southern shuffle. The recording was deliberately dry and close, as if the band were in a room with you, at a time when big reverb was the norm.","It was received as a landmark and has stayed one, a foundation stone of what later came to be called Americana. The Night They Drove Old Dixie Down, told from a defeated Confederate's point of view, has drawn sharper criticism in recent decades. Arguments over songwriting credits between Robertson and the others became bitter in later life.","Start with Up on Cripple Creek for Hudson's clavinet and wah pedal, then The Night They Drove Old Dixie Down. Notice how often voices overlap rather than harmonise neatly. Then compare with Sweetheart of the Rodeo, released a year earlier."],"john-prine-john-prine":["A debut of plain, sure-footed songs. Prine's voice is dry and a little rough, and his band stays out of the way. The songs are tender and funny in the same breath: Hello in There on loneliness in old age, Angel from Montgomery sung as a middle-aged woman, Sam Stone on a wounded veteran's addiction, Paradise on strip mining in Kentucky. The detail is small and domestic; the effect is enormous.","Prine was a mail carrier and army veteran in Chicago, singing at open-mic nights, when Kris Kristofferson heard him and helped him to a contract with Atlantic. The album was recorded in Memphis with producer Arif Mardin and released in 1971, as the Vietnam War dragged on. Prine was widely billed as a new Dylan, a label he shrugged off.","Paradise names the Peabody Coal Company and Muhlenberg County, Kentucky, where Prine's parents came from and where surface mining had destroyed the landscape. Listen for how gently he sings it. Angel from Montgomery was written from the viewpoint of a woman, something his male peers rarely did; Bonnie Raitt's 1974 version made it a standard.","It won strong reviews but modest sales at the time; its songs were spread through covers. It is now regarded as one of the great singer-songwriter debuts. Prine's reputation rose steadily over five decades, and his death in 2020 during the pandemic prompted a wide reassessment of his craft.","Start with Hello in There and Angel from Montgomery, then Paradise. Listen for the jokes placed right next to grief, and for how little the band does behind him. Then hear Bonnie Raitt's version of Angel from Montgomery to see how the song travelled, and Townes Van Zandt's Pancho and Lefty for a darker sibling in the same trade."],"townes-van-zandt-pancho-and-lefty":["A border ballad about a Mexican bandit and the friend who may have betrayed him. Van Zandt sings in a quiet, grave baritone over fingerpicked guitar and discreet accompaniment. The story is told in fragments, with dust, desert and a lonely hotel in Cleveland, and never quite says what happened. The melody rises and falls like an old cowboy song, but the storytelling is modern: elliptical, cinematic, morally unresolved.","Van Zandt, from a wealthy Texas family, lived much of his life in poverty, drifting and struggling with alcohol and depression. The song appeared on The Late Great Townes Van Zandt, a 1972 album on the small Poppy label. It had little impact until others sang it: Emmylou Harris in 1977, and above all Willie Nelson and Merle Haggard, whose 1983 duet was a country number one.","Van Zandt often said he did not know where the song came from and that it arrived nearly complete. Listen for what the lyric withholds: we never learn whether Lefty betrayed Pancho or simply survived him. Pancho may nod to Pancho Villa, but the song is not a history. The mystery is the design, and it lets every singer take a side.","It is now among the most admired songs in country and folk writing, frequently cited by songwriters as a model. Van Zandt himself sold few records in his lifetime; admiration came mainly from fellow writers such as Steve Earle, who called him one of the best ever. His original recording is still less known than the Nelson–Haggard version.","Listen to Van Zandt's version first, in a quiet room. Then play the Nelson–Haggard duet and notice how a hit arrangement brightens it. Steve Earle's album of Van Zandt songs, Townes, is a good next step."],"steve-earle-guitar-town":["Country songs played with rock-and-roll swagger. Earle sings in a raspy Texas drawl over twangy electric guitars and a lean band, the Dukes. The songs are about working men, small towns, cars and the urge to leave: the title track, Someday and Hillbilly Highway. It sounds like Nashville crossed with Bruce Springsteen, crisp and tuneful but with dirt under its fingernails.","Earle had spent a decade as a Nashville songwriter and scuffling rockabilly act. MCA released this debut in 1986, produced by Tony Brown and Emory Gordy Jr., in the same wave that brought Dwight Yoakam and Randy Travis: a turn back to traditional sounds after the pop-heavy country of the early 1980s. It found an audience on country radio and among rock critics at once.","Listen to the guitar sound. Earle's twangy electric parts were deliberately bright and hard, halfway between Buck Owens's Bakersfield honky-tonk and heartland rock. Someday, about a young man pumping gas and dreaming of escape, uses the same working-class vocabulary as Springsteen, but its setting is the country side of the tracks.","It was a critical and commercial success, and it has since been credited as a precursor of alt-country. Earle's later albums moved further toward rock and politics, and his troubles with addiction and prison in the early 1990s interrupted his career. After his return he became one of the elder statesmen of Americana.","Start with Someday, then the title track. Notice the tight, economical arrangements, no song much over four minutes. Compare with Merle Haggard for the older working-man tradition, and then Copperhead Road for Earle's harder turn."],"steve-earle-copperhead-road":["Earle's hardest-rocking album. The title track opens with a droning figure that sounds like bagpipes before drums crash in, telling a three-generation family story of moonshine, war and illegal crops in the Tennessee hills. The rest of the record mixes storytelling ballads, loud country rock and the Irish-tinged Johnny Come Lately, recorded with the Pogues. It is heavier and angrier than Guitar Town.","Released in 1988 by MCA's rock-oriented imprint Uni, the album was marketed to rock fans because country radio found Earle too rough. The Vietnam War and its veterans were a live subject in late-1980s America, after films like Platoon; several songs carry the war's shadow. Earle was also in the grip of addiction during this period.","The title track's narrator is a Vietnam veteran, the grandson and son of bootleggers, who brings home new techniques from the war. Listen for how the drone at the start imitates Scottish pipes, a hint at the Scots-Irish ancestry of Appalachia that the whole song trades on. Johnny Come Lately sets a World War II homecoming beside a Vietnam one.","The title song became Earle's best-known track, a staple of rock radio and line-dance floors. The album sits awkwardly between country and rock, which divided critics at the time. It is now recognised as a key border-crossing record for Americana.","Play the title track loud, listening for the moment the drums crash in after the droning opening, and follow the story across its three generations. Then hear Johnny Come Lately with the Pogues for the Irish side. Going back to Guitar Town afterwards shows how far and how fast Earle travelled in two years."],"lucinda-williams-lucinda-williams":["A lean, guitar-based record by a Louisiana-born singer with a drawling, cracked voice. The songs are about desire, restlessness and places: Passionate Kisses, Changed the Locks, Crescent City. The band plays unfussy rock and roll, country shuffle and blues, with none of Nashville's gloss. Williams sings as if thinking aloud, slurring and stretching vowels, and the directness of the writing is the point.","Williams, daughter of the poet Miller Williams, had made two folk-blues albums on Folkways in the late 1970s and then spent years unsigned; country labels found her too rock, rock labels too country. The British independent Rough Trade released this in 1988. It sold modestly but circulated among musicians and critics.","Two songs here became hits for other artists: Mary Chapin Carpenter's Passionate Kisses won a Grammy in 1994, and Tom Petty recorded Changed the Locks. Listen for how Williams's own version of Passionate Kisses sounds defiant, even impatient, while Carpenter's is sunnier: the same words, a different self.","It is now regarded as a founding document of Americana, a genre name that did not yet exist. Critics singled out her writing immediately; the recognition from a wider public came a decade later with Car Wheels. The album was reissued with bonus material in 2014.","Start with Passionate Kisses, then Changed the Locks. Pay attention to her vowels: she makes a single word carry an argument. Then jump to Car Wheels on a Gravel Road to hear what ten years did."],"lucinda-williams-car-wheels-on-a-gravel-road":["A set of songs soaked in the American South: Louisiana back roads, lost loves, dead friends. Williams's voice is weary and tough, the band loose and rootsy, with slide guitar and accordion colouring the rock rhythm. The title song remembers a child's view from the back seat of a car; Drunken Angel mourns a songwriter; Lake Charles is a road elegy. Every track has a place name in it or nearby.","Recorded over roughly six years, through several studios and producers, including a period with Steve Earle and Ray Kennedy and a final polish with Roy Bittan of Springsteen's E Street Band. Williams's perfectionism became notorious. Mercury released it in 1998, and it won the Grammy for Best Contemporary Folk Album.","Listen to 2 Kool 2 Be 4-Gotten, which echoes the Delta blues world of Robert Johnson, a nod to the region's Black musical history. Drunken Angel is about the Texas songwriter Blaze Foley, killed in 1989. The album's scrupulous naturalism, from gravel under tyres to crickets, was built through dozens of retakes, not captured casually.","It was a critical landmark and her commercial breakthrough, topping many year-end polls. Some critics found the long gestation over-polished; most heard the opposite, a record that sounds lived-in. It remains the album most often used to define what Americana means.","Begin with the title track, then Lake Charles and Drunken Angel. Listen for the concrete nouns in every line. Afterwards hear Robert Johnson's Cross Road Blues to feel the ground under 2 Kool 2 Be 4-Gotten."],"uncle-tupelo-no-depression":["A punk trio plays country songs, and the collision sounds natural. Jay Farrar and Jeff Tweedy trade lead vocals over loud, distorted guitars and thrashing drums, then drop suddenly to acoustic guitars and hushed singing. The songs deal with small-town decline, drinking and dead-end jobs in the American Midwest. The title track is an old Carter Family song about the Depression, played straight.","Farrar, Tweedy and drummer Mike Heidorn grew up in Belleville, Illinois, a working-class town across the river from St. Louis. They played punk in their teens and discovered their parents' country records. This debut was recorded in Boston and released by the small Rockville label in 1990, as grunge was forming; it sold little at first.","The album's title and its cover of the Carter Family's No Depression in Heaven, from 1936, give the game away: Uncle Tupelo heard the hard-times songs of the 1930s as the true ancestors of punk. Listen for how the band keeps the old hymn's plain melody while raising the volume, and for how Farrar's and Tweedy's voices divide the album between gravity and warmth.","It is regarded as the founding record of alternative country; a magazine founded in 1995 took the name No Depression, and alt-country fans became known by it. The band split in 1994 into Son Volt and Wilco. Critics now debate whether the label alt-country helped or trapped the music that followed.","Play Graveyard Shift for the punk side, then the title track for the hushed country side, and notice how sudden the switch is. Listen to the Carter Family's 1936 original afterwards. Then hear Son Volt's Trace and Wilco's Yankee Hotel Foxtrot to follow the two singers as their paths split after 1994."],"son-volt-trace":["Jay Farrar's first album after Uncle Tupelo. His deep, grave voice sings of rivers, highways and the Midwest, alternating slow acoustic ballads with fiddle and lap steel against loud, distorted rock songs. Windfall, the opening track, is a song about driving at night with the radio on; Tear Stained Eye and Drown follow. The mood is melancholic, the sound open and spacious.","Uncle Tupelo split in 1994. Farrar formed Son Volt with Tupelo drummer Mike Heidorn and the brothers Jim and Dave Boquist, and Warner Bros. released Trace in 1995, the same year Tweedy's Wilco debuted. Alt-country was becoming a scene with its own magazine and radio format, and the two ex-bandmates were compared at every turn.","Listen to the fiddle and lap steel on Windfall: the arrangement almost imitates an old radio signal fading in and out, matching the song's subject, a late-night AM station on the road. Farrar's lyrics are strewn with Mississippi River imagery; the river runs through the whole record like a map.","It was the better-received of the two post-Tupelo debuts and remains Son Volt's high point. Critics then assumed Farrar would be the more important songwriter; Wilco's later experiments reversed that judgment. Trace is still considered one of the purest alt-country albums.","Start with Windfall, ideally in a car at night, and listen to the fiddle and steel drifting like a fading radio. Then play Drown for the loud side of the band. Compare with Wilco's Yankee Hotel Foxtrot to hear where the other half of Uncle Tupelo went, and why fans argued about them for years."],"wilco-yankee-hotel-foxtrot":["Folk-rock songs dissolving into noise, static and electronic texture. Jeff Tweedy sings in a frail, conversational voice; beneath him the band builds melodies that keep being interrupted by radio hiss, clanging percussion and drones. I Am Trying to Break Your Heart opens in a clatter; Jesus, Etc. is a lovely string-laced ballad; Poor Places ends in a wall of noise. Country's plain storytelling is still there, but half buried.","Wilco recorded it in Chicago in 2001 with Jim O'Rourke mixing. Reprise, their label, rejected it as uncommercial and released the band. Wilco streamed the album free on their website in September 2001, just after the attacks on New York, and Nonesuch, another Warner label, released it in April 2002. Songs about towers and war suddenly seemed prophetic.","The title comes from a shortwave numbers-station recording, a woman's voice reading the phonetic alphabet, taken from The Conet Project compilation. Listen for it at the end of Poor Places. The documentary I Am Trying to Break Your Heart filmed the sessions, including the departure of multi-instrumentalist Jay Bennett, and the album's story became part of its reception.","It was hailed as a landmark and became a symbol of artists defeating a major label through the internet. Some alt-country fans felt the band had abandoned its roots. Critics now see it as proof that the Uncle Tupelo tradition could absorb experimental rock and electronics without losing its heart.","Start with Jesus, Etc., the most straightforward song, then I Am Trying to Break Your Heart. Listen for the static, radio fragments and clatter as part of the music, not interference. Afterwards hear No Depression to measure the distance travelled from Uncle Tupelo, and watch the documentary of the sessions if you want the backstory."],"gillian-welch-revival":["Gillian Welch and David Rawlings sing new songs that sound a century old. Their two voices lock in close, dry harmony; their two guitars interlock, Welch's rhythm steady, Rawlings's lead bright and angular. The songs are about mountain poverty, orphanhood, moonshine and faith: Orphan Girl, Annabelle, Tear My Stillhouse Down. The sound is sparse, slow and dusty, with occasional muted drums and bass added by producer T Bone Burnett.","Welch, raised in Los Angeles, fell in love with the Stanley Brothers at college and moved to Nashville, where she met Rawlings at Berklee in Boston. Almo Sounds released this debut in 1996. Emmylou Harris had already recorded Orphan Girl on Wrecking Ball in 1995. Welch's authenticity as a Californian writing Appalachian songs was questioned from the start.","Listen to Rawlings's guitar. He plays an old small-bodied archtop guitar with a sharp, pinched tone, and his lead lines often cut across the melody rather than supporting it. The duo's harmonies deliberately avoid sweetness, imitating the brother-duet sound of the Stanleys and the Louvins.","It won strong reviews and a Grammy nomination, and it helped create the audience for O Brother four years later. The authenticity debate has faded; Welch is now recognised as among the best songwriters of her generation. Some prefer later albums for their variety, but Revival remains the purest statement.","Start with Orphan Girl, then compare Emmylou Harris's version on Wrecking Ball from the year before. Listen closely to the interplay of the two guitars, his darting and hers steady, and to the dry, unsweetened harmony. Then go back to the Stanley Brothers' Rank Stranger to hear the sound the duo learned from."],"gillian-welch-time-the-revelator":["Just two voices and two guitars, with a banjo here and there, recorded in Nashville's old RCA Studio B. The songs are slow and long, bending mountain music toward meditation: Revelator opens; Elvis Presley Blues remembers the King's shaking hips; Everything Is Free comments on the music industry; I Dream a Highway, nearly fifteen minutes, closes the album in a drifting reverie. The mood is dreamlike and patient.","Welch and Rawlings, having left their label, founded their own, Acony Records, and released this in 2001. Napster and file-sharing were upending the music business; Everything Is Free takes up the subject calmly, as a question of how a musician lives. The O Brother soundtrack, on which Welch appeared, was then making old-time music newly popular.","Studio B was where Elvis and the Everly Brothers recorded; Welch and Rawlings chose it for its room sound. Listen to the room ambience on Elvis Presley Blues, and for how the song makes Elvis a figure of folk myth, like John Henry. The album's title plays on the old gospel song John the Revelator.","It was received as a major record and is often ranked the finest of the 2000s in its field. Some reviewers found the long songs slow; others praised the courage of that pace. Its release on the duo's own label became a model for independent roots musicians.","Hear Elvis Presley Blues first, then Everything Is Free. Leave time for I Dream a Highway and let it drift. Afterwards hear Blind Willie Johnson's John the Revelator for the gospel source of the title."],"alison-krauss-so-long-so-wrong":["Contemporary bluegrass of great delicacy. Alison Krauss sings in a clear, pure, almost weightless soprano; her band, Union Station, plays with precision and restraint. Jerry Douglas's dobro glides between verses, the banjo rolls softly, and ballads alternate with fast instrumentals. Dan Tyminski takes lead on some songs with a harder, more traditional voice. The textures are silky, the tempo mostly unhurried.","Krauss, a fiddle prodigy from Illinois, signed with the independent Rounder label as a teenager. By the mid-1990s a greatest-hits collection had made her a platinum-selling star, remarkable for bluegrass. So Long So Wrong, released by Rounder in 1997, was a full band album that won the Grammy for Best Bluegrass Album.","Listen to how the voice is placed in the mix, close and soft rather than projected, much as in pop balladry. Traditional bluegrass singers shout to carry over the band; Krauss's intimacy was a studio-age innovation that brought the genre to listeners who had never heard of Bill Monroe. The instrumentals show that the band's chops are as hard as any traditional outfit.","It was praised as a high-water mark of modern bluegrass and won the Grammy for Best Bluegrass Album. Traditionalists occasionally found Krauss's sound too smooth, closer to pop balladry than to the hard, projected singing of Monroe or Ralph Stanley, and the old argument over what counts as real bluegrass followed her for years. Her popularity nonetheless opened the genre to new audiences, and Union Station's players went on to form the core of the O Brother soundtrack.","Start with the title track, then a Dan Tyminski lead vocal for contrast between his hard traditional voice and her soft one. Listen for Jerry Douglas's dobro sliding under the singing, and for the tight instrumentals. Afterwards go back to Bill Monroe's Blue Moon of Kentucky to measure how far bluegrass singing had moved."],"alison-krauss-raising-sand":["Robert Plant of Led Zeppelin and Alison Krauss sing old and obscure songs together, softly. Their voices, his husky, hers crystalline, blend in close harmony over dark, reverberant arrangements: tremolo guitars, brushed drums, spare fiddle. Songs by the Everly Brothers, Townes Van Zandt, Tom Waits and Allen Toussaint are slowed into a hazy, mysterious mood. It sounds like a late-night radio show from 1958 heard through a desert haze.","Producer T Bone Burnett chose the songs and the musicians, including guitarist Marc Ribot. Rounder released it in 2007. Plant, the most famous hard-rock singer of the 1970s, and Krauss, bluegrass's biggest star, seemed an unlikely pair; the gamble paid off. In 2009 it won the Grammy for Album of the Year.","Listen to how the singers rarely belt. Plant, once famous for howling, sings almost at a whisper, and the arrangements leave space where one expects a solo. Gone Gone Gone comes from the Everly Brothers, and the vocal blend imitates their close fifths and thirds. Townes Van Zandt's Nothin' becomes a brooding drone.","It was a critical and commercial success and swept the Grammys. Some bluegrass fans missed Krauss's band; some rock fans missed Plant's roar. It is now seen as one of the defining records of the 2000s Americana boom, alongside O Brother.","Start with Please Read the Letter, then Gone Gone Gone, and listen to how the two voices blend rather than compete. Notice how the reverb and tremolo guitars create distance, as if the songs were drifting in from another decade. Then play the Everly Brothers to hear the harmony tradition Plant and Krauss are borrowing from."],"t-bone-burnett-o-brother-where-art-thou":["A soundtrack for a Coen brothers film set in 1930s Mississippi, compiled and produced by T Bone Burnett. It mixes old recordings with new performances in old styles: Ralph Stanley's unaccompanied O Death, the Soggy Bottom Boys' Man of Constant Sorrow, Alison Krauss, Gillian Welch and Emmylou Harris singing a lullaby, the Fairfield Four, and a 1959 prison work-song field recording. Gospel, bluegrass and blues sit side by side.","The Coens built their film around the music, recording much of it before shooting. Released in 2000 on Mercury's roots label Lost Highway, the album received almost no radio play but sold several million copies, won the Grammy for Album of the Year in 2002, and spawned the Down from the Mountain concert tour. It turned Americana into a mass-market category.","George Clooney mimes in the film, but the voice of Man of Constant Sorrow belongs to Dan Tyminski of Union Station. Listen for the field recording of Po' Lazarus, made by Alan Lomax at a Mississippi prison; its lead singer, James Carter, was traced decades later and presented with royalties. Ralph Stanley's O Death is sung in the old unaccompanied Baptist style.","Its success astonished the music industry and prompted a revival of interest in bluegrass and old-time music. Some traditionalists grumbled that the album polished the old sounds; most musicians in the field credit it with reviving their careers. It remains one of the best-selling soundtracks ever.","Start with Ralph Stanley's unaccompanied O Death, then the Soggy Bottom Boys' Man of Constant Sorrow, then the prison work song Po' Lazarus. Notice how old recordings and new ones sit side by side without jarring. Then go to Harry Smith's Anthology of American Folk Music for the older originals the album echoes."],"carolina-chocolate-drops-genuine-negro-jig":["A string band of young Black musicians playing old-time Piedmont fiddle and banjo music. Rhiannon Giddens's strong voice leads ballads and blues; fiddle and banjo drive dance tunes; bones, jug and beatboxing supply rhythm. The repertoire mixes traditional tunes with modern covers, including Blu Cantrell's R&B hit Hit 'Em Up Style played as a string-band song. The sound is lively, rough-edged, and plainly joyful.","Giddens, Dom Flemons and Justin Robinson met at the Black Banjo Gathering in North Carolina in 2005 and apprenticed themselves to Joe Thompson, an elderly African American fiddler. Their mission was to reclaim the Black roots of the string band, erased by the racial marketing of records in the 1920s. Nonesuch released this in 2010, produced by Joe Henry; it won the Grammy for Best Traditional Folk Album.","The title probably comes from an old 19th-century tune title; the band reclaims the offensive label deliberately. Listen for the banjo, which descends from West African gourd lutes brought by the enslaved. The album quietly argues that bluegrass and country began partly as Black music, a history that the Stanleys and Monroe knew and the record business obscured.","It was widely praised and became a touchstone for the growing movement to recognise Black contributions to country and folk. Giddens went on to a major solo career and a MacArthur fellowship. Some critics found the album's range uneven; its historical argument is now widely accepted.","Start with Hit 'Em Up Style, then a traditional fiddle tune, and notice that the two do not sound as far apart as you expect. Listen for the bones and beatbox driving the rhythm. Then hear Elizabeth Cotten and Mississippi John Hurt for earlier Black Piedmont traditions that this band set out to honour."],"j-d-crowe-and-the-new-south":["An all-acoustic bluegrass album with a modern feel. J.D. Crowe's banjo leads a quintet with Tony Rice on guitar, Ricky Skaggs on mandolin and fiddle, and Jerry Douglas on dobro. They play bluegrass standards alongside songs by Gordon Lightfoot and folk writers, with smooth harmonies and crisp, clean solos. The tempo is brisk, the sound polished and exact, and the dobro and guitar play melody lines as fluid as jazz.","Crowe, a Kentuckian who had played with Jimmy Martin, had led an electric band in the early 1970s. For this Rounder release in 1975 the group went acoustic, but kept its pop and folk repertoire. The small independent Rounder label, founded by students in Massachusetts, was becoming the home of new bluegrass, and this album became one of its best sellers.","Listen to Tony Rice's guitar solos: he built on Doc Watson's and Clarence White's flatpicking and made the guitar a full lead voice in bluegrass. The cat number, Rounder 0044, is how bluegrass players still refer to the record. Almost every member went on to lead a major band: Rice, Skaggs and Douglas shaped the next thirty years of the music.","It is often called the most influential bluegrass album of the 1970s, bridging Monroe's tradition and the progressive bluegrass that followed. Critics praise its balance of polish and drive: the repertoire drew on Gordon Lightfoot and contemporary folk writers, yet the playing stayed acoustic and disciplined. Players still refer to it simply by its catalogue number, as jazz fans do with Blue Note records, and its line-up is regarded as one of the great bluegrass ensembles.","Listen to one track three times: once for the banjo, once for Tony Rice's guitar, once for Jerry Douglas's dobro. Notice how clean and exact the ensemble is. Then compare with Foggy Mountain Breakdown to hear how the bluegrass band sound had evolved in twenty-five years, and follow any player whose sound you like into his own records."],"jason-isbell-southeastern":["A quiet, mostly acoustic album of songs about sobriety, love and death. Isbell's voice is clear and steady; the arrangements are spare, with fingerpicked guitar, soft fiddle from Amanda Shires, and occasional full-band rock songs. Cover Me Up is a love song about recovery; Elephant describes a friend dying of cancer; Live Oak is a dark ballad about a man hiding his past. The mood is intimate and direct.","Isbell had played in the Alabama band Drive-By Truckers until 2007 and struggled with alcohol. In 2012 he went to rehab and married Shires. Producer Dave Cobb recorded this in Nashville, and it came out on Isbell's own Southeastern Records in 2013. It became his breakthrough, embraced by Americana radio and critics, largely outside the Nashville country establishment.","Listen to Elephant: the song names the illness only once and circles it, refusing melodrama, much as John Prine's Hello in There describes old age through a few household details. Isbell's craft lies in plain, exact particulars rather than big images. Cover Me Up, the opening song, is about fear of relapse as much as romance, and Amanda Shires's fiddle and harmony, heard across the record, tie the album to the marriage it describes.","It won Album of the Year at the Americana Music Honors in 2014 and ranks among the most admired records of the 2010s, largely without help from mainstream country radio. It re-established the confessional singer-songwriter as central to Americana after a decade dominated by bands. Critics often compare Isbell's craft with Prine's and his Southern working-class settings with Springsteen's; some found the record too quiet after his Drive-By Truckers work, a view that has faded.","Start with Cover Me Up, then Elephant, and listen for the small details that carry the weight, a name, a place, a joke. Notice how rarely the band gets loud. Then compare with John Prine's Hello in There, another song that finds grief in ordinary rooms, and with Lucinda Williams's Car Wheels on a Gravel Road."],"sturgill-simpson-metamodern-sounds-in-country-music":["Honky-tonk country with a cosmic twist. Simpson's deep baritone recalls Waylon Jennings; the band plays straight classic country, with pedal steel and twangy guitar, until songs veer into psychedelic sound: backwards guitars, swirling reverb. Turtles All the Way Down muses on religion, mind-altering experiences and love; It Ain't All Flowers dissolves into a tape-loop haze. A cover of the 1980s synth-pop song The Promise turns into a country ballad.","Simpson, a Kentuckian and former Navy sailor and railroad worker, made this on a small budget in Nashville with Dave Cobb and released it on his own label in 2014. It appeared when mainstream country was dominated by polished pop-rock, and it was hailed as a return to outlaw roots. The title nods to Ray Charles's 1962 album Modern Sounds in Country and Western Music.","Listen to It Ain't All Flowers: the backwards guitars and tape effects come from Beatles-era psychedelia, not from country at all. Simpson reportedly took the idea of metamodernism from writing on contemporary philosophy, and the album's mix of sincerity and irony follows it. The Promise, originally by When in Rome, was chosen to show that any song can be a country song.","It won widespread acclaim and made Simpson a leading voice of independent country; his next album was nominated for Album of the Year. Some critics found the outlaw-revival label reductive; he himself resisted it. It remains a touchstone for country musicians working outside Nashville.","Start with Turtles All the Way Down, then The Promise, and listen for the moment classic honky-tonk gives way to swirling psychedelic sound. Notice how short and tight the whole record is. Then hear Willie Nelson's Red Headed Stranger for the outlaw tradition, and the Beatles' Tomorrow Never Knows for the tape tricks."],"ike-turner-rocket-88":["A jump-blues boogie about a new Oldsmobile, sung by saxophonist Jackie Brenston over Ike Turner's band, the Kings of Rhythm. Turner's piano opens with a hammered boogie figure, the saxes riff in unison, and underneath everything sits a fuzzy, buzzing guitar doubling the bass line. It lasts under three minutes and never leaves its groove. The sound you remember is that guitar: dirty, blurred and loud, a noise no respectable record of 1951 was supposed to make.","The band drove up from Clarksdale, Mississippi, to Sam Phillips's Memphis Recording Service in March 1951. Phillips, not yet running his own label, leased the master to Chess in Chicago, which credited it to Jackie Brenston and His Delta Cats, leaving Turner's name off. It became a number-one rhythm-and-blues hit. Car songs were already a jump-blues staple; the Rocket 88 was Oldsmobile's fast new V8, a symbol of postwar Black mobility and money.","The fuzz was an accident. On the drive up, guitarist Willie Kizart's amplifier fell from the car and its speaker cone was torn; Phillips reportedly stuffed it with paper and recorded it anyway. That broken speaker is one of the first distorted guitar sounds on a hit record, the ancestor of every fuzz pedal. Listen, too, for how closely the tune follows earlier boogie car songs such as Jimmy Liggins's Cadillac Boogie; it was a genre piece, made new by its sound.","Its claim to be the first rock and roll record was made by Sam Phillips himself and repeated by historians ever since, though it has rivals going back to the 1940s and many musicians hear it simply as good jump blues. The argument matters less than what followed: the royalties built Phillips's Sun label, Bill Haley covered it within months, and Turner nursed the grievance of the missing credit for the rest of his life.","Play it loud and listen past the vocal to the guitar and bass under the saxophones; that grinding low buzz is the point. Then play Louis Jordan's jump records from the late forties to hear how little the song form changed and how much the sound did. Follow with Bill Haley's cover and Little Richard's Good Golly Miss Molly, which borrowed Turner's piano introduction."],"big-joe-turner-shake-rattle-and-roll":["A twelve-bar blues at an easy, rolling tempo: Big Joe Turner's enormous voice, a riffing saxophone section and a backbeat you could set a clock by. Verse after verse he orders his partner out of bed and into the kitchen, with the title phrase shouted back by the band as a chorus. It is relaxed rather than frantic, which is exactly why it swings; the power comes from the size of the voice, not the speed.","Turner was a Kansas City veteran in his forties when Atlantic recorded this in New York in early 1954. The song was written by the label's arranger Jesse Stone under the name Charles Calhoun. Atlantic's rhythm-and-blues records were beginning to be bought by white teenagers, and within months Bill Haley's Comets had covered it for Decca, with the lyrics cleaned up, and taken it into the pop charts.","Compare the two versions side by side and the story of early rock and roll is in the differences. Turner's lyric is frankly adult and full of blues double meanings; Haley kept the tune and the chorus but swapped out the suggestive verses for harmless ones. Elvis Presley's 1956 version mixes the two. Listen also for the saxophone riff that answers each vocal line: it is the Kansas City big-band habit of riffing, shrunk to a small combo.","It topped the rhythm-and-blues chart and has been in the canon of rock's origins ever since, a standard example of how Black records were covered and sanitised for white audiences. Turner's own career revived on the back of it, and later critics, rediscovering his 1930s boogie-woogie sides, came to hear him as a bridge between swing-era Kansas City and the rock and roll of the fifties.","Start with Turner's original and notice how little he strains; the voice is huge without shouting. Then hear Haley's cover and ask what was lost and what was gained. Turner's earlier Roll 'Em Pete with pianist Pete Johnson shows where the style came from; the Atlantic compilations of his fifties singles are the best way in."],"bill-haley-rock-around-the-clock":["Two minutes and ten seconds of countdown and release: Haley counts the hours from one o'clock onward over a stop-time rhythm, then the band crashes into a twelve-bar shuffle with slapped double bass, drums hitting the backbeat and a honking sax. A guitar solo of rapid-fire repeated notes rips through the middle. The shape is simple, the tempo brisk, the mood cheerful; it is a dance record by a band that had come up playing country and western swing.","Recorded in New York in April 1954 for Decca, with Milt Gabler producing; Gabler had made Louis Jordan's jump-blues hits and aimed to give Haley the same drive. On release it did only modestly. In 1955 the film Blackboard Jungle used it over the opening credits, and teenagers danced in the cinema aisles; it went to number one and began rock and roll's spread across the world, including riots at screenings abroad.","Listen to Danny Cedrone's guitar solo: its breakneck repeated-note run had already appeared, almost note for note, on Haley's earlier record Rock the Joint. Cedrone died within months of the session, before the record became famous. The song itself, by Max Freedman and James Myers, had been recorded first by another band; Haley's slapped bass and drum backbeat are what made it different.","For decades it was spoken of as the record that started rock and roll, a claim later historians complicated by pointing to the Black rhythm-and-blues records that came before it. Haley himself, older, plump and friendly, was quickly displaced by Elvis. But its sales and its film moment made it a genuine turning point in the music business, and it remains one of the best-selling singles ever made.","Listen first to the stop-time opening and the moment the full band hits; that release is the whole trick. Then listen for the slapped bass clicking under the beat. Put it beside Louis Jordan's records, which Gabler also produced, to hear the lineage, and beside Elvis's Sun sides to hear how much wilder the next year sounded."],"elvis-presley-thats-all-right":["Three men and no drummer: Elvis strumming an acoustic guitar and singing, Scotty Moore's electric guitar picking bright country licks, and Bill Black slapping a double bass for rhythm. The song is a blues about a lover who has gone, taken fast and light, with Elvis's voice leaping up into a high, playful register. The room echo, Sam Phillips's slap-back, makes three players sound like a band. It is under two minutes of pure buoyancy.","On 5 July 1954, at Sun Records in Memphis, a session for ballads was going nowhere when, during a break, Presley began clowning on Arthur Crudup's 1946 blues. Phillips asked them to do it again and recorded it. Disc jockey Dewey Phillips played it repeatedly on WHBQ within days. The B-side was Bill Monroe's bluegrass waltz Blue Moon of Kentucky, sped up: a Black blues and a white country song back to back.","The pairing on the two sides of the single is the whole programme of rockabilly: Crudup's Mississippi blues on one side, Monroe's bluegrass on the other, and both played the same way. Listen to how Moore's guitar never plays the blues licks you might expect but country fingerpicking. And listen for the absence of drums; the rhythm is carried entirely by Black's bass and Presley's strumming, which is why it floats.","Local radio listeners reportedly assumed the singer was Black, and some country stations would not play it. It sold modestly outside the South. Its reputation grew backwards: once Presley was famous, this first single was recognised as the moment the synthesis happened, and critics from Greil Marcus onward have treated the Sun recordings as his finest work. Crudup, for his part, received little money from it for years.","Hear Arthur Crudup's original first, then this. Notice how Presley keeps the melody but changes the feeling from resignation to delight. Listen to the whole run of Sun singles in order, ideally in a mono transfer, and pay attention to the way the echo makes the small room sound enormous."],"elvis-presley-mystery-train":["A train song driven by a chugging, locomotive rhythm: Scotty Moore's guitar plays a looping figure, Bill Black slaps the bass, and Elvis sings of a long train that has taken his woman and might bring her back. The song is a blues but the feel is ominous and propulsive, with the echo thickening everything. It builds to a lighter, triumphant final verse, as if the train is now bringing her home.","Junior Parker had recorded the song for Sun in 1953 as a slow, brooding blues. Presley's version was his fifth and last Sun single, released in 1955; by the end of that year Sam Phillips had sold his contract to RCA for about $35,000 to keep the label solvent. The record reached the national country charts, and Elvis was already touring the South as a sensation.","Moore's guitar figure was borrowed from another Parker record, Love My Baby, grafted onto this song. At the very end, listen closely and you can hear Elvis laugh, a moment of release left in the take. Critic Greil Marcus named his 1975 book on American music after this record, reading the train as a symbol of everything that carries American music forward and away.","Many critics consider it the best of the Sun sides, the one where the country and blues sources are most completely melted together. It stands at the end of Elvis's Sun period, and so has often been heard as a farewell to the small, strange world of Memphis before the fame machine. The debt to Junior Parker is now credited and discussed as fully as the performance itself.","Play Junior Parker's 1953 original, then this, and listen to how the tempo and the guitar transform a lament into a charge. Listen for the laugh at the end. Then hear Johnny Cash's train songs from the same studio for a sense of the Sun sound across artists."],"elvis-presley-heartbreak-hotel":["A slow, stark blues about loneliness, sung almost in a whisper and then a moan, with a walking bass and a guitar that stabs a chord at the end of each vocal line and then leaves silence. Floyd Cramer's piano tinkles in the background, and a bluesy guitar solo arrives halfway. The whole thing is drenched in echo, so the voice seems to come from the end of a long empty corridor. It is less than two and a half minutes of gloom made thrilling.","Elvis's first RCA recording, made in Nashville in January 1956 with his Sun trio plus Cramer and Chet Atkins. The song was by Mae Boren Axton and Tommy Durden, reportedly prompted by a newspaper story about a lonely man's suicide. RCA executives worried it sounded nothing like the Sun singles. Television appearances that spring pushed it to number one, and it became his first million-seller.","The echo was not added electronically in the usual way; the engineers reportedly used a hallway in the building as an echo chamber, which is why the voice sounds cavernous. Listen to how much of the record is silence: the band stops at the end of each line, and the tension comes from the gaps. It is, in effect, a torch song played as a blues.","Its success settled RCA's gamble and made Presley a national figure within weeks. In Britain it was a revelation: John Lennon, Keith Richards and others later remembered hearing it on the radio as a turning point in their lives. Some purists preferred the lighter Sun sound, but the record's darkness made rock and roll seem capable of real emotional weight.","Listen to the gaps: count how long the band stays silent after each line. Then compare it to That's All Right to hear how the same trio became something heavier in a bigger studio. Seek a mono release; stereo remixes blur the echo that makes it."],"elvis-presley-hound-dog":["A furious, fast twelve-bar blues that barely lasts two minutes and a quarter: drums like gunfire, Scotty Moore's guitar spraying two wild solos, the Jordanaires chanting behind, and Elvis snapping out insults at a worthless dog of a man. It hits hard from the first beat and never lets up. The drum fills between choruses, D.J. Fontana rattling around the kit like a machine gun, are the sound most people remember.","Jerry Leiber and Mike Stoller wrote the song for Big Mama Thornton, whose growling 1953 version was a rhythm-and-blues number one. Elvis learned a comic rewrite from a Las Vegas lounge act, Freddie Bell and the Bellboys, and performed it on television in 1956 to outrage. He recorded it in New York in July 1956, working through dozens of takes; it was coupled with Don't Be Cruel and sold in millions.","The lyric Elvis sings is not quite Thornton's: the Bellboys had changed it into a song about an actual dog, with a line about rabbits that makes little sense next to her version, which is an adult woman telling off a freeloading man. Leiber reportedly disliked Elvis's take for exactly that. Listen, too, to the way the Jordanaires' clapping and chanting sits almost buried; it is a gospel quartet habit turned secular.","It was a huge double-sided hit and became a battleground: his hip-swinging television performances of it drew condemnation from critics and clergy, and Steve Allen had him sing it in a tuxedo to a basset hound. Since then the debate has shifted to Big Mama Thornton, whose original is now often preferred and whose small reward from the song has become a standard example of how the business treated Black women artists.","Hear Big Mama Thornton's 1953 original first, a slow, menacing blues with Johnny Otis's band, and then this. Notice the tempo, the drums and the change of meaning. Listen to the guitar solos for how raw and clumsy they are allowed to be; that roughness is part of the excitement."],"chuck-berry-maybellene":["A car chase in two and a half minutes: the singer in a V8 Ford chasing an unfaithful girlfriend in a Cadillac over the hills, reported like a sports commentary. The beat is a fast country two-step turned to rhythm and blues, Johnnie Johnson's piano rolls underneath, and Berry's guitar solo in the middle is jagged and bright. His diction is crisp and precise; every word lands, which was new in a genre used to shouted blues.","Berry came to Chicago in May 1955 carrying a tape; Muddy Waters sent him to Leonard Chess, who was more interested in his country-flavoured song than his blues. Maybellene was adapted from Ida Red, a western-swing tune made famous by Bob Wills. Disc jockey Alan Freed was given a co-writing credit, a common payola arrangement, and plugged it heavily. It went to number one on the rhythm-and-blues chart and into the pop top ten.","Under the rock and roll surface is a hillbilly fiddle tune: listen to the song's structure and the chorus melody and you can hear Ida Red. That country root is why the record sounded so strange on Black radio and so familiar on white. Freed's name in the credits, and that of another associate, took a share of Berry's royalties for decades.","It was an instant national hit and established Chess as a rock and roll label as well as a blues one. Historians now often put it forward as the true beginning of rock and roll, because it fused country and rhythm and blues so naturally in the hands of a Black writer. Berry's fight to recover his credit became an early chapter in rock's long argument over authorship and exploitation.","Listen to it as a story: the chase, the rain, the engine, the catch. Then find Bob Wills's Ida Red to hear the tune's country origin. Follow with Johnny B. Goode to hear how much Berry's guitar sound developed in three years."],"chuck-berry-roll-over-beethoven":["A manifesto disguised as a party song. It opens with Berry's ringing guitar introduction, then his voice asks a disc jockey for rhythm and blues and tells the great classical composers to make room. The band is a tight quartet with Johnnie Johnson's piano filling every gap. The tone is joking rather than angry, but the message is clear: a new music has arrived, and it has a beat.","Released by Chess in 1956, when rock and roll was being attacked by parents, preachers and the press. Berry reportedly wrote it with his sister Lucy in mind, who monopolised the family piano with classical pieces when he wanted to play rhythm and blues. Its claim that the old music should step aside matched a moment when radio was fragmenting and teenagers were buying records for themselves.","Berry names Tchaikovsky as well as Beethoven, telling the older composer to pass the news along, which gives the song its comic twist: it is written as a message to be delivered. Listen for the way the introduction's double-string guitar figure recurs as the solo; the whole song is built on that riff. The Beatles' 1963 cover, sung by George Harrison, turned it into a British invasion standard.","Critics have always read it as a statement of rock and roll's cultural challenge to high art. Its tone is now heard as playful rather than hostile; Berry admired classical music, and the song mocks snobbery more than Beethoven. It entered the rock canon through its covers, especially the Beatles', and remained a concert staple for Berry for sixty years.","Hear it next to the opening of Beethoven's Fifth, the music Berry is joking about. Then listen to the Beatles' version for how a British band hears it seven years later. Pay attention to the drive of Johnson's piano, which does as much as the guitar to push the song forward."],"chuck-berry-school-days":["A day at an American high school told in sharp, crowded verses: the classroom, the teachers, the lunchroom, then the release of the final bell and a run down to the jukebox. Each sung line is answered by a guitar phrase, voice and instrument trading like two people in conversation. The final verse turns from school to a hymn of praise for rock and roll itself. It is loose, fast and witty, a miniature film of teenage life.","Released in 1957, the year Berry toured the country on package shows and appeared in rock and roll films. It became one of his biggest hits, topping the rhythm-and-blues chart. Berry was then thirty, far older than the teenagers he wrote about; he observed them from outside, with a novelist's eye for detail. American high schools were newly desegregating, and the song's schoolroom is pointedly undescribed by race.","The call-and-response between voice and guitar comes from the blues and from gospel, where a preacher and congregation answer each other. Berry turns it into comedy: the guitar seems to comment on each line. Listen for the closing tribute to rock and roll, which became a slogan in its own right and gave its title to a 1987 documentary about Berry.","It has always been praised as one of the most observant of Berry's teenage songs, a model for the narrative songwriting of the Beach Boys and the Beatles. Some critics find the formula wearing thin by this point in his run, but the lyric's detail and the guitar dialogue keep it fresh, and its closing lines are among the most quoted in rock.","Listen to it as a conversation: hear each vocal line, then wait for the guitar's answer. Follow the day from morning to the jukebox. Then hear Sweet Little Sixteen and Johnny B. Goode, recorded soon after, to follow Berry's portrait gallery of American youth."],"chuck-berry-johnny-b-goode":["The most famous guitar introduction in rock: a burst of ringing double-string phrases over a stop, then the band falls in. The song tells of a poor country boy near New Orleans who can play the guitar like ringing a bell and dreams of seeing his name in lights. The chorus is a crowd urging him on. It is the rock and roll success story in two minutes and forty seconds, and the guitar tells it as much as the words.","Recorded in Chicago for Chess and released in 1958, at the height of Berry's run. It is largely autobiographical, and named partly after Goode Avenue in St. Louis, where Berry grew up, and partly after his pianist Johnnie Johnson. Berry later said he changed the hero's race in the lyric so that radio would play it, a small act of self-censorship that speaks to its moment.","The opening guitar figure is not wholly original: it closely follows the introduction Carl Hogan played on Louis Jordan's 1946 jump-blues record Ain't That Just Like a Woman, which Berry acknowledged. The debt runs straight from jump blues to rock. In 1977 NASA placed the song on the Voyager Golden Record, making it the only rock and roll recording to have left the solar system.","It was a pop and rhythm-and-blues hit and has since been named repeatedly by critics and musicians as the defining rock and roll record. Virtually every guitarist of the sixties learned the introduction. Debate now focuses less on its greatness than on Berry's own paradox: a Black artist writing the archetypal American rock success story, then watching others become far richer playing it.","Listen to the introduction a few times on its own, then hear Louis Jordan's Ain't That Just Like a Woman to catch the source. Notice how the stop-time verses build tension. Then hear it played by others, from the Beatles' circle to Jimi Hendrix, to see how it became a common language."],"little-richard-tutti-frutti":["It begins with a shouted string of nonsense syllables, a drummer's rhythm put into the mouth, and then the band slams in. Little Richard pounds the piano in hard, even eighth notes, the saxophones honk, Earl Palmer's drums drive the beat, and every verse is punctuated by whoops and a high falsetto shriek. The words are about two girls, but the words hardly matter. It lasts two and a half minutes and feels like being shoved.","Recorded in September 1955 at Cosimo Matassa's J&M studio in New Orleans, with producer Bumps Blackwell and a band of the city's best session players. The session was going badly until, in a break, Richard played a bawdy song from his club act; Blackwell had songwriter Dorothy LaBostrie clean up the words on the spot. Released by Specialty, it became a rhythm-and-blues hit, and Pat Boone's tame cover sold even more.","The famous opening shout was, by Richard's own account, a vocal imitation of a drum pattern he had been singing to himself while washing dishes in Macon. Listen to the piano: those relentless, even eighth notes replaced the lilting shuffle of older rhythm and blues and pointed toward the straight rock beat of the sixties. The original club lyric was sexually explicit; LaBostrie's version kept only the tune and the excitement.","It was received as a novelty by many adults and as a shock by others; its gender-blurring, gospel-shouting performer frightened the respectable. Since then its standing has only risen: it was among the first records added to the Library of Congress's National Recording Registry in its category, and critics often call it the moment rock and roll found its voice. The Boone cover is now remembered mostly as a cautionary tale.","Play it loud and follow the piano's even hammer rather than the voice. Then play Pat Boone's version for the contrast. After that, hear a gospel shouter like Marion Williams, whom Richard admired, to understand where the whoops come from."],"little-richard-long-tall-sally":["A breakneck twelve-bar about a gossipy uncle, a woman who knows how to have a good time, and a lot of ducking back in the alley to avoid being seen. It is even faster and harder than Tutti Frutti: the piano hammers, the saxophones blast short riffs, Lee Allen tears off a tenor solo, and Richard's voice leaps into falsetto at the end of lines. It lasts barely two minutes.","Recorded for Specialty in New Orleans and released in 1956. Bumps Blackwell later said they deliberately made the words tumble out too fast for a white crooner to copy, after Pat Boone's cover of Tutti Frutti had outsold the original. Boone covered it anyway. The song was credited to Richard, Blackwell and Enotris Johnson, a woman who reportedly brought in the idea for a song about a relative.","Listen to the pace of the lyric: whole phrases are compressed into a breath, a deliberate barrier to imitation. The song's hero is a man hiding from his wife, a comic scenario borrowed from vaudeville and blues. Richard's falsetto whoop at the end of lines was studied by Paul McCartney, whose version with the Beatles in 1964 copies it almost exactly.","It topped the rhythm-and-blues chart and made the pop top ten, outselling Boone's version this time. It has since become one of the most covered rock and roll songs; the Beatles made it the closer of many shows. Critics regard it as Richard at his most concentrated, the pure distilled essence of his Specialty sound.","Try to follow the words at speed; then give up and ride the piano. Compare McCartney's version with the Beatles to hear how closely a young Liverpool singer copied a Georgia shouter. Then hear the rest of the Specialty sides collected on Here's Little Richard."],"little-richard-heres-little-richard":["Twelve tracks, most under two and a half minutes, collecting the singles that made Little Richard's name: Tutti Frutti, Long Tall Sally, Rip It Up, Ready Teddy, Slippin' and Slidin', Jenny, Jenny and others. Heard together they have astonishing consistency: the same pounding piano, honking saxes and New Orleans rhythm section, and the voice switching between shouts, groans and a high gospel whoop. A few slower blues give breathing space, but the pace rarely drops.","Released by Specialty in 1957, Richard's debut album was assembled from recordings made in New Orleans and Los Angeles over the previous two years. In the fifties the album was an afterthought to singles in rock and roll; this one was a collection of hits for fans who wanted them in one place. Within months of its release, Richard announced on an Australian tour that he was quitting rock and roll for the ministry.","Listen to the backing band, much of it the same New Orleans players who backed Fats Domino and others at Cosimo Matassa's studio. Drummer Earl Palmer's playing across these tracks is a document of the shift from shuffle rhythm to the straight rock beat. Richard's gospel upbringing is everywhere: the moans and whoops come from Pentecostal worship, turned to profane use he later regretted.","It became one of the most celebrated albums of fifties rock, regularly placed in critics' lists of the greatest ever made. Its reputation rests on the songs rather than any album concept. Richard himself, torn between church and stage, spent much of his life alternately disowning and reclaiming these records. Today he is honoured as a founder who received too little credit and pay in his prime.","Play it straight through and let the sameness become a force. Listen for the moments where the saxophone solo takes over and then Richard's voice crashes back. Pair it with Fats Domino's hits, cut in the same studio, to hear how different a singer can make the same band sound."],"fats-domino-the-fat-man":["A rolling New Orleans boogie: Domino's left hand walks the bass while his right hammers repeated chords, the band riffs behind him, and he sings a cheerful self-portrait about his size and his nickname. In the middle he hums a wordless solo that imitates a muted trumpet. It is relaxed and bouncy rather than wild. The piano is the whole engine; everything else fills in around it.","Recorded at Cosimo Matassa's J&M studio in New Orleans in December 1949 and released by Imperial early in 1950. It was the first product of Domino's partnership with trumpeter and bandleader Dave Bartholomew, and it reportedly sold a million copies over the following years. It was adapted from Junker Blues, a song about addiction associated with Champion Jack Dupree, rewritten as a harmless piece of self-mockery.","Listen to the wordless vocal break: Domino imitates a trumpet with his voice, a trick from the New Orleans street tradition. The drumming, possibly by Earl Palmer, uses a heavy backbeat that would become standard in rock and roll. The song's model, Junker Blues, had a darker subject, mentioned only in passing here, which Bartholomew replaced with a comic portrait.","Its commercial success made Imperial and Domino and has been cited as one of several claimants to the first rock and roll record, years before the term was current. Critics praise it now as the purest example of New Orleans rhythm and blues and the gentle, piano-led strain of early rock that ran parallel to the harder Memphis and Chicago sounds.","Follow the left hand of the piano: it is a boogie-woogie bass line that the whole band follows. Then listen for the humming solo. Hear Jelly Roll Morton or James P. Johnson for the long New Orleans and stride piano lineage behind it, and then Domino's later hits for the smoother sound."],"fats-domino-aint-that-a-shame":["A lament about a lover who has left, sung with such warmth and good humour that it hardly sounds sad. It opens with Domino's voice alone on the title line, then the band falls in with a steady backbeat. Domino's piano triplets roll under the whole song, and a tenor saxophone takes a brief solo. At under two and a half minutes, it is a model of economy: two verses, a chorus and a lilt.","Written by Domino and Dave Bartholomew, it was released by Imperial in 1955, just as rock and roll was being noticed by white audiences. It became Domino's first big crossover pop hit. Pat Boone's cover went to number one on the pop chart, a notorious example of white artists covering Black records; Domino's original also sold strongly, and his audience kept widening.","John Lennon said this was the first song he learned to play, on a banjo his mother taught him. Listen for the piano triplets under a straight backbeat; that combination is a New Orleans hallmark. Boone reportedly wanted to correct the grammar of the title, which tells you what the cover versions were designed to do.","It was received as an irresistible pop record and has remained a standard, covered by Cheap Trick and many others. The story of the Boone cover has come to stand for a whole period of the business. Domino's own version has long since outlasted Boone's in critical esteem, and his easygoing style is increasingly recognised as a crucial ingredient of early rock.","Listen for the triplets in the piano: count three in each beat. Hear Pat Boone's cover once, then return to Domino to feel the difference. Follow with Blueberry Hill to hear his style applied to an older pop standard."],"fats-domino-blueberry-hill":["A 1940 Tin Pan Alley song about a lost romance, remade as a slow, rolling New Orleans ballad. The piano opens with rippling triplets, Domino sings in his warm, relaxed voice with its Creole vowels, and the band sways behind him. There is no hurry and no display, just a steady rock and a melody sung with affection. It is the sound of rock and roll at its most tender.","The song had been recorded by Glenn Miller's band and sung by Gene Autry in a 1941 film. Domino recorded it in Los Angeles in 1956, and Imperial released it that autumn; it became his biggest pop hit. He was touring constantly and appearing in rock and roll films, a star of the new music who was also, with his standards, reaching older listeners.","Accounts say Domino could not get through a complete take without fumbling the words, and the released record was edited together from several; listen for the join if you can find it. His Creole accent colours the vowels in a way that makes a New York song sound like Louisiana. The piano triplets under a slow beat became a New Orleans signature later heard in soul ballads.","It reached number two on the pop chart and number one in rhythm and blues and is his most enduring record. Critics have praised the way it bridges generations, a prewar pop song made new. It showed that rock and roll could absorb the older repertoire rather than simply opposing it, a lesson Elvis and the Beatles also took.","Listen to the piano introduction and the slow, swaying beat, then hear Glenn Miller's version from 1940 to see what Domino changed. Follow with Otis Redding's ballads to hear the New Orleans triplet feel travel into soul."],"bo-diddley-bo-diddley":["One chord and one rhythm for two and a half minutes. Bo Diddley's guitar, thick with tremolo, pounds out a syncopated pattern that everyone knows even if they do not know its name, while Jerome Green's maracas and a tom-tom drum lock into it. The lyric is a string of playground boasts about a mockingbird and a diamond ring. There is no chord change, no melody to speak of; the rhythm is the song.","Recorded in Chicago in 1955 and released on Checker, a Chess subsidiary, with I'm a Man on the flip side. It topped the rhythm-and-blues chart. Later that year Diddley appeared on Ed Sullivan's television show; asked to sing a popular country hit, he played his own song instead, and was reportedly banned from the programme. The Chess label was simultaneously launching Chuck Berry.","The beat, often written out as a shave-and-a-haircut pattern, has roots in the African-American hambone body-slapping tradition and in older Caribbean and African rhythms. Listen to the guitar: Diddley built his own tremolo effect, and the rhythm guitar is used as a drum. The lyric is adapted from the children's lullaby Hush Little Baby, recast as a swaggering boast.","It was a hit and a puzzle for critics who expected songs to change chords. Over time its influence became obvious: the Bo Diddley beat turned up in Buddy Holly, the Rolling Stones, the Who, the Strangeloves and countless others. Diddley felt, with justice, that his contribution was undervalued and underpaid, a complaint now widely accepted.","Clap along and find the pattern. Then listen to how the maracas and the guitar interlock. Follow with Buddy Holly's Not Fade Away and the Rolling Stones' version of it to hear the beat travel; then hear I'm a Man, the B-side, which Muddy Waters answered with his own record."],"bo-diddley-who-do-you-love":["A menacing boast in a voodoo register: Bo Diddley lists the uncanny credentials of a suitor who wears a snake for a necktie and lives in a house made of rattlesnake hide, and asks the woman whom she will choose. The band, with a sharp lead guitar from Jody Williams, drives hard over a single chord. It is faster and harder than his first record, with a raw edge that feels close to punk.","Released on Checker in 1956. Diddley's Chicago recordings drew on the same Southern migrant culture as Muddy Waters's; the hoodoo imagery of charms and tricks was familiar from blues lyrics going back decades. Rock and roll was at its commercial peak, and Chess used Diddley's distinctive style to compete with the flood of rockabilly.","Listen for Jody Williams's lead guitar, whose stinging lines influenced later Chicago players. The imagery is not random: snake skins, graveyards and skulls are hoodoo conjurer images from Southern folk magic, the same world as Muddy Waters's mojo songs. The song's second life came through covers: Ronnie Hawkins's 1963 version with a young band later known as The Band, and a sprawling psychedelic version by Quicksilver Messenger Service.","It was not a large hit on release but became one of Diddley's most covered songs, and critics praise it as one of the rawest records of the fifties. Its lyric's supernatural boasting links it to both blues tradition and to the swagger of later rock and hip-hop. Its reputation grew steadily through the sixties revival.","Listen for the lead guitar's stabs between vocal lines, then hear Ronnie Hawkins's version for how a Canadian bar band pushed it harder still. Pair with Muddy Waters's mojo songs to hear the shared folk-magic world."],"carl-perkins-blue-suede-shoes":["A stop-time count-off, a twelve-bar blues, and a cheerful warning: do whatever you like to me, but leave my shoes alone. Perkins's guitar solos are clean, bright country licks with blues bends, the bass slaps, and the whole band sits in Sam Phillips's slap-back echo. It is loose and friendly, the sound of a Tennessee dance band discovering rhythm and blues.","Perkins wrote it in late 1955, reportedly after watching a dancer at a gig fuss over his shoes; Johnny Cash is also credited with suggesting the idea. Sun released it on New Year's Day 1956, and it became the label's first million-seller, crossing to the pop, country and rhythm-and-blues charts. In March, on the way to a television appearance, Perkins was badly injured in a car crash, and Elvis's cover took over.","Listen to the stop-time opening: the band hits a chord, falls silent, and Perkins counts in the verse. That arrangement is borrowed from blues and jump records. His guitar style, country fingerpicking with bluesy string bends, became the model for George Harrison, who learned many of Perkins's songs. The Beatles recorded three of them.","It was a landmark hit for Sun and made Perkins briefly a star. Critics have always regarded it as a quintessential rockabilly record and Perkins as one of the most gifted of the Sun artists; his bad luck with the car crash became part of the story. Elvis's version is better known, but many prefer Perkins's, lighter and more swinging.","Hear Perkins's original first, then Elvis's 1956 RCA version, and notice how Elvis pushes the tempo and the drama. Listen to the guitar solos for the mix of country and blues. Follow with Johnny Cash's Sun records and Jerry Lee Lewis's to hear the whole label."],"jerry-lee-lewis-whole-lotta-shakin-goin-on":["A pounding boogie where Jerry Lee Lewis sings, talks, growls and hammers the piano with a left hand like a pile-driver. The band is spare: guitar and drums. Halfway through, he drops to a near-whisper, coaxing the listener to shake, before crashing back. The piano runs up and down the whole keyboard in glissandos. It sounds improvised because largely it was.","The song had been recorded by Big Maybelle in 1955. Lewis played it in clubs and reportedly recorded it at Sun in a single take in 1957. Radio stations hesitated over its suggestiveness, but a television appearance on Steve Allen's show that summer, where Lewis kicked away his piano stool, sent it to the top of the country and rhythm-and-blues charts and near the top of pop.","Listen to the spoken section: Lewis drops the tempo of his delivery while the band keeps going, a preacher's technique from his Pentecostal upbringing, the same one his cousin Jimmy Swaggart used in the pulpit. His left hand plays a boogie bass line with an almost mechanical steadiness while the right hand plays anything it likes.","It was denounced by some as indecent and loved by teenagers; its commercial success made Lewis a star. Critics now rank it among the most exciting of all rock and roll records and treat Lewis's piano style as a founding element of rock keyboard playing. Its reputation survived the scandal that wrecked his career a year later.","Follow the left hand all the way through: it never stops. Then listen for the spoken breakdown and the moment the full sound returns. Hear Big Maybelle's original, then Little Richard's piano records to compare two different kinds of wildness."],"jerry-lee-lewis-great-balls-of-fire":["Under two minutes of piano fury: Lewis hammers chords with both hands, sings about love that shakes his nerves and rattles his brain, and sweeps his hand down the keyboard in glissandos. The drums keep a strict, hard backbeat. It is tight and fast, with none of the loose talking of his previous hit. The sound of the record is almost all piano and voice.","Written by Otis Blackwell and Jack Hammer and recorded at Sun in late 1957. Lewis sang it in the film Jamboree, and it became his biggest international hit. Months later, on a British tour, the press discovered he had married his thirteen-year-old cousin, Myra Gale Brown; the tour collapsed and his American pop career with it.","At the session, a tape survives of Lewis arguing with Sam Phillips about whether rock and roll was the devil's music, Lewis insisting it was sinful and he could not save souls singing it. Knowing this, listen to the title phrase, which comes from a biblical image of fire, and hear a Pentecostal preacher's son singing against his own conscience.","It was a huge hit and is his signature song; Lewis's fall made it a symbol of rock and roll's first collapse, when its stars died, were jailed, drafted or disgraced. Critics treat it as the peak of his style. The 1989 biopic of the same title revived interest; historians now dwell on the recorded argument as a key document of rock's religious tensions.","Listen for the glissandos and the hard, double-handed chords. Then find the recorded argument between Lewis and Phillips to hear the struggle behind the song. Follow with Little Richard's records, and his decision to quit for the church, for the same tension in another life."],"buddy-holly-thatll-be-the-day":["A mid-tempo rock song about a lover's threat to leave, answered with defiant disbelief. It opens with a sliding guitar phrase, then Holly's voice, full of hiccups and swoops, carries a catchy verse and chorus, with backing voices answering. Jerry Allison's drums are busy and inventive. At two and a quarter minutes, it is compact and perfectly balanced, a small-group rock record where every part is a hook.","The title came from a line John Wayne repeats in the 1956 film The Searchers. Holly had recorded the song in Nashville for Decca in 1956, but the label shelved it. In February 1957 he recut it at Norman Petty's studio in Clovis, New Mexico, with the Crickets; Brunswick released it under the band's name, and it went to number one in the United States and Britain.","The first recording the teenage Quarrymen, John Lennon's band before the Beatles, ever made in 1958 was a version of this song. Listen to the guitar solo, which is less a display than a restatement of the melody, and to Allison's drums, far more varied than most rock drumming of the period. Clovis gave them unlimited studio time, which let them polish.","It made Holly and the Crickets stars overnight and established a model of the self-contained band that wrote and played its own material. Critics now see it as the first of a small number of records that shaped the sixties British bands more than any others. Holly's early death, less than two years later, sealed its place in rock's memory.","Listen for the hiccup in Holly's voice and the busy drums. Then hear the Quarrymen's 1958 home recording to understand what young British musicians took from it. Follow with Peggy Sue and Not Fade Away to hear how Holly's small band kept reinventing itself."],"buddy-holly-peggy-sue":["A love song built on a drumbeat: Jerry Allison plays continuous rolling paradiddles on the snare, a hypnotic rattle under the whole song. Holly sings the girl's name over and over, stretching and hiccuping it, and his guitar strums bright chords, then slams out a solo of loud, ringing strums rather than single notes. It is simple, obsessive and joyful.","Recorded at Norman Petty's Clovis studio in 1957 and released under Holly's own name on Coral, while the Crickets' records appeared on Brunswick, a label arrangement that let the same band have two careers. The song was originally called Cindy Lou; Allison asked Holly to rename it after his girlfriend, Peggy Sue Gerron. It became a top-three pop hit.","Allison reportedly practised the drum pattern in a separate room from the band so the echo and balance would work. Listen to the guitar solo: at the moment it begins, the tone suddenly becomes louder and brighter, reportedly because a bandmate reached over and flipped the pickup switch on Holly's guitar mid-performance. Petty's echo on the drums gives them their hypnotic rattle.","It was a major hit and has remained one of Holly's best-loved records. Critics praise its restraint and ingenuity: few notes, big effect. Its drum part is one of the most celebrated in rock and roll, and Holly's name-song style influenced generations of songwriters. A sequel, Peggy Sue Got Married, followed after his death.","Listen to the drums alone for the first time through. Then listen for the switch in guitar tone at the solo. Follow with the Beatles' early records to hear how much they learned about strummed-chord solos and close drum work."],"buddy-holly-not-fade-away":["A love song built on the Bo Diddley beat, with percussion reportedly played on a cardboard box. Holly's voice promises that his love will not fade, the Crickets answer in chorus, and the guitar strums the syncopated pattern with almost nothing else. The spareness is the point: it is a rhythm, a vow and some handclaps, and it grips.","Recorded at Clovis in 1957 and released as the B-side of the Crickets' Oh, Boy!. The song was credited to Charles Hardin, Holly's given names, and producer Norman Petty, who took a share of many Holly compositions. Holly borrowed the rhythm from Bo Diddley, whose records were being played across the country, and gave it a lighter, Texan feel.","Listen to the percussion: drummer Jerry Allison is said to have played on a cardboard box rather than a drum kit, which gives the dry, papery thump. The song's afterlife is large: the Rolling Stones made it their first big British hit in 1964, turning it into a harmonica stomp, and the Grateful Dead played it hundreds of times in concert.","As a B-side it was not a hit in 1957; its importance came through its covers, which made it a rock standard. Critics now hear it as a bridge between Bo Diddley's Chicago rhythm and the British bands of the next decade, and as one of Holly's most direct and unadorned records.","Clap the Bo Diddley rhythm along with it, then hear Bo Diddley's own record. Then listen to the Rolling Stones' 1964 version and hear how the same song became something rougher in London."],"everly-brothers-bye-bye-love":["Two voices in close harmony, singing a breakup song so bright and brisk that the sadness barely registers. It opens with a strummed acoustic guitar figure, then Don and Phil Everly sing together, one voice a third above the other, bending together on each word. The rhythm is a country shuffle with a rock and roll backbeat. The sound you remember is the harmony: two brothers who sound like one voice split in two.","Written by Felice and Boudleaux Bryant, a Nashville husband-and-wife team, the song had reportedly been turned down by dozens of acts before the Everlys recorded it for Cadence in 1957. It went to number two on the pop chart and topped the country chart. The brothers had grown up singing on their family's radio show in Iowa and Tennessee.","Listen to the opening guitar: Don Everly used an open tuning and a strum pattern he learned from Bo Diddley's records, a rhythm-and-blues trick hidden under a country sound. The close harmony descends from the Appalachian brother duets, the Louvin Brothers and the Delmore Brothers, who themselves came from gospel and country traditions.","It was a huge crossover hit and established the Everlys as stars. Critics recognise their harmony as the source of the Beatles', Simon and Garfunkel's and the Byrds' vocal sound; Paul McCartney and Art Garfunkel both named them as models. The song remains a standard example of how country tradition entered rock and roll.","Listen to the harmony alone: try to follow one voice and then the other. Then hear the Carter Family's close harmonies to catch the country source, and the Beatles' Please Please Me for how the Everly sound was reused."],"everly-brothers-all-i-have-to-do-is-dream":["A slow, dreamy ballad in which the two voices sing about a love that exists only in sleep. Chet Atkins's electric guitar shimmers with tremolo, a single ringing chord pulsing in the background, and the Everlys' harmony is at its most intimate. The song moves slowly and gently, with a rising bridge. It is the sound of late-fifties teenage longing, delicate rather than wild.","Written by Boudleaux Bryant and recorded in Nashville in 1958 for Cadence, with some of the city's best session musicians. It became one of the few records to top the pop, country and rhythm-and-blues charts at once, and was a number one in Britain. It was part of the softening of rock and roll in 1958, as its wildest stars faded.","Listen to the guitar: Chet Atkins's tremolo figure is the opening sound and the recurring glue, a single chord shimmering like water. The brothers' harmony is so close that on some syllables the two voices seem to merge into one tone, a quality that close-harmony singers call blend. The subject, dreaming of love rather than having it, links to a long line of pop night songs.","It was an enormous hit and one of the most successful records of the Everlys' career. Critics praise it as the finest example of their harmony and of the Nashville studio sound of the late fifties. It has been covered many times, and its dreamy mood influenced the Beach Boys and later pop balladry.","Listen for the tremolo guitar in the background, then focus on how the two voices merge. Compare with Bye Bye Love for the contrast of mood. Then hear Simon and Garfunkel's close harmonies to trace the influence."],"wanda-jackson-lets-have-a-party":["A frantic party song, barely two minutes long, in which Wanda Jackson's raspy, growling voice cuts through a hard-driving rockabilly band with pounding piano and slashing guitar. The lyric is a simple invitation to dance and celebrate. What you hear is the voice: rough, snarling and joyful, a female counterpart to Little Richard and Elvis, with none of the politeness expected of women singers in 1958.","The song was written by Jessie Mae Robinson and first sung by Elvis Presley in his 1957 film Loving You. Jackson recorded it for Capitol in 1958, and it appeared on her album; in 1960 a disc jockey began playing the album track, and Capitol released it as a single, making it her first pop hit. She had toured with Elvis, who encouraged her to sing rock and roll.","Listen to the way Jackson growls on certain words, a vocal rasp she developed to match the male rockabilly singers; it came from her country training, where she had been a yodelling honky-tonk singer. The record is also a reminder that the rockabilly label Capitol found in Gene Vincent and in Jackson was run by the same producer, Ken Nelson.","It was a modest pop hit in 1960 but gave Jackson her enduring reputation as the queen of rockabilly. Critics and punk musicians rediscovered her in later decades; she was inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame in 2009, and Jack White produced an album for her in 2011. Her importance as a pioneer woman in rock is now secure.","Listen to Elvis's version in Loving You first, then Jackson's, and notice how much harder she sings. Follow with her Fujiyama Mama and then with Little Richard, whose wildness she matched."],"gene-vincent-be-bop-a-lula":["A breathy, echo-soaked rockabilly song in which Gene Vincent almost whispers the nonsense title and a loving description of his girl, then yelps. The Blue Caps back him with a slapping double bass, crisp drums and Cliff Gallup's lead guitar, which bursts out in two hot solos. The echo makes Vincent's voice seem to float, and the contrast between hush and scream is the song's excitement.","Recorded in Nashville in May 1956 for Capitol, whose producer Ken Nelson hoped Vincent would rival Elvis; the song was co-credited to his manager. It became a top-ten hit. Vincent had a badly injured leg from a motorcycle accident and wore a brace. His career faded in the United States, but in Britain and France he remained a star into the sixties.","Listen to Cliff Gallup's guitar solos: fast, clean and full of unexpected jazz-influenced runs, they inspired later guitarists including Jeff Beck, who recorded an album of Gallup's parts. Also listen for drummer Dickie Harrell's scream behind the vocal, a wild moment in a controlled record. The echo on Vincent's voice was part of the record's appeal.","It was a hit and remains Vincent's signature. Critics praise Gallup's playing and the record's moody sound; Vincent is often seen as rock and roll's first outsider, a model for the leather-clad rebel image that British rockers and punks took up. John Lennon was a fan.","Listen for the contrast between the whisper and the yelp, then focus on Gallup's guitar. Then hear Eddie Cochran, Vincent's friend and touring partner, and Jeff Beck's tribute to Gallup's playing."],"eddie-cochran-summertime-blues":["A teenage complaint in two minutes: a boy wants to go out, but his boss, his parents and even his congressman stand in the way. Cochran strums heavy, chunky chords on the guitar, handclaps punctuate the rhythm, and between vocal lines a deep voice plays the authority figure who says no. It is funny, angry and catchy, with a stop-and-start structure that throws the complaints into relief.","Written by Cochran and his manager Jerry Capehart and recorded in Hollywood in 1958 for Liberty. Cochran was a studio experimenter who overdubbed his own guitar and bass parts. The song became a top-ten hit. Less than two years later, on a British tour with Gene Vincent in April 1960, Cochran was killed in a taxi crash in Wiltshire, aged 21.","Listen to the deep voice answering each complaint: it is Cochran himself, lowering his voice to play the boss, parent and politician. The guitar sound, heavy strummed chords rather than a lead line, was unusual and anticipated the power chords of sixties rock. The Who's thunderous version on Live at Leeds and Blue Cheer's distorted one turned it into a hard-rock anthem.","It was received as a novelty hit and has become one of the most enduring songs of teenage protest in rock and roll. Critics see Cochran's early death as a great loss to rock; his studio methods and chord-based guitar playing link the fifties to the sixties. It has been covered by the Who, Blue Cheer, the Clash and many others.","Listen for the deep answering voice and the handclaps. Then hear the Who's version to feel how the song's anger became volume. Pair it with Gershwin's Summertime for a completely different idea of the season."],"the-beatles-rubber-soul":["Fourteen songs in thirty-six minutes, and the first Beatles album with no filler covers. The sound is acoustic guitars, close three-part harmony and a new density of keyboards: a harmonium under 'We Can Work It Out', a fuzz bass on 'Think for Yourself', Harrison's sitar threading through 'Norwegian Wood'. Ringo Starr's drums sit back in the mix. You will know it by the elastic vocal blend of 'Nowhere Man', three voices in bare octaves before the band enters.","Recorded at EMI's Abbey Road studios in about four weeks in the autumn of 1965, squeezed between tours and a Christmas deadline. Bob Dylan had shown the group that pop lyrics could carry doubt and dislike, and the Byrds had shown that folk chords could ring through electric twelve-strings. Lennon and McCartney were twenty-five and twenty-three, newly wealthy, newly bored of screaming. The title is a joke about plastic soul, a phrase they picked up in America.","The guitar solo in 'In My Life' is George Martin playing a piano part at half speed and doubling the tape speed on playback, which is why it sounds like a harpsichord no harpsichord could manage. Listen also to 'Norwegian Wood': the sitar doubles the vocal line exactly rather than improvising around it, a Western tune wearing an Indian instrument rather than an Indian raga.","Reviewers at the time heard a good pop record; the reappraisal came quickly. Brian Wilson has said repeatedly that an album with no weak track sent him to make Pet Sounds, and that record sent the Beatles back to the studio for Revolver. The American Capitol version, which drops songs and adds two from Help!, is a different and folkier record; the British running order is the one to hear.","Start with 'Norwegian Wood' and 'In My Life', then play side two straight through for the shift from the sly to the tender. The 2009 stereo remaster is clean, but the 1965 mono mix is the one the band approved and the vocals sit harder in it. Follow with Pet Sounds and hear the argument continue."],"the-beatles-revolver":["Fourteen songs in thirty-five minutes, each a different sound world. 'Eleanor Rigby' is scored for eight string players with no Beatles instruments at all; 'Love You To' opens on solo sitar and tabla; 'Got to Get You into My Life' brings in brass; 'Tomorrow Never Knows' rides tape loops over one chord. Harrison gets three songs, his largest share. The gesture you will recognise is the hard, dry, compressed guitar of 'Taxman'.","Recorded at Abbey Road across the spring of 1966 with George Martin producing and the twenty-year-old Geoff Emerick newly promoted to engineer. The four-track machines had not changed but the working method had: bouncing tracks, close-miking drums against EMI rules, running tape backwards, and the artificial double-tracking device built in-house by Ken Townsend. None of it could be reproduced on stage, and the band stopped touring that August.","The count-in at the start of 'Taxman' is a decoy, dubbed over the real one, and the stabbing guitar solo is McCartney's rather than Harrison's. 'I'm Only Sleeping' contains a guitar part written out backwards, played, and then reversed on tape. On 'Yellow Submarine' the party noises were made in the studio with the band and friends blowing through straws into buckets of water.","It arrived without much noise, since the Beatles were still a screaming phenomenon and few critics listened this closely, and it has risen steadily since, often above Sgt. Pepper in polls, on the grounds that the invention serves the songs rather than the concept. The American release again dropped three Lennon songs. Debate now centres on the 2022 remix, which unpicks the four-track masters with software and gives the drums a weight vinyl never had.","Play it in sequence: the album is built as a shuffle of extremes, and the jolt from 'Here, There and Everywhere' to 'Yellow Submarine' is deliberate. Notice how little reverb there is; everything sits close and dry. Then hear the two records it argues with, Pet Sounds a few months earlier and Sgt. Pepper a year later."],"the-beatles-tomorrow-never-knows":["Three minutes over a single unchanging chord, with Ringo Starr's drum figure heavy and looped and a tambura drone underneath. Five tape loops, the best known a speeded-up guitar that sounds like a seagull, were run from machines around the building and faded in live at the mixing desk. Lennon's voice arrives ordinary, then switches halfway to a thin, wavering tone fed through a rotating Leslie speaker cabinet.","Recorded at Abbey Road in April 1966, the first song taped for Revolver. Lennon's words are adapted from The Psychedelic Experience, Timothy Leary's rewriting of the Tibetan Book of the Dead. McCartney had been going to Stockhausen and Berio events in London and making loops at home on a domestic Brenell recorder with the erase head disabled, so the tape saturated on itself into something metallic.","The loops are not synchronised to the beat and never repeat in the same alignment, so the texture keeps shifting while not a single note changes. Lennon asked to sound like a chanting lama on a mountaintop; the Leslie cabinet, normally used for organs, was the studio's answer. The backwards guitar cutting in at the halfway point is reportedly the 'Taxman' solo reversed.","It closed Revolver where most listeners expected a rocker, and reviewers in 1966 largely passed over it. Its standing grew with everything it made possible: the loop as a compositional unit in pop, the drone as a harmonic bed, the voice treated as raw material. Producers still chase Starr's drum sound. Some hear a tourist's version of Indian music in the drone, and that criticism has weight.","Listen once for the drums alone, since the pattern never varies and that is what lets everything else float, then again on headphones for the loops entering from the sides. Play Stockhausen's Gesang der Jünglinge afterwards for where the technique came from, and the Velvet Underground for where the drone went."],"the-beatles-strawberry-fields-forever":["Four minutes that begin with a Mellotron flute setting, a keyboard playing tapes of real flutes, and end with a false fade, a return, and a swirl of backwards cymbal, cellos and trumpets. Lennon sings slightly behind the beat throughout. There is no conventional chorus: the title line opens the song and then recedes, and the harmony keeps sliding a step lower than the ear expects.","Written in Almería in the autumn of 1966 while Lennon was filming How I Won the War, his first long stretch away from the band. Strawberry Field was a Salvation Army children's home near his aunt's house in Liverpool. Recorded at Abbey Road over five weeks, an unheard-of span then, and issued in February 1967 as a double A-side with 'Penny Lane'. The single stopped at number two in Britain.","Two completely different recordings are spliced about a minute in: a lighter band take and a later scored version, in different keys and at different tempos. Lennon wanted the first half of one and the second of the other, so Martin and Emerick slowed the faster tape and sped the slower until the pitches met. The join is audible once you know where to listen.","Some reviewers found it wilfully obscure; it has since become the record people reach for when dating the moment pop left its own conventions. The mono and stereo mixes differ in the ending. The 1996 Anthology release of the early takes, voice and guitar alone, began a long argument about whether the orchestral version improved the song or buried it.","Play the Anthology demo first and then the finished single: the distance between them is the story. Listen for the splice about sixty seconds in, and stay past the fade, where the coda returns with a tape-manipulated voice muttering under the cellos. Pair it with 'Good Vibrations', built the same modular way a few months earlier."],"the-beatles-sgt-pepper":["Thirteen songs presented as a show by a fictional band, several running together without gaps. The palette is wide: a forty-piece orchestra on two tracks, an Indian ensemble on 'Within You Without You', harmonium and harpsichord, a fairground collage on 'Being for the Benefit of Mr Kite!'. It ends with 'A Day in the Life', where an orchestral crescendo rises twice out of nothing and a piano chord is left to decay for forty seconds.","Recorded at Abbey Road between December 1966 and April 1967 over some seven hundred studio hours, with the band no longer touring and EMI's four-track machines linked in pairs to stretch capacity. Peter Blake and Jann Haworth's cover, a crowd of cardboard heroes, cost more than most albums did to make. It appeared in Britain in late May 1967 and became the sound of that summer.","The orchestral crescendo was written as an instruction rather than as notes: each player was to move from the lowest note on their instrument to the highest over twenty-four bars, in their own time. The British pressing ends with a fifteen-kilohertz tone audible mainly to dogs, then a loop of babble cut into the run-out groove that repeats until the needle is lifted. Stockhausen stands in the back row of the cover.","It was received as proof that pop could be art, won four Grammys, and then spent decades being blamed for exactly that. Critics now often prefer Revolver, and the charge that Pepper is an album of production rather than songs is old and not baseless. The 2017 remix, made from the session tapes, restores punch that four-track bouncing had flattened and has moved opinion again.","Hear it in one sitting, in mono if you can: the band sat through the mono mix and left the stereo to the engineers. Listen for the crowd noise stitching the first two tracks together, and for the two crescendos in 'A Day in the Life', the second of which stops dead. Then play The Velvet Underground & Nico, released ten weeks earlier, for the opposite view of 1967."],"the-beatles-abbey-road":["Seventeen tracks, the last eight run together as a medley of unfinished songs on side two. A Moog synthesizer appears on a Beatles record for the first time, in the white-noise wind of 'Here Comes the Sun' and the wordless triple-tracked harmonies of 'Because'. 'I Want You (She's So Heavy)' grinds through a repeated figure for eight minutes and stops mid-bar. The recognisable sound is the clean, warm bass under close vocal thirds.","Recorded in the summer of 1969 after the fraught Get Back sessions, on EMI's new eight-track desk, with George Martin back at the band's request: they asked to make one more record the old way. Lennon and McCartney were pulling apart, Harrison brought 'Something' and 'Here Comes the Sun', Starr sang 'Octopus's Garden'. It was the last album the four of them recorded together.","The chords of 'Because' came from Lennon hearing Ono play the opening of Beethoven's Moonlight Sonata and asking her to play it backwards. 'Her Majesty', twenty-three seconds long, was cut from the medley and ordered destroyed; the engineer spooled it onto the end of the tape out of habit, and the accident became the first hidden track. The closing guitar solos alternate McCartney, Harrison and Lennon in rotation.","Reviews were mixed, and some heard the medley as an admission that nobody could finish a song. Sales said otherwise, and the side-two sequence became a model for how to end an album. Argument continues over whether the medley is a suite or a scrapbook, and the sleeve produced an absurd rumour that McCartney had died. Harrison's two songs are now usually named the best on it.","Start at 'You Never Give Me Your Money' and let side two run to the end without stopping. Listen for the Moog in 'Because' and for the three guitar voices trading in 'The End'. If you want the sound as it was, the 1969 stereo mix is the only one the band made; the 2019 remix is brighter and pulls the bass forward."],"the-rolling-stones-satisfaction":["Three and three-quarter minutes built on a three-note guitar figure played through a fuzzbox, doubled by Bill Wyman's bass and pushed along by Charlie Watts's flat, unhurried backbeat. Jagger sings in a drawl that has become a shout by the last verse, complaining about advertising, hotel rooms and being told what to want. There is no solo. The riff simply returns, louder, with tambourine.","Richards woke in a Florida motel in May 1965, taped the riff on a cassette machine and fell asleep again; the rest of the tape is snoring. The band recorded it at Chess in Chicago and then at RCA in Hollywood during their third American tour, at the height of the marketing of British groups as an invasion. Andrew Loog Oldham, their manager and producer, released it in America first.","The fuzz tone was meant to be temporary. Richards heard the line as a horn riff in the Stax manner and thought the Gibson Maestro pedal a placeholder for brass; he was outvoted and the guide sound went out to the world. Otis Redding's cover, cut months later, restores the horns and shows what Richards had in mind. Watts plays almost nothing, and the space is what makes it move.","It was the Stones' first American number one and the record that turned them from a cover band into writers. Critics then heard insolence; critics now hear the first pop hit about consumer dissatisfaction rather than romantic disappointment. The fuzz tone was copied within months and was a cliché by 1967, which makes hearing its original strangeness an act of effort.","Play the mono single rather than an album version; the riff is more brutal when everything sits in one place. Count how few drum fills there are. Then play Otis Redding's version with horns for the record Richards wanted, and 'You Really Got Me' for the British riff that came first."],"the-rolling-stones-beggars-banquet":["Ten songs, mostly acoustic guitars recorded hot and close. 'Sympathy for the Devil' builds from congas and piano to a chorus of whoops, with Keith Richards's solo cutting across it; 'Street Fighting Man' has no electric guitar on it at all; 'Salt of the Earth' and 'Prodigal Son' go back to gospel and country blues. Brian Jones, fading out of the band, plays sitar and slide. The sound is dry, rhythmic and unglamorous.","Recorded at Olympic Studios in London in 1968 with Jimmy Miller producing, after the psychedelic misfire of Their Satanic Majesties Request. Paris and Chicago were in the streets, and 'Street Fighting Man' was written around the Grosvenor Square demonstration. Decca refused the original lavatory-wall sleeve and held the record up for months, so it finally appeared in a plain white invitation card instead.","'Street Fighting Man' was recorded onto a mono cassette machine, deliberately overloaded, and then transferred to tape: the distortion is a cheap microphone clipping. Charlie Watts plays a tiny 1930s practice kit that folded into a case. Under the chorus sit Dave Mason's shehnai and Brian Jones's sitar, which is why the track sounds both thin and enormous at once.","It restored their reputation at a stroke and began the run of four albums usually counted as their best. 'Sympathy for the Devil' drew accusations of devil worship that the band enjoyed and then regretted after Altamont. Godard filmed the sessions for One Plus One, which preserves the song assembling itself. Critics still argue whether the turn to roots music was conviction or a shrewd reading of the room.","Start with 'Street Fighting Man' and listen for the absence of electric guitar. Then 'Sympathy for the Devil', counting the instruments as they enter across the first two minutes. Watch Godard's footage afterwards. The mono mix, restored on recent reissues, has more punch than the stereo, where the acoustic guitars spread thin."],"the-rolling-stones-let-it-bleed":["Nine songs that open and close on apocalypse. 'Gimme Shelter' starts with a shivering tremolo guitar and builds to Merry Clayton's duet with Jagger; 'Love in Vain' is a Robert Johnson song rearranged with country steel guitar; 'Midnight Rambler' changes tempo three times; 'You Can't Always Get What You Want' opens with a choir and ends with French horn, gospel piano and a long fade.","Recorded in London and Los Angeles through 1968 and 1969 with Jimmy Miller, across the death of Brian Jones and the arrival of Mick Taylor, who plays on two tracks. It was released on the eve of the Altamont festival, where the band played while a man was killed in front of the stage. The decade's violence had arrived in the audience rather than on the news.","The choir on 'You Can't Always Get What You Want' is the London Bach Choir, singing an arrangement by Jack Nitzsche, and its members later objected to the company the album's other lyrics kept. Al Kooper plays both the French horn introduction and the piano. On 'Gimme Shelter', Clayton's voice cracks on a high line about murder, and Jagger can be heard reacting to it.","Reviews were strong and have stayed strong, and the record is usually paired with Beggars Banquet as the band's peak. Altamont bound it to a narrative about the sixties ending, which some critics find lazy and others unavoidable. Merry Clayton went uncredited on the sleeve for years; the 2013 documentary Twenty Feet from Stardom finally made her name known to general listeners.","Begin with 'Gimme Shelter' and play it loud enough to hear the guitar shiver before the drums come in. Then 'You Can't Always Get What You Want' in full, for the distance travelled from choir to gospel piano. Compare it with Abbey Road, released two months earlier: two British bands closing the same decade in opposite moods."],"the-kinks-you-really-got-me":["Two and a quarter minutes, almost no chords beyond two shifted up and down the neck, and a guitar tone that sounds torn rather than distorted. Ray Davies sings in a strangled shout; the band stops dead before the solo, which Dave Davies plays in a scramble of wrong-sounding notes. The song rises a whole step twice to keep the pressure climbing. There is no bridge and no let-up anywhere.","Recorded in London in the summer of 1964 after the band's first two singles failed and Pye was threatening to drop them. Davies has said the song began slower and bluesier before they raised the tempo. Shel Talmy produced, and the Kinks were teenagers from Muswell Hill playing ballrooms. A persistent story that the session guitarist Jimmy Page played the solo has been denied by everyone involved, Page included.","The sound came from vandalism. Dave Davies slit the speaker cone of a small green Elpico amplifier with a razor blade, then fed that amplifier into a larger Vox, producing a tearing distortion that no pedal then on sale could make. It is not overdrive; it is a broken loudspeaker recorded honestly, three years before fuzz became something you could buy.","It went to number one in Britain and gave the band a career. Its influence is claimed by hard rock and punk alike: two-chord riffing, and distortion as a structural element rather than a fault. Musicologists point out that the song is modal rather than blues-based, which is part of why it does not sound American. Later Davies songs are subtler, but nothing he wrote travelled further.","Play it beside 'Satisfaction' and 'My Generation' for three different answers to the same question about how loud a pop single can be. Listen for the two key changes, each a lurch upward. Then go straight to 'Waterloo Sunset' to see how far the same writer moved in three years."],"the-kinks-waterloo-sunset":["Three minutes in a slow, rocking sway, with a guitar figure that keeps falling and a chorus of wordless harmonies washed in tape echo. Ray Davies half-speaks verses about a narrator who watches the river from his window while Terry and Julie cross the bridge below. Two guitars, bass, drums and voices, almost no ornament, and a bass line that walks underneath the whole thing like traffic.","Recorded at Pye's studio in London in the spring of 1967, when the Kinks were banned from touring America and stuck at home writing about it. Davies was twenty-two and had recently been ill; the song began, he has said, with a memory of the Thames seen from a hospital window. The names Terry and Julie belong to nobody in particular, though the public decided they were Terence Stamp and Julie Christie.","The chorus harmonies were sung and then drenched in tape echo rather than stacked thickly, which is why they sound like a crowd heard at a distance. Pete Quaife's bass line hardly repeats a full bar the same way. And the narrator never goes outside: the entire lyric is set at a window, an English pop song about declining to join in.","It reached number two in Britain, and its standing has only grown; British writers regularly name it the finest single of the decade. It has been read as elegy, as agoraphobia, and as a farewell to a London about to be demolished. Davies has resisted every reading. Its failure in America at the time is part of why the Kinks became a cult rather than a stadium band.","Listen with headphones for the echo on the harmonies and for the way the guitars fall away in the last chorus. Then play Vaughan Williams's The Lark Ascending and notice the same English habit of watching something from a distance and saying nothing about yourself. Any decent mono reissue will do; the stereo mix scatters the voices."],"the-kinks-village-green-preservation-society":["Fifteen short songs in thirty-nine minutes, no hit single and no attempt at one. Harpsichord, Mellotron and acoustic guitar carry most of it and the drums stay polite. Songs list what is being lost, from draught beer to steam trains to the village green itself, while others look at photograph albums, old friends turned strangers, and a cat. The signature is the harpsichord under Ray Davies's flat, affectionate delivery.","Made through 1968 while the band was still barred from America, and released in November of that year into total indifference: it sold in the low thousands. Davies had assembled much of it as a solo project before it became a Kinks album. Britain was demolishing its Victorian centres and raising tower blocks, so the record's nostalgia is specific and recent rather than picturesque.","The Mellotron stands in for an orchestra the band could not afford, and on 'Wicked Annabella' it is turned to something closer to horror. The album exists in twelve-track and fifteen-track versions because Davies revised it after a European pressing had already gone out. 'Big Sky' is the one song where the nostalgia breaks: a god who looks down on all this and does nothing.","It sold almost nothing and is now the Kinks album most often called their best, a reversal that took twenty years and a generation of British songwriters saying so in public. Some hear conservatism; others hear a very early warning about heritage turned into a commodity. The 1998 and 2018 reissues, with their alternative running orders, turned the argument into a hobby.","Play it in the afternoon, whole, and let the songs stay small. Listen for the harpsichord and the Mellotron doing the work of strings. Start with 'Do You Remember Walter?' if you want the heart of it. Then play the Velvet Underground's White Light/White Heat from the same year and marvel at what else 1968 sounded like."],"the-who-my-generation":["Three minutes at a hectic clip. Roger Daltrey stutters the words over a riff Pete Townshend plays in blunt block chords, and John Entwistle takes a bass solo, twice, high and clattering, one of the first on a pop single. The song shifts up a key three times, each lift tightening the screw, and ends in feedback with Keith Moon's drums collapsing over themselves.","Recorded in London in October 1965 with Shel Talmy, who had produced the Kinks. Townshend was twenty and writing for an audience of mods in parkas. Entwistle kept breaking strings and the solo took several attempts on a Danelectro bass, whose strings could not then be bought separately. The stutter has been explained as amphetamine speech, as blues phrasing and as a joke; Townshend has offered all three.","The key changes are the trick: each arrives without preparation and each pushes Daltrey's voice nearer to breaking. Listen to Moon, who plays almost no backbeat at all and treats the kit as a lead instrument, filling every gap the riff leaves. The ending is not a fade but the band dismantling the arrangement in real time, which is exactly what they did on stage.","It reached number two in Britain and became a generational slogan, endlessly quoted and endlessly misread as a wish to die young. Townshend has spent decades pointing out the irony of the line while continuing to sing it into his seventies. Historians put it at the head of the line that runs to punk; the Sex Pistols' contempt for hippies is its direct heir.","Play the single mix loud and follow Moon rather than the words. Then listen again to the bass solo on its own terms, as a sound nobody had put on a hit record. For the same year's rage in different accents, compare it with 'Satisfaction' and 'You Really Got Me'."],"the-who-sell-out":["A pop album disguised as a pirate radio broadcast, its songs interrupted by jingles, deodorant advertisements and station idents the band recorded themselves. 'I Can See for Miles' is the hard centre, one chord held under a rising vocal for most of its length. 'Rael' at the end is a six-minute mini-opera. The production is bright and treble-heavy, all guitars and cymbals, with harmonies that owe more to California than to Shepherd's Bush.","Made in 1967 as the British government closed the offshore pirate stations that had helped make the Who famous; Radio London went silent that August. Townshend was writing longer forms and losing interest in singles. The sleeve, with Daltrey sitting in a bath of baked beans, was a pop-art joke about selling out, staged some years before the phrase turned sour.","The tune inside 'Rael' comes back two years later as 'Sparks' and the 'Underture' in Tommy: the mini-opera was a rehearsal for the real one. The jingles are new recordings by the band rather than licensed broadcast tape, which is why they sit in the same sound world as the songs and the seams are so hard to find.","It sold modestly and was treated as a novelty. Its standing now rests on the songs and on the idea, borrowed by everyone from Frank Zappa to the skit-laden hip-hop album, that a record can imitate a medium. The stereo and mono mixes differ substantially. 'I Can See for Miles' underperformed in Britain, which Townshend took badly enough to mention for the rest of his life.","Let it run without skipping, since the jingles are the point and they time the record. Listen to 'I Can See for Miles' for the single chord held under the verse and for Moon's rolling entrance. Then play Tommy and catch the 'Rael' theme coming back."],"the-who-tommy":["A double album of seventy-five minutes telling of a boy struck deaf, dumb and blind who becomes a pinball champion and then a messiah. The band plays it almost alone, with guitars, bass, drums, a little piano and French horn, so the opera is carried by recurring themes rather than by orchestration. 'Overture', 'Sparks' and 'Underture' state and restate the material; 'Pinball Wizard' is the song everyone knows.","Written by Townshend across 1968 and recorded at IBC in London over six months between tours. He had become a follower of Meher Baba, and the story's silence, sight and release come from that. The Who were broke and the record had to work. They played much of it live from 1969, including at Woodstock and in opera houses, which the press found either brave or ridiculous.","The 'Overture' is not decoration: nearly every theme in the piece appears there first, and 'Underture' spends ten minutes developing the 'Sparks' figure without a word sung. Entwistle plays the French horn parts himself. The narrative has holes the band patched later, since the 1975 film and the 1993 stage version both rewrite the ending, but the musical argument holds together.","Reviewers split between those who took the form seriously and those who found the story tasteless; the songs about abuse, written by Entwistle, still draw criticism. It sold hugely and made the Who a stadium band. Whether it is an opera is a question that has never been settled and probably cannot be, since it has no recitative and no singers other than the band.","Play the 'Overture' and then 'Underture' back to back to hear the material worked rather than merely sung. Otherwise start at side one and accept the plot as a frame. Compare it with a real opera about an outsider destroyed by a crowd, Britten's Peter Grimes, and the differences will teach you more than the similarities."],"the-beach-boys-pet-sounds":["Thirteen songs in thirty-six minutes, hardly any of it played by the Beach Boys: Brian Wilson wrote the arrangements, booked Los Angeles session players, then brought the group in to sing. You hear bass harmonica, harpsichord, accordion, bicycle bells, sleigh bells and an electro-theremin. The vocal stacks are the signature, four and five parts moving in contrary motion under Carl Wilson's lead on 'God Only Knows'.","Recorded in Hollywood in the first months of 1966 while the rest of the band toured Japan. Wilson had stopped touring in 1964 after a breakdown on a plane; Tony Asher, an advertising copywriter, wrote most of the lyrics with him. Capitol wanted surf songs and put out a hits compilation months later to compensate. Wilson has said the record began as an answer to Rubber Soul.","Wilson mixed in mono because he is deaf in one ear, which is why no stereo mix existed until 1997. Listen to the basses: there are often two, one of them holding a note that clashes with the chord above it, and that friction is what gives songs like 'You Still Believe in Me' their ache. The last sounds on the record are Wilson's dogs and a passing train.","It sold less than the group's surf records and Capitol treated it as a failure, while in Britain it was received as a landmark at once. McCartney has named 'God Only Knows' his favourite song and credited the album with pushing the Beatles towards Sgt. Pepper. The argument now is about Smile, the record Wilson abandoned next, and whether Pet Sounds is a peak or a first step never taken.","Start with 'God Only Knows' and 'Caroline, No', then hear the album whole for the two instrumentals that act as breathing space. Use the mono mix, since that is the record Wilson made. Afterwards play Rubber Soul, which set it off, and Sgt. Pepper, which answered it, and 1966 will make more sense."],"the-beach-boys-god-only-knows":["Two minutes fifty of shifting ground. A French horn and sleigh bells open it; the first chord is not the key it ends in, and the song never quite settles anywhere. Carl Wilson sings the lead in a plain, high voice. The bridge drops to a bare figure on harpsichord and plucked bass, and the last minute is a round, two vocal lines chasing each other while a third sings underneath, fading out unresolved.","Recorded in Hollywood in March 1966 for Pet Sounds with a studio full of session players. Asher and Wilson argued about the title, since using the word God in a pop lyric was thought likely to keep a record off American radio, and in the United States it was issued as the B-side of 'Wouldn't It Be Nice'. In Britain it was the A-side and a hit.","The song barely states its own key. The opening chord belongs elsewhere, the verses keep sliding, and the resolution you have been waiting for arrives only as the fade begins. Brian gave the lead to his brother Carl because he thought that voice had the innocence the words needed, and those words open by admitting the singer may be wrong about everything.","Musicians have led the praise: McCartney has called it the best song ever written and said it reduced him to tears, and 'Here, There and Everywhere' on Revolver is his answer to it. Critics point to the harmonic ambiguity, to the way the round refuses closure, and to the fact that a love song begins with the word 'may'. The 1997 stereo mix reveals the round more clearly; the mono keeps its mystery.","Listen to the last forty-five seconds and try to follow one voice in the round, then play it again and follow another. Notice that you cannot say what key it is in and that this does not feel like a problem. Then play 'Here, There and Everywhere' for the reply from Liverpool."],"the-beach-boys-good-vibrations":["Three minutes forty that behave like four songs joined end to end. Cellos play triplets under the verses, the chorus rides a wavering high tone from an electro-theremin, and a section in the middle drops to organ and a whispered vocal before the harmonies flood back. Nothing repeats the way pop songs repeat, and the ending is a new idea rather than a reprise of an old one.","Built over roughly seven months in 1966 across four Los Angeles studios, at a cost often reported near fifty thousand dollars, which would have been extraordinary for a single. Wilson recorded sections separately, chose the best of each and spliced them, using the studio the way a film editor uses a cutting room. Mike Love wrote the released lyric, replacing earlier words.","The instrument on the chorus is not a theremin. Paul Tanner, a trombonist, had built a slide-controlled oscillator that produced a similar tone but could be played accurately, and it is his machine you hear. The sections were recorded in different rooms with different players, so the joins are audible if you listen for the change in the air around the instruments.","It went to number one in America and Britain and is generally treated as the high point of the pop single as a constructed object. It also broke its maker, since the method it proved led straight into Smile, which collapsed. Critics disagree over whether the modular approach produced a whole or a brilliant assembly, and Wilson's later live performances, which run it straight through, sharpen the question.","Listen for the moment around two minutes when the record empties out to organ and a single voice, then rebuilds. Try to count the sections. Then play 'Strawberry Fields Forever', spliced from two takes a few months later, and hear two studios arriving at the same conclusion."],"the-byrds-mr-tambourine-man":["Two and a half minutes: one verse of Dylan's song and the chorus twice, with the rest cut away. What you remember is the introduction, Jim McGuinn's twelve-string Rickenbacker compressed until each note rings into the next, and then the three-part harmony, cool and level where Dylan was ragged. The bass runs a melodic line of its own and the drums keep a soft, insistent shuffle underneath.","Recorded in Los Angeles in January 1965. Columbia's producer did not trust the band, so apart from McGuinn's guitar the backing is played by session musicians, the Wrecking Crew; only the voices and the twelve-string are the Byrds. Dylan's own recording had not yet been issued when they cut it. McGuinn sang deliberately between Dylan's phrasing and Lennon's, aiming at both markets at once.","The compression is the record. The engineer rode the twelve-string hard so that decaying notes swell instead of dying, producing the shimmer that every jangling guitar record since has chased. McGuinn came to the instrument from banjo and cites Bach's counterpoint for the picking patterns he plays: listen to the introduction as three lines rather than as one melody.","It went to number one on both sides of the Atlantic and invented folk rock as a commercial category overnight, which Dylan noticed before he went electric at Newport that July. Purists complained about the cut verses and the session players. The trimming is now usually praised as ruthless editing rather than vandalism, and Dylan himself is reported to have approved.","Listen to the first eight seconds alone, then hear the harmony arrive. Compare Dylan's own recording, released on Bringing It All Back Home a few weeks before the single, for what was added and what was taken away. Then play 'Eight Miles High' to hear the same guitar turned loose."],"the-byrds-eight-miles-high":["Three and a half minutes that open with a climbing bass figure and then a guitar solo with nothing rock and roll about it: McGuinn plays fast modal runs on the twelve-string, doubling back on himself, closer to a saxophone than to a guitar. The harmonies are icy and the drums push hard. A second solo tears the song apart before a last verse that describes London as cold and unfriendly.","Recorded in Los Angeles in January 1966, after an earlier version cut at RCA was rejected by Columbia, and released that March. Gene Clark wrote the words about the flight and the city following the band's disastrous first British tour. McGuinn and David Crosby had been playing Coltrane and Ravi Shankar records on the tour bus. American radio banned it as a drug song, which the band denied and which killed its chart run.","The guitar solo is modelled on Coltrane's saxophone, as the band has always said, and specifically on his habit of hammering at one phrase until it breaks open. The Indian element is in the drone and the sliding vocal line rather than in any Indian instrument: there is no sitar on the record. The rejected RCA version, issued decades later, is faster and wilder.","It reached the American top twenty despite the ban and is usually called the first psychedelic hit, or the first raga rock record, depending on who is arguing. Gene Clark left the band within weeks of its release. Later critics have often preferred the RCA version. The question of whether rock could absorb jazz improvisation without sounding like a party trick starts here and is not settled.","Listen to the first solo twice, once for the notes and once for how little it resolves. Then play a Coltrane record from the early sixties and a Ravi Shankar raga, and the family resemblance will be obvious. The 1996 remaster brings the bass forward, which helps the climb at the start."],"the-byrds-sweetheart-of-the-rodeo":["Eleven songs, thirty-two minutes, of country music played straight: pedal steel, fiddle, banjo and close harmony, with Dylan songs, a Merle Haggard prison song, a Louvin Brothers hymn and a Woody Guthrie ballad among them. The twelve-string jangle is almost entirely absent. Gram Parsons sings lead on several tracks in a plain, cracked tenor and McGuinn sings the rest. Nothing on the record winks at the audience.","Recorded in Nashville and Los Angeles in 1968, months after Parsons joined the band, with Nashville session players filling out the sound. The Byrds played the Grand Ole Opry that March and were heckled for their hair. Parsons's previous label claimed his recordings, so several of his lead vocals were replaced by McGuinn before release; some were restored in 1997. He left the band before the album appeared.","The replaced vocals leave a strange doubleness: on 'The Christian Life' and 'You Don't Miss Your Water' you can hear either singer depending on the edition, and the songs change meaning with the voice. Parsons sings the Louvin Brothers' hymn without a trace of irony, which is the whole argument of the record. Listen for Lloyd Green's pedal steel answering every vocal line like a second singer.","It sold worse than any Byrds album before it and confused both audiences: rock listeners took it for a joke and country radio would not play long-haired Californians. It is now the origin point for country rock, the Eagles and everything filed as Americana. Critics still argue whether Parsons or McGuinn owns the record, and the 1997 reissue with Parsons's vocals restored is often treated as the real one.","Start with 'Hickory Wind' and 'The Christian Life', then set the 1997 reissue's Parsons vocals against the released ones. Listen for the pedal steel as a second voice rather than as decoration. Then hear Copland's Appalachian Spring for another city artist's idea of rural America, made twenty-four years earlier."],"the-velvet-underground-and-nico":["Eleven songs that swing between lullaby and assault. John Cale's electric viola drones under 'Venus in Furs' and 'Heroin'; Lou Reed plays guitars tuned so that every string sounds the same note; Maureen Tucker plays standing, mostly mallets on a bass drum laid on its side, with no cymbals at all. Nico sings three songs in a low German-accented monotone. 'European Son' ends in a crash of breaking glass.","Recorded mostly in New York in the spring of 1966 and then held back for a year while Verve worked out what to do with it. Andy Warhol was the producer in the sense of a patron: he paid, insisted on Nico, and drew the banana for the sleeve. The band played behind films and dancers as part of Warhol's Exploding Plastic Inevitable, which is where most of New York first heard them.","Cale had spent 1963 to 1965 in La Monte Young's Theatre of Eternal Music, holding single pitches for hours at a time, and the viola drones here are that discipline transplanted into a rock band. Reed's 'ostrich' tuning puts every string on one note, so a strummed chord is really a single pitch with many timbres. 'The Black Angels' Death Song' is that tuning at its harshest.","It sold poorly and was largely ignored. Brian Eno's remark that everyone who bought a copy started a band has become the standard shorthand and is roughly true of punk, post-punk and indie rock alike. The banana sleeve was withdrawn over a lawsuit concerning a photograph on the back. Warhol's role is still argued: some say he did nothing, others that doing nothing was the production.","Play 'Venus in Furs' first for the viola, then 'Sunday Morning' for the celesta and the sweetness, so you hear both halves of the band. The mono mix, restored in 2012, is closer to what they intended. Then play La Monte Young's Trio for Strings, and Sgt. Pepper for the same year's other idea of what a record could be."],"the-velvet-underground-white-light-white-heat":["Six tracks, forty minutes, recorded loud and distorted on purpose. 'The Gift' is a short story read deadpan by Cale in one channel while the band plays an instrumental in the other. 'Sister Ray' takes seventeen minutes over one chord, organ and guitar fighting for the same space, recorded in a single take with the engineer reportedly walking out. Nothing is polished and the meters stay in the red throughout.","Recorded in New York in September 1967 after Nico and Warhol had gone, with Tom Wilson producing and the band playing at stage volume in the studio. They had been touring relentlessly and had stopped caring whether anyone liked them. Reed and Cale were already in conflict about how far the noise should go, and Cale left the following year.","'Sister Ray' has no overdubs and no edits: what you hear is one performance in which Cale's overdriven organ gradually swamps Reed's guitar, and Reed plays louder rather than give way. The distortion is not an effect but amplifiers and a mixing desk pushed past their limits. In 'The Gift' you can listen to either channel alone and get a complete, quite different piece.","It sold worse than the first album and was dismissed as unlistenable. Its standing grew with every noise band that followed, from the Stooges to Sonic Youth and the whole 1980s American underground. Critics disagree about whether the harshness is a position or a tantrum. Reed said late in life that it was the truest record he ever made.","Try 'Sister Ray' with the volume higher than is comfortable and follow the organ rather than the vocals; the piece is a duel and you should pick a side. Hear 'The Gift' once in each channel. Then play the Kinks' Village Green from the same year for the widest gap two records of 1968 can manage."],"jimi-hendrix-are-you-experienced":["Eleven tracks in about forty minutes played by a trio: Hendrix's guitar, Noel Redding's bass and Mitch Mitchell's jazz-inflected drums. 'Purple Haze' opens on two notes at the widest interval the ear finds unstable; 'Foxy Lady' begins with a single shivering note of feedback; 'Third Stone from the Sun' floats over slowed-down speech; the title track runs on backwards drums and backwards guitar. Hendrix sings almost apologetically over playing that does not apologise.","Recorded in London between October 1966 and April 1967, weeks after Chas Chandler of the Animals brought the unknown Hendrix over from New York and built a band around him. Sessions moved between three studios as the budget allowed, with Eddie Kramer engineering some of them. British guitarists went to watch him and came away shaken. The American and British editions have different tracks and different covers.","The feedback is composed rather than stumbled into. Hendrix worked out where to stand in relation to his amplifier so that the note which howled was the note he wanted, which is why 'Foxy Lady' opens on a pitch and not a squeal. He played a right-handed Stratocaster upside down and restrung, so the controls fall under his hand and the springs behind the bridge respond differently.","It was a hit in Britain at once and made him famous in America after Monterey that June. The early framing as showman, spectacle and guitar-burner took years to shake and Hendrix resented it. Modern criticism concentrates on the writing and the studio work rather than the flames. The 1997 remasters supervised by Kramer and the Hendrix family are the standard editions; earlier reissues shuffled the running order.","Start with 'Third Stone from the Sun' rather than the hits, for the range of sound in a single track. Then 'Purple Haze' for the riff and 'Manic Depression' for Mitchell's drumming, which is nearer to Elvin Jones than to rock. Use the British track listing. Follow with Electric Ladyland to hear him take over the studio."],"jimi-hendrix-electric-ladyland":["A double album of seventy-five minutes that ranges from the fifteen-minute 'Voodoo Chile', a blues jam with organ and a second bassist, to the two-minute pop of 'Crosstown Traffic' and the underwater drift of '1983... (A Merman I Should Turn to Be)'. The cover of Dylan's 'All Along the Watchtower' carries four solos in four different tones. Hendrix produced it himself, and the guitars are layered rather than caught live.","Recorded in 1968 in London and mostly at the Record Plant in New York, with Hendrix booking endless hours and filling the room with guests: Steve Winwood, Jack Casady, Al Kooper and Buddy Miles all play on it. Chandler quit as producer over the waste of time and money. Redding, sidelined by Hendrix's habit of playing the bass himself, began drifting away, and the band broke up within a year.","The swirling sound on '1983' and 'House Burning Down' is phasing done by hand: two tape machines running the same material while an engineer's thumb slows one reel fractionally against the other. No pedal existed for it yet. 'All Along the Watchtower' was recorded not long after Dylan released it, and Dylan liked the arrangement enough to adopt it, playing the song Hendrix's way ever since.","It topped the American chart and drew mixed reviews, with complaints about length and self-indulgence that read oddly now. Hendrix disliked the British sleeve, which put naked women on the front without his knowledge. Assessments have risen steadily: the record is generally taken as the fullest picture of what he could do, and the argument has moved to what the unfinished fourth album might have been.","Play 'Voodoo Chile' and '1983' as a pair, one caught live in the room and one built in layers. Listen for the phasing as a physical motion rather than an effect. Compare 'All Along the Watchtower' with Dylan's own version, then go back to Are You Experienced and hear how far eighteen months took him."],"jimi-hendrix-star-spangled-banner-woodstock":["Four minutes of solo electric guitar, the band silent behind him, played on a white Stratocaster at the close of Woodstock on a Monday morning to a field that had mostly gone home. The melody is stated plainly and then attacked: at the line about the rockets' red glare the notes break into dive-bombs, sirens and something like falling ordnance, before the tune reassembles and runs straight into 'Purple Haze'.","Woodstock, 18 August 1969, delayed by rain and by the sheer scale of the crowd, so the headliner played after eight in the morning to perhaps thirty thousand people out of the hundreds of thousands who had come. American troops were in Vietnam in their greatest numbers, and the anthem was not a neutral thing to play. Hendrix, who had served briefly in the 101st Airborne, never called it protest.","He had been playing the anthem in concert for over a year, so this was not a spontaneous gesture and other recordings exist. Listen for the quotation of 'Taps', the American military funeral call, dropped in near the end. The feedback that stands for bombs is pitched rather than random: the howling notes belong to the melody he is taking apart.","Reactions split immediately between desecration and patriotism, and Hendrix told an interviewer he thought it was beautiful. It has since been used in documentaries as shorthand for an entire decade, which flattens it. Musicians point out what critics miss, the control required to make a guitar imitate artillery while keeping the tune intact. The Woodstock film made it famous; the full morning set is better than its reputation.","Watch it rather than only hearing it: the stillness of the playing against the violence of the sound is the point. Listen for 'Taps'. Then play Ives's Variations on America, written by a teenager in the 1890s, for an earlier American pulling a patriotic tune out of shape, and Penderecki's Threnody for what an orchestra can do with the same subject."],"cream-disraeli-gears":["Eleven tracks in thirty-three minutes from a trio who improvised at length on stage and were held to pop lengths in the studio. 'Sunshine of Your Love' rides a descending riff Jack Bruce wrote on bass, which Ginger Baker turns around by putting the accents in the wrong place; 'Strange Brew' and 'Outside Woman Blues' are blues repainted in psychedelic colours. Clapton's tone is thick with wah pedal and sustain.","Recorded at Atlantic Studios in New York in 1967 with Tom Dowd engineering and Felix Pappalardi producing, squeezed into a few days between American dates, with Martin Sharp's dayglo sleeve painted afterwards. Cream had formed the year before out of the British scene, Bruce and Baker from the Graham Bond Organisation and Clapton from John Mayall's Bluesbreakers, and were already being billed as a supergroup.","'Sunshine of Your Love' came out of a night at a Jimi Hendrix show: Bruce went home and wrote the riff, and Baker suggested the tom-heavy pattern that makes the beat feel displaced. Pete Brown, a poet rather than a musician, wrote most of the words, which is why the imagery is stranger than blues lyrics usually are. Clapton's solo on 'Strange Brew' copies Albert King's phrasing closely, in places note for note.","It sold heavily in America and made Cream stars there before Britain caught up. Their reputation has swung: worshipped in 1968, dismissed in the punk years as the beginning of the guitar solo as an affliction, and re-examined since as a rhythm-section story, since Bruce and Baker were jazz players who happened to be very loud. The long live jams have aged less well than these compressed songs.","Start with 'Sunshine of Your Love' and follow Baker's drums rather than the riff. Then hear 'Strange Brew' beside Albert King's playing, and Robert Johnson's recordings behind all of it. For the loud trio done differently in the same year, play Are You Experienced."],"the-doors-the-doors":["Eleven tracks with no bass player: Ray Manzarek covers the bass line with his left hand on a keyboard while his right plays the organ. 'Light My Fire' runs to seven minutes of modal soloing over two alternating chords; 'Alabama Song' is a Brecht and Weill cabaret number played on a marxophone; 'The End' closes the record with eleven minutes of drone, Spanish-tinged guitar and a spoken section on the Oedipus story.","Recorded in Los Angeles in August 1966 at Sunset Sound with Paul Rothchild producing and Bruce Botnick engineering, in about a week, because the band had played the songs nightly at the Whisky a Go Go and knew them cold. Elektra signed them partly on the recommendation of Arthur Lee of Love. The single edit of 'Light My Fire', with the solos removed, went to number one in America.","Manzarek had a classical piano training, and the circling introduction to 'Light My Fire' owes more to keyboard exercises than to rhythm and blues. The solos on the same song are modal, built on two chords that swap rather than resolve, which is a jazz procedure and not a pop one. 'Alabama Song' comes from Brecht and Weill's Mahagonny, and the band keeps its lurching cabaret meter intact.","It was a slow-burning success that exploded when the edited 'Light My Fire' reached number one, and the album stayed on the American chart for two years. Critics then and now divide over Morrison, poet or poseur, and 'The End' draws the sharpest opinions. Musicians tend to point past him to the trio, whose keyboard bass, jazz drumming and unshowy guitar make a texture nobody else had.","Hear 'Light My Fire' in full rather than the single edit, and follow the organ solo into the guitar solo as one argument. Then 'The End', preferably late and loud. Compare Weill's original 'Alabama Song', and play Love's Forever Changes for the other side of the same Los Angeles year."],"love-forever-changes":["Eleven songs in forty-three minutes for a rock band, a small string section and mariachi-style trumpet. The guitars are mostly acoustic and finger-picked, and the strings and brass answer the voices rather than swell behind them. Arthur Lee sings in a light, conversational tenor about a city he does not trust. Bryan MacLean's 'Alone Again Or' opens the album with a Spanish-flavoured figure and the trumpet solo that became the record's signature.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1967 for Elektra. The band was in poor shape, through heroin, exhaustion and a refusal to tour, and the label brought in session players for the first sessions, which shamed the group into playing the rest themselves. David Angel wrote the string and horn arrangements. Lee, a Black bandleader in a white rock scene, believed he would die young and made the record as a farewell.","The arrangements are not decoration: the trumpet in 'Alone Again Or' answers a line the singer does not finish, and the strings in 'You Set the Scene' change the harmony under a repeated vocal note. Lee could not read music and sang the parts to Angel, which is why the horn lines phrase like a voice. Listen for how much gunfire and paranoia there is in the words of a supposedly gentle record.","It sold poorly in America, did better in Britain, and the band fell apart within a year. Its standing has risen continuously since the 1980s, with British musicians among its loudest advocates. Critics read it as the dark twin of the Summer of Love, made a few miles from where the sunshine records were being cut. Lee performed it with orchestras in the years before his death in 2006.","Play 'Alone Again Or' and then 'You Set the Scene', the album's bookends, and then the whole thing in order on a quiet evening. Listen to the horns as characters rather than as backing. Then hear Pet Sounds for Californian orchestration of a sunnier kind, and Rodrigo's Concierto de Aranjuez for where that Spanish guitar colour comes from."],"the-zombies-odessey-and-oracle":["Twelve songs, thirty-five minutes, built on Rod Argent's keyboards and the band's close harmony rather than on guitars. A Mellotron supplies the choirs and strings. 'Care of Cell 44' is a cheerful pop song about a woman coming out of prison; 'A Rose for Emily' is voice and piano alone; 'Butcher's Tale' is a harmonium and a shaking voice about the Somme; 'Time of the Season' runs on a breath, a handclap and a bass hook.","Recorded at Abbey Road and Olympic in 1967 on the studio time the band had left, in rooms the Beatles had just vacated. The Zombies had already decided to split and made the record as a closing statement, paying for the stereo mix themselves when the label would not. It appeared in 1968 to no sales at all, and 'Time of the Season' became an American hit a year later, when there was no band left to tour it.","The Mellotron is there because an orchestra was unaffordable, and its slightly seasick pitch is what gives the record its light-headedness. Argent had sung in a cathedral choir as a boy, and the massed vocal writing follows choral habits rather than pop ones: parts cross, and the harmony is balanced rather than arranged as lead plus backing. The title is misspelled because the sleeve artist got it wrong and nobody corrected him.","It sold almost nothing and the band had gone by the time anyone noticed. Reissues, and a slow campaign by musicians who found it second-hand, turned it into one of the most admired British albums of its period, and the surviving members began performing it whole in 2008. Critics argue about whether its sweetness is naive or a deliberate mask over songs about prison, war and abandonment.","Start with 'Care of Cell 44' and read the words while you listen: the gap between tune and subject is the record's method. Then 'Butcher's Tale' for the opposite. Use the stereo mix the band paid for out of their own pockets. Pair it with the Kinks' Village Green, made the same year from the same English materials."],"pink-floyd-piper-at-the-gates-of-dawn":["Eleven tracks, most of them Syd Barrett's, that swing between two poles: short, childlike songs about gnomes, cats, scarecrows and a bicycle, and long instrumental voyages built from feedback, echo and Rick Wright's reedy Farfisa organ. Astronomy Domine opens it with a descending guitar riff and a voice reading out names of stars; Interstellar Overdrive, nearly ten minutes long, starts on a stabbing chromatic riff and then dissolves into drift and noise before the riff snaps back.","Recorded at Abbey Road in the spring of 1967, produced by Norman Smith, while the Beatles were making Sgt. Pepper down the corridor. Pink Floyd were the house band of London's underground, playing the UFO club to light shows. The title comes from the mystical chapter of Kenneth Grahame's The Wind in the Willows. Within a year Barrett, unravelling under LSD and pressure, had been eased out of the group he had named and led.","Listen closer: Chapter 24 sets words taken almost directly from hexagram 24 of the I Ching, 'Return' — the same Chinese oracle book John Cage had used to compose by chance sixteen years earlier. And the whimsical Bike does not end as a song at all: it tips into a collage of clockwork, bells and looped, sped-up voices that points straight at the tape experiments Floyd would build their later career on.","British reviewers took it as the best record of the London underground; the American release dropped three songs and reordered the rest, and sold modestly. Its reputation rose as Barrett's did, posthumously and mythically. The standing argument is whether it is a Barrett album with a band attached or the first Pink Floyd record; the long pieces suggest the band, the songs suggest a singular, fragile writer.","Start with Astronomy Domine for the space-rock sound, then play Bike to hear Barrett's other face, the Edwardian nursery rhyme gone slightly wrong. Give Interstellar Overdrive your full attention and notice how the band hold a single idea together with almost nothing. Seek the original British mono mix, which the band attended; the stereo was made more hastily."],"pink-floyd-echoes":["A twenty-three-minute piece that filled the whole second side of Meddle. It begins with a single high piano note sounding like sonar, pulsing through a rotating speaker. Two verses, sung in close harmony by David Gilmour and Rick Wright, describe an albatross, an underwater world and two strangers meeting. Then a long funk groove, a stretch of wind and eerie cries with almost no rhythm at all, and a slow rebuild to the final verse and a rising, spiralling choir of tape.","After Barrett's departure the band spent two years searching for a way to write together. Echoes was assembled from fragments the four had worked on separately, stitched into one shape over months in several London studios. It showed the group, now led jointly by Roger Waters's words and Gilmour's guitar, how to make long form feel inevitable. The next year they played it to the empty Roman amphitheatre in the film Live at Pompeii.","Listen closer: the screaming seabird cries in the middle section are Gilmour's guitar played through a wah-wah pedal plugged in the wrong way round. The ping at the start came from Wright's piano, amplified through a Leslie speaker. And the chromatic rising figure in the verses later reminded Waters so strongly of a song in Andrew Lloyd Webber's Phantom of the Opera that he said so publicly.","Fans treat it as the group's great long piece, the hinge between the psychedelic Floyd and the Floyd of Dark Side. Critics at the time were more cautious about Meddle as a whole. A persistent fan theory claims it syncs with the final 'Jupiter and Beyond' sequence of Kubrick's 2001, a film otherwise scored with Ligeti; the band have shrugged it off.","Listen in one sitting, loudly, with the lights down. Notice the single piano note at the start and wait for it to return near the end: that return is the whole architecture. In the long middle, stop waiting for a tune and listen to the wind. Then hear Ligeti's Atmosphères, the other kind of space music, for comparison."],"pink-floyd-dark-side-of-the-moon":["Ten tracks run together as one continuous suite about the pressures that drive people mad: time, money, travel, conflict, death. A heartbeat opens and closes it. In between come Time with its explosion of clocks, Money in a limping seven-beat metre over a loop of cash registers, a synthesizer sequence racing in stereo on On the Run, and The Great Gig in the Sky, where Clare Torry improvises a wordless vocal over Rick Wright's piano. Spoken fragments drift through the whole.","Recorded at Abbey Road in 1972 and early 1973 with engineer Alan Parsons, after the band had road-tested the suite in concert for a year. The studio now had sixteen tracks and the band had an EMS synthesizer, which made the machine textures possible. Roger Waters wrote all the lyrics, the first time one member took the words entirely. Hipgnosis's cover, a prism splitting light, carried no band name.","Listen closer: the voices were collected by Waters, who wrote questions on cards — 'When was the last time you were violent?' — and recorded answers from roadies, their wives, and Abbey Road's doorman Gerry O'Driscoll, whose line 'There is no dark side of the moon really' ends the record. Paul McCartney was asked too, but his answers were too guarded to use.","It sold on a scale almost nothing had, staying on the American album chart for years, and became the default test record for home stereos. That ubiquity made it a target for punk, which heard it as the smug sound of rock's aristocracy. Torry later sued for co-writing credit on her vocal and settled, receiving credit on later pressings. Its craft is now rarely disputed; its depth still is.","Hear it complete, in order, on good headphones, for the stereo movement of the synthesizers and footsteps. Start by following the heartbeat and noticing when it comes back. Then listen to The Great Gig in the Sky twice: once for Torry, once for the piano beneath her. Compare the tape collage with Varèse's Poème électronique to hear where the idea came from."],"pink-floyd-wish-you-were-here":["Five tracks, framed by the two halves of Shine On You Crazy Diamond, a slow, huge elegy that opens with glassy synthesizer chords and a four-note guitar call before the vocal ever arrives. Between them sit Welcome to the Machine, all throbbing synthesizer and mechanical doors; Have a Cigar, a sneer at record-industry executives sung by the folk singer Roy Harper; and the title song, a plain acoustic ballad that begins as if heard through a car radio.","Made at Abbey Road in 1975 by a band exhausted and alienated by the success of Dark Side, uncertain whether they wanted to keep going. Roger Waters shaped the record around absence: Syd Barrett's absence, and the band's own absence from their work. The theme turned eerily literal during the sessions, when an overweight man with a shaved head and eyebrows wandered into the studio; it took the band some time to recognise Barrett.","Listen closer: the radio being tuned at the start of the title song passes a fragment of Tchaikovsky's Fourth Symphony before settling on Gilmour's twelve-string. At the very end of that song, deep in the mix, is a violin: Stéphane Grappelli, the great jazz violinist, was in the building and recorded a part that was all but buried. The four-note guitar figure of Shine On became known as 'Syd's theme'.","Reviews at the time were mixed, some finding it slow and bitter after Dark Side. The band members themselves, especially Gilmour and Wright, later named it their favourite. It has become the Floyd album people are most likely to call their best, largely because of its grief, which feels more personal than the general anxieties of its predecessor.","Let Shine On take its time: the vocal arrives only after about eight minutes, and the wait is the point. Listen to the title song as a conversation between two guitars, one on the radio and one in the room. Then go back to Barrett's own songs on The Piper at the Gates of Dawn and hear who is being mourned."],"king-crimson-in-the-court-of-the-crimson-king":["Five long tracks. 21st Century Schizoid Man bursts in with a distorted vocal and a riff doubled by guitar and saxophone, then an instrumental middle section of breakneck unison runs. After it come the pastoral flute of I Talk to the Wind, the doom-laden Epitaph with its banks of Mellotron strings, the drifting Moonchild, and the title track, a medieval-sounding pageant for Mellotron choir. The contrast of brutality and ceremony is the record's signature.","Released on Island in October 1969, months after the band's first concerts, and the same summer they had played to a vast audience supporting the Rolling Stones in Hyde Park. Robert Fripp, Ian McDonald, Greg Lake and Michael Giles made the music; Peter Sinfield wrote the lyrics and ran the lights. They produced it themselves after abandoning sessions with an outside producer. The cover, a screaming face, was the only album cover its painter, Barry Godber, ever made.","Listen closer: the Mellotron, a tape-replay keyboard designed to imitate strings and choirs, wobbles and sags slightly in pitch, and that instability is part of the eerie sound. The band's concerts in 1969 closed with their own arrangement of Mars from Holst's The Planets; the grinding, ceremonial weight of Epitaph and the title track owes a clear debt to it.","It was received as a shock: a debut that seemed to arrive fully formed and outflank the psychedelic bands. It is regularly named the first true progressive rock album. The debate is over Moonchild, twelve minutes of which are quiet free improvisation that many listeners skip; defenders hear it as the band's link to the avant-garde, detractors as padding.","Play 21st Century Schizoid Man loud and listen for the moment the saxophone and guitar lock into the unison passage. Then play Epitaph and Holst's Mars side by side and hear how an English orchestral tradition passed into rock through the Mellotron. Seek the 2009 remix by Steven Wilson for clarity, or an early Island pressing for weight."],"king-crimson-larks-tongues-in-aspic":["Six tracks bookended by the two parts of the title piece. Part One opens with thumb piano and chimes so quiet you may turn the volume up, then crashes into violent, jagged riffs, before ending with solo violin. Between the parts: a delicate ballad, a slow violin-led lament, a sleazy mid-tempo song, and The Talking Drum, a hypnotic bass and percussion groove that swells for minutes before a shrieking violin tears it straight into Part Two's hammering, odd-metred riff.","Robert Fripp dissolved the first Crimson lineups and built a new one in 1972: John Wetton on bass and voice, Bill Bruford, who had just left Yes, on drums, David Cross on violin, and Jamie Muir, a free-improvising percussionist. Fripp talked of the influence of Bartók's string quartets and of Hendrix in equal measure. The group improvised heavily in concert and the album distilled that.","Listen closer: Muir's percussion includes chains, sheets of metal, bells, a thumb piano and toys; the bag of laughter at the very end of Easy Money is a novelty laughing bag. Muir left soon after the recording to live in a Buddhist monastery in Scotland, so this is the only studio record with him on it. Listen for the moment in Part One where the violin alone is left standing.","Reviews were puzzled and then admiring; it has come to be heard as the start of the band's second, heavier and more improvisational identity, a line that runs into later metal and math rock. Arguments centre on the production, which many feel undersells the band's live power, and on the scarcity of Muir's contributions in the final mix.","Turn it up for the quiet start of Part One, then brace. Listen to The Talking Drum as one long crescendo and try to notice exactly when it becomes unbearable. Afterwards hear the finale of Bartók's Fourth Quartet and the Sacrificial Dance from The Rite of Spring: the repeated, stamping, off-balance accents are the same family."],"king-crimson-red":["Five tracks from a band reduced to a trio of Robert Fripp, John Wetton and Bill Bruford, with guests. The title track is an instrumental of grinding, dissonant guitar chords over a heavy, lurching beat. Fallen Angel and One More Red Nightmare are songs; Providence is an edited free improvisation; and Starless, twelve minutes long, moves from a mournful ballad through a long, tense build on a repeating bass figure to a furious return of its opening melody on saxophone.","Recorded in London in the summer of 1974 after violinist David Cross left. By the time Red was released that autumn, Fripp had already disbanded King Crimson, declaring the rock band format exhausted. Old members returned as guests: Ian McDonald and Mel Collins on saxophones. Providence came from a concert in Providence, Rhode Island, on the band's last American tour.","Listen closer: the middle of Starless is built on the simplest material imaginable, a two-note guitar figure repeated while bass and drums grow heavier beneath it, and it is the refusal to change that creates the tension. The cover photographs the three musicians, and the back shows a needle pushed into the red of a meter: the album's title is also a warning about volume.","It sold modestly and appeared as the band vanished, so its reputation grew later. Kurt Cobain named it among his favourite records, and heavy and alternative musicians since have claimed its dense, dissonant weight as an ancestor. It is now often ranked with the debut as the group's peak, and argued over only for Providence, which some find an interruption.","Start with the title track to hear the three-man attack, then play Starless in full and let the long middle build do its work. Listen for the moment McDonald's saxophone brings back the opening theme. Compare the grinding chords with Bartók's Fifth Quartet to hear where Fripp's harmonic language came from."],"yes-fragile":["Nine tracks: four full-band pieces, including Roundabout and the long Heart of the Sunrise, alternating with five short showcases, one for each member. Roundabout opens with a harmonic chime on acoustic guitar played backwards, then Chris Squire's bass, loud, trebly and melodic, drives the whole thing. Heart of the Sunrise swings between a frantic riff in unison and near-silent vocal passages. Steve Howe's solo, Mood for a Day, is Spanish-flavoured classical guitar.","Made in London in 1971, the first Yes album with Rick Wakeman, who arrived from the Strawbs with an arsenal of keyboards including Mellotron, Minimoog and piano. Money was short and the band needed a record fast after The Yes Album; the solo spots were partly a way to fill it. It was also Roger Dean's first Yes cover, the fragile planet that began a long visual identity.","Listen closer: Wakeman's solo track, Cans and Brahms, is his arrangement for keyboards of the third movement of Brahms's Fourth Symphony, the jaunty Allegro giocoso, with synthesizer lines taking the woodwind parts. He was under contract to another label, which stopped him recording his own composition. He later called it one of the weakest things he did, which is why many listeners skip a small window into the classical debt of the whole band.","Roundabout, edited as a single, gave Yes an American hit and opened the US market for progressive rock. Critics admired the playing and complained about the lyrics, which Jon Anderson built for sound more than sense. The solo tracks remain the album's contested part; Heart of the Sunrise is usually cited as the real preview of what the band would do next.","Follow Squire's bass all the way through Roundabout: it is a lead instrument. Then play Cans and Brahms directly after the Allegro giocoso of Brahms's Fourth and hear the translation. End with Heart of the Sunrise and notice how much of its drama comes from the silences."],"yes-close-to-the-edge":["Three tracks. The title piece, almost nineteen minutes in four parts, opens with birdsong and running water, erupts into frantic, apparently chaotic group playing, then settles into a song. Its third part, I Get Up I Get Down, falls almost still for choral voices before a pipe organ crashes in and leads to a triumphant return. And You and I is a folk-like hymn in several sections; Siberian Khatru is an energetic, riff-driven closer.","Recorded in London in 1972 with engineer Eddy Offord, who assembled the long piece from many short takes spliced on tape. Jon Anderson drew on Hermann Hesse's novel Siddhartha, about a young man's spiritual search by a river. It was the last record with Bill Bruford, who left for King Crimson just before the tour. On that tour, Yes walked on to the finale of Stravinsky's Firebird played over the speakers.","Listen closer: the organ in I Get Up I Get Down is a real church organ, recorded by Rick Wakeman at St Giles-without-Cripplegate in the City of London and fitted into the multitrack afterwards. The opening chaos is also structured: the guitar keeps returning to fixed phrases that come back later as the song's melodies, so the storm contains the calm in seed form.","Many fans and critics consider it the band's peak and a definitive example of the long progressive suite. Detractors, including the early punk press, made it the emblem of rock's pretensions: side-long songs, mystical lyrics, keyboards in capes. The splice-by-splice construction also raised the question, still asked, of whether this is composition or studio editing.","Take the title track whole and in order. Notice how it returns at the end to birdsong and water: the record is a circle. When the organ enters, think of the Firebird finale that opened their concerts and hear how Yes borrowed its sense of a slow, luminous ending that becomes a blaze."],"genesis-selling-england-by-the-pound":["Eight tracks about an England being sold off: Arthurian knights among supermarket offers, gang warfare in Epping Forest, a lawnmower-loving teenager. Dancing with the Moonlit Knight opens with Peter Gabriel's voice alone, then gathers into a fast, intricate band workout. Firth of Fifth begins with a long solo piano introduction by Tony Banks and climaxes on Steve Hackett's sustained, singing guitar solo. The Cinema Show ends in a long keyboard passage in an uneven metre.","Recorded in London in the summer of 1973 by Gabriel, Banks, Mike Rutherford, Hackett and Phil Collins, several of whom had met as pupils at Charterhouse school. Britain was sliding into inflation, strikes and the loss of manufacturing; the title was borrowed from a Labour Party slogan. Gabriel's stage costumes and stories were making the band a strong concert draw, and I Know What I Like gave them a British hit single.","Listen closer: the last track, Aisle of Plenty, brings back the melody of the unaccompanied opening and turns its question — where has my country gone — into a list of supermarket names and bargain offers, puns on the chains of the day. The record is a circle from lost Albion to the checkout. Banks's piano opening of Firth of Fifth was dropped from their concerts because he could not play it reliably live.","It was well reviewed and sold strongly in Britain. It is now often ranked as the finest Genesis album of the Gabriel era. Debates circle Gabriel's lyrics, clever and densely allusive to some ears, arch to others, and the long Battle of Epping Forest, which some find overcrowded with words.","Start with Firth of Fifth for the piano opening and Hackett's solo. Then listen to the whole album from the unaccompanied first line to the reprise at the end. Compare the English pastoral colour of the quiet passages with Vaughan Williams's Tallis Fantasia: both look for an old England inside a modern one."],"genesis-the-lamb-lies-down-on-broadway":["A double album of twenty-three tracks telling one surreal story. Rael, a young Puerto Rican graffiti artist in New York, is swallowed by a cloud over Times Square and wakes in an underground world of cages, grotesque creatures and seductive lamia, searching for his brother. The songs are shorter and harder than on earlier Genesis records, with more synthesizer, ambient interludes and a streetwise, sardonic vocal. The title song opens it on a strutting piano figure.","Written and recorded in 1974 in a rented country house in Wales and a mobile studio. Peter Gabriel insisted on writing nearly all the lyrics, and relations frayed; he also left for a period when William Friedkin, director of The Exorcist, discussed a screenplay with him, and his first child was born with complications. The long tour that followed staged the story with slides and costumes, and Gabriel left the band when it ended.","Listen closer: the sleeve credits Brian Eno with 'Enossification' of Gabriel's voice. Eno was working in the same studio complex and treated some vocals through his electronics. The brief instrumentals — The Waiting Room especially, an improvised passage of noise — are the band's nearest brush with the experimental rock of the time.","Reception was divided: the story was baffling and the double length forbidding, and some fans missed the pastoral Genesis. Its reputation has risen steadily, and it now stands as one of the few progressive concept albums with the ambition of a rock opera and a genuinely strange imagination. Arguments over the plot's meaning go on, and Gabriel has admitted he does not fully know either.","Treat the first side as a set of songs rather than a plot: the title track, Fly on a Windshield and Carpet Crawlers. Then follow Rael through the whole thing with a lyric sheet. Hear it next to Bowie's Diamond Dogs era and Eno's early solo work to catch the mid-seventies turn from pastoral to urban."],"jethro-tull-aqualung":["Eleven tracks split into two sides, the first a gallery of London street characters, the second titled My God and aimed at organised religion. The title track opens on a lurching, heavy guitar riff and a portrait of a destitute old man, then shifts to acoustic guitar and back. Ian Anderson's breathy, overblown flute and his acoustic guitar alternate with Martin Barre's electric lead. Locomotive Breath begins with a long piano introduction before a relentless riff kicks in.","Recorded in London at the end of 1970 and the start of 1971, in Island Records' new Basing Street studio, with a line-up that now included the keyboard player John Evan and bassist Jeffrey Hammond. The British folk revival and the blues boom were both part of Tull's background. Anderson's anger at the Church of England, and at the gap between its teaching and its establishment, shaped the second side.","Listen closer: the title character grew out of photographs of homeless men on the Thames Embankment taken by Anderson's then wife, Jennie, who also co-wrote the song. Anderson has always denied that Aqualung is a concept album, and that critics insisted it was provoked him to make Thick as a Brick as a parody of one. Listen for the short acoustic interludes that link the sides.","It became the band's best seller and gave rock radio a set of standards, Locomotive Breath and the title song among them. Some critics found the religious polemic heavy-handed; others hear the whole as the peak of flute-led hard rock. The question of whether it is a concept album, which Anderson resents, keeps being asked.","Start with the title track and notice the sudden shifts between electric riff and quiet acoustic verse. Then play My God and hear the church as antagonist. As a contrast, listen afterwards to a Bach chorale cantata such as Wachet auf, which is what the English church once meant musically."],"jethro-tull-thick-as-a-brick":["One continuous piece across both sides of an LP, broken only because the record had to be turned over. It begins with Ian Anderson alone on acoustic guitar singing the melody that will come back at the end, then passes through dozens of sections: folk ballads, hard-rock riffs, organ interludes, rhythmic breaks, a march, a stretch of glockenspiel and flute. The band keeps returning to a handful of themes, which gives the long span its shape.","Made in London in December 1971 with the new drummer Barriemore Barlow. After critics called Aqualung a concept album, Anderson set out to make, as he put it, the mother of all concept albums as a joke. The lyrics are attributed to Gerald Bostock, a fictional eight-year-old poet disqualified from a literary contest. The record reached number one in the United States.","Listen closer: the original sleeve was a twelve-page mock provincial newspaper, the St Cleve Chronicle, with the story of Bostock's disqualification, a review of the album itself, puzzles, and village news, written mostly by the band. The satire is aimed at small-town England as well as at progressive rock's pomp, and the parody works only because the music is so earnest in its craft.","It was a hit and a puzzle: some reviewers missed the joke, others missed the music behind it. Now it is often taken as both a sendup and one of the finest examples of the thing it mocks. Anderson returned to Bostock forty years later with a sequel, which revived the question of how serious the original ever was.","Hear it complete in one sitting, and listen for the opening acoustic melody's return at the very end. Keep a list of the recurring themes as they reappear. Then compare it with Genesis's Selling England by the Pound, another portrait of English life written with long form in mind."],"can-tago-mago":["A double album that moves from songs to chaos. The first record holds Paperhouse, Mushroom and Oh Yeah, tight grooves under Damo Suzuki's murmured, half-sung vocals, and the eighteen-minute Halleluhwah, a relentless funk beat from Jaki Liebezeit that never lets up. The second record goes into the dark: Aumgn and Peking O are collages of shrieks, organ, drum machine and radio, before Bring Me Coffee or Tea eases it back to earth.","Can were formed in Cologne in 1968 by musicians from outside rock: Holger Czukay and Irmin Schmidt had studied with Stockhausen, Liebezeit came from free jazz, Michael Karoli was a young rock guitarist. Suzuki was a Japanese traveller they had found busking in Munich. They recorded in a castle near Cologne, Schloss Nörvenich, on a two-track machine, improvising for hours and editing afterwards.","Listen closer: many of the album's pieces were cut and assembled by Czukay from long improvisations on tape — the way Stockhausen's studio assembled electronic works — so the grooves are partly edited, not simply played. On Oh Yeah the vocal runs partly backwards. Halleluhwah's drum pattern is played by hand, with metronomic precision, for over eighteen minutes.","Little noticed in Britain or America on release, it became one of the most cited records by later musicians: post-punk bands, Radiohead, electronic producers. Critics now treat it as the band's central statement and the core of what the English press called krautrock, a term the Germans disliked. The second record still divides listeners between those who hear adventure and those who hear noise.","Start with Halleluhwah and resist waiting for a change: let the drums put you in a trance. Then try Aumgn at night. Hear Terry Riley's In C next to it for another way of building music from repetition, and Stockhausen's Gesang der Jünglinge for the idea of a studio as a composer's instrument."],"can-ege-bamyasi":["Seven tracks, tighter and more song-shaped than Tago Mago. Pinch opens on a tense, busy drum pattern; Vitamin C is a lean, catchy groove with Damo Suzuki's cryptic lyrics; Soup lurches from a riff into several minutes of noise. I'm So Green is almost pop. Spoon, which closes it, rides on an early drum machine and a hypnotic organ line. Everything is built on Jaki Liebezeit's economical drumming and Holger Czukay's sparse, low bass.","Recorded in 1972 at Inner Space, the studio Can had built in a former cinema in Weilerswist near Cologne. Spoon had been written as the theme for a German television thriller, Das Messer, and became a hit single in West Germany; the money paid for the equipment. The title and cover come from a tin of okra from a Turkish grocery, a sly nod to Cologne's growing immigrant communities.","Listen closer: the album was finished under deadline pressure, and some of the tracks were pieced together from earlier jams by Czukay's tape editing. Listen to Spoon's drum machine: the band laid it under a live drummer, putting the human and the mechanical side by side a few years before Kraftwerk made the machine the whole point.","It sold better than Tago Mago in Germany and was admired but little bought abroad. Its reputation grew with each generation of admirers; hip-hop producers and indie rock bands have sampled and covered it. Critics sometimes call it the most accessible Can record, which undersells its strangeness.","Begin with Vitamin C and Spoon, then listen to Soup to hear how easily the band drops into chaos and climbs out. Follow the bass on its own for a whole track: it says very little and matters enormously. Pair with Neu!'s first album for the other face of the same Rhineland scene."],"neu-neu":["Six tracks by a duo, Klaus Dinger on drums and Michael Rother on guitar and bass. Hallogallo, ten minutes long, is the manifesto: a steady, straight, forward-driving beat, bright chiming guitars layered in loops, and almost no change of harmony, like driving on an empty motorway. The rest of the record moves between that pulse and its opposite: slow, washed-out pieces of drones and effects, water sounds, and scraps of tape.","Dinger and Rother had both passed briefly through an early Kraftwerk line-up in Düsseldorf in 1971. They recorded Neu! in a few days in Hamburg late that year with Conny Plank, the engineer who would shape much of German experimental rock. Their stated aim was music free of American and British models: no blues, no swing, no rock and roll heritage, just forward motion.","Listen closer: Dinger's beat, later called motorik, is not machine-made. It is a human drummer playing a very simple pattern with tiny, constant variations, which is why it breathes. Im Glück, the slow track, has the sounds of oars and water from a rowing boat. Listen for how Plank's mixing makes the guitars appear and fade as if passing by.","It sold only modestly at first. Its influence was delayed and immense: David Bowie and Brian Eno admired it, the motorik beat passed into post-punk, and much later into indie rock and electronic music. Critics now often place Hallogallo among the most consequential rock tracks of its decade. Its slower tracks are still less discussed than they deserve.","Listen to Hallogallo on headphones while walking or travelling, and let the beat carry you. Then compare it with Terry Riley's In C and Reich's Drumming: all three find variety inside strict repetition. Then hear Bowie's Low to see where the pulse went next."],"faust-faust-iv":["Eight tracks that swing between noise and song. The opener, Krautrock, is twelve minutes of a swelling drone of distorted guitars that takes several minutes before the drums finally enter and drive it forward. After that come songs with an odd, deadpan gentleness — The Sad Skinhead with its lopsided reggae-like bounce, the soft, dreamy Jennifer — alongside collages and fragments. The record keeps changing its mind about what it is.","Faust were assembled in 1971 in the Hamburg area by the journalist Uwe Nettelbeck and lived together in a converted schoolhouse in Wümme. By 1973 they had come to Britain and signed with the new Virgin label, which had sold their collage album The Faust Tapes at the price of a single. Faust IV was recorded at Virgin's Manor studio in Oxfordshire; Virgin dropped them soon afterwards.","Listen closer: the opening track mocks the British press label for German rock by taking it as its title. In 1972 the band had also backed Tony Conrad, a founding member of La Monte Young's drone ensemble, on his record Outside the Dream Syndicate, and the long unmoving drones of Krautrock carry that lineage from the New York minimalist underground into rock.","Sales were weak and the band broke up soon after, to reform decades later. It is now taken as one of the key German experimental rock albums and, oddly, one of the most listenable Faust records. Critics argue whether its gentleness betrays the band's anarchy or proves how wide it was.","Hear Krautrock with patience: the arrival of the drums is the event. Then go to Jennifer for the contrast. Pair it with La Monte Young's Trio for Strings to hear the drone in its original form, and with Can's Tago Mago for a sister band's approach to the same problem."],"soft-machine-third":["A double album with four pieces, one per side. Facelift, by bassist Hugh Hopper, is heavy fuzz organ and noise. Slightly All the Time is gentle, jazz-inflected and long-breathed. Moon in June is Robert Wyatt's, a drifting vocal suite with wandering, whimsical lyrics. Out-Bloody-Rageous by Mike Ratledge begins and ends with shimmering tape loops of electric piano, with a propulsive jazz-rock piece in between led by Elton Dean's saxophone.","Soft Machine came out of Canterbury, a psychedelic group of the UFO club era that had become a largely instrumental band moving toward jazz. Recorded in London in 1970 and released by CBS. That August, the band became the first rock group to play the BBC Proms at the Royal Albert Hall, a sign of how serious their music had come to seem. Moon in June was the last major piece Wyatt sang with them.","Listen closer: the tape loops at the start of Out-Bloody-Rageous work like Terry Riley's keyboard pieces, with interlocking phrases slowly drifting against each other. The band's original guitarist Daevid Allen had worked with Riley's tape-delay techniques in the mid-sixties. Moon in June also refers wryly to the band's own situation, with Wyatt singing about playing to audiences and missing home.","It did well for an unwieldy double album and remains the band's most admired record. The jazz press liked it more than the rock press did. Arguments are about balance: Moon in June is Wyatt's personal voice against a band moving away from songs, and many fans hear the album as the moment the group split in two.","Start with Out-Bloody-Rageous and listen for the loops dissolving into the band. Then hear A Rainbow in Curved Air by Terry Riley to find the source. Finish with Moon in June for Wyatt's voice, which led him on to his solo records."],"frank-zappa-freak-out":["A double album by the Mothers of Invention with Frank Zappa writing, arranging and leading. The first sides are satirical songs — Hungry Freaks, Daddy; Who Are the Brain Police?; parodies of doo-wop and teen ballads that are also loving recreations of them. The last side is The Return of the Son of Monster Magnet, a long, improvised collage of percussion, grunts and voices recorded late at night in the studio.","Released on Verve in 1966, produced by Tom Wilson, who had also produced Bob Dylan and the Velvet Underground. It was among the first rock double albums and one of the first debut albums conceived as a whole. Los Angeles's counterculture was taking shape, and Zappa set out to satirise both the straight world and the hippies about to arrive, while practising an older, stranger love of avant-garde music.","Listen closer: the sleeve prints a long list of people who shaped the band's music, and among the rock and rhythm-and-blues names sits Edgard Varèse, whose Ionisation Zappa had discovered as a teenager on a record he tracked down by mail. The percussion collage of the last side is Zappa's early answer to Varèse's all-percussion piece, filtered through a Hollywood studio.","It sold poorly and confused critics; later it was recognised as a pioneering concept album that inspired, among others, the Beatles as they began Sgt. Pepper. Opinions divide between those who hear it as sharp satire and those who find its mockery sour. Its avant-garde final side is now its most admired part.","Start with Hungry Freaks, Daddy and Who Are the Brain Police?, then skip to the final side. Hear Varèse's Ionisation next: then you understand what Zappa meant when he called himself a composer who used a rock band."],"frank-zappa-were-only-in-it-for-the-money":["Nineteen short tracks run together with spliced-in dialogue, tape tricks and sudden cuts, so the record plays as a single collage. The songs mock hippie conformity as much as the police and suburban parents: Who Needs the Peace Corps?, Flower Punk, Absolutely Free. Between them are fragments of whispered conversation and odd sound effects. The last piece, The Chrome Plated Megaphone of Destiny, is several minutes of avant-garde tape and percussion music with no rock at all.","Recorded in New York and Los Angeles in 1967, the year of the Summer of Love, by the Mothers of Invention. Zappa despised what he saw as hippie commercialism. The cover and gatefold, by Cal Schenkel, parodied the Beatles' Sgt. Pepper photograph, and the release was delayed and redesigned after legal worries. MGM/Verve also censored lines they found risky without Zappa's permission.","Listen closer: The Chrome Plated Megaphone of Destiny comes with a sleeve instruction to read Kafka's In the Penal Colony while listening. It is Zappa's homage to the musique concrète and electronic music of Varèse, whose Poème électronique had been played to crowds at the Brussels World's Fair a decade before. Zappa's 1984 remix, with newly recorded bass and drums, remains controversial.","Critics were impressed by its savagery and wit, and it has come to be heard as one of the great satirical records. Some find its attacks on hippies self-righteous; others hear it as an early warning about the commercial capture of rebellion. Fans argue about which mix to hear, and many prefer the original 1968 version.","Hear the whole record without stopping and treat the collage as music, not interruption. Then read Kafka and hear the last piece again. Compare it with Varèse's Poème électronique and The Beatles' Sgt. Pepper to catch the argument between art and pop."],"frank-zappa-hot-rats":["Six tracks, five instrumental. Peaches en Regalia opens it with a bright, intricate tune for layered woodwinds, keyboards and guitar, a miniature under four minutes. Willie the Pimp features Captain Beefheart's growled vocal over a long, snarling guitar solo from Zappa. The rest mixes long, jazz-leaning grooves with electric violin from Sugarcane Harris and Jean-Luc Ponty with composed, chamber-like interludes. Ian Underwood plays most of the wind and keyboard parts.","Zappa had disbanded the Mothers of Invention and recorded this in Los Angeles in 1969 as his first solo album. It was one of the first records made on sixteen-track tape, which let Underwood build whole woodwind sections himself. Released on Zappa's Bizarre label via Reprise, it arrived as rock and jazz were beginning to mingle, weeks before Miles Davis recorded Bitches Brew.","Listen closer: the layered woodwind lines in Peaches en Regalia, played by one man, recall the chamber writing of Stravinsky's Octet for winds, a composer Zappa revered alongside Varèse. The album is dedicated to his newborn son, Dweezil. It Must Be a Camel is full of angular, hard-to-sing intervals typical of Zappa's composed style.","It was well received and sold especially strongly in Britain. It is now often cited as one of the first jazz-rock albums and the best entry to Zappa. Some fans still debate whether the long solos or the composed miniatures are the heart of it; the 1987 CD remix altered some tracks and is still disputed.","Start with Peaches en Regalia and count how many instruments you hear, then remember it was mostly one player. Hear Willie the Pimp for Zappa the guitarist. Next, play Stravinsky's Octet: the same clear, bright wind textures."],"captain-beefheart-trout-mask-replica":["Twenty-eight tracks on a double album, credited to Captain Beefheart and his Magic Band. The guitars play interlocking, jagged lines that seem to ignore each other, in different metres and tempos, while the drummer follows none of them yet holds it together. Over this Don Van Vliet roars, recites, growls and croons surreal poetry in a deep blues voice. There are a cappella poems, field recordings, and bursts of free-jazz saxophone and bass clarinet.","Van Vliet, from California's Mojave Desert, was a friend of Frank Zappa since high school. He and the band lived for months in a small house in Woodland Hills, rehearsing under exhausting, often abusive conditions. Drummer John French transcribed Van Vliet's ideas, played on piano or whistled, into parts. Zappa produced it for his Straight label, largely recording the band live in one intensive session.","Listen closer: the parts are not improvised. For all their apparent chaos, the guitar lines were written, taught and learned note for note over months, so the collisions are composed. Van Vliet sang many of his vocals without headphones, following the faint leakage from the studio, which is one reason voice and band so often seem to drift apart.","It sold little and baffled many; some thought it a joke. It has gradually become a central text of experimental rock, admired by punk, post-punk and art-rock musicians. The debate over whether it is great music or an ordeal continues, and critics now also weigh the album against accounts of how Van Vliet treated the band.","Start with Ella Guru, Moonlight on Vermont and Pachuco Cadaver, the more song-like tracks. Try hearing each guitar separately. Then listen to Ives's Putnam's Camp, where two marching bands collide, and hear a similar idea of music as simultaneous, independent layers."],"roxy-music-roxy-music":["Nine tracks that sound like no-one else in 1972. Re-make/Re-model opens with party chatter, then a brash, chaotic rock band in which each player takes a brief solo break. Ladytron starts in near silence with oboe and synthesizer before exploding. Bryan Ferry's voice is arch, theatrical and vulnerable at once. Andy Mackay's saxophone and oboe, Phil Manzanera's guitar, and Brian Eno's synthesizer treatments give it an unsettling, collage-like texture.","Recorded in London in 1972 and produced by Peter Sinfield, lyricist of King Crimson. Ferry had studied fine art at Newcastle under the pop artist Richard Hamilton, and conceived Roxy Music as a work of art: glamorous, retro and modern at once. Eno had no training as a player; he treated the band's sound with a synthesizer and tape machines at the mixing desk.","Listen closer: in Re-make/Re-model the chorus spells out a car registration number, CPL 593H, the number plate of a car driven by a woman Ferry admired in the song. During the solo breaks each player quotes something, and the bassist plays the riff of the Beatles' Day Tripper. The sleeve looks like a glamour magazine, part of the art-school concept.","It surprised critics who found it both absurd and brilliant; it reached the UK top ten, and the single Virginia Plain followed. It is now often seen as the start of art rock's glam wing, a key influence on punk and new wave. Arguments centre on whether the pastiche is ironic or sincere.","Start with Re-make/Re-model and listen for each solo break in turn. Then hear Ladytron and notice the quiet opening. Compare with David Bowie's Ziggy Stardust from the same year: both use glam as a costume for something stranger."],"roxy-music-for-your-pleasure":["Eight tracks, darker than the debut. Do the Strand is a manic invitation to a new dance that does not exist. Editions of You is fast, with a sharp synthesizer solo from Eno. In Every Dream Home a Heartache is a slow, menacing love song to an inflatable doll that seems to end, then returns in a blast of guitar. The Bogus Man is nine minutes of repetitive, sinister groove, and the title track fades out on a haze of tapes.","Recorded in London in early 1973 and released that March. It was Brian Eno's last album with the band: his friction with Ferry and his growing stage profile led him to leave that summer. Ferry's lyrics had become more sardonic, about consumer luxury and loneliness. The cover shows the model Amanda Lear leading a black panther on a leash against a nighttime city skyline.","Listen closer: the false ending of In Every Dream Home a Heartache lasts several seconds before the band returns, now drenched in phasing, as if the song had come back from the dead. The Bogus Man's steady, trancelike groove is closer to Can than to British rock, and it points to the direction Eno would take on his solo albums.","Reviews were strong and it reached the UK top five. It is now often ranked as Roxy's finest album, the peak of Ferry and Eno together. Some prefer the lighter, more commercial Roxy that followed; most critics hear this as the band at its most daring.","Start with Editions of You for energy, then hear In Every Dream Home a Heartache in the dark and wait for the return. Then play The Bogus Man straight after Can's Ege Bamyasi and hear the kinship."],"david-bowie-space-oddity":["A five-minute song told as a dialogue between Ground Control and the astronaut Major Tom, who drifts away into space. It opens with strummed acoustic guitar and a countdown, then swells with strings and a lift-off. The small, buzzing electronic sound is a stylophone, a toy keyboard played with a stylus. The ending leaves Major Tom floating, cut off from Earth, as the orchestra rises and falls away.","Released as a single in July 1969, days before the launch of Apollo 11. Bowie had seen Stanley Kubrick's 2001: A Space Odyssey, and the title is a pun on it. Producer Gus Dudgeon took charge because Bowie's usual producer, Tony Visconti, disliked the song as a novelty. Rick Wakeman, later of Yes, played Mellotron on the session.","Listen closer: the song is less about space than about isolation; Major Tom's calm voice as he loses contact is chilling. In the middle eight, the chord sequence climbs as the strings swell, mimicking lift-off. Kubrick's 2001 had opened with Richard Strauss's Also sprach Zarathustra, and the song borrows its grand, slowly rising sense of cosmic awe.","It became Bowie's first hit and was reportedly played during British coverage of the moon landing, ironic given the lyric's ending. It was later re-released and reached number one in Britain in 1975. Critics now treat it as the first great Bowie song and a founding myth of his work; he returned to Major Tom in later songs.","Listen for the stylophone and the countdown. Then hear the opening of Also sprach Zarathustra and imagine the film Bowie had just seen. Follow Major Tom forward to Ashes to Ashes and Blackstar to see how the character haunted Bowie's work."],"david-bowie-ziggy-stardust":["Eleven songs loosely about Ziggy, a bisexual alien rock star who comes to an Earth with five years left to live, becomes a messiah, and is destroyed by his fans and his ego. Five Years opens it on a lonely drum pattern; Starman is a radio-ready anthem; Ziggy Stardust tells the story in a riff; Suffragette City is hard rock and roll; Rock 'n' Roll Suicide ends in a desperate, swelling crescendo: 'You're not alone.'","Recorded in London in late 1971 and early 1972 with the Spiders from Mars — Mick Ronson on guitar, Trevor Bolder on bass, Woody Woodmansey on drums — and co-producer Ken Scott. Glam rock was flourishing. Bowie's Starman performance on Top of the Pops in July 1972, his arm around Ronson, became a defining moment for a generation of British teenagers.","Listen closer: Mick Ronson arranged the strings, and his orchestral touches are everywhere. Starman's leap on the word 'star-man' echoes the octave leap of 'Somewhere' in Over the Rainbow. Ziggy drew on real figures, including the eccentric rock singer Vince Taylor and the Legendary Stardust Cowboy. Bowie killed Ziggy on stage at the Hammersmith Odeon in July 1973.","It made Bowie a star and has never really fallen from critical favour. Some find the concept thin — the album isn't a strict narrative — and the rock 'n' roll pastiche obvious. Others credit it with making theatrical identity and ambiguous sexuality central to rock. Its influence on punk, new wave and pop spectacle is hard to overstate.","Hear the whole album in order: the drum of Five Years at the start, the drama of Rock 'n' Roll Suicide at the end. Notice Ronson's guitar and strings. Then compare with Roxy Music's debut, released the same month, for the other face of glam."],"david-bowie-low":["Two halves. Side one is seven short, fragmentary songs and instrumentals, with processed drums that crack like gunshots, bright synthesizers and minimal lyrics about retreat and recovery: Sound and Vision, Always Crashing in the Same Car. Side two is four slow instrumentals. Warszawa is a dark, spacious piece with a wordless choir sung by Bowie in an invented language; Subterraneans ends with a lonely saxophone.","Recorded in the autumn of 1976 at the Château d'Hérouville near Paris and at Hansa Studios in West Berlin, after Bowie had moved to Berlin to escape cocaine addiction in Los Angeles. Tony Visconti co-produced; Brian Eno contributed synthesizers, treatments and ideas. Bowie and Eno both admired Neu! and Kraftwerk. RCA, expecting a commercial successor to Station to Station, was dismayed.","Listen closer: the drum sound came from an Eventide Harmonizer, a new digital effect, which Visconti used to drop the pitch of the snare and create a thudding crack. Bowie said that Weeping Wall drew on the interlocking mallet patterns of Steve Reich. Warszawa was built largely by Eno from a piano phrase during a few days when Bowie was away.","Reviews were mixed and RCA was unhappy, but it sold respectably. Its reputation rose quickly among musicians: post-punk, new wave, synth-pop and ambient all borrowed from it. It is now often named one of Bowie's greatest albums. In 1992 Philip Glass wrote his Symphony No. 1 on its themes, a rare classical tribute to a rock album.","Hear side one as a set of fragments, then sit with side two in the dark. Listen closely to the drum sound, which influenced a decade of production. Then hear Glass's Symphony No. 1 'Low' to see how a minimalist composer read it, and Reich's Drumming for Weeping Wall's source."],"david-bowie-heroes":["Ten tracks following Low's pattern: songs on the first side, instrumentals mostly on the second. The title song builds for six minutes on a droning organ, a chugging rhythm, and a sustained, feedback-laden guitar line, while Bowie's voice rises from calm to a desperate shout. Beauty and the Beast and Joe the Lion are jagged rock; Moss Garden features Bowie on Japanese koto, and V-2 Schneider salutes Kraftwerk's Florian Schneider.","Recorded at Hansa Studios in West Berlin in the summer of 1977, within sight of the Berlin Wall, with Tony Visconti and Brian Eno. Robert Fripp of King Crimson flew in and recorded his guitar parts rapidly, playing over backing tracks he had barely heard. Eno's Oblique Strategies cards shaped some decisions. It was the only one of the three Berlin albums made entirely in the city.","Listen closer: Visconti recorded the vocal of the title song with microphones at different distances and gated them so that the more Bowie shouted, the more the distant microphones opened, making his voice seem to fill the room. The lovers kissing by the Wall were Visconti and the singer Antonia Maass, a detail Bowie revealed only years later.","The title song was not a big hit on release but became one of Bowie's defining anthems; he performed it in West Berlin in 1987 near the Wall, and it was widely cited after his death. The album is sometimes seen as a more extroverted but less radical sequel to Low. Philip Glass also based a symphony on it.","Hear the title song on headphones and follow Fripp's guitar and the voice as they rise. Then listen to the instrumentals, especially Moss Garden. Compare with King Crimson's Red to hear Fripp in another setting, and with Low for the first panel of the diptych."],"brian-eno-here-come-the-warm-jets":["Ten songs of bright, strange, loud pop. Needles in the Camel's Eye opens on a wall of layered, droning guitars and a cheerful melody. Baby's on Fire is mocking and sinister, with a long, searing guitar solo by Robert Fripp. Cindy Tells Me and On Some Faraway Beach are sweet and elegiac. The title track ends it with a blissful wash of distorted guitars and faint voices, which prefigures shoegaze.","Recorded in London in 1973, soon after Eno left Roxy Music. He assembled players who would not normally work together, including Fripp, John Wetton, Phil Manzanera and Andy Mackay, and directed them by gestures and by dancing rather than by instructions. Eno, a non-musician who had studied art at Winchester, treated the studio and the band as raw material to shape.","Listen closer: Eno often wrote lyrics by singing nonsense syllables over the backing tracks, then finding words that fit the sounds, so the lyrics carry more rhythm than meaning. On Some Faraway Beach delays its vocal for more than two minutes. The layered guitars came partly from recording and treating a guitar many times over.","It sold modestly but reached the British top thirty and was praised by critics as witty and adventurous. It is now often seen as a model for art pop, new wave and later indie rock. Debate centres on the tension between the prettiness of the tunes and the abrasive textures, which some think Eno resolved better on later records.","Start with Needles in the Camel's Eye and Baby's on Fire. Then hear the title track as a picture of light rather than a song. Compare with Roxy Music's For Your Pleasure to hear what Eno took from and left behind in the band."],"brian-eno-another-green-world":["Fourteen short tracks, only five of them songs. The instrumentals are small, still landscapes of synthesizer, treated guitar and piano: the title piece, Sombre Reptiles, Becalmed. The songs are gentle and strange: St. Elmo's Fire with Robert Fripp's fast, twisting guitar solo, I'll Come Running, Everything Merges with the Night. The whole feels like a series of paintings rather than a rock record.","Recorded in London in 1975 with a few collaborators, including Phil Collins on drums, Percy Jones on bass, Fripp and John Cale on viola. Eno arrived at the studio without songs. He used Oblique Strategies, a deck of cards with instructions such as 'Honour thy error as a hidden intention', created with the painter Peter Schmidt, to make decisions and break deadlock.","Listen closer: the Oblique Strategies owe a clear debt to John Cage's use of chance procedures, which Eno had studied at art school. The title piece is barely more than a minute and a half and became the theme tune for the BBC arts programme Arena. Listen for the treated guitars, often recorded and slowed or altered so they no longer sound like guitars.","Warmly received, it has grown to be regarded as one of Eno's greatest records and a bridge between his song albums and his ambient work. Critics debate whether it is rock at all, and many hear it as the start of a new, texture-first kind of pop. Its influence on David Bowie's Low was immediate.","Listen from start to finish without skipping: the short tracks form a single journey. Notice the sudden change of mood between songs and instrumentals. Then hear Satie's Gymnopédie No. 1, which Eno admired, and Cage's Music of Changes to see the principles behind his methods."],"led-zeppelin-led-zeppelin":["Nine tracks that swing between Chicago blues played at enormous volume and moments of folk-guitar quiet. Good Times Bad Times opens with a clipped riff and a bass drum that seems to fire in triplets; You Shook Me and I Can't Quit You Baby slow the blues to a crawl; Dazed and Confused descends into a long, eerie middle section; Black Mountain Side is a solo acoustic guitar instrumental with tabla. The sound you recognise it by is space: loud, dry, and physically close.","Jimmy Page formed the band in 1968 from the wreckage of the Yardbirds, with singer Robert Plant, drummer John Bonham and the session bassist and arranger John Paul Jones. They recorded the album at Olympic Studios in London in the autumn of 1968, in roughly thirty-six hours of studio time, with Page producing and paying for it himself. That ownership let him negotiate an unusually generous deal with Atlantic Records before anyone at the label had heard a note.","Listen closer: during Dazed and Confused Page plays his guitar with a violin bow, producing moans and glissandi no pick can make. The record is also a lesson in borrowing. Two songs are by the Chicago bassist and writer Willie Dixon; Babe I'm Gonna Leave You and Dazed and Confused were later re-credited to include the folk writers Anne Bredon and Jake Holmes, whose versions the band had heard.","Rolling Stone's first review was scornful, calling the band a weaker cousin of Jeff Beck's group, and many rock critics held the line for years. Audiences paid no attention; the album sold steadily on the strength of the band's ferocious American tours. The borrowing question has never gone away: admirers hear transformation, detractors hear uncredited appropriation of Black and folk songwriters, and both have a case.","Start with Good Times Bad Times and listen only to Bonham's right foot. Then play Muddy Waters's or Otis Rush's versions of the Dixon songs next to Zeppelin's to hear what volume and slowness do to the blues. Black Mountain Side rewards headphones. The 2014 remaster is clean without being flattened."],"led-zeppelin-ii":["Nine tracks built around riffs rather than songs. Whole Lotta Love opens on a two-bar guitar figure, then breaks down into a middle section of theremin whoops, echoing drums and voice before the riff returns. Heartbreaker stops dead for an unaccompanied guitar solo; Moby Dick is a drum feature for Bonham; Ramble On and Thank You show the gentler, acoustic side. The record is tighter and harder than the first, and its riffs became the vocabulary of hard rock guitar for a decade.","Made on the run in 1969, between tour dates in Britain and America, in studios in London, Los Angeles, New York and Vancouver, then mixed by Page with engineer Eddie Kramer in New York. The band were already selling out large halls in the United States, and the album was built partly from riffs worked out on stage. Tolkien, whom Plant was reading, drifts into Ramble On.","Listen closer: the eerie delayed voice in Whole Lotta Love comes from studio echo that bled onto the tape and was kept rather than erased. Its words are closely modelled on Willie Dixon's You Need Love, recorded by Muddy Waters in 1962, and The Lemon Song reworks Howlin' Wolf's Killing Floor; both debts were eventually settled in court and credited.","It replaced the Beatles' Abbey Road at the top of the American album chart at the end of 1969, which critics read as a changing of the guard. Reviewers still divided: some heard empty bombast, others the most exciting guitar record since Hendrix. The later lawsuits made it a central text in arguments about how British rock treated the Black musicians it learned from.","Play Whole Lotta Love loud and follow the middle section as a piece of sound collage rather than a song. Then hear Muddy Waters's You Need Love and Howlin' Wolf's Killing Floor. Heartbreaker's unaccompanied solo is the moment many guitarists of the next generation started practising."],"led-zeppelin-iv":["Eight tracks with no title on the sleeve, only four symbols. Black Dog trades unaccompanied vocal lines with a riff that keeps wrong-footing the beat; The Battle of Evermore is a mandolin duet with the folk singer Sandy Denny; Stairway to Heaven grows over eight minutes from acoustic guitar and recorders to a full electric climax, getting faster as it goes. The album ends with When the Levee Breaks, a slow, enormous blues built on one of the most sampled drum sounds ever recorded.","Much of it was recorded in early 1971 at Headley Grange, a draughty former workhouse in Hampshire, using the Rolling Stones' mobile recording truck parked outside. Stung by reviewers who called them a hype, the band removed their name from the cover and let the music stand on its own. Page, a collector of occult books, designed his own symbol; the others chose theirs.","Listen closer: Bonham's drums on When the Levee Breaks were recorded in the stairwell of Headley Grange, with microphones hung high above the kit, which is why they boom like artillery. The song itself is a 1929 blues by Memphis Minnie and Kansas Joe McCoy about the great Mississippi flood. And the recorders at the start of Stairway are John Paul Jones, overdubbing several parts himself.","The album sold in the tens of millions and Stairway became the most requested song on American rock radio without ever being a single there. It later attracted mockery as the ultimate emblem of rock excess. In 2016 a Los Angeles jury rejected a claim that its opening copied the band Spirit's Taurus, a verdict upheld on appeal in 2020 in a case closely watched by songwriters.","Start with When the Levee Breaks on good speakers, then hear Memphis Minnie's original to measure the distance. Listen to Stairway as a single long crescendo, noticing where the drums first enter. Then play Going to California to hear how quiet this band could be."],"black-sabbath-black-sabbath":["The record opens with rain, thunder and a distant tolling bell, and then a guitar plays three notes: a low one, the same note an octave up, and a note a tritone away, the interval medieval theorists avoided. That slow, dragging riff is the title track and, for many, the birth of heavy metal. The rest mixes blues jams, a harmonica-driven song about a wizard, the bass-led N.I.B. and a long closing cover, all played at a crushing, deliberate pace.","Four working-class musicians from Aston in Birmingham, a city of factories and bomb sites, who had been playing blues as Earth. They renamed themselves after a horror film and recorded the album in about a day in October 1969 at Regent Sound in London, with producer Rodger Bain, essentially playing their live set. Guitarist Tony Iommi had lost two fingertips in a sheet-metal factory accident and played with homemade plastic caps and slackened strings.","Listen closer: the tritone at the heart of the title track is the same interval Saint-Saëns built into the retuned solo violin of Danse macabre, the classical shorthand for the devil. Bassist Geezer Butler has linked the band's darkness to his love of Holst's Mars, with its grinding ostinato. The album was released in Britain on Friday the 13th of February 1970.","Critics were contemptuous; Lester Bangs dismissed it in Rolling Stone and British reviewers treated it as a novelty. It sold regardless, through word of mouth among young listeners who recognised something new. Its reputation has only risen, and it now stands at the head of every genealogy of metal, doom and stoner rock.","Play the title track in the dark and let the first minute of rain run its course. Then play Holst's Mars and Saint-Saëns's Danse macabre and hear the family resemblance. N.I.B. shows Butler's bass leading rather than following. The original Vertigo vinyl mix is the one to seek."],"black-sabbath-paranoid":["Eight tracks, three of which became metal's bedrock. War Pigs opens with an air-raid siren and a slow, lurching riff before accelerating into an assault on the generals who send the poor to die. Paranoid is under three minutes of fast, driving guitar. Iron Man lumbers in on a distorted voice and a riff anyone can hum. Between them sit Planet Caravan, a hushed, jazzy drift with bongos, and Hand of Doom, a slow study of a soldier's heroin addiction.","Recorded in June 1970, only months after the debut, again with Rodger Bain, at Regent Sound and Island Studios in London. The Vietnam War was at its height and the band's audience was young, working class and draftable. The album was going to be called War Pigs; the label, nervous about the war, renamed it after the single, leaving a sleeve illustration of a warrior that no longer matched the title.","Listen closer: the title song was written in minutes as filler because the album was short, and became the band's biggest hit. Ozzy Osbourne's voice at the start of Iron Man is fed through an electronic modulator, and on Planet Caravan it is run through a rotating Leslie organ speaker, which is why it seems to swim around the room.","Critics again sneered and audiences again ignored them; it was a British number-one album. Its standing grew as punk, thrash and grunge musicians all named it as a source. War Pigs is now heard as one of the most direct anti-war statements in rock, a reading the band's satanic reputation long obscured.","Start with War Pigs and notice how the band shift tempo as one body, with no click track. Play Planet Caravan straight after Iron Man to hear the range. Compare Bill Ward's swing, learned from jazz drummers, with the stiffer metal drumming that followed."],"black-sabbath-master-of-reality":["The shortest and heaviest of the early Sabbath albums. Sweet Leaf, Children of the Grave, Lord of This World and Into the Void are built on riffs pitched lower than anything the band had played, thick and sludgy, with Bill Ward's drumming galloping underneath. Between them sit two short instrumentals, the classical-guitar miniature Orchid and the tiny Embryo, and Solitude, a quiet ballad with flute. After Forever is, unexpectedly, a defence of Christian faith.","Recorded in 1971 with producer Rodger Bain after a year of relentless touring. Iommi, playing through pain in his damaged fingertips, tuned his guitar down by three semitones to slacken the strings, and Butler followed on bass. The band were now hugely popular in America and held in contempt by most critics, a pattern that would define metal for twenty years.","Listen closer: the coughing that opens Sweet Leaf is Iommi, caught on tape after smoking in the studio, and the song is an unambiguous love letter to cannabis. The downtuning, born of an injury, is the album's real legacy: it gave the riffs their low, muddy weight, and nearly every doom and stoner band since has tuned down in imitation.","Reviewers were dismissive at release. Its influence surfaced in the 1990s, when Kyuss, Sleep, Monster Magnet and a whole scene of desert and doom bands treated it as scripture, and when grunge guitarists named it as a favourite. After Forever continues to complicate the lazy picture of Sabbath as a satanic band.","Play Children of the Grave loud and follow Ward's galloping drums. Then Orchid, to hear the classical guitar Iommi loved. Into the Void, the closer, is the template for doom metal: listen for the moment the slow riff finally doubles its speed."],"deep-purple-concerto-for-group-and-orchestra":["A three-movement concerto by Deep Purple's organist Jon Lord, in which the rock band takes the part of the solo instrument. The first movement sets band and orchestra against each other, taking turns and rarely combining; the second, slow movement is the most lyrical, with a sung section; the finale finally brings the forces together and features an extended drum solo inside the orchestral texture. The sound is part Hammond organ and electric guitar, part English film score.","Performed on 24 September 1969 at the Royal Albert Hall in London by Deep Purple and the Royal Philharmonic Orchestra, conducted by the composer Malcolm Arnold, who defended the project to sceptical players. Lord was classically trained and wanted to test whether the two worlds could meet on equal terms. It was one of the first concerts by the band's new line-up with Ian Gillan and Roger Glover.","Listen closer: the orchestra and band rarely play the same material at once; Lord wrote it as a debate, and much of the first movement is each side answering the other. The original score was lost; for a 1999 revival at the Albert Hall it was reconstructed by ear from the recording, and Lord took the chance to revise it.","Classical critics were polite to patronising, and some orchestra members openly resented the evening. Rock fans bought it, but the band soon turned hard toward the riff-based rock of In Rock, partly in reaction. Today it is valued as a sincere pioneer of rock-and-orchestra collaboration, flawed as a concerto but not as an idea.","Start with the second movement, where the blend works best. Then hear the finale for the moment when the whole orchestra and the band finally play together. Compare Metallica's 1999 concert with the San Francisco Symphony to see how far the idea travelled."],"deep-purple-machine-head":["Seven tracks in which Ritchie Blackmore's guitar and Jon Lord's distorted Hammond organ duel like two soloists in a Baroque concerto. Highway Star opens at full speed with solos for both; Smoke on the Water rides on a four-note riff in parallel fourths that every beginner guitarist learns; Lazy starts as a long organ improvisation and slides into a shuffle; Space Truckin' closes on a relentless riff. It is hard rock with a keyboard as heavy as the guitar.","Deep Purple went to Montreux in December 1971 to record in the town's casino with the Rolling Stones' mobile studio. During a Frank Zappa concert on 4 December, someone fired a flare gun, and the casino burned down. The band watched the smoke drift over Lake Geneva, then recorded the album in the corridors of the nearly empty Grand Hotel, and wrote a song about the whole affair.","Listen closer: Blackmore has said the guitar solo on Highway Star was built on a Bach-style chord sequence, and it moves through its harmonies in running patterns like a Baroque violin line, a decade before neoclassical metal made this a school. Lord put his Hammond organ through a Marshall guitar amplifier, which is why it growls rather than sings.","It was a British number-one album and Smoke on the Water became one of the most recognised riffs in rock. Critics treated the band as a lesser Zeppelin; guitarists never did. Its reputation rests less on the words than on the playing, and Highway Star is now cited as a founding text of speed and neoclassical metal.","Start with Highway Star and hear the organ solo and the guitar solo as a contest. Notice that the Smoke on the Water riff is two notes played together, not one. For the Bach connection, play the Toccata and Fugue in D minor on a big organ straight afterwards."],"queen-bohemian-rhapsody":["A six-minute song in sections with no chorus. It opens with unaccompanied multitracked voices, turns into a piano ballad in which a young man confesses a killing to his mother, gives way to Brian May's guitar solo, then bursts into a mock-operatic scene of dozens of voices trading names like Scaramouche, Galileo, Figaro and Beelzebub. A furious hard-rock section follows before the piano returns for a quiet, resigned coda and a single gong stroke.","Written by Freddie Mercury and recorded in 1975 for the album A Night at the Opera, named after a Marx Brothers film, with producer Roy Thomas Baker, across several studios including Rockfield in Wales. EMI thought a six-minute single unplayable on radio; the DJ Kenny Everett broadcast it repeatedly anyway. A cheaply made promotional film helped make it a hit and anticipated the music video.","Listen closer: the operatic section was built from dozens of overdubbed vocal passes by Mercury, May and Roger Taylor on sixteen-track tape, bounced down so many times that the tape was said to have worn nearly transparent. The name Figaro nods to Rossini and Mozart, and the section is a pastiche of Italian opera buffa ensemble, as much comedy as drama.","It spent nine weeks at number one in Britain and returned there after Mercury's death in 1991. Some critics thought it pretentious nonsense and still do. Mercury refused to explain the words; readings range from a confession of his sexuality to pure invention. Its appearance in the 1992 film Wayne's World made it a hit for a second generation of Americans.","Listen once straight through, then again counting the sections and noticing that none repeats. Follow May's guitar, which sings like a second voice. Then play the overture to Rossini's Barber of Seville and hear the comic energy Queen were parodying."],"rush-2112":["The first side is a twenty-minute suite in seven parts, from Overture to Grand Finale, telling a science-fiction story: in a future society run by the priests of the Temples of Syrinx, a young man finds an old guitar, discovers music, and is crushed when the priests reject it. Geddy Lee's high voice, Alex Lifeson's guitar and Neil Peart's elaborate drumming carry the drama. The second side holds five shorter, unrelated songs.","The Toronto trio had been told by their label, Mercury, that their previous album had sold badly and they should make something more commercial. They did the opposite, with producer Terry Brown, and it became their breakthrough. Peart, the band's lyricist, credited the story's inspiration in the liner notes to the writings of Ayn Rand, a dedication that dogged the band for decades.","Listen closer: near the end of the Overture the band briefly quote Tchaikovsky's 1812 Overture, a nod to the grandiose classical programme music the suite imitates. The Discovery section is Lifeson tuning and exploring an acoustic guitar, played as if for the first time, so the story's central event happens in sound rather than in words.","It saved the band's career and gave them a loyal audience that critics could never dent. Reviewers mocked the ambition and, especially in Britain, attacked the Rand dedication as politically suspect. Peart later distanced himself from Rand's politics while defending the story's point about individual creativity. Among musicians it is one of the most studied records in rock.","Hear the first side in one sitting as a single piece. Follow Peart's drums, which change pattern with each section. Then play the 1812 Overture for the quotation, and Yes's Close to the Edge for the side-long suite Rush were answering."],"ac-dc-highway-to-hell":["Ten songs built from the simplest materials in rock: open chords, a steady mid-tempo beat, and space. Malcolm Young's rhythm guitar and Cliff Williams's bass lock with Phil Rudd's drums; Angus Young's lead guitar stabs and squeals above; Bon Scott sings with a leering, joking rasp. The title track opens on a riff of three chords and a pause. Touch Too Much and Shot Down in Flames show the new, cleaner production that gave the band a larger audience.","The Australian band, led by the Scottish-born brothers Malcolm and Angus Young, had toured relentlessly in Britain and America. Atlantic wanted a radio hit, and after sessions with another producer were abandoned, the band worked in London with the South African producer Robert John 'Mutt' Lange, whose precise, layered backing vocals and clean separation of the instruments changed their sound. It was Bon Scott's last album; he died in London in February 1980.","Listen closer: the power of the band lies in what is left out. Malcolm's rhythm guitar mostly plays open chords with the notes let ring, and the drums rarely fill. Lange's gang choruses are stacked from many takes of the band's voices, a pop technique that makes the refrains feel like a crowd singing along.","It was the band's first American success, and its reputation as the best record of the Bon Scott years has only grown. Critics long treated AC/DC as crude; musicians, from punk to hip-hop producers, praised the groove. The title, meant as a joke about life on tour, drew protests from religious groups in America.","Start with the title track and count how much silence sits inside the riff. Listen to the rhythm section alone for a verse. Then play Back in Black to hear what the same producer did with a new singer a year later."],"ac-dc-back-in-black":["Ten tracks that open with the slow tolling of a bell. Hells Bells unfolds over a picked guitar figure before the band crash in; the title track rides a riff built on stops and hard accents; You Shook Me All Night Long has a chorus designed for stadiums. Brian Johnson's voice is higher and harsher than Bon Scott's. The production is spacious and exact, each guitar in its own speaker, the drums dry and enormous.","Made in the months after Bon Scott's death, when the band considered stopping altogether. They hired Brian Johnson, a singer from Newcastle, and recorded with Mutt Lange at Compass Point Studios in Nassau, in the Bahamas, in the spring of 1980. The all-black sleeve was a mourning band for Scott. Atlantic released it only five months after his death.","Listen closer: the bell on Hells Bells was cast specially for the band, and the whole record is shaped as a memorial, from the opening toll to the title song, which the Youngs framed as a tribute. The rhythm guitar is often recorded without heavy distortion; it is the precision, not the gain, that makes it sound huge.","It is among the best-selling albums in history. Critics who had dismissed the band grudgingly admitted its craft, and it is now a standard reference for rock production. Some fans still argue that the Scott era had more wit; almost no one argues with the riffs.","Play Hells Bells from the first toll and notice how long the band wait before coming in. Compare Johnson on Shoot to Thrill with Scott on Highway to Hell. For a cross-tradition view, set the bell beside Mussorgsky's Great Gate of Kiev, another piece that uses bells as monument."],"judas-priest-sad-wings-of-destiny":["Nine tracks in which Birmingham blues-rock turns into something new. Victim of Changes, nearly eight minutes long, moves through a stomping riff, a ballad section and a climax where Rob Halford's voice climbs to an operatic scream. The Ripper is a short gothic horror tale; Tyrant and Genocide are fast and harmonised; Epitaph is a piano ballad in the style of Queen. The two guitars of Glenn Tipton and K. K. Downing trade and harmonise lines throughout.","Judas Priest came from the same industrial West Midlands as Black Sabbath, and recorded this, their second album, for the small Gull label on a tiny budget in 1975. Hard rock in Britain was losing ground to punk and pub rock; the band pointed in the opposite direction, toward drama, precision and range, with Halford's classically inflected voice at the centre.","Listen closer: the twin-guitar harmony, two lead guitars playing the same line a third apart, is the sound that defined heavy metal for the next decade, from Iron Maiden to Metallica. Victim of Changes stitches together two earlier songs from different band members, which is why its halves feel like different pieces.","It sold modestly and the band left Gull, losing the rights to the recordings. Its reputation was made retrospectively, by the New Wave of British Heavy Metal bands and American thrash musicians who treated it as a textbook. Critics now often call it the first album of pure heavy metal, stripped of the blues.","Start with Victim of Changes and follow Halford's voice from croon to scream. Listen for the passages where both guitars play in harmony. Then play Iron Maiden's Hallowed Be Thy Name to hear where the sound went."],"judas-priest-british-steel":["Nine concise, hard-edged songs, most around four minutes, built on marching tempos and chanted refrains. Breaking the Law and Living After Midnight are short, catchy and simple; Metal Gods is a slow, mechanical stomp about robots taking over; Rapid Fire opens the record at a sprint. Compared with the band's earlier epics, everything is stripped down: the twin guitars play tight, clipped riffs and the production is dry and hard.","Recorded early in 1980 with producer Tom Allom at Tittenhurst Park, the country house near Ascot that had belonged to John Lennon and then Ringo Starr. The New Wave of British Heavy Metal was at its height, and Britain was entering the unemployment and industrial decline of the early Thatcher years; the title and the razor-blade sleeve pointed to the steel industry of the band's native Midlands.","Listen closer: many of the sound effects were homemade in the house. The band smashed milk bottles for the breaking glass in Breaking the Law and shook trays of cutlery to make the marching robots in Metal Gods. Halford's leather-and-studs stage image, adopted a little earlier, was fixed as metal's uniform around this record.","It was the band's commercial breakthrough and made them stars in Britain and America. Some fans felt the short songs were a sell-out after the ambition of the 1970s records; the band defended them as focus. It is now regarded as the record that set heavy metal's visual and sonic image for the 1980s.","Start with Metal Gods and listen for the cutlery. Then play Breaking the Law and notice how few notes the riff uses. Compare it with Sad Wings of Destiny to hear a band trading drama for punch."],"motorhead-ace-of-spades":["Under three minutes of speed. Lemmy Kilmister's bass opens alone, a distorted, galloping rumble played more like a rhythm guitar than a bass, before 'Fast' Eddie Clarke's guitar and Phil Taylor's drums hit at full tilt. Lemmy sings in a gravelly shout about gambling as a way of life, and midway the whole band stops dead for a moment before tearing back in. It is loud, fast and rough, with no ballad softness anywhere.","Lemmy had been a roadie for Jimi Hendrix and bassist in the space-rock band Hawkwind before forming Motörhead in 1975. The song, recorded with producer Vic Maile for the album of the same name, came out in 1980 as punk and the New Wave of British Heavy Metal were both on the rise, and Motörhead were one of the few bands both audiences accepted.","Listen closer: Lemmy plays chords on a Rickenbacker bass through a Marshall guitar amplifier, with the treble turned up, which is why it is so hard to tell bass from guitar. He always said Motörhead played rock and roll, not metal, and underneath the noise the song's shape is closer to Little Richard than to Black Sabbath.","It became Motörhead's signature and a staple of British pop culture. Critics were slow to take it seriously, but thrash musicians, notably Metallica's Lars Ulrich, a devoted fan, credited Motörhead with showing that metal could be as fast and raw as punk. It is now heard as the bridge between the two.","Play it loud and listen to the bass in the opening seconds. Then play Little Richard's Long Tall Sally and hear the same forward drive at half the volume. Follow it with Metallica's Master of Puppets to hear what the next generation took."],"van-halen-van-halen":["Eleven tracks, mostly short and bright, played with the energy of a bar band. Runnin' with the Devil opens on a throbbing bass and car horns; Eruption, under two minutes, is a solo guitar showpiece full of sounds no one had heard; a cover of the Kinks' You Really Got Me follows straight on. Ain't Talkin' 'bout Love and Atomic Punk show the heavier side. David Lee Roth's showman vocals and the high harmonies of Michael Anthony frame Eddie Van Halen's guitar.","The band came from Pasadena, California, where they had spent years playing parties and clubs. Produced by Ted Templeman for Warner Bros., the album was recorded quickly in 1977, mostly live in the studio with few overdubs. Eddie, son of a Dutch jazz clarinettist, had trained as a classical pianist as a child and built his own guitar from spare parts.","Listen closer: in Eruption, Eddie taps the fretboard with a finger of his picking hand, producing rapid cascades of notes. A passage near the end is often identified as coming from a violin étude by Rodolphe Kreutzer, a trace of his classical training. The piece was reportedly recorded almost by accident, when the producer heard him warming up.","It sold enormously and critics were largely charmed, if puzzled by the combination of party music and virtuosity. Guitar magazines treated Eruption as a revolution, and two-handed tapping swept through rock guitar within a few years. The debate since is whether the imitators diluted it; the original still sounds new.","Play Eruption and You Really Got Me as one piece, as the album does. Then hear the Kinks' original. Listen to Eruption next to Paganini's Twenty-Fourth Caprice to hear two instrumentalists re-inventing what their instrument can do."],"iron-maiden-the-number-of-the-beast":["Eight tracks driven by Steve Harris's galloping bass, a rapid three-note pattern like horses' hooves, and the harmonised guitars of Dave Murray and Adrian Smith. Run to the Hills tells the colonisation of America from both sides; the title track opens with a spoken passage from the Book of Revelation before Bruce Dickinson screams in; Hallowed Be Thy Name, seven minutes long, follows a condemned prisoner's last hour through slow opening, mounting speed and a long instrumental climax.","Iron Maiden came out of the East London pub scene as leaders of the New Wave of British Heavy Metal. This was their first album with Bruce Dickinson, a trained, operatic singer, recorded quickly in early 1982 with producer Martin Birch. Their mascot, a grinning corpse called Eddie drawn by Derek Riggs, appears on the sleeve as a puppet master controlling the devil.","Listen closer: Harris has said the title song grew from a nightmare after watching the film Damien: Omen II, and its imagery also draws on Robert Burns's Tam o' Shanter. The spoken introduction is by the actor Barry Clayton. The gallop in the bass is so central that it is now called, simply, the Maiden gallop.","It was the band's first British number-one album. In America, religious groups accused them of Satanism and held record burnings, which the band noted mostly helped sales; the songs, as Harris pointed out, warn against evil rather than praise it. It is regarded as one of the defining metal albums of the 1980s.","Start with Hallowed Be Thy Name and follow the tempo as it builds. Listen to the bass alone on Run to the Hills. Then hear Holst's Mars, whose relentless rhythm Maiden's gallop resembles."],"iron-maiden-rime-of-the-ancient-mariner":["A fourteen-minute retelling of Coleridge's 1798 poem, the closing track of the album Powerslave. It moves in sections: a driving opening, verses that paraphrase the poem's story of the sailor who shoots an albatross and brings a curse on his ship, a becalmed middle section of creaking timbers and a slow bass line under a spoken passage from the poem itself, then a rebuilding of speed through guitar solos to a final return of the opening theme.","Written by bassist Steve Harris, the band's main composer, and recorded with Martin Birch for Powerslave, released in 1984 at the height of the band's world touring. Harris had studied the poem at school. Heavy metal was by then a mass youth culture in Britain, Europe and America, and Maiden brought unashamedly literary subject matter to it.","Listen closer: the creaking and wind in the middle section are sound effects, played under Harris's bass line to depict the ship becalmed, a direct piece of programme music. The band quote Coleridge's own verse in the spoken passage, making it one of the few rock songs that set a canonical poem nearly as written.","Fans treat it as one of the band's greatest achievements; critics have alternately praised its ambition and laughed at a metal band reading Coleridge. English teachers have been known to play it in class. It stands with Rush's 2112 as proof that long-form narrative did not die with 1970s progressive rock.","Read the poem first, or at least its outline. Then follow the song as a story, noticing where the music stops to depict stillness. Pair it with the overture to Wagner's Flying Dutchman, another cursed sailor told in sound."],"metallica-master-of-puppets":["Eight tracks, most over six minutes, built like suites. Battery opens with gentle Spanish-sounding acoustic guitars that are suddenly overrun by fast, down-picked riffing. The title track, eight and a half minutes, frames a slow, melodic middle section with relentless riffs about addiction's control. Welcome Home (Sanitarium) builds from a ballad to fury; Orion is a long instrumental featuring Cliff Burton's melodic bass. The playing is extremely fast, exact and tight.","Metallica emerged from the San Francisco Bay Area thrash scene, which combined British metal with the speed of punk. The album was recorded in 1985 in Copenhagen with Danish producer Flemming Rasmussen and released by Elektra without a radio single or video. Bassist Cliff Burton was killed in a tour-bus accident in Sweden in September 1986, six months after its release.","Listen closer: Burton had studied music theory and loved Bach, and his classical influence is audible in the harmonised, contrapuntal lines of Orion and the chord progressions of the slow sections. The Thing That Should Not Be borrows from H. P. Lovecraft, and Welcome Home takes its setting from One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest.","It sold steadily with almost no promotion and is widely ranked as the greatest thrash album. In 2016 it became the first metal recording selected for the US Library of Congress's National Recording Registry. Debate among fans centres on whether it or its predecessor, Ride the Lightning, is the peak; almost no one places anything later above it.","Start with the title track and listen for the moment the riffing drops away to a quiet, clean guitar melody. Hear Orion for Burton. Then play a Bach organ fugue for the contrapuntal line of thinking that Burton brought to the band."],"metallica-metallica":["Twelve tracks, slower, heavier and simpler than the band's thrash albums. Enter Sandman builds from a quiet guitar figure to a huge, grinding riff; Sad but True is slower still; The Unforgiven and Nothing Else Matters are ballads, the latter with an orchestral string arrangement. The sound is massive and polished, with the drums and bass given more weight than ever. Known from its all-black cover as the Black Album.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1990 and 1991 with producer Bob Rock, known for commercial hard rock. The band wanted a record that would sound as good on radio as on stage, and the sessions were long and fractious. Released in August 1991, just before Nirvana's Nevermind, it arrived as hard rock was about to be remade by grunge.","Listen closer: the strings on Nothing Else Matters were arranged by the film composer Michael Kamen, who later conducted the band with the San Francisco Symphony in 1999. Much of the album's weight comes from what is removed: shorter songs, fewer riffs per song, and the bass, inaudible on the previous album, pushed well forward.","It sold enormously and became one of the best-selling albums in America. Many thrash fans accused the band of selling out, an argument that still runs. Critics who had ignored metal took it seriously. It is now seen as the point where metal entered the mainstream on its own terms.","Start with Enter Sandman and notice how slowly it builds. Then hear Nothing Else Matters for Kamen's strings. Compare with Master of Puppets to hear what the band gave up and gained."],"slayer-reign-in-blood":["Ten tracks in under half an hour, played at speeds that seemed barely possible. Angel of Death opens with a scream and a torrent of riffing; the songs run together with almost no pauses; the guitar solos of Kerry King and Jeff Hanneman are chaotic, deliberately atonal whirls. Raining Blood closes the record with a slow, pounding introduction, a burst of speed, and then the sound of heavy rain. Dave Lombardo's double-bass drumming drives everything.","Slayer came from the Los Angeles suburbs and were signed by Rick Rubin to Def Jam, a label then known for hip-hop. Rubin produced, giving them a clean, dry sound that made the speed intelligible. Columbia, Def Jam's distributor, refused to release it because of its content, and it came out through Geffen instead.","Listen closer: the album's chaotic guitar solos are not random; they use chromatic and whole-tone patterns more familiar from twentieth-century classical music than from blues. Angel of Death describes the crimes of Josef Mengele at Auschwitz; the band insisted it was a documentary account, and the accusations of sympathy it provoked were never supported.","Initially dismissed or ignored by critics and denounced by moral campaigners, it became a foundation stone of death metal and extreme music worldwide. Rubin's production has been praised as a model of clarity. Arguments continue over Angel of Death and whether depicting atrocity without comment is responsible.","Play it straight through in half an hour. Listen to Lombardo's drums alone for a minute. Then play Penderecki's Threnody for the Victims of Hiroshima, another work that finds a musical language for mass death."],"guns-n-roses-appetite-for-destruction":["Twelve tracks of hard, bluesy rock and roll. Welcome to the Jungle opens with a guitar echo like a siren and Axl Rose's scream; Paradise City builds from a singalong to a fast, frantic ending; Sweet Child o' Mine opens on a looping, bell-like guitar figure by Slash and becomes a love song. Elsewhere the band sing about addiction, violence and life on the Los Angeles streets with a sneer that recalls the Rolling Stones and punk.","Guns N' Roses formed in Los Angeles in 1985, living in poverty on the Sunset Strip, where glam metal bands were dominant. They were signed by Geffen and recorded the album with producer Mike Clink. Its original sleeve painting was withdrawn after complaints and replaced by a cross of skulls. For almost a year it sold slowly, until MTV began to play their videos.","Listen closer: the famous guitar figure that opens Sweet Child o' Mine began as a joke finger exercise Slash played in rehearsal, jumping between strings. The guitars of Slash and Izzy Stradlin are placed hard left and right, like the Stones' Keith Richards and Ron Wood, and trade roles constantly.","It became the best-selling debut album in American history and Sweet Child o' Mine reached number one. Critics were split between those who heard the return of dangerous rock and roll and those who heard misogyny and aggression, and both views persist. It is often credited with ending the reign of glam metal and preparing the way for grunge.","Start with Welcome to the Jungle and listen for the two guitars answering each other. Then Sweet Child o' Mine. Play it next to the Stones' Let It Bleed and Aerosmith, and you will hear where the swagger came from."],"ozzy-osbourne-blizzard-of-ozz":["Nine tracks built around the guitar of Randy Rhoads. Crazy Train opens on a fast, jagged riff and a solo full of classical-sounding runs; Mr. Crowley begins with a solemn church-organ introduction and has two long guitar solos; Dee is a short piece for classical guitar alone; Revelation (Mother Earth) builds from a piano ballad to a fast, dramatic climax. Ozzy Osbourne's plaintive voice is the same, but the music around it is lighter, faster and more ornate than Sabbath.","Osbourne was fired from Black Sabbath in 1979 and was thought finished. He formed a new band with Rhoads, a young Californian guitarist who taught at his mother's music school in Burbank, and recorded the album in England in 1980 with bassist Bob Daisley and drummer Lee Kerslake. Rhoads was killed in a light-aircraft crash in Florida in March 1982, aged twenty-five.","Listen closer: Dee, written for Rhoads's mother Delores, is a small piece of classical guitar music in the style of the Spanish Romantics, and Rhoads's electric solos draw on scales and harmonic sequences from the Baroque and Romantic periods. Mr. Crowley takes its subject from the occultist Aleister Crowley, and its organ introduction was played by Don Airey.","It restored Osbourne's career and made Rhoads a guitar hero; his early death froze his reputation at its height. It is now seen as a key record in bringing classical technique into metal, preparing the neoclassical school. Suicide Solution was the subject of a 1980s lawsuit blaming it for a death; the case was dismissed.","Start with Mr. Crowley, and listen to the solos as composed pieces with a beginning, middle and end. Then play Dee and set it beside Tárrega's Recuerdos de la Alhambra. Finish with Crazy Train for the riff."],"yngwie-malmsteen-rising-force":["Eight tracks, mostly instrumental, in which the Swedish guitarist plays at speeds that set a new standard. Black Star opens with a short classical guitar introduction before the electric theme; Far Beyond the Sun is a long, fast instrumental in the harmonic minor scale; Icarus' Dream Suite, which Malmsteen gave a mock opus number, is built on the famous Adagio in G minor. His guitar sounds like a violin: bright, vibrato-heavy, and full of arpeggios swept across the strings.","Malmsteen grew up in Stockholm and moved to Los Angeles in 1983, where he played briefly with two American metal bands before this debut under his own name. Its line-up included the keyboard player Jens Johansson and drummer Barriemore Barlow, formerly of Jethro Tull. It arrived when guitar virtuosity was the subject of magazines, schools and instructional videos.","Listen closer: Malmsteen's models were not guitarists but Paganini and Bach; he has said he learned to hear the guitar as a violin after watching a television programme about Paganini. The Adagio in Icarus' Dream Suite is usually attributed to Albinoni but was largely composed in the twentieth century by Remo Giazotto, so the neoclassical source is itself a neoclassical piece.","Guitarists were overwhelmed; critics accused Malmsteen of empty speed and excess, and the charge has followed him. It founded neoclassical metal and a school of 'shred' players. Opinion has settled that the debut is his most musical record and that his influence on technique is beyond dispute.","Start with Icarus' Dream Suite and play the Adagio in G minor straight after. Then hear Paganini's Twenty-Fourth Caprice and listen for the arpeggios in both. Far Beyond the Sun is the showpiece; listen for the harmonic minor scale's exotic step."],"megadeth-rust-in-peace":["Nine tracks of fast, intricate thrash, packed with riffs that change every few bars. Holy Wars... The Punishment Due opens with a rapid, twisting riff and moves through a Spanish-sounding interlude; Hangar 18, about alien bodies hidden by the US government, is almost all guitar solo trading between Dave Mustaine and Marty Friedman; Tornado of Souls holds one of the most admired guitar solos in metal. The playing is precise, the tempos restless.","Mustaine, fired from Metallica in 1983, formed Megadeth and fought addiction through the 1980s. For this album he assembled a new line-up with guitarist Marty Friedman, a shred player who drew on Japanese and Middle Eastern scales, and drummer Nick Menza. It was released in 1990, at the end of the Cold War, and much of it is about nuclear weapons, religious conflict and government secrecy.","Listen closer: Holy Wars was prompted by the conflict in Northern Ireland, which Mustaine saw on a visit. Friedman's solos use exotic scales and bent notes that sound almost like a koto or an oud rather than blues guitar, which is why they stand out so sharply from Mustaine's more chromatic playing.","Critics praised it as the band's most disciplined album and it remains widely regarded as one of the high points of thrash. It is often set against Metallica's Black Album of the following year: one band refining thrash's complexity, the other stripping it away.","Start with Holy Wars and follow the riffs as they change. Listen to Tornado of Souls for Friedman's solo. Then play Metallica's Master of Puppets to hear two ex-bandmates' rival versions of thrash."],"pantera-vulgar-display-of-power":["Eleven tracks at mid-tempo rather than thrash speed, built on stop-start, syncopated riffs that hit like punches. Mouth for War and A New Level open with heavy, grinding grooves; Walk stomps on a lurching riff that the whole band plays together; This Love and Hollow move from quiet ballad passages into explosions. Phil Anselmo's voice ranges from a clean croon to a hardcore shout; Dimebag Darrell's guitar is thick, precise and full of squealing harmonics.","Pantera came from Arlington, Texas, where they had spent the 1980s as a glam metal band before changing direction with Anselmo on vocals. Produced by Terry Date and released in 1992, just as grunge was overturning hard rock, the album offered a harder alternative. Dimebag Darrell was shot dead on stage in Ohio in 2004, and his brother, drummer Vinnie Paul, died in 2018.","Listen closer: much of the heaviness comes from rhythm rather than speed. Walk's riff sits in a lopsided shuffle that seems to drag against the beat, and the band often stop dead for a beat before hitting again, a technique borrowed from hardcore punk. Dimebag's guitar had its mid-range scooped out, which gives it a hard, dry bite.","It sold strongly without radio support and made Pantera one of the most influential metal bands of the 1990s. Critics credit it with founding groove metal and influencing nu-metal. Its aggression and some of Anselmo's later public conduct have complicated the band's legacy.","Start with Walk and follow the riff's lurch. Then play Mouth for War for the stop-start rhythm. Compare with Black Sabbath's Master of Reality to hear how slowness and weight came back into metal after thrash."],"emperor-in-the-nightside-eclipse":["Eight long tracks in which fast, tremolo-picked guitars and blasting drums are wrapped in layers of synthesizer choirs and strings. The result is less a riff than a wall of sound: the guitars shimmer rather than chug, the voice is a high shriek buried in the mix, and above it all the keyboards play slow, grand chord sequences like a church organ or film score. Into the Infinity of Thoughts opens with a long, atmospheric build before the storm breaks.","Emperor were teenagers in the Norwegian black metal scene of the early 1990s, which rejected commercial death metal and was marked by church arsons and violence; two members were later imprisoned. The album was recorded in 1993 at the Grieghallen studio in Bergen with the engineer Eirik 'Pytten' Hundvin, who recorded much of that scene. Ihsahn, the principal composer, was eighteen.","Listen closer: the keyboards carry most of the melodic content, often moving in slow chords that resemble late-Romantic orchestral music, while the guitars supply a blur of texture. The studio sits inside the concert hall named after Edvard Grieg, and Ihsahn has cited classical and film music, as well as Bathory and Slayer, among his models.","Initially an underground cult record, it came to be seen as the founding text of symphonic black metal. Critics now regard it as one of the key extreme metal albums; the criminal history of the scene around it remains a dark shadow over any appreciation of its music.","Listen on headphones and let the texture wash over you rather than following the riffs. Focus on the keyboards and hear the harmonic movement beneath the noise. Then play Sibelius's Tapiola for another Nordic forest in sound."],"meshuggah-destroy-erase-improve":["Tracks built on riffs that repeat in odd, shifting lengths while the drummer's cymbal keeps a steady four-beat pulse, so the music seems to fold over itself. Future Breed Machine opens the album with a mechanical, stuttering riff and robotic samples. Between the riffs, Fredrik Thordendal plays fluid, jazz-fusion solos that sound utterly unlike metal. Jens Kidman's voice is a flat, rhythmic bark, used almost like another drum.","Meshuggah came from Umeå in northern Sweden and recorded this, their second album, for the German label Nuclear Blast. It appeared as extreme metal was fragmenting into subgenres. The band's interest lay in rhythm and structure more than speed or darkness, and they drew on jazz fusion, especially the guitarist Allan Holdsworth, as much as on metal.","Listen closer: tap your foot to the cymbal, then try to count the guitar riff. The riff's length does not divide into the bar, so it drifts against the pulse until the two realign many bars later, a technique closer to Steve Reich's phasing or Conlon Nancarrow's player-piano studies than to metal convention.","Little noticed at first outside metal circles, it is now seen as the seed of the 2000s 'djent' movement and of progressive metal's rhythmic turn. Musicians and theorists study the band's polymeters, and critics who once found them cold now praise their rigour.","Start with Future Breed Machine and follow the cymbal, not the guitar. Then listen for Thordendal's solos, which sound like Mahavishnu Orchestra dropped into a metal record. Compare with Reich's Clapping Music, another piece built on patterns sliding against one another."],"opeth-blackwater-park":["Eight long pieces, most around ten minutes, that alternate death-metal fury with acoustic folk and progressive-rock passages. Mikael Åkerfeldt switches between a death growl and a clean, melancholy singing voice; distorted riffs give way to fingerpicked guitars and quiet piano, then return. The Drapery Falls and the title track are built like extended suites with recurring themes, and Harvest is a gentle, almost pastoral song in the middle of the storm.","Opeth came from Stockholm and had spent the 1990s mixing death metal with 1970s progressive rock. For this, their fifth album, they worked with producer Steven Wilson of the British prog band Porcupine Tree, who added keyboards and studio textures. The title comes from an obscure early-1970s progressive band that Åkerfeldt admired.","Listen closer: Åkerfeldt's acoustic passages borrow from Swedish folk melody and 1970s English progressive rock, and themes introduced quietly early in a song often return later with full distortion. Wilson's production smooths the switches between extremes, so the joins between fury and calm are almost invisible.","It brought Opeth international recognition and is widely regarded as one of the key progressive-metal albums. Critics who disliked death growls found it a gateway, while metal purists sometimes found it too soft. Its approach influenced a generation of bands mixing extreme and progressive styles.","Start with Harvest, then play The Drapery Falls and notice how the acoustic theme at the start returns. Listen to the title track as a whole suite. Compare with King Crimson's Red for the progressive rock ancestry."],"tool-lateralus":["Thirteen tracks, some long and interlinked, in which Danny Carey's intricate drumming, Justin Chancellor's melodic bass and Adam Jones's textured guitar build slowly shifting structures in unusual meters. Schism moves through changing time signatures; the title track, nearly ten minutes, grows from a quiet bass line to a vast climax. Maynard James Keenan's voice floats above in a calm, measured way rather than shouting. Short ambient pieces link the longer songs.","Tool came from Los Angeles and were one of the most successful hard-rock bands of the 1990s while avoiding conventional promotion. Lateralus, released in May 2001, entered the American charts at the top despite its length and complexity. The band toured with King Crimson later that year, acknowledging a debt to that band's interlocking guitar and rhythm parts.","Listen closer: the title track is built on the Fibonacci sequence. Its main pattern cycles through meters of nine, eight and seven beats, and 987 is itself a Fibonacci number; the syllables of the vocal lines follow the sequence too. The same numbers have been traced, controversially, in Bartók's Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta.","Schism won a Grammy for best metal performance. Critics were impressed by the ambition and some found it overlong. Among musicians it is a standard text for odd meters in rock. Its mathematical structures have been debated in countless articles and fan analyses.","Start with Schism and try to count the beats as they change. Then play the title track and notice how it grows. Compare with Bartók's Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta and King Crimson's Red, both built from the same instinct for pattern."],"the-stooges-fun-house":["Seven tracks, recorded almost as the band played them live: four men in a room, turned up past the point of politeness. Side one is a run of blunt, grinding riff songs, Down on the Street, Loose, T.V. Eye and the slow, smouldering Dirt. Side two adds Steve Mackay's honking tenor saxophone and falls apart on purpose, ending in L.A. Blues, five minutes of pure feedback, screaming and free-jazz squall. The sound you remember is Ron Asheton's wah-soaked guitar grinding against Iggy Pop's howl.","The Stooges came out of Ann Arbor, Michigan, a college town near Detroit, where their neighbours the MC5 were preaching revolution. Their first album, produced by John Cale in 1969, had sold poorly. For the second, Elektra sent them to Los Angeles in May 1970 with Don Gallucci, once the organist of the Kingsmen, who had the sense to strip the studio bare and record the band standing together at full volume, much as they sounded on stage in a Detroit ballroom.","Every song was taken many times over, and in 1999 an archive box set released the complete sessions: take after take of the same handful of songs. Listening to it reveals how deliberate the chaos was. The band played these riffs night after night, and the album takes were chosen from runs that sound nearly identical. What seems like a band collapsing is a band holding a very tight groove very hard.","It sold even worse than the debut, and by 1974 the Stooges had broken up amid drugs and debt. Mainstream critics largely ignored it. Its reputation was built by the people it inspired: the London and New York punks of 1976, who treated it as scripture, and later writers such as Lester Bangs. Today it is often ranked above the debut, though some still prefer the leaner songs of the first record or the rawer Raw Power.","Start with T.V. Eye at a volume that slightly worries you; listen to how the drums and bass lock into one repeated figure while the guitar sprays over the top. Then play Fun House itself and follow the saxophone, which turns a rock band into something close to a free-jazz session. Compare it with Coltrane's Ascension to hear what Iggy was reaching for."],"patti-smith-horses":["Eight tracks in which a poet takes over a garage band. Patti Smith half-sings, half-chants, drifting from verse into free association while Lenny Kaye's guitar, Richard Sohl's piano and a plain rhythm section build slowly underneath. Short songs such as Redondo Beach, a lopsided reggae lilt, sit beside long improvised pieces: Birdland and the nine-minute Land, which swerves into an old dance-craze hit. The opening, where her own poem collides with the rock standard Gloria, is the moment everyone knows.","Smith had spent the early seventies in New York as a poet, playwright and rock critic, reading her verse over Kaye's guitar at St Mark's Church. By 1975 her band was a fixture at CBGB alongside Television, and Clive Davis signed her to his new Arista label. The album was recorded at Electric Lady Studios with John Cale of the Velvet Underground producing, a relationship both later described as combative. Robert Mapplethorpe took the plain black-and-white cover photograph.","Birdland grew from a book, Peter Reich's memoir of his father, the psychoanalyst Wilhelm Reich, and a boy's fantasy of being lifted away by a spaceship; Smith improvised it largely in the studio. Elegie, the closing track, is a quiet memorial to Jimi Hendrix, whose studio they were working in. Tom Verlaine of Television plays guitar on Break It Up, a song about a dream of Jim Morrison.","Critics received it as an event: a woman fronting a rock record with the authority of Dylan and the rawness of the garage bands, and doing so as a poet. It did modestly in the charts but became a founding text for the punk scene in both New York and London. The only lasting dispute is over Cale's production, which some hear as too polished for what the band did on stage.","Play Gloria first and listen for the point where the band finally catches up with her voice and the song ignites. Then give Land its full length without interruption; it is built like a jazz solo, rising and falling on its own logic. For comparison, hear Dylan's Bringing It All Back Home, the record that first taught rock how to sound like poetry."],"ramones-ramones":["Fourteen songs in under half an hour, almost none longer than two and a half minutes, no guitar solos, no slow numbers, every one counted in with a shouted one-two-three-four. Johnny Ramone plays nothing but fast downstroked chords, a steady buzz like a machine; Dee Dee's bass doubles him; Tommy's drums never show off. Over it, Joey sings sweet, simple bubblegum melodies about boredom, glue-sniffing, girls and comic-book violence. Blitzkrieg Bop, with its stadium chant, is the calling card.","Four young men from Forest Hills, Queens, all taking the surname Ramone, had honed their act at CBGB on the Bowery since 1974. Seymour Stein signed them to Sire, and the album was cut in February 1976 at Plaza Sound, inside the Radio City Music Hall building, for a few thousand dollars. It arrived at the height of long progressive-rock albums and polished soft rock, and on purpose it sounded like the opposite of both.","Producer Craig Leon mixed the record like an early Beatles album, with the guitar hard in one speaker and the bass in the other. Put on headphones and you can hear the band pulled apart into its parts. Among the originals sits a cover of Let's Dance, a 1962 hit for Chris Montez: the Ramones saw themselves as a sixties pop band played too fast, not as vandals.","It sold poorly in America and got little radio play, but critics in New York and London took notice at once. When the band played the Roundhouse in London on 4 July 1976, much of the future British punk scene was reportedly in the audience or nearby. Its standing has only risen since; the debate now is whether its best songs are simply great pop, which the band always claimed.","Listen straight through; the album is designed to be heard as one breathless rush, and the sameness is the point. Then listen for Joey's melodies, which are gentler and more tuneful than the noise suggests; I Wanna Be Your Boyfriend is practically a girl-group song. Try it on headphones to hear the split-channel mix, then play Marquee Moon to hear the other road out of CBGB."],"ramones-rocket-to-russia":["The Ramones' third album keeps the formula, fourteen short songs at a gallop, but gives it a cleaner, brighter sound and a much stronger pop hook. Rockaway Beach is a surf song, Sheena Is a Punk Rocker a teen anthem, and the band covers two early-sixties dance records, Do You Wanna Dance? and the Trashmen's Surfin' Bird. Between them come comic songs about lobotomies, family misery and a dance called the Cretin Hop. The guitar still buzzes, but the melodies now come first.","Recorded in the summer of 1977 and produced by Tommy Ramone with Tony Bongiovi, it came out as British punk was peaking and Sire was pushing hard for an American radio hit. The band wanted to be the Beach Boys and the Ronettes as much as the Stooges, and this is the record where that ambition is clearest. Its release was overshadowed by Elvis Presley's death that August, and it never became the hit the label hoped for.","Rockaway Beach was written by Dee Dee Ramone, the band's surfer, about the beach at the end of the A train in Queens; it is a deliberate tribute to the Beach Boys, with the handclaps and the stacked harmony vocals, played at double speed. Joey sings every harmony himself, overdubbed, which is why the backing vocals sound so eerily like him.","Reviews were warm and many critics now call it the band's most complete album, the point where the joke and the craft balance perfectly. It sold better than the first two but still disappointed the band. The central argument about the Ramones, whether they were a primitive gesture or a great songwriting band, is usually settled in favour of songwriting on the strength of this record.","Start with Rockaway Beach and Sheena Is a Punk Rocker back to back, and hear how much sixties pop is hiding inside the noise. Then compare their Surfin' Bird with the 1963 original: the Ramones barely changed it, which says everything about where they came from. Pair it with the Buzzcocks' singles for the British cousin."],"television-marquee-moon":["Eight songs built around two guitars that talk to each other rather than one leading and one strumming. Tom Verlaine and Richard Lloyd weave clean, bright, trebly lines in and out like a string duet, while Fred Smith and Billy Ficca play with a jazz lightness. Verlaine's voice is thin and strained, his words oblique. The ten-minute title track is the centrepiece: a clipped, interlocking riff that opens out into a long solo climbing steadily towards a shimmering peak, then settles back.","Television had helped invent the CBGB scene in 1974, persuading the club's owner to let them play original songs. Their early bassist Richard Hell left to form his own band, taking the torn-shirt look that London would copy. By the time Elektra signed them, they were the scene's most musically ambitious band. The album was recorded in New York in late 1976 with Andy Johns, known for his work with Led Zeppelin, co-producing with Verlaine.","Listen to how rarely the two guitars play the same thing. The parts are composed to interlock, so that each fills the rests in the other; the effect is closer to a Bach invention than to rock rhythm guitar. Verlaine loved Albert Ayler and Coltrane, and his long solos grow from a small motif the way a jazz improvisation does. The title track is reportedly a first take.","It sold modestly in America but reached the British top thirty, where the young post-punk bands heard it as proof that punk did not have to be crude. Critics' polls have kept it near the top of the decade's rock albums ever since. The argument it still provokes is whether Television were a punk band at all, or the last great guitar band of the sixties tradition.","Play the title track first and resist skipping: track the two guitars, one in each speaker, and notice when the solo begins to climb. Then try See No Evil and Venus for the tighter, song-sized version of the same interplay. Afterwards play the Ramones' debut, made a few blocks away the same year, to hear how wide the scene was."],"sex-pistols-never-mind-the-bollocks":["Twelve songs, including all four singles, played at mid-tempo rather than the breakneck speed people remember. Steve Jones's guitars are layered into a thick, glossy wall; Paul Cook's drums are steady and heavy. Over it, Johnny Rotten sneers, rolls his r's and spits consonants, turning songs about the monarchy, the record industry, abortion, boredom and bad holidays into accusations. Holidays in the Sun opens with the sound of marching boots; Anarchy in the U.K. is the declaration of intent.","Managed by Malcolm McLaren, who ran a clothes shop on the King's Road, the Pistols had been dropped by EMI after swearing on teatime television and by A&M after a week. Virgin finally released the album in October 1977, after the singles had already caused national scandal. Glen Matlock, who co-wrote most of the songs, had been replaced by Sid Vicious, who could barely play. Chris Thomas, who had worked with Roxy Music and on Pink Floyd's Dark Side of the Moon, produced.","Most of the bass is not Sid Vicious but Steve Jones, who overdubbed guitar after guitar and much of the bass line as well; Vicious reportedly appears clearly only on Bodies. The album's power comes from careful studio work by a craftsman producer, which is the quiet joke under punk's amateur myth. The band's attack on the industry, E.M.I., is a meticulously polished product of it.","Record shops that displayed it were threatened with prosecution under indecency laws; a Nottingham case against a Virgin store failed. It went to number one in Britain regardless. Some punks found it too slick, and the band split within three months. Its standing as one of rock's most influential records is secure; the argument is whether its songs or its surrounding scandal mattered more.","Play Holidays in the Sun and Anarchy in the U.K. loud and listen to the guitars; there are far more of them than a four-piece should have. Pay attention to Rotten's phrasing, which bends every word into sarcasm. Then play Pink Floyd's Dark Side of the Moon, made in the same studio culture four years earlier, and hear what the Pistols were attacking."],"sex-pistols-god-save-the-queen":["A three-minute single that borrows the title of the national anthem and turns it against the Crown. A chugging, mid-paced riff, doubled and redoubled on guitar, drives under Johnny Rotten's vocal, which treats the monarchy as a tourist attraction propping up a dead system and ends with a chant denying the country any future. The tune is catchy, almost a pub singalong, which is exactly what made it dangerous.","It was released in May 1977 to coincide with Queen Elizabeth II's Silver Jubilee, when Britain was hung with bunting. A&M had pressed copies and then dropped the band, destroying most of them; Virgin put it out instead. The BBC banned it, many shops refused to stock it, and on 7 June the band played it from a boat on the Thames, ending in arrests. Jamie Reid's sleeve defaced the Queen's official portrait with ransom-note lettering.","The song was written before the Jubilee was on anyone's mind, under the title No Future; the timing was McLaren's marketing coup. And the official chart is itself part of the story: the single stalled at number two behind Rod Stewart in Jubilee week, while many in the industry believed it was actually outselling him, and the suspicion of a fix became part of its legend.","Members of the band were attacked in the street in the weeks after release. For its defenders it was the most direct political statement a British pop record had made; for its critics it was provocation for sale. Decades later it is played on the BBC it was banned from, and the argument has shifted to how far its rebellion was a manager's shop-window stunt.","Listen once for the riff and the tune, which are much more conventional than their reputation. Then listen for Rotten's delivery, how he stretches and snarls single words. Afterwards play Walton's Crown Imperial or Elgar's first Pomp and Circumstance march, the ceremonial music of the same monarchy, to hear precisely the sound this record set out to puncture."],"the-clash-the-clash":["Fourteen fast, shouting songs about being young and broke in London: dead-end jobs, tower blocks, riot, boredom with American culture. Joe Strummer's hoarse bark alternates with Mick Jones's higher, more tuneful voice; the guitars are thin, trebly and urgent. Then, in the middle of side two, the band slows to a crawl and plays a six-minute cover of Police and Thieves, a Jamaican reggae song, and the whole record changes colour. White Riot and London's Burning are the anthems.","Formed in 1976 around Strummer, Jones and Paul Simonon, the Clash signed to CBS for a large advance, which some punks saw as betrayal. The album was cut in a few weekends at CBS's London studio with their sound engineer Mickey Foote producing, and Terry Chimes on drums. They lived in west London near the Notting Hill Carnival, where the 1976 clashes between young Black Londoners and police had shaped their politics, and they heard reggae constantly.","Police and Thieves was written and sung by Junior Murvin and produced by Lee Scratch Perry at his Black Ark studio in Kingston in 1976. The Clash made no attempt to play it like Jamaicans; they played it as a punk band feeling for a heavy offbeat, and the gap is audible. Perry reportedly disliked the cover at first, but it led directly to their working together within months.","British critics hailed it at once as the definitive punk album. CBS in America judged it too crude and held it back until 1979, releasing an altered version; meanwhile it became one of the best-selling import records in the United States. Arguments continue over which version is better and whether the band's later sophistication improved on this directness.","Start with Janie Jones and London's Burning to catch the rush, then go straight to Police and Thieves and hear the band switch gears. Listen to how Strummer and Jones trade lines like two street-corner speakers. For the source, hunt down Junior Murvin's original Police and Thieves, and hear how Perry's production floats where the Clash's version marches."],"the-clash-complete-control":["A furious three-minute single in which the Clash complain about their own record company and their own management. Mick Jones's guitar builds in sheets of chords, Topper Headon's drums push hard, and Strummer's shouted verses give way to a chorus the band yells together. Near the end the song boils over: Strummer calls out Jones's guitar by name, and Jones answers with a ragged, overdriven solo that sounds like the tape is about to split.","CBS had released Remote Control from the band's first album as a single without asking them, and the song is their public reply, aimed also at manager Bernie Rhodes and at the business of punk itself. It was produced by Lee Scratch Perry, the Jamaican producer, who was in London in the summer of 1977 working with Bob Marley. The Clash had covered Perry's Police and Thieves; this was the result of their meeting.","What you hear is not quite what Perry left. He reportedly tamed the guitars and brought up the drums and bass in the Jamaican manner, and the band and Mickey Foote later remixed it to push the guitars back to the front. Listen underneath, though, and the echo on the drums and the space around Simonon's bass still carry Perry's fingerprints: a dub producer's ears on a punk record.","Critics at the time called it one of the best singles of the year, and many still rank it as the Clash's finest. It reached the lower half of the British top forty. Strummer himself pointed to it as the moment the band stopped just reacting and started commenting on its own position. It is often cited as the first real collaboration between punk and reggae production.","Listen for the build: the song keeps promising a release and withholding it until the final minute. Pay attention to the drums, which have a boomy, roomy echo quite unlike the dry first album. Then compare with the Clash's Police and Thieves, and afterwards with London Calling, where this reggae sensibility becomes part of their whole language."],"the-clash-london-calling":["A double album of nineteen songs that treats punk as one style among many. There is rockabilly (Brand New Cadillac), ska (Rudie Can't Fail), reggae (Paul Simonon's own Guns of Brixton), New Orleans rhythm and blues with horns (Jimmy Jazz), soul, and pure pop (Train in Vain). The title track opens it: a marching, ominous rhythm, stabbing guitar and Strummer's warning voice announcing floods, meltdowns and war. The sound is fuller and warmer than before, with piano, organ and a brass section.","By 1979 the Clash had broken with their manager, were in debt to CBS and had lost their rehearsal room. They rehearsed in a garage-studio in Pimlico and recorded at Wessex Studios in north London with Guy Stevens, a wild veteran producer who threw chairs and ladders to raise the energy. Britain had just elected Margaret Thatcher; unemployment was rising; the Three Mile Island nuclear accident was fresh. They insisted the double album be sold for the price of a single.","Train in Vain, the most radio-friendly song, is not listed on the original sleeve: it was added so late that the artwork had already been printed. The cover photograph by Pennie Smith, Simonon smashing his bass on stage in New York, sits under pink and green lettering copied from Elvis Presley's 1956 debut album, a deliberate claim to the whole rock and roll lineage.","It was a critical triumph on both sides of the Atlantic and gave the band their first American hit. Because it was released in the United States in January 1980, Rolling Stone later named it the best album of the 1980s. Some early punks saw its range as a retreat into rock tradition; that view has almost vanished, and it is now the band's most celebrated record.","Hear it as a double album, a side at a time. Begin with the title track, then Guns of Brixton for the reggae bass, then Lost in the Supermarket for Mick Jones's softer pop. Notice how the horns and piano change what a punk band can be. Afterwards play the band's first album to hear how far they travelled in two and a half years."],"buzzcocks-spiral-scratch":["A four-song seven-inch EP, about ten minutes long, recorded in an afternoon. Howard Devoto's flat, arch voice sneers over Pete Shelley's scratchy guitar and a rhythm section that barely holds together. Breakdown and Time's Up are tight bursts of nerves; Boredom is the famous one, its whole guitar solo made from two notes repeated over and over, a joke on the virtuoso rock solo that is also genuinely thrilling. Friends of Mine closes it at a sprint.","Devoto and Shelley, students in Bolton, had travelled to London to see the Sex Pistols in early 1976 and then booked them into Manchester's Lesser Free Trade Hall that summer, gigs now credited with starting the city's music scene. With money borrowed from family and friends they recorded Spiral Scratch in December 1976 and released it themselves on their own label, New Hormones, in January 1977. Martin Hannett, credited as Martin Zero, produced.","The back cover credits list how many takes and overdubs each song needed, as if the band were proud of how little work had gone into it. That honesty was part of the message: here was a record anyone could make. Hannett, just beginning, would go on to produce Joy Division, and Devoto left the Buzzcocks within weeks to form Magazine.","The first thousand copies sold out by mail order and through a handful of shops, and it was repressed many times. It is now regarded less for its music than for what it proved: that a band could bypass the record industry entirely. The independent labels of the next decade, Factory and Rough Trade among them, are its direct descendants.","Play Boredom and count the notes in the solo; then notice how exciting two notes can be when the band behind them is this nervous. Listen to how thin and cheap the recording sounds, and hear that as part of the point. Follow it with Ever Fallen in Love, made after Devoto left, to hear Shelley's pop gift take over."],"buzzcocks-ever-fallen-in-love":["A two-and-a-half-minute love song at punk speed. John Maher's drums open with a rolling tom pattern, the guitars kick in with a descending hook, and Pete Shelley sings in a high, pleading voice about loving someone who is wrong for you, circling back to a title question he cannot answer. There is no solo to speak of; the song is chorus, verse, chorus, with the guitar hook repeated between, and it ends almost before you notice.","Released in September 1978 and included on the band's second album, Love Bites, it was produced by Martin Rushent for United Artists, part of a run of singles that made the Buzzcocks the great pop band of British punk. Shelley, now the singer, wrote about love from a deliberately unspecified point of view; he was openly bisexual, and his songs rarely say whether the beloved is a man or a woman.","The title phrase reportedly came from the 1955 film musical Guys and Dolls, which Shelley caught on television while staying in a guesthouse in Edinburgh; a line of dialogue lodged in his head. Listen, too, to the guitars: there are two parts, one chiming, one chugging, and the tension between them gives the song its sense of being pulled two ways.","It reached the British top twenty and became the band's best-known song, later covered by Fine Young Cannibals and many others. Critics now treat Buzzcocks' singles as proof that punk could make perfect pop, and Shelley's ungendered love songs are increasingly written about as a quiet landmark in queer pop history.","Listen for the drum pattern at the start, which is as memorable as the tune. Then follow Shelley's voice as it climbs in the chorus, straining just enough to sound sincere. Put the Buzzcocks' singles compilation Singles Going Steady on next, then the Ramones' Rocket to Russia, for the American version of the same fast, sweet pop."],"wire-pink-flag":["Twenty-one songs in thirty-five minutes, several under a minute, one under thirty seconds. Wire take punk's economy to the point of abstraction: a song states an idea, repeats it once and stops, often mid-thought. Colin Newman's clipped voice, Bruce Gilbert's blocky guitar chords and Robert Gotobed's dead-straight drumming leave a lot of air. Some songs are pop (Mannequin), some are drones (the title track), some are just a riff and a shout (12XU). The abrupt endings are the thing you remember.","Wire came together among art-school students in London in 1976, arriving at punk from painting, graphic design and conceptual art rather than from rock and roll. EMI's Harvest label, home of Pink Floyd, signed them and assigned the producer Mike Thorne. They played at the Roxy club in Covent Garden, where the scene was already hardening into a uniform, and they set out, in their own account, to remove everything from rock that was not necessary.","The shortness is a method, not a mood: songs end the moment their idea is used up, which makes the album feel like a sketchbook of possible punk songs. Listen to Three Girl Rhumba; its opening guitar figure became, nearly twenty years later, the basis of Elastica's hit Connection, a borrowing settled out of court.","It sold little on release, and some critics found it too cold. Its reputation grew steadily as American hardcore bands took up its brevity (Minor Threat covered 12XU) and as later British bands cited it. It is now often named as the first great art-punk album and the bridge from punk to post-punk.","Listen straight through without pausing; the effect accumulates. Notice how each song ends: rarely on a big chord, usually just stopping. Try Ex Lion Tamer and Mannequin for the pop side, then the title track for the drone. Then play Pink Floyd's Echoes, twenty-three minutes long on the same label, to hear the idea Wire were arguing against."],"wire-chairs-missing":["Wire's second album keeps the short-song discipline but slows down and opens up. Synthesizers and keyboard washes appear; guitars are treated with echo and chorus; songs such as French Film Blurred and Mercy stretch into slow, circling moods. Outdoor Miner, a gentle, strange pop song about an insect that burrows through leaves, is the heart of it, with a piano line added for the single. I Am the Fly and Practice Makes Perfect keep the angular bite.","Made at Advision Studios in London in 1978, again with Mike Thorne, who now played keyboards on the record and effectively became a fifth member. By then punk's first wave had burned out and the British post-punk scene, with Joy Division, Magazine and Siouxsie and the Banshees, was forming. Wire, never comfortable in punk's uniform, used the studio as an instrument, influenced by the art-rock records of Roxy Music and Brian Eno.","The single of Outdoor Miner was lengthened for radio by adding a new middle section, a rippling piano solo played by Thorne, because the album version was judged too short to be played on air. It is a nice irony for the band of the twenty-eight-second song: they had to pad their best pop single to make it respectable.","Reviews were mixed at first, with some punks hearing betrayal in the synthesizers and slower tempos; others heard progress. It now stands with Pink Flag and 154 as one of a trio of albums often called the most inventive run of post-punk records. Later bands from R.E.M. to My Bloody Valentine cited it.","Start with Outdoor Miner, then French Film Blurred, and listen to how much space is left in the sound. Notice the synthesizer colours Thorne adds without ever taking over. Compare the sound world with Brian Eno's Another Green World, released three years earlier, which showed rock musicians how to build songs from texture."],"joy-division-unknown-pleasures":["Ten songs that sound as though recorded in an empty concrete building at night. Stephen Morris's drums are clipped and precise, Peter Hook's bass plays melody high up the neck, Bernard Sumner's guitar comes and goes in bursts, and Ian Curtis sings in a deep, level baritone about loss of control, isolation and dread. Disorder opens at a nervous gallop; She's Lost Control, with its hissing machine-like drums, and Shadowplay are the best known. Silence and echo are as present as the instruments.","Joy Division formed in Salford and Manchester after the Sex Pistols' 1976 gigs there, and first called themselves Warsaw after a track on David Bowie's Low. Tony Wilson's new independent label Factory released the album in June 1979, recorded at Strawberry Studios in Stockport with Martin Hannett. Manchester was a city of empty mills and redevelopment; Curtis, working at a job centre, had recently been diagnosed with epilepsy.","Hannett treated the band as raw material. He had Morris record drums piece by piece, fed them through a new digital delay unit, added sounds of breaking glass and a lift shaft, and pushed everything into cavernous space. The band, who were loud and heavy on stage, reportedly disliked the result. Peter Saville's sleeve shows radio signals from the first pulsar ever discovered, stacked into a white-on-black mountain range.","Reviews were strong and it sold steadily through independent shops, though it was not a hit. Its reputation grew enormously after Curtis's death in 1980. The long-running argument is Hannett's: whether he made the record or stole it from the band. Most listeners now accept that the eerie space he created is inseparable from what the album means.","Start with Disorder and pay attention to Hook's bass, which carries the tune while the guitar does the rhythm. Then play She's Lost Control and New Dawn Fades. Listen on good headphones to hear Hannett's space around each instrument. Next, try Bowie's Low, the record that named them, and Closer, where the sound grows darker still."],"joy-division-closer":["Nine songs, darker and more varied than the debut. Atrocity Exhibition opens with tribal tom-toms and a guitar that sounds like metal being scraped; Isolation is driven by a thin, bright synthesizer pulse; Heart and Soul is a slow, circling trance. The album ends with two slow pieces, The Eternal and Decades, built on hushed keyboards and a funeral pace, with Curtis's voice deeper and more resigned than ever. The last side is the part people remember.","Recorded in March 1980 at Britannia Row in London, a studio owned by Pink Floyd, again with Martin Hannett. The band had toured hard; Curtis's epilepsy was worsening under heavy medication, and his marriage was failing. On 18 May 1980, the night before Joy Division were due to fly to their first American tour, he took his own life at twenty-three. Closer was released two months later, and everyone heard it as a farewell.","The sleeve, designed by Peter Saville with a photograph of a tomb in Staglieno Cemetery in Genoa, was chosen before Curtis died; the funereal image that seems to speak so directly was an accident of timing that upset the band. Listen too for the synthesizers, which Sumner and Morris were just beginning to use; they point straight towards the electronic music of New Order.","It was received with reverence, shaded by grief, and reached the British top ten. Critics have long debated how much to read the lyrics as a suicide note; the band and Curtis's family have resisted that reading, pointing out that the songs were written as art. It is often placed beside Unknown Pleasures as one half of post-punk's central statement.","Hear side two as one piece: Heart and Soul through Decades, without interruption. Then go back to Isolation and notice how close the rhythm is to dance music, the seed of what came next. Listen to Siouxsie and the Banshees' Juju, made a year later, for another take on the same gothic space."],"joy-division-love-will-tear-us-apart":["A three-and-a-half-minute single in which a love song is turned inside out. The drums rattle fast and bright, Peter Hook's bass plays the melody, and a thin, high synthesizer line hangs over it like a second voice. Curtis sings in a calm, tired baritone about a marriage going cold, about routine and resentment, and the title line comes back like a verdict. It is the most tuneful thing Joy Division made, and the saddest.","Recorded at Strawberry Studios in early 1980 with Martin Hannett, and released by Factory in June, a month after Curtis's death. Curtis was then married, with a baby daughter, and had begun a relationship with another woman; the song is widely heard as being about his own marriage. It reached the British top twenty, the band's first real hit, and Curtis never saw it.","The title is usually taken as a bitter reply to Love Will Keep Us Together, a sunny 1975 hit by the Captain and Tennille. The title is also the inscription on Curtis's memorial stone in Macclesfield, chosen by his widow. There are two different recorded versions, and the one on the single is not the one the band first cut.","It has become Joy Division's signature, voted repeatedly among the best singles ever made in British polls and covered by artists from Paul Young to Swans. Some fans prefer the band's more severe album tracks, but few dispute that this is where Curtis's writing and the band's playing meet most perfectly.","Listen for how the song is built: the bass carries the tune, the guitar is almost incidental, and the synthesizer line is what stays in your head. Then listen to Curtis, who never raises his voice. Next hear New Order's Blue Monday, made by the same three survivors three years later, and notice the thread of Hook's bass running straight through."],"public-image-ltd-metal-box":["An hour of music built on bass. Jah Wobble's deep, round bass lines repeat for minutes on end, like Jamaican dub; the drums lock onto them; over the top, Keith Levene's guitar clangs, scratches and shimmers like broken glass, and John Lydon wails and chants. Albatross opens with ten minutes of this; Poptones rocks gently while Lydon narrates something grim; Swan Lake, also known as Death Disco, twists a famous ballet tune into a howl of grief. Songs dissolve into long grooves rather than choruses.","After the Sex Pistols collapsed in early 1978, Lydon, dropping the name Rotten, formed Public Image Ltd with Levene, who had been an early member of the Clash, and Wobble, a non-musician friend who learned bass from reggae records. Lydon had already chosen reggae and dub records for a well-known London radio show. Virgin released the album in November 1979 as three twelve-inch discs at 45 rpm inside a film-canister tin, to get the deepest possible bass.","In Swan Lake, Levene's guitar plays, distorted and out of shape, the famous oboe theme from Tchaikovsky's ballet, while Lydon sings about watching his mother die of cancer. Listen, too, for the drums: the band went through several drummers during the sessions and Levene played some parts himself, which is why the kit sounds different from track to track.","Critics called it a radical break, and it quickly became the model for a whole strand of post-punk that treated rock as a groove to be taken apart. The tin was impractical and damaged the records, and it was soon reissued as a double album, Second Edition. The main debate now is which of the three players was most responsible for its sound.","Put on Albatross, turn up the bass until you can feel it, and let it run; listen to how little the bass line changes. Then play Swan Lake and try to catch the Tchaikovsky theme inside the noise. Afterwards go back to Can's Tago Mago, a record Lydon admired, and to any King Tubby dub album you can find."],"gang-of-four-entertainment":["Twelve songs that sound like funk played by a band that distrusts pleasure. Dave Allen's bass and Hugo Burnham's drums lay down tight, danceable grooves; Andy Gill's guitar cuts across them in short, metallic slashes, then stops dead, leaving holes. Jon King's lyrics, often argued between two voices, pick apart love, leisure, advertising and the Northern Ireland conflict. Damaged Goods and At Home He's a Tourist are the singles; Anthrax, which ends it, opens with a minute of pure feedback.","The band met as students at Leeds University, where the art school was steeped in Marxist theory, Situationism and the politics of Rock Against Racism. Leeds in the late seventies was marked by unemployment and National Front marches. Their first EP came out on the small Fast Product label in 1978; EMI signed them, and this album followed in September 1979, taking its title as an ironic comment on what a pop record is supposed to be.","On Anthrax, two vocals run at once in separate channels: in one, King sings a love song that refuses to be a love song; in the other, Gill speaks flatly about why the band do not write love songs. Listen on headphones and you can follow either. The band walked away from a Top of the Pops appearance rather than change a single word in At Home He's a Tourist about contraceptives.","Reviews were excellent, sales modest. Its influence proved enormous: American bands from R.E.M. and the Red Hot Chili Peppers to the dance-punk revival of the 2000s cited Gill's guitar and Allen's bass. Some find its politics heavy-handed; the usual defence is that the music makes the argument, since the gaps in the rhythm are as political as the lyrics.","Start with Damaged Goods and listen to the guitar: count the silences. Then play Anthrax on headphones and switch your attention between left and right. For the kinship, hear Weill and Brecht's Threepenny Opera, whose method of making the audience think rather than swoon is the same idea in theatre."],"talking-heads-fear-of-music":["Eleven songs, most titled with a single plain word: Mind, Paper, Cities, Air, Heaven, Animals, Drugs. David Byrne's anxious, yelping voice worries at ordinary things as if they were threats. The music is tense and twitchy, full of stiff funk rhythms and odd textures. I Zimbra opens it with African-style percussion and a chant of nonsense syllables; Life During Wartime, a nervous dance song about living underground in a city at war, is the best known; Heaven is a slow, quiet ballad about a place where nothing happens.","Talking Heads came out of the Rhode Island School of Design and the CBGB scene of 1975. This was their second album produced with Brian Eno, and it was recorded partly in the loft of drummer Chris Frantz and bassist Tina Weymouth, using a mobile recording truck parked outside. New York in 1979 was a city of arson, near-bankruptcy and blackouts, and that unease runs through the lyrics.","The words of I Zimbra are not an African language at all: they come from Gadji beri bimba, a sound poem by the Zurich Dada poet Hugo Ball, written in 1916. Robert Fripp plays the chiming guitar on it. The sleeve is black and embossed to feel like the diamond-plate metal of a subway grating, an idea of keyboardist Jerry Harrison.","Reviews were strongly positive and it did respectably in America. Critics have often treated it as the transitional record between the art-punk of the early Heads and the polyrhythmic Remain in Light, and it is sometimes overshadowed by both. More recent writers have argued that it is their most original and unsettling album.","Start with Life During Wartime, then I Zimbra, then Heaven, to hear the album's three moods: panic, rhythm and stillness. Listen to how Eno places odd sounds at the edges of the mix. Then play Eno's Another Green World and Bowie's Heroes, where Fripp's guitar does something similar."],"talking-heads-remain-in-light":["Eight tracks, each built from many short, interlocking parts, guitars, bass, percussion, keyboards, that repeat without changing chords, stacked until they form a dense, shifting pattern like a West African drum ensemble. Byrne sings in a stream of half-sermon, half-list over the top, often answered by a chorus of voices. Born Under Punches and Crosseyed and Painless are driving funk; Once in a Lifetime, with its preacher's cadence and floating chorus, is the famous one; the last side turns dark and slow.","Talking Heads and Brian Eno went to Compass Point Studios in the Bahamas in 1980 with no songs, only the idea of building tracks from grooves. Byrne and Eno had been listening to Fela Kuti's Afrobeat and reading John Miller Chernoff's book on African rhythm. The band was expanded with guitarist Adrian Belew, trumpeter Jon Hassell and singer Nona Hendryx, and the tracks were assembled by looping and editing tape.","Byrne wrote most of the words after the music was finished, improvising nonsense syllables over the grooves and then finding words to fit their shapes; much of Once in a Lifetime came from imitating radio evangelists. Listen to the trumpet on The Overload and Houses in Motion: that blurred, electronically treated sound is Jon Hassell, whose Fourth World ideas shaped the whole record.","Critics hailed it at once as a breakthrough, and it now sits near the top of lists of the best albums of the 1980s. It also caused lasting rifts: Frantz and Weymouth felt the songwriting credits favoured Byrne and Eno. Some writers have questioned the ethics of borrowing African music, while others note that the band credited their sources openly and were widely heard in Africa itself.","Play Born Under Punches and try following a single part, the bass, then one guitar, then the percussion, noticing how each is simple and the sum is not. Then play Once in a Lifetime and The Overload. Afterwards, hear Steve Reich's Drumming, built on the same West African principle of interlocking cells, and Miles Davis's On the Corner."],"siouxsie-and-the-banshees-the-scream":["Ten songs of stark, jagged sound. John McKay's guitar grinds and shrieks, Steven Severin's bass pounds, Kenny Morris plays mostly on the tom-toms rather than the cymbals, and Siouxsie Sioux sings in a cold, commanding voice, never pleading. Songs deal with suburban breakdown, a butcher's shop, anonymity in the crowd. Jigsaw Feeling and Mirage are fast and cutting; Switch closes it in shifting sections. Their version of the Beatles' Helter Skelter, slowed and stripped, turns the song menacing.","Siouxsie and Severin were part of the Bromley contingent, young Bowie and Roxy Music fans who followed the Sex Pistols in 1976. The Banshees first played at the 100 Club Punk Festival that September, improvising for twenty minutes with Sid Vicious on drums. Record labels were slow to sign them; fans sprayed graffiti demanding it. Polydor finally did in 1978, and the album was co-produced with a young Steve Lillywhite.","Metal Postcard is about John Heartfield, the German artist whose photomontages mocked the Nazis in the 1930s; the song is an anti-fascist statement at a time when punk's flirtation with Nazi imagery was a live controversy, including in Siouxsie's own early wardrobe. Listen, too, for the drums: Morris's avoidance of cymbals gives the whole album its hollow, tribal thud.","Reviews were largely enthusiastic and the album reached the British top twenty. Critics quickly heard it as one of the first records to move past punk into something new; later writers credit it as a founding record of gothic rock, a label the band always rejected. Some have argued that its reputation has been overshadowed by later, more polished Banshees albums.","Start with Jigsaw Feeling and Mirage for the edge, then Helter Skelter for the menace; compare it with the Beatles' original. Listen for the drums, which avoid the cymbals almost entirely. Follow with Joy Division's Unknown Pleasures, released the next year, to hear another way of making punk cold and spacious."],"siouxsie-and-the-banshees-juju":["Nine songs of dark, glittering sound built around John McGeoch's guitar, which ripples in fast, chiming patterns and flanged swirls rather than chords, and Budgie's tumbling tom-tom drumming. Spellbound opens it at a gallop, guitars cascading; Arabian Knights sways over an Eastern-sounding figure; Halloween and Night Shift turn slow and menacing; Voodoo Dolly closes with seven minutes of building tension. Siouxsie's voice is stronger and more theatrical than before, now riding the band rather than cutting through it.","By 1980 the original guitarist and drummer had walked out, and the band had rebuilt with Budgie, formerly of the Slits, and McGeoch, formerly of Magazine. Juju, released in June 1981 and co-produced with Nigel Gray, was their third album with Polydor and their most commercially successful to that point. Britain was in recession and riots broke out in several cities that summer.","Listen to McGeoch's guitar on Spellbound: he often used a fast acoustic-like strum alongside flanged electric arpeggios, so that the guitar seems to glitter. Many guitarists of the next decade, including Johnny Marr and the Edge, cited him. The songs lean into horror films and folk tales, a playful theatre of darkness rather than despair, which is where they differ from Joy Division.","Critics praised it and it reached the British top ten. Its reputation has grown as the gothic rock it anticipated became a vast international scene; the band continued to reject the label. Many fans and writers now consider it the Banshees' finest record, especially for McGeoch's playing.","Start with Spellbound and follow McGeoch's guitar, then Arabian Knights and Night Shift. Notice how Budgie's drums roll around the kit rather than keeping a plain beat. Then compare with The Scream to hear how far the band travelled in three years, and with Joy Division's Closer for the darker cousin."],"the-fall-hex-enduction-hour":["An hour of relentless, repetitive rock with two drummers pounding at once, a bass locked on one figure, guitars scraping and droning, and Mark E. Smith's voice ranting, sneering and muttering over it all. Songs rarely change chord and go on for many minutes. The Classical opens it at full tilt; Hip Priest is slow, creeping and funny; Winter is a long hypnotic drone; And This Day ends the album in ten minutes of controlled chaos. Smith's voice, part poet, part pub bore, holds it together.","The Fall formed in Prestwich, north Manchester, in 1976 around Smith, the only permanent member through dozens of line-ups. By 1982 they had left Rough Trade and put this out on the small Kamera label. Part of it was recorded in a studio in Reykjavík during an Icelandic tour and part in a disused cinema in Hitchin, Hertfordshire. Thatcher's Britain, with its unemployment and the Falklands conflict brewing, supplied Smith's targets.","Smith's reference points were not other rock bands but Captain Beefheart and the German group Can, whose singer he later saluted in the song I Am Damo Suzuki. The two drummers do not simply double each other; they play slightly different patterns, which gives the grooves their lurching, lumbering feel. Hip Priest later turned up on the soundtrack of The Silence of the Lambs.","It sold better than expected and the disc jockey John Peel, the band's great champion, played it constantly. Critics now often name it as the Fall's best album, though with more than thirty studio records there is no consensus. The perennial argument about the Fall is whether Smith was a great writer or a great provocateur; this album is the strongest case for both.","Start with Hip Priest, then The Classical; follow the two drummers, one in each channel. Do not expect choruses; listen to Smith as you would a stand-up comic or a street preacher. Then hear Can's Tago Mago and Beefheart's Trout Mask Replica, the two records behind the Fall's method."],"devo-q-are-we-not-men":["Eleven songs of jerky, robotic rock. The rhythms are stiff and stop-start, the guitars chop in short spasms, synthesizers bleep and burble, and Mark Mothersbaugh's voice yelps and hiccups. The Devo version of (I Can't Get No) Satisfaction, with the famous riff removed and replaced by a twitching new pattern, is the record's calling card; Jocko Homo is the manifesto, a chant-and-response about humanity going backwards; Mongoloid and Uncontrollable Urge are the other favourites.","Devo came out of Kent State University in Akron, Ohio, where Gerald Casale had been a student during the 1970 National Guard shootings. From that, and from the collapse of Akron's rubber industry, came their theory of de-evolution: humanity was regressing. David Bowie and Iggy Pop championed them; Brian Eno produced the album at Conny Plank's studio near Cologne in West Germany, the room where Kraftwerk's and Neu!'s early records were made.","The name Jocko Homo comes from a 1924 anti-evolution pamphlet, one of the found objects Devo loved. The album is quieter about its synthesizers than you might expect: Eno reportedly added many electronic sounds that the band then turned down or removed, and the working relationship was tense. Their Satisfaction works as a critique of the original, draining the swagger from it.","Reviews were divided, some critics finding them a novelty act; others heard a sharp satire of American conformity. It reached the British top twenty. Their breakthrough came later with Whip It, but this debut is now usually rated their best, and its fusion of rock, electronics and concept has come to look prophetic.","Play Satisfaction beside the Rolling Stones' original and listen for what has been removed. Then play Jocko Homo, which switches between an odd meter and a straight beat. Listen for the synthesizer sounds at the edges. Next, try Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine, released the same year, for the European version of the same idea."],"blondie-parallel-lines":["Twelve songs that turn the CBGB scene into polished pop. Hanging on the Telephone opens with a ringing phone and a breathless rush; One Way or Another is a stalking rocker; Sunday Girl is sixties girl-group pop; Picture This is sweet and wry. Heart of Glass, the album's biggest hit, rides a disco beat locked to a drum machine and a bubbling synthesizer. Debbie Harry's cool, knowing voice ties it all together, playful and detached at once.","Blondie, formed by Harry and guitarist Chris Stein, had been a CBGB band since 1974 but were regarded as lightweight next to Television. For their third album Chrysalis brought in the Australian producer Mike Chapman, a hit-maker for glam bands, who drilled them hard at the Record Plant in New York. Disco was at its commercial peak, and punk purists were appalled that a CBGB band would touch it.","Heart of Glass began years earlier as a slower song, and its final form owes much to Kraftwerk and to Giorgio Moroder's disco: the band used a Roland drum machine and synchronised synthesizer, then very new. The album also covers two older songs: Hanging on the Telephone, by the little-known Los Angeles band the Nerves, and I'm Gonna Love You Too, a Buddy Holly number.","It sold many millions and made Blondie stars around the world; Heart of Glass reached number one in both Britain and the United States. Some punk loyalists saw it as a sell-out; critics today mostly regard it as one of the great pop albums and the proof that new wave could conquer the charts without losing its wit.","Start with Hanging on the Telephone and One Way or Another for the rock side, then Heart of Glass; listen to the steady, mechanical pulse under the drums. Notice how Harry's voice stays cool even when the music gets hot. Then play Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine for the electronic source, and Buddy Holly for the pop one."],"blondie-rapture":["A slow, slinky disco-funk groove, with swooning backing vocals and a drifting saxophone, over which Debbie Harry first sings a dreamy, half-whispered verse and then launches into a long rap. The rap itself is playful nonsense: a surreal tale about a man from Mars who eats cars and people, delivered in a deadpan sing-song. In the middle she name-checks two real figures of the New York hip-hop scene. The groove, not the melody, is the hook.","Harry and Chris Stein had been going uptown to Bronx and Harlem parties with the graffiti artist Fab 5 Freddy, where they saw DJs and rappers at work before hip-hop had reached the radio. Rapture was on the album Autoamerican, produced by Mike Chapman and released in late 1980, and became a single early in 1981. The Sugarhill Gang's Rapper's Delight had been the first rap hit only a year before.","The rap names Fab 5 Freddy and Grandmaster Flash; in the video Freddy appears, and so does the painter Jean-Michel Basquiat, standing in as the DJ when Flash did not turn up. The debt was repaid almost at once: Flash cut Rapture into his own landmark turntable mix, The Adventures of Grandmaster Flash on the Wheels of Steel, later that year.","It went to number one in the United States, making it the first chart-topping single there to feature rap, and for many listeners it was their first taste of the form. Some hip-hop artists have praised it as a generous introduction; others have found Harry's rap amateurish. It is now seen as an important, if awkward, bridge between downtown new wave and uptown hip-hop.","Listen for the change in the middle, when the singing stops and the rap begins, and notice how the groove underneath does not change at all. Then play the Sugarhill Gang's Rapper's Delight for comparison, and afterwards Grandmaster Flash's Adventures on the Wheels of Steel to hear Rapture being cut up and used."],"the-slits-cut":["Ten songs of loose, springy, reggae-rooted post-punk. Tessa Pollitt's bass is deep and round, the drums are sparse, and Viv Albertine's guitar scratches and chimes in short bursts, leaving space. Ari Up sings, yelps, laughs and whoops, in a voice untouched by rock convention. Songs mock shopping, dating, housework and the expectations placed on young women. Typical Girls, the single, lurches through several tempos; Shoplifting is a riot; Instant Hit and Newtown drift with dub echoes.","The Slits were among the first all-female punk bands, formed in London in 1976 when Ari Up was fourteen. They toured with the Clash and faced open hostility and violence. By 1979 their original drummer had left and they had embraced reggae. Island signed them and paired them with Dennis Bovell, a leading British reggae and lovers-rock producer, who recorded them at Ridge Farm in Surrey, with Budgie, later of Siouxsie and the Banshees, on drums.","Listen for Bovell's dub craft: instruments drop out suddenly, echoes trail from a single snare hit, and the bass is mixed louder than the guitar, as on a Jamaican record. The cover photograph, the three members naked but for loincloths and mud, was a deliberate challenge to the male gaze, and remains controversial. Ari Up was the stepdaughter of John Lydon.","Reviews were mostly warm, some bewildered, and it reached the British top thirty. Its reputation has grown immensely; it is now seen as one of the key post-punk records and a founding document of feminist rock, cited by the riot grrrl movement. The cover still divides opinion, some reading it as empowerment, others as provocation.","Start with Typical Girls and Instant Hit, and follow the bass line, which leads everything. Listen for the moments when instruments vanish and echo takes over. Then hear PiL's Metal Box, from the same year, for another post-punk band in thrall to dub, and seek out a Dennis Bovell dub record to hear the source."],"suicide-suicide":["Seven songs made by two men and some cheap machines. Martin Rev plays a battered electric organ fed through effects, riffing on one or two chords over a primitive rhythm box; Alan Vega croons, whispers, hiccups and screams like a rockabilly singer lost in an echo chamber. Ghost Rider and Rocket U.S.A. are minimal, pulsing rock; Cheree is a tender lullaby; Frankie Teardrop is ten minutes about a factory worker driven to violence and despair, punctuated by screams that are genuinely hard to hear.","Vega, a sculptor, and Rev, a jazz keyboard player, formed Suicide in New York in 1970, playing in art spaces and later at CBGB and Max's Kansas City. They were the scene's outsiders: no guitars, no drummer. The album was recorded quickly for the small Red Star label, co-produced by Marty Thau and Craig Leon, who had made the Ramones' debut. Touring with the Clash and Elvis Costello in Britain in 1978, they faced furious, violent audiences.","Vega's model was not a punk singer but Elvis Presley and the rockabilly echo of Sun Records; listen to his hiccups and reverb and Ghost Rider becomes a 1950s record played by robots. Vega had seen the Stooges and credited Iggy with showing him what a performer could do. Rev's rhythm box was a cheap preset unit, and its tinny limitations are the sound.","Few people bought it and many critics dismissed it. Its influence has grown out of all proportion: synth-pop duos such as Soft Cell, industrial music, Bruce Springsteen's bleak Nebraska and later electronic rock all owe it something. The main argument now is whether Frankie Teardrop is art or ordeal.","Start with Ghost Rider and Cheree, and notice how little is happening and how much atmosphere it makes. Listen for Vega's rockabilly echoes. Approach Frankie Teardrop when you are ready for it. Then play Neu!'s first album for another kind of hypnotic repetition, and Kraftwerk's Autobahn for machines played with a very different temperament."],"new-order-blue-monday":["Seven and a half minutes of machine-driven dance music made by a rock band. It opens with a stuttering, programmed kick drum, then a pulsing synthesizer bass sequence and a chord of choir-like voices enter. Peter Hook's high, melodic bass guitar comes in over the machines, and Bernard Sumner sings a detached, understated vocal about emotional coldness. The drum pattern, with its rapid-fire bursts, is instantly recognisable.","After Ian Curtis's death, the three surviving members of Joy Division formed New Order with keyboard player Gillian Gilbert, and threw themselves into the electronic dance music they heard in New York clubs. Blue Monday was released on Factory in March 1983 as a twelve-inch single only, with Peter Saville's die-cut sleeve modelled on a computer floppy disk. It became one of the best-selling twelve-inch singles ever made.","The song began as a machine that could play the band's encores for them; the programming was so elaborate that it effectively ran by itself. The choir sound is reportedly taken from Kraftwerk's Radio-Activity, and the drum pattern owes something to disco productions of the time. The sleeve was so costly to make that Factory is said to have lost money on every early copy sold.","It was a hit around the world, and remixed and rereleased more than once. Critics hailed it as the moment post-punk met club music; it influenced house and techno producers, and New Order's crossover from rock to dance shaped the whole of British music in the following decade. Some Joy Division fans took it as a betrayal of what the band had been.","Listen to the opening minute as the parts are introduced one by one; notice how Hook's bass guitar is the only obviously human instrument. Then play Joy Division's Love Will Tear Us Apart to hear what survived the transition. Afterwards hear Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express and Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock, the other great fusions of European electronics and the dance floor."],"rem-murmur":["Twelve songs in about forty-four minutes, built on Peter Buck's ringing, arpeggiated Rickenbacker, Mike Mills's melodic bass and Bill Berry's light, tidy drumming. Over the top, Michael Stipe sings half-buried in the mix, vowels smeared so that the words become texture. 'Radio Free Europe' and 'Talk About the Passion' are the handholds, but the record's identity is its haze: acoustic guitars, piano and backing harmonies layered into a warm, humid Southern murk that sounds both old and new.","R.E.M. formed in Athens, Georgia, in 1980, a college town whose scene also produced the B-52's. After a self-released single on the tiny Hib-Tone label and the Chronic Town EP, they recorded their debut album for I.R.S. Records with Mitch Easter and Don Dixon in Charlotte, North Carolina, early in 1983. The band had resisted the label's attempt to pair them with a slicker producer. In a year dominated by synthesisers and MTV, a guitar band from the South singing indistinctly was an unlikely proposition.","Listen to how often Mills's bass carries the actual tune while Buck plays chords around it, and how his high backing vocal sings a second, different melody against Stipe's. The guitar rarely takes a solo; Buck said he disliked them. 'Radio Free Europe' is a re-recording of the Hib-Tone single, faster and cleaner, and comparing the two shows how deliberately Easter and Dixon softened the band into something dreamier.","Rolling Stone named Murmur its album of the year for 1983, ahead of Michael Jackson's Thriller, which says much about how critics were hungry for an alternative. It sold modestly at first but became the founding document of American college rock, the network of campus radio stations and small clubs that R.E.M. toured relentlessly. Arguments about what Stipe was actually singing have never been settled; he has suggested that was partly the point.","Start with 'Perfect Circle', the slow song with two pianos, where the fog is at its most beautiful. Then play 'Radio Free Europe' loud and notice how the bass pushes the song forward. Don't strain to catch the words; let the voice work as another instrument. Afterwards, hear the Byrds' jangle it inherited and the Smiths' Hatful of Hollow from across the Atlantic."],"rem-automatic-for-the-people":["Twelve songs, mostly slow, mostly acoustic, about ageing, loss and memory. Mandolin, piano, accordion and a string section replace the electric jangle; 'Drive' opens in a hush, 'Everybody Hurts' builds from an arpeggio in the manner of an old soul ballad, and 'Nightswimming' is just piano, strings and voice recalling teenage summers. Only 'Ignoreland' and 'Man on the Moon', a song about the comedian Andy Kaufman, raise the pulse. It is a quiet record made by the biggest band in the world.","After Out of Time (1991) and 'Losing My Religion' had made R.E.M. global stars, the band set out to make a harder rock album and ended up with the opposite. Recorded in several studios including Criteria in Miami and Bearsville in New York, with Scott Litt producing, it drew string arrangements for several songs from John Paul Jones, once of Led Zeppelin. The title comes from the slogan of a soul-food restaurant in Athens, Georgia. The band did not tour it.","'Nightswimming' was built on a circling piano figure by Mike Mills; listen for the oboe that enters late and shadows the strings, one of the few wind sounds on the record. Listen to how 'Man on the Moon' quotes Elvis's vocal hiccups and wanders through Kaufman's world of wrestling and hoaxes. The record's closing trio moves from Kaufman's death-defying jokes to a funeral-quiet goodbye.","Released in October 1992, just as grunge was peaking, it was received as a brave swerve and sold many millions. Many critics and the band members themselves came to rank it as their finest record. 'Everybody Hurts' took on a long second life as a song of comfort, used in suicide-prevention campaigns. Some early fans missed the Southern haze of the 1980s albums; the album's stature has grown steadily since.","Hear it in order, at night. Notice how 'Drive' refuses to explode, and how 'Everybody Hurts' uses the simplest means to reach people at their lowest. 'Nightswimming' is the centre; listen to the piano and strings and the way Stipe's voice is, for once, perfectly clear. Compare Nick Drake's Bryter Layter and its string-lit melancholy."],"pixies-surfer-rosa":["Thirteen short songs, raw and room-shaped: David Lovering's drums sound as though recorded in a concrete hall, Joey Santiago's guitar squeals and scrapes, Kim Deal's bass is plain and propulsive, and Black Francis switches from a croon to a throat-tearing scream within a line. 'Bone Machine', 'Where Is My Mind?', 'Gigantic' (sung by Deal) and 'Cactus' are its landmarks. Studio chatter and giggles are left between songs, so the record feels like overhearing a band at work.","Pixies formed in Boston in 1986 after Charles Thompson (Black Francis) and Santiago met at university. Their first full album was recorded in about ten days late in 1987 by Steve Albini, then of the band Big Black, who disliked the term producer and aimed simply to capture the band in a room. The British label 4AD released it in March 1988; in America it was an import. Spanish phrases and surreal images drew on Francis's time as a student in Puerto Rico.","The lyrics mix Catholic imagery, UFOs, sex and violence into a private mythology; Francis has said much of it is sound as much as sense. Listen for the ambient spill: Albini placed microphones to pick up the room itself, which is why the snare explodes. 'Gigantic', co-written by Deal and Francis, is the only Pixies single led by her voice, and the one that hints at the Breeders she would later form.","It barely sold at first but became a musicians' record. Kurt Cobain said it was the model for Nirvana's sound and hired Albini for In Utero because of it. 'Where Is My Mind?' found a mass audience when it closed the film Fight Club in 1999. Albini publicly disparaged the band not long after, which only adds to the legend. It is now widely treated as one of the formative American rock albums of the decade.","Play 'Bone Machine' first and notice the drum sound before anything else. Then 'Where Is My Mind?': the ghostly wordless voice floating over the chords is Kim Deal. Listen for the lurch from whisper to howl in almost every song, then play Nirvana's In Utero, made by the same engineer five years later."],"pixies-doolittle":["Fifteen songs, most under three minutes, that alternate verses sung almost under the breath with choruses that crash in at full volume. 'Debaser' opens with a bass line and a manic shout; 'Wave of Mutilation' is surf rock gone strange; 'Here Comes Your Man' is a sunny pop song; 'Monkey Gone to Heaven' adds cellos and violins to a song about the environment and numerology. It is tighter, brighter and more melodic than Surfer Rosa without losing its oddness.","Recorded in late 1988 in Boston with the English producer Gil Norton, who pushed the band towards cleaner, shorter arrangements, much to Black Francis's irritation. It was released in April 1989 by 4AD in Britain and Elektra in America, the band's first major-label distribution there. Vaughan Oliver's sepia cover and booklet, pairing each song with an image, were part of 4AD's house style. Tensions between Francis and Kim Deal were already growing.","'Debaser' is a song about the 1929 surrealist short film Un Chien Andalou by Luis Buñuel and Salvador Dalí, which Francis admired. 'Monkey Gone to Heaven' assigns the numbers five, six and seven to man, the devil and God, a bit of home-made numerology dropped into an environmental song. Listen to how often the band stops dead just before a chorus, so that silence becomes the loudest thing in the song.","It reached the UK top ten and became Pixies' best-selling record, a college-radio standard in America. Kurt Cobain admitted that 'Smells Like Teen Spirit' was his attempt to write a Pixies song, borrowing the quiet-verse, loud-chorus pattern. Critics still debate whether Norton's polish improved the band or tamed it; many rank Doolittle and Surfer Rosa as a pair rather than choose.","Play 'Debaser', 'Tame' and 'Hey' in a row and track the dynamic switch each time. 'Tame' is the loud-quiet idea in its purest form, breath and scream alternating. Then put on Nevermind's first track and hear the template reused. Vinyl or the 2014 remaster both keep the dry, punchy sound."],"sonic-youth-evol":["Nine songs where noise begins to take the shape of pop. Guitars tuned to odd open chords chime, drone and clang; 'Tom Violence' and 'Shadow of a Doubt' drift like dream sequences, while 'Starpower', sung by Kim Gordon, is nearly a pop song with a warped undertow. The record ends with 'Expressway to Yr Skull', a long build that collapses into feedback. Compared with their earlier records, the atmosphere is spookier and more cinematic.","Sonic Youth came out of New York's downtown art and no-wave scene of the early 1980s. EVOL was their first album for the Californian punk label SST and the first with drummer Steve Shelley, whose steadier playing gave the songs a spine. Its imagery drew on B-movies and underground film: the cover is a still of Lydia Lunch from a Richard Kern film. The title is love spelled backwards.","On the original vinyl, 'Expressway to Yr Skull' ends in a locked groove, so the final drone repeats until the listener lifts the needle; the sleeve listed its duration as infinite. The guitar tunings were often achieved with cheap instruments and screwdrivers or drumsticks jammed under the strings, making each guitar a prepared instrument in the manner of Cage's prepared piano.","Received in the underground press as a step towards songcraft without surrendering noise. It sold modestly but established the band's college-radio following and the growing respect of musicians. Critics now tend to hear it as the start of the run that led to Daydream Nation, with 'Expressway to Yr Skull' as a live showpiece that anticipated much of the post-rock of the next decade.","Start with 'Expressway to Yr Skull' and listen to how chords are made from guitars that ring in sympathy rather than being played in the usual way. Then try 'Starpower' for the pop side. Hear it alongside Glenn Branca's The Ascension to catch the harmonic clouds Thurston Moore and Lee Ranaldo absorbed in his guitar orchestras."],"sonic-youth-daydream-nation":["A double album of fourteen songs and more than seventy minutes, built on two guitars in unusual tunings that shimmer, grind and chime in a way no standard-tuned band can match. 'Teen Age Riot' opens with Kim Gordon murmuring before a clean, driving riff kicks in; 'Silver Rocket' breaks into a tunnel of noise; 'The Sprawl' and ''Cross the Breeze' stretch out; the closing 'Trilogy' runs together three linked pieces. Long instrumental passages feel like weather moving over the city.","Recorded in the summer of 1988 in New York with Nick Sansano and released that October on the independent Enigma label, it was the band's last album before signing to Geffen. It caught New York in the late Reagan years: the Lower East Side, suburban sprawl, the underground's contempt for mainstream culture. The cover reproduces Gerhard Richter's painting of a single candle, a strikingly still image for such loud music.","'Teen Age Riot' imagines a fantasy America in which J Mascis of Dinosaur Jr is president. The inner sleeves assign each member a symbol, a knowing joke at Led Zeppelin's fourth album. Listen to the guitars: each song often needed its own instrument set to a different tuning, sometimes with objects under the strings, so the band toured with a rack of guitars. The harmonies you hear are frequently overtones rather than played notes.","Critics received it as the peak of American underground rock, and it topped many year-end polls. It has since been added to the Library of Congress National Recording Registry. Some find its length self-indulgent; others argue the long, drifting passages are precisely the point, where rock borrows the time scale of minimalism. Its influence on shoegaze, post-rock and indie guitar has been wide and lasting.","Start with 'Teen Age Riot' and listen to how the clean guitar melody rides above a bed of ringing overtones. Then play 'The Sprawl' through to its long instrumental coda. Compare Terry Riley's In C or La Monte Young's drones, which Moore and Ranaldo knew well, and notice how both use repetition to let harmonics bloom."],"glenn-branca-the-ascension":["Five instrumental pieces for a small guitar orchestra: four electric guitars, bass and drums. The guitars are strummed hard and fast on strings often tuned to a single pitch or a few, so that dense waves of sound build and pile up until the overtones begin to sing on their own. Pieces such as 'Lesson No. 2' and the title track climb steadily in intensity for many minutes, more like a slow, loud chord change than a rock song.","Branca came to New York from Pennsylvania in the mid-1970s through experimental theatre and the no-wave scene, where punk energy met gallery art. He formed bands and then larger ensembles of electric guitars, playing in clubs rather than concert halls. This first full album appeared on the small 99 Records label in 1981, with a cover drawing by the artist Robert Longo. His players included Lee Ranaldo, later of Sonic Youth; Thurston Moore also played in Branca's ensembles.","Many of the harmonies you hear are not being played by anyone: when many loud guitars ring on related pitches, the overtones combine to produce phantom tones and choirs. Branca rethought the guitar as a source of the harmonic series, a thread that runs from La Monte Young's sustained tones. Listen to the title track's last minutes, where the ear begins to hear bells that no one struck.","Rock critics heard it as a noise record; classical listeners argued over whether it belonged to minimalism. Branca went on to write symphonies for up to a hundred guitars, and John Cage reportedly found one of them oppressive, a notorious exchange that shows how far Branca stood from Cage's cool. Its main legacy runs through the players who passed through his ensembles, above all Sonic Youth.","Play the title track loud, sit still and wait for the phantom tones to appear. Then play Sonic Youth's 'Expressway to Yr Skull' and hear the idea shrunk to rock-song scale. Compare Reich's Drumming, where repetition also lets new patterns emerge that nobody is playing."],"husker-du-zen-arcade":["A double album of twenty-three tracks that follows, loosely, a boy who leaves an unhappy home and finds the outside world no better. Hardcore punk at breakneck speed sits beside acoustic songs, a piano interlude, backwards tapes and the fourteen-minute instrumental 'Reoccurring Dreams' that closes it. Bob Mould's guitar is a roaring wall of distortion that still carries melody; Grant Hart's drumming is frantic yet tuneful. 'Pink Turns to Blue' and 'Something I Learned Today' are the easiest ways in.","Hüsker Dü came from Minneapolis and toured constantly on the American hardcore circuit. They recorded Zen Arcade for SST in a few days in 1983, mostly in single takes because money and time were short. It appeared in July 1984, the same month SST released the Minutemen's double album Double Nickels on the Dime. A punk band making a double concept album was close to heresy in a scene that despised excess.","Most of the songs are first takes, which is why small mistakes and bleed survive; the band valued momentum over polish. The story ends by revealing that the whole journey was a dream, which is why the long instrumental is titled as it is. Hart and Mould split the songwriting, and learning to tell their voices apart is part of the fun: Hart the melodist, Mould the storm.","Critics outside punk took notice, and it won unusually strong reviews for a hardcore record, proof that the form could hold ambition. Some punks found it pretentious. Its influence has been long: melodic songs buried in distortion anticipate the Pixies, Nirvana and a whole generation of emotional punk. Many listeners find the story vague; the emotional arc carries the record better than the plot.","Try 'Something I Learned Today', then the Hart-written 'Pink Turns to Blue', to hear the melodies inside the noise. Then take the whole second side as a single sweep. Compare the Who's rock operas or Pink Floyd's concept records, and notice how Hüsker Dü does it in half the studio time."],"the-smiths-how-soon-is-now":["Almost seven minutes of throbbing, swampy guitar. Johnny Marr's rhythm part pulses on a heavy tremolo effect, bass and drums lock into a slow, hypnotic groove, and eerie slide-guitar harmonics wail above like sirens. Morrissey sings calmly, almost conversationally, about crippling shyness and a night at a club where no one talks to him. It sounds nothing like the jangle the Smiths were known for: dark, heavy and circular.","The Smiths, formed in Manchester in 1982, were the defining band of British independent rock in the 1980s, signed to Rough Trade. The song was recorded in 1984 with the producer John Porter and first appeared as a B-side, then on the compilation Hatful of Hollow that November, before Rough Trade issued it as a single early in 1985. Many felt it was wasted as a flip-side.","The tremolo effect was created by running the guitar through several amplifiers and matching their wobble to the song's tempo, a laborious process in a pre-digital studio; Marr has linked the idea to the shimmering tremolo of Bo Diddley. The wailing guitar is several slide parts layered with harmonics. The song's quiet, steady vocal against the churning music is its whole drama.","As a single it reached only the UK top thirty, partly because fans already owned it, but it became their most famous song in America, a staple of college and alternative radio, and a cover version later became a television theme. The band itself was divided about it. It is often cited as the moment British indie absorbed dance-floor hypnosis; its guitar sound has been endlessly analysed and imitated.","Listen on headphones to separate the layers: the throbbing rhythm guitar in the centre, the slide wailing at the edges, the bass pulling the whole thing forward. Then compare Bo Diddley's 'Who Do You Love?' and hear the old tremolo idea turned into a haunted engine."],"the-smiths-the-queen-is-dead":["Ten songs that swing from fury to tenderness to broad comedy. The title track storms in on Mike Joyce's rolling tom-toms and a wah-wah guitar; 'Bigmouth Strikes Again' gallops; 'I Know It's Over' is a slow, bleak ballad; 'Cemetry Gates' and 'Frankly, Mr Shankly' are witty and light. The close, 'There Is a Light That Never Goes Out', pairs a lush string arrangement with a romantic and morbid fantasy. Johnny Marr's guitars shimmer throughout; Andy Rourke's bass is unusually melodic.","Recorded in 1985 with engineer Stephen Street, the album was delayed for months by a legal battle between the Smiths and Rough Trade, and released in June 1986. Britain was in the middle of Thatcherism, the miners' strike just ended; Morrissey's title and lyrics jabbed at monarchy, the Church and the music industry. Rourke was fighting addiction and was briefly sacked during this period.","The album opens with a brief fragment of Cicely Courtneidge singing a wartime music-hall song, taken from the 1962 film The L-Shaped Room. The strings on 'There Is a Light' and elsewhere were played by Marr on an Emulator sampling keyboard, not by an orchestra, despite the band's anti-synthesiser reputation. The cover photograph shows the French actor Alain Delon.","Reaching number two in Britain, it was hailed on release and has regularly topped lists of the greatest British albums. Morrissey's later public statements have made the record harder for some to love, a debate about separating art from artist that continues. Musically, the consensus is firm: it is the Smiths' most complete statement, balancing Marr's arrangements with Morrissey's wit.","Start with 'There Is a Light That Never Goes Out', the song most fans name first. Then go back to the title track and notice how the drums, bass and guitar feedback make a single tidal sound. Enjoy the humour: 'Frankly, Mr Shankly' is a music-hall number at heart. Compare the Kinks' Village Green for a different vision of English decline."],"my-bloody-valentine-isnt-anything":["Twelve songs of warped, woozy guitar rock. Kevin Shields's guitar bends in and out of tune in slow waves; the male and female voices, his and Bilinda Butcher's, are sung softly and mixed low; Colm Ó Cíosóig's drums are fast and busy. 'Soft as Snow (But Warm Inside)', 'Lose My Breath' and 'Feed Me with Your Kiss' show the range, from dreamy drift to punk ferocity. The sound is sensual and disorienting, like hearing music through a fever.","My Bloody Valentine, formed in Dublin and settled in London, spent several years as a fairly conventional indie band before the 1988 EP You Made Me Realise introduced their new sound. Isn't Anything, their first full album for Alan McGee's Creation label, was recorded that year in Wales. Its release in November 1988 pointed the way towards the scene the British music press would call shoegaze.","Shields had begun to develop his 'glide guitar' technique, strumming while holding the tremolo arm so that every chord slides slightly off pitch. The effect is audible everywhere here, but not yet as dominant as on Loveless. Listen for how the vocals are pushed back so far that they sit inside the guitars, making the singers part of the texture rather than the focus.","The British weeklies received it with enthusiasm, and the album has stayed in the shadow of Loveless while being admired in its own right; some fans prefer its rawer, more rhythmic energy. Musicians in the early 1990s treated it as a toolbox. Its reputation grew further when Loveless took years to follow and the band vanished for two decades.","Put on 'Soft as Snow (But Warm Inside)' and listen for the queasy pitch-bending of the guitar. Then 'Feed Me with Your Kiss', which is far louder and faster. Compare Dinosaur Jr's You're Living All Over Me, which Shields admired, and hear how two bands made fuzz tender."],"my-bloody-valentine-loveless":["Eleven songs in which guitars melt into a single, swooning mass of sound. The chords seem to warp and bend like vinyl left in the sun; voices float above or inside, almost wordless; drums are often loops. 'Only Shallow' opens with a blast of noise, 'When You Sleep' is a woozy pop song, 'To Here Knows When' dissolves into a haze, and 'Soon', driven by a dance beat, ends the album in euphoria. It is loud but strangely soft.","Recorded between 1989 and 1991 in a long list of studios, as Kevin Shields searched for exactly the sounds he heard in his head, Loveless became notorious for its cost to Creation Records; figures of around a quarter of a million pounds have been quoted, though Shields disputes them. It came out in November 1991 at the high point of the British shoegaze scene. The band then went silent for over twenty years.","Many of the album's drum parts are samples and loops rather than live playing, because Ó Cíosóig was ill and unsettled for much of the sessions. Shields also sampled his own feedback and played it back on keyboards, so some of what sounds like guitar is a sampler. Butcher's vocals were reportedly often recorded soon after she woke, which helps explain their sleepy intimacy. Listen closely and the bending chords are always moving.","Critics were dazzled, but sales could not cover the cost, and Creation dropped the band. Its reputation grew steadily, and it is now widely ranked among the most influential albums of its era, a touchstone for shoegaze, dream pop and ambient-leaning rock. Its absence of successors for twenty-two years made it a legend. Arguments continue about the best mastering; Shields supervised remasters in 2012 and 2021.","Play 'Only Shallow' loud and let the noise wash over you, then 'To Here Knows When' at lower volume, when it seems to breathe. Don't try to find words. Compare Ligeti's Atmosphères or Eno's Discreet Music, where sound also becomes a slowly shifting cloud; Loveless is their rock cousin."],"slint-spiderland":["Six long songs that move between near silence and sudden eruptions. Clean, interlocking guitar figures repeat and shift in odd rhythmic patterns; Brian McMahan speaks or whispers stories more than sings them; bass and drums hold back until the music explodes. 'Breadcrumb Trail' describes a day at a carnival, 'Nosferatu Man' is jagged and heavy, and 'Good Morning, Captain', a sea story in fragments, ends in a scream. The mood is tense, lonely and unresolved.","Slint were four young musicians from Louisville, Kentucky, who emerged from the local hardcore scene. They recorded Spiderland with producer Brian Paulson in Chicago in 1990, and it was released by the Touch and Go label in March 1991. By then the band had already split up, so there was no tour, no press campaign and very little explanation. The cover photograph, taken by their friend Will Oldham, shows the members' heads above water in a quarry.","The songs are carefully composed, not jammed: guitar parts in unusual metres that fit together like clockwork, with repeats that change by one note or beat. The spoken passages are mixed very low, so you have to lean in, which makes the explosions more shocking. The sleeve included a brief notice seeking female vocalists, a quiet hint of the band's direction had it continued.","Barely noticed on release, it built a reputation by word of mouth and is now regarded as a founding text of post-rock and math rock, a word-of-mouth classic of the kind that the pre-internet decade produced. Critics have debated whether its bleakness is a pose or authentic adolescent unease. Bands such as Mogwai have cited it directly, and it was reissued in a large box set in 2014.","Play it from the start in a quiet room at a volume that lets you hear the whispers, and let the climaxes arrive as a shock. Focus on 'Good Morning, Captain': notice how long it waits before the full band comes in. Compare King Crimson's Red for another heavy, angular instrumental language."],"nirvana-smells-like-teen-spirit":["Five minutes built on four power chords. Kurt Cobain plays the riff alone, clean, before Dave Grohl's drums crash in and the band goes full volume; the verses drop to a quiet, sly bass-and-guitar pattern; the chorus explodes again with Cobain's voice shredding. A strange, sloppy guitar solo simply repeats the vocal melody. The lyric is a mocking jumble of apathy and slogans, hard to decode and designed to be.","Released as the first single from Nevermind in September 1991, the song was expected to be a college-radio track. MTV put its video, directed by Samuel Bayer and set at a chaotic high-school pep rally, on heavy rotation, and the song went global. Its title came from graffiti a friend, Kathleen Hanna of Bikini Kill, scrawled on Cobain's wall, referring to a deodorant brand he didn't know existed.","Cobain said he was trying to write a Pixies song, taking their quiet-verse, loud-chorus pattern. The guitar solo is almost exactly the vocal melody, a punk refusal of virtuosity. Listen to Krist Novoselic's bass in the verses: it carries the song, while the guitar plays just two scraped notes. Cobain also joked about the riff's resemblance to Boston's 'More Than a Feeling'.","It reached the top ten in the US and is widely held to have ended hair metal and brought alternative rock to the mainstream in one stroke. Cobain grew to resent it and at times refused to play it. Rolling Stone and others have placed it near the top of lists of the greatest songs. Its fame has made it easy to underrate how odd and ironic it actually is.","Listen for the switch from the clean opening to the full band at about seven seconds. Then focus on the verses: how little happens, and how much tension that creates. Compare 'Debaser' or 'Tame' from Pixies' Doolittle to hear the model, and notice what Nirvana added: heavier drums and a much bigger chorus."],"nirvana-nevermind":["Twelve songs of punk energy and pop melody, with 'Smells Like Teen Spirit', 'In Bloom', 'Come as You Are' and 'Lithium' making up the first half. Dave Grohl's drums are huge and precise, Krist Novoselic's bass is solid, and Kurt Cobain's guitar is often clean and chorused in the verses and heavily distorted in the choruses. 'Polly' is acoustic, 'Territorial Pissings' pure punk, and 'Something in the Way' closes with hushed guitar and cello.","Recorded at Sound City in Van Nuys in May 1991 with producer Butch Vig and mixed by Andy Wallace for Geffen's DGC label, the album aimed modestly to sell a few hundred thousand copies. Nirvana came from Aberdeen and Olympia, Washington, and had released Bleach on Sub Pop in 1989; Grohl had joined only months earlier. The album arrived in September 1991 and knocked Michael Jackson's Dangerous from number one on the American chart in January 1992.","'Something in the Way' was recorded with Cobain lying on a couch in the control room, because he could not get the quiet feel he wanted in the live room. Later pressings contain a hidden track, 'Endless, Nameless', a chaotic noise jam that begins after about ten minutes of silence after the last song. 'Polly' is a disturbing story sung from a criminal's point of view, handled with unsettling calm.","Its success reshaped the industry, sending labels searching for Seattle bands and turning underground rock into the mainstream. Cobain later complained that the mix was too slick, comparing it to a commercial record, and turned to Steve Albini for the next album. Debates about whether its polish betrayed punk continue, but its songwriting is widely admired, and it sits near the top of most greatest-albums lists.","Hear the first four tracks together to understand the album's impact, then skip to 'Drain You', which Cobain named as a favourite. Notice how nearly every song follows the quiet-loud template, yet each has a distinct melody. Afterwards play Pixies' Doolittle and Hüsker Dü's Zen Arcade to hear what Cobain loved."],"nirvana-in-utero":["Twelve songs, rawer and angrier than Nevermind. Grohl's drums sound enormous and live in the room, the guitars are more jagged, and Cobain's voice swings between despair and fury. 'Heart-Shaped Box' is slow and heavy, 'Scentless Apprentice' is a relentless assault, 'Dumb' and 'All Apologies' add cello and tenderness, and 'Pennyroyal Tea' is a quiet song that erupts. The songs mix confessional pain with sardonic comments on fame and the press.","After the vast success of Nevermind, Cobain wanted to return to a rougher sound and hired Steve Albini, who had made Pixies' Surfer Rosa. The band recorded at Pachyderm Studio in Cannon Falls, Minnesota, in February 1993, working quickly. Their label worried the record was uncommercial, and two songs were remixed by R.E.M.'s producer Scott Litt. The album was released in September 1993, less than a year before Cobain's death.","Albini recorded the band live and placed many microphones around the drums to capture the room; Grohl's snare seems to explode because of it. Cobain's lyrics refer to his medical troubles, the pressures of fame and his marriage. Some retailers stocked a version with a title altered on the back cover. 'Frances Farmer Will Have Her Revenge on Seattle' takes up the story of the Hollywood actress mistreated by her home city.","Released amid public argument between band, label and producer over its sound, it reached number one and has since been praised as Nirvana's bravest record, often preferred by critics to Nevermind. Albini's original mixes, later released on anniversary editions, are still debated against Litt's remixes. Cobain's death in April 1994 made the album's themes of pain and exposure impossible to hear neutrally.","Start with 'Heart-Shaped Box' and notice how the verses creep while the chorus explodes. Then 'All Apologies', whose cello makes it a farewell. Compare Pixies' Surfer Rosa for the same engineer's room sound, and PJ Harvey's Rid of Me, recorded at the same studio months earlier."],"nirvana-mtv-unplugged-in-new-york":["Fourteen songs recorded live in one set, acoustic and hushed. Nirvana play only a few of their hits, choosing instead deep cuts and covers: David Bowie's 'The Man Who Sold the World', the Vaselines' 'Jesus Doesn't Want Me for a Sunbeam', three songs by the Meat Puppets with two of that band's members joining, and, to finish, the traditional song Lead Belly made famous, 'Where Did You Sleep Last Night'. Grohl plays with brushes, a cellist adds weight, and Cobain's voice cracks and roars.","Recorded on 18 November 1993 at Sony Music Studios in New York for MTV's acoustic concert series, the performance was broadcast that December. Cobain insisted the stage be decorated with lilies and black candles, like a funeral. After his death in April 1994, the recording was released that November and topped the American chart. The set list was his choice, much to MTV's frustration at the lack of hits.","Listen to the final song, where Cobain pauses before the last line and his voice breaks into a howl; the performance was done in one take, and he refused an encore because he said he could not top it. Cobain had learned the song from Lead Belly's recordings, and he spoke of Lead Belly as his favourite performer. The Meat Puppets songs were a deliberate gesture to share his audience with a band he loved.","It won a Grammy for best alternative album and has often been ranked with Nevermind as Nirvana's best record. The funeral atmosphere, noticed at the time, became almost unbearable after his death. Some argue it is his finest singing. It was also a key moment in bringing Lead Belly and early folk blues to a new generation of listeners.","Hear the Lead Belly song first, then go back and listen to Lead Belly's own recording to hear how the old folk tune travelled. Notice Grohl's brushes and the cello's weight on 'Pennyroyal Tea' and 'Something in the Way'. Compare Johnny Cash's American Recordings, made the same year, another stripped-down record haunted by mortality."],"pearl-jam-ten":["Eleven songs of big, classic-rock-rooted grunge. Stone Gossard's riffs and Mike McCready's long, bluesy solos draw on Led Zeppelin and Hendrix more than punk; Jeff Ament's bass is fluid; Eddie Vedder's deep baritone, full of vibrato, is the signature. 'Once', 'Even Flow' and 'Alive' come early, 'Jeremy' tells the story of a troubled boy, and 'Black' is a slow, aching breakup song. The mood is earnest and anguished rather than ironic.","Gossard and Ament had been in Mother Love Bone, whose singer Andrew Wood died in 1990. They sent instrumental demos to a San Diego surfer and petrol-station attendant, Vedder, who wrote lyrics over them and was invited to Seattle. The album was recorded early in 1991 at London Bridge Studios and released by Epic in August, before Nevermind. It sold slowly at first before becoming one of the best-selling rock albums of the decade.","Vedder's first demo lyrics formed a loose trilogy about a son's discovery of family secrets, of which 'Alive' is the first part; he has said the story drew on his own life. 'Jeremy' is based on a real incident in a Texas school, handled in the song with restraint. Listen to McCready's solo in 'Alive', a long, soaring piece of classic rock that punk-minded listeners found almost shocking in 1991.","Its success made Pearl Jam one of the biggest bands in the world, and Kurt Cobain publicly dismissed them as careerists, though the two later made peace. Critics were divided at first between those who heard old-fashioned arena rock and those who heard genuine emotion. It was remixed by Brendan O'Brien in 2009, and fans still argue about which mix to prefer.","Start with 'Black', where Vedder's voice carries a slow build to a cathartic end. Then 'Alive' for the guitar solo. Notice how much of the sound comes from the 1970s rather than from punk; set it beside Nirvana's Nevermind to hear grunge's two faces in the same year."],"dinosaur-jr-youre-living-all-over-me":["Nine songs where extreme volume meets lazy, melodic singing. J Mascis drawls in a voice close to Neil Young's while his guitar roars through fuzz and Marshall amplifiers, erupting into long, melodic solos; Lou Barlow's bass is heavy and busy; Murph's drums are punk fast. 'Little Fury Things', 'Sludgefeast' and 'The Lung' define the sound, and the album closes with 'Poledo', a strange home-taped collage by Barlow that points towards lo-fi.","Dinosaur Jr came from Amherst, Massachusetts, from the hardcore punk scene, and moved towards a mix of punk energy and 1970s guitar rock. Their second album was recorded with Wharton Tiers in New York and released by SST late in 1987. They had been forced to add 'Jr' after a legal complaint from an older band called the Dinosaurs. Sonic Youth championed them.","Lee Ranaldo of Sonic Youth adds backing vocals to 'Little Fury Things'. Listen to the contrast between Mascis's guitar, which is enormously loud, and his voice, which barely rises above a murmur; the tension between the two is the band's whole character. 'Poledo', recorded by Barlow on cassette at home, shows the sound of the band he would form next, Sebadoh.","Received in the underground as a new kind of noisy melodic rock, the album inspired musicians more than it sold; Kevin Shields of My Bloody Valentine cited its influence. Internal tensions led to Barlow's departure after the next record. It is now seen as a key bridge from hardcore to alternative rock and a forerunner of grunge's sound.","Play 'Little Fury Things' loud and notice how the guitar solo sings a tune rather than showing off speed. Then compare My Bloody Valentine's Isn't Anything to hear the fuzz being turned dreamy, and Nirvana's Nevermind to hear the same mix made bigger."],"pavement-slanted-and-enchanted":["Fourteen songs recorded cheaply and messily, with out-of-tune guitars, drums that wobble in and out of time and Stephen Malkmus's half-sung, half-spoken vocals. Underneath the scruff are sharp melodies: 'Summer Babe (Winter Version)', 'Trigger Cut' and 'Here' are almost sweet, while 'Conduit for Sale!' and 'No Life Singed Her' are noisy and absurd. The lyrics are witty, fractured and allusive, full of odd phrases, and the album sounds casual yet carefully built.","Pavement began as a studio project for Malkmus and Scott Kannberg in Stockton, California. They recorded in the home studio of drummer Gary Young, for very little money, around the end of 1990 and into 1991. Cassettes circulated among critics before Matador Records released it in April 1992. It arrived just as grunge was going mainstream, and its offhand, low-budget sound was an alternative to the new corporate alternative.","Listen to Young's drumming: loose, occasionally falling out of time, and part of the band's appeal. The strongest influence is the English post-punk band the Fall, whose tape-hiss sound and declamatory vocals Malkmus admired. The guitars are often tuned slightly apart, making the chords sound blurry and bittersweet. Many fans first heard it on a dubbed cassette, which suits the sound.","Critics adored it, and it became a defining record of the lo-fi movement and 1990s indie rock. Some found its sloppiness affected; defenders argued that it was a rejection of rock's heroics. It has been widely reissued and remains a model for bands that want to sound unpolished and literate at the same time.","Start with 'Summer Babe' and 'Here', and notice how the melodies survive the noise. Then play 'Trigger Cut' and listen for the backing chants. Compare Pearl Jam's Ten from the year before to hear two opposite versions of American guitar rock in the early 1990s."],"pavement-crooked-rain-crooked-rain":["Twelve songs, cleaner and more tuneful than Slanted and Enchanted but still casual and witty. 'Cut Your Hair' is a catchy sendup of the music business, 'Gold Soundz' a wistful pop song, 'Range Life' a country-tinged daydream that mocks other bands, 'Stop Breathin' a slow drift, and 'Fillmore Jive' a long, sardonic farewell to classic rock. There is even a short jazz pastiche, '5-4=Unity'. The guitars are loose and bright; Malkmus's singing is relaxed and knowing.","By 1993 Gary Young had left, replaced on drums by Steve West, and percussionist Bob Nastanovich was more firmly part of the band. The album was recorded in New York and released by Matador in February 1994. That was the high point of grunge, two months before Kurt Cobain's death, and Pavement found themselves on MTV and in the mainstream conversation despite themselves.","'5-4=Unity' is a playful tribute to Dave Brubeck's 'Take Five', including its lopsided five-beat meter. 'Range Life' pokes fun at the Smashing Pumpkins and Stone Temple Pilots, which led to a real feud. The album's title and imagery nod to the California landscape and suburban life. Listen for the classic-rock guitar clichés Malkmus slips in ironically, especially in 'Fillmore Jive'.","Received as the band's most accessible record, it brought them their only real hit with 'Cut Your Hair'. Some early fans missed the noise, but it is generally ranked alongside or above Slanted and Enchanted. Critics debated whether its irony was a way of holding back feeling; others heard real tenderness in 'Gold Soundz'. It remains a touchstone for American indie rock.","Start with 'Gold Soundz' and notice how its melody is at once sad and cheerful. Then play '5-4=Unity' right after Brubeck's 'Take Five'. Finish with 'Fillmore Jive', whose long fade feels like the end of rock's golden age seen from a skate park."],"radiohead-the-bends":["Twelve songs of guitar rock, soaring and anxious. Three guitars (Thom Yorke, Jonny Greenwood and Ed O'Brien) interlock in shimmering layers; Yorke's falsetto rises over them. 'Planet Telex' and the title track are loud, 'Fake Plastic Trees' builds from acoustic guitar to a full band, 'High and Dry' and 'Just' became hits, and 'Street Spirit (Fade Out)' ends the album in quiet despair. It is bigger and more varied than their debut, and full of dread about modern life.","After their 1993 hit 'Creep', Radiohead feared being a one-hit band. They recorded The Bends in 1994 with the producer John Leckie, at Abbey Road and other studios, with Nigel Godrich engineering; Godrich would become their longtime producer. Parlophone released it in March 1995, in the middle of Britpop, but Radiohead's gloom set them apart from the celebratory mood of Oasis and Blur.","Yorke recorded the vocal for 'Fake Plastic Trees' in a single session after reportedly seeing Jeff Buckley perform in London, and Buckley's high, vulnerable style colours the album's singing. Greenwood's guitar on 'Just' is a dizzying run of notes climbing through the scale. 'Planet Telex' began as a late-night session with a drum loop, a hint of the electronic turns to come.","It sold slowly in America but was acclaimed in Britain and by fellow musicians, and its sound shaped a generation of British guitar bands from Coldplay on. Some fans still prefer it to OK Computer for its directness. It is usually treated as the bridge from their early grunge-influenced rock to the experiments of the later albums.","Start with 'Fake Plastic Trees' and listen to how the song grows, instrument by instrument. Then 'Street Spirit', whose arpeggiated guitar never changes. Compare R.E.M.'s Automatic for the People, which Yorke admired, for another major rock band exploring quiet despair."],"radiohead-ok-computer":["Twelve songs about technology, travel, alienation and dread. 'Airbag' opens with chopped-up drums; 'Paranoid Android' is a six-minute suite in several sections; 'Exit Music (for a Film)' moves from acoustic guitar to crushing distortion; 'Karma Police' and 'No Surprises' are gentle melodies with an edge. 'Fitter Happier', a list of lifestyle slogans read by a computer voice, sits in the middle. The sound is dense and spacious, guitars and keyboards blended into shimmering textures.","Recorded mostly in 1996 and early 1997 at St Catherine's Court, a Tudor mansion near Bath, with Nigel Godrich now co-producing, the album was released by Parlophone in May 1997. Britpop was at its height and the New Labour government was days old; the band's fears of globalisation, the internet and consumer life seemed prophetic. 'Exit Music' was written for Baz Luhrmann's film Romeo + Juliet.","'Climbing Up the Walls' ends with a cluster of strings written by Jonny Greenwood for sixteen players, each set a quarter-tone apart, a debt to Penderecki's Threnody. The drums on 'Airbag' were sampled and chopped, inspired by DJ Shadow. 'Fitter Happier' was spoken by the Macintosh text-to-speech voice. Listen to 'Let Down': the guitars play in a different meter from the drums.","Critics hailed it immediately, and it won a Grammy and was nominated for the Mercury Prize. It was added to the Library of Congress National Recording Registry in 2015. Some found it portentous; others heard the defining rock album of the late 1990s, anticipating the digital age. Its influence on rock, and its reputation as a concept album, remain lively debates.","Play 'Paranoid Android' and follow its sections, as you might the movements of a symphony. Then play 'Climbing Up the Walls' right after Penderecki's Threnody to hear the debt. Notice the details: harpsichord-like guitars, Mellotron, backward sounds, all of which reward headphones."],"radiohead-kid-a":["Ten tracks that trade guitar rock for electronics, jazz and orchestral sound. 'Everything in Its Right Place' opens with warm synthesiser chords and fractured vocals; 'The National Anthem' has a bass riff and a chaotic brass section; 'How to Disappear Completely' floats on strings and ondes Martenot; 'Treefingers' is ambient; 'Idioteque' is a glitchy dance track. Yorke's voice is often processed until it becomes another instrument.","Exhausted by the fame of OK Computer, Thom Yorke had grown tired of rock and immersed himself in electronic music from the Warp label. The band recorded in Paris, Copenhagen, Gloucestershire and Oxford in 1999 and 2000, with Nigel Godrich producing, and released Kid A in October 2000 without singles or conventional videos. The album went to number one in America despite this. Early internet leaks and short animated clips promoted it.","Jonny Greenwood plays the ondes Martenot, the early electronic instrument heard in Messiaen's Turangalîla-Symphonie, on 'How to Disappear Completely', where his string writing slides in clusters descended from Penderecki. 'Idioteque' is built on samples from early computer music by Paul Lansky and Arthur Kreiger. The brass on 'The National Anthem' was told to play like a free-jazz band; Yorke cut up words and drew lines from a hat.","Critics and fans were divided at first; some called it a wilful retreat, others a landmark. Within a decade it was topping lists of the best albums of the 2000s. It is often credited with leading rock towards electronic music, and with showing that a big band could abandon a formula. Debate continues about whether it is truly radical or a smart fusion of existing ideas.","Start with 'How to Disappear Completely' and listen for the ondes Martenot's wavering tone over the strings; then hear it in Messiaen's Turangalîla. Play 'Idioteque' loud for the beat. Let 'Treefingers' be a pause. Greenwood's There Will Be Blood score is the next step."],"radiohead-in-rainbows":["Ten songs, warmer and more intimate than Kid A, often built on interlocking patterns. '15 Step' starts with a skittering beat in five; 'Nude' floats on strings; 'Weird Fishes/Arpeggi' builds from overlapping guitar arpeggios; 'Reckoner' has a falsetto melody over tambourine and piano; 'House of Cards' is slow and hazy; 'Videotape' closes with piano and quietly shifting drums. The mood is sensual and melancholy, closer to love songs than the band had come in years.","Out of contract with EMI, Radiohead announced In Rainbows with ten days' notice and released it as a download on 10 October 2007, letting buyers pay whatever they chose. A physical version followed at the end of the year. Produced by Nigel Godrich over long sessions in several places, including a country house in Wiltshire, it was a sensation in debates about digital music, piracy and the future of the record industry.","'Nude' had been played live since 1997 and was only now completed. The interlocking guitar lines of 'Weird Fishes' use the same principle of phase and pattern that runs through Steve Reich's music; Reich later wrote Radio Rewrite (2012) on songs from Radiohead, and Greenwood recorded Reich's Electric Counterpoint. Listen to '15 Step' for the sound of children cheering, and to 'Videotape' for a drum pattern that shifts its accent.","The pay-what-you-want release dominated news coverage, sometimes obscuring the music, but critics praised it as one of the band's best records and it later won two Grammys. Industry arguments about whether it helped or harmed musicians continue. Many fans rank it as their favourite Radiohead album, more human and approachable than Kid A.","Play 'Weird Fishes/Arpeggi' and follow the guitar lines as they overlap; then hear Reich's Electric Counterpoint, where one guitar is layered many times. 'Reckoner' is the heart of the record; notice how the falsetto floats over the rhythm. Listen late at night."],"pj-harvey-rid-of-me":["Fourteen songs, raw, violent and extremely dynamic. The title track opens with a whispered verse and a hammering chorus; '50ft Queenie' is a boastful, fast stomp; 'Man-Size Sextet' replaces the band with scraping strings; 'Dry' is slow and aching; and there is a snarling cover of Bob Dylan's 'Highway 61 Revisited'. Polly Harvey's voice ranges from a whisper to a howl, and her guitar is jagged. The songs explore sex, power and gender with dark humour.","Harvey grew up in rural Dorset, and her first album, Dry, appeared in 1992 on the independent label Too Pure. For her second, on Island, the trio of Harvey, bassist Steve Vaughan and drummer Rob Ellis recorded at Pachyderm Studio in Minnesota with Steve Albini late in 1992. The album was released in 1993; within months the band had broken up and Harvey continued alone.","Albini recorded the quiet passages very quietly and the loud ones very loud, so that the volume leaps can be startling at home; the vocals are often low in the mix. Harvey's love of Captain Beefheart shows in the jagged rhythms and bluesy growl, and she has spoken of growing up on her parents' records of Beefheart and the blues. Listen to how Ellis's backing vocals add ghostly falsetto.","Critics praised it as ferocious and original, though some thought Albini's production made it hard to hear. It was nominated for the Mercury Prize. Debate about whether Harvey was a feminist artist began here; she resisted labels, preferring to play characters. It remains one of the most intense rock albums of the 1990s and was made months before Nirvana's In Utero at the same studio.","Play the title track and let the quiet verses draw you in before the chorus erupts. Then hear 'Man-Size Sextet' for the strings, and '50ft Queenie' for sheer energy. Compare Captain Beefheart's Trout Mask Replica for the blues and angles she loved."],"pj-harvey-let-england-shake":["Twelve songs about war, England and its landscape, many of them centred on the Gallipoli campaign of 1915. Harvey plays autoharp and sings in a high, clear voice, far from her 1990s howl; the arrangements are sparse, with guitar, keyboards, drums and occasional samples of bugles and old records. 'The Words That Maketh Murder', 'The Glorious Land' and 'On Battleship Hill' are standouts. The tunes are catchy, even jaunty, against lyrics describing carnage.","Harvey spent several years writing, reading war poetry, history and soldiers' testimony from Gallipoli to Iraq and Afghanistan. The album was recorded in 2010 in a nineteenth-century church in Eype, Dorset, near her home, with longtime collaborators John Parish, Mick Harvey and Jean-Marc Butty. It was released in February 2011, while British troops were fighting in Afghanistan. The photographer Seamus Murphy made a short film for each song.","Harvey borrows from old records: the title track uses a sample of the 1950s novelty song 'Istanbul (Not Constantinople)', one song briefly quotes Eddie Cochran's 'Summertime Blues', and 'The Glorious Land' has a bugle call. Listen to how brisk tunes and a bright autoharp are set against descriptions of dead soldiers, a contrast that makes the horror more vivid. The recording church adds natural reverb.","It won the Mercury Prize in 2011, making Harvey the first artist to win it twice, and topped many critics' lists for the year. Critics compared it to the war poets such as Wilfred Owen. Some found its tone too detached; most heard it as a searching study of patriotism and violence and Harvey's most ambitious work.","Hear the title track and notice the sample running under the autoharp. Then 'On Battleship Hill', whose high, fragile vocal evokes a haunted landscape. Compare Britten's War Requiem, which sets Wilfred Owen, and Butterworth's A Shropshire Lad rhapsody, written by a composer who died at the Somme."],"neutral-milk-hotel-in-the-aeroplane-over-the-sea":["Eleven songs built on Jeff Mangum's strummed acoustic guitar and fervent, nasal voice, surrounded by fuzzed bass, flugelhorn and trumpets, singing saw, accordion and organ, all recorded with lots of distortion. 'The King of Carrot Flowers' opens in three parts, 'In the Aeroplane Over the Sea' is a sing-along waltz, 'Holland, 1945' is a loud rush, and 'Oh Comely' is an eight-minute solo performance. The songs mix love, death and surreal imagery.","Mangum was part of the Elephant 6 collective, a group of friends from Ruston, Louisiana, who made psychedelic-tinged indie pop. The album was recorded in 1997 in Denver with Robert Schneider of the Apples in Stereo, with Julian Koster, Jeremy Barnes and Scott Spillane, and released by Merge in February 1998. Mangum had been reading The Diary of Anne Frank, which affected him deeply and runs through the lyrics.","Anne Frank appears in several songs, though never named in a way that explains the album; Mangum imagines her as a child who might be reborn. The singing saw, played by Koster, is the eerie whistling sound. At the end of 'Oh Comely', someone in the room can be heard reacting to Mangum's performance, which was reportedly captured in one take.","Modestly received at first, it grew into a cult classic through word of mouth and the early internet, and by the 2000s was widely considered one of the best indie albums ever made. Mangum largely withdrew from public life, adding to its mystique. Some find his voice grating and the imagery overwrought; devotees hear a rare sincerity.","Start with the title track: notice the singing saw and the horns. Then 'Holland, 1945' for its joyful noise. Hear 'Oh Comely' alone at night. Compare Nick Drake's Pink Moon for an intimate voice and guitar, and the Beatles' Sgt Pepper for the brass-band psychedelia."],"the-strokes-is-this-it":["Eleven songs, taut and minimal, in about thirty-six minutes. Two guitars, Nick Valensi's and Albert Hammond Jr.'s, play interlocking, clean, choppy lines; Nikolai Fraiture's bass pulses; Fabrizio Moretti's drums are precise and almost machine-like; Julian Casablancas sings through a distorted, lo-fi filter with a bored drawl. 'Last Nite', 'Someday', 'Hard to Explain' and 'The Modern Age' are the hits. It sounds casual, cool and effortless.","The Strokes were five friends from Manhattan who played Lower East Side clubs. Their debut EP created a buzz in Britain, and Rough Trade released the album in the summer of 2001, before RCA's American release in October. It arrived just after 9/11 in America, where 'New York City Cops' was dropped and the cover changed. Produced by Gordon Raphael in a small basement studio, it marked a return to lean guitar rock after years of nu-metal and electronica.","Casablancas recorded his vocals through a small amplifier to get the distorted, far-off sound; Raphael deliberately avoided modern gloss. Moretti's drums were recorded to sound like a drum machine, part of the band's goal of making rock sound like 1970s New York filtered through electronic music. 'Last Nite' so resembled Tom Petty's 'American Girl' that Petty later acknowledged it with amusement.","It was hailed as the saviour of rock and topped many polls of the decade's best albums, launching the garage-rock revival along with the White Stripes. Some critics found it derivative of the Velvet Underground and Television; others argued it was a synthesis rather than a copy. Its style influenced many British bands, including the Arctic Monkeys and the Libertines.","Play 'Last Nite' and listen to how the two guitars fit together like gears. Then 'Hard to Explain', with its machine-like drums. Hear it after Radiohead's Kid A, released a year earlier, to understand the two directions rock split into at the turn of the century."],"arcade-fire-funeral":["Ten songs, big, anthemic and emotional. Violins, accordion, piano and organ are woven around guitars and drums; many voices chant together. The four 'Neighborhood' songs imagine children escaping into snowstorms and tunnels; 'Wake Up' is a huge wordless chant; 'Rebellion (Lies)' drives on a pulsing bass; 'Une année sans lumière' is in French; 'In the Backseat', sung by Régine Chassagne, closes in grief. The mood is childlike, desperate and joyful at once.","Arcade Fire formed in Montreal around Win Butler and Chassagne, a married couple. While they were recording the album in 2003 and 2004, several relatives of band members died, including Butler's grandfather, the bandleader and steel-guitar pioneer Alvino Rey; hence the title. Merge released it in September 2004. Its success, driven by blogs and a glowing Pitchfork review, showed how the internet could make an indie band famous.","The strings were arranged by Owen Pallett and Sarah Neufeld, bringing a chamber-music sound. The band recorded in a Montreal studio with all members shouting and singing to create the choral effect. Listen for how the French-language song reflects Montreal's bilingualism and Chassagne's Haitian heritage. The 'Neighborhood' songs form a loose narrative of children growing up in a world of blackouts.","It was widely hailed as one of the best albums of the decade; David Bowie became a fan and performed with the band. Some critics found its emotion overblown; others heard a rare sincerity. It paved the way for 2000s indie rock's taste for big choruses and many-member bands, and led to later Grammy success with The Suburbs.","Start with 'Wake Up', and notice how the whole band sings the melody. Then 'Neighborhood #1 (Tunnels)', whose piano builds slowly. Compare Neutral Milk Hotel's In the Aeroplane Over the Sea for another cathartic, homemade indie sound, and Bowie's Heroes for the grandeur."],"bob-dylan-like-a-rolling-stone":["Six minutes of four verses and a chorus that keeps growing. It opens with a single crack of the snare drum, then piano, Mike Bloomfield's stinging guitar and, floating over everything, Al Kooper's organ, a high wavering line that answers each phrase. Dylan does not so much sing as sneer, pitching every chorus as a question thrown at a fallen socialite who has lost her place in the world. The song never resolves; it simply fades while still at full pressure.","Dylan wrote it in June 1965, back from a draining English tour, out of what he described as a long page of angry prose. Producer Tom Wilson cut it at Columbia's Studio A in New York over two days. Columbia hesitated to issue a six-minute single, since radio wanted three, but it went out in July and climbed to number two in America, kept from the top by the Beatles' 'Help!'. Five days after release Dylan played it, loud and electric, at the Newport Folk Festival.","Kooper was a guitarist who talked his way to the organ bench and did not really know the chords, so he watched the other players' hands and came in a fraction late on each change. That hesitation is the organ sound everyone remembers. The first day's takes tried the song as a waltz; the session tapes, since issued, let you hear it turn from three beats to four and find its swagger.","It was heard at once as a rupture: a pop single with the length, bile and imagery of a poem. Rolling Stone put it at the top of its list of the greatest songs in 2004 and again in 2021. The argument now is less about its worth than about blame: whether it ended the folk revival's innocence or simply told the truth about it. Its handwritten draft sold at auction in 2014 for about two million dollars.","Listen first for the opening drum shot and the organ entering behind the voice; then follow how each chorus gets angrier than the last. Hear it next to 'Blowin' in the Wind' to measure how far he travelled in two years, and next to the Rolling Stones' 'Satisfaction', released weeks earlier, for the other great rock single of that summer."],"bob-dylan-highway-61-revisited":["Nine songs, eight of them fully electric, with a blues band sound that is rough, bright and fast. 'Like a Rolling Stone' opens; 'Tombstone Blues' and the title track race; 'Ballad of a Thin Man' creeps over a slow piano and organ; 'Just Like Tom Thumb's Blues' is a weary border-town lament. The record closes with 'Desolation Row', eleven minutes of acoustic guitar and a Spanish-tinged second guitar, a carnival parade of figures from history, scripture and literature.","Made in New York in the summer of 1965, the weeks around Newport, with Mike Bloomfield on lead guitar and Al Kooper on keyboards; Bob Johnston took over as producer from Tom Wilson after the first single. U.S. Route 61 runs from New Orleans up through the Mississippi Delta to Duluth, Minnesota, where Dylan was born: the road of the blues migration, of Delta crossroads legends, of the pilgrimage north that made Chicago blues.","The title song begins with a comic retelling of Abraham ordered to sacrifice his son (Dylan's own father was named Abram), and its manic bursts come from a toy police siren credited to Dylan himself. 'Desolation Row' was recorded with Nashville guitarist Charlie McCoy, who happened to be visiting New York and improvised the lacework second guitar line on the spot.","Critics and fellow musicians took it as proof that rock could be art, and the Beatles, the Byrds and the Stones all listened closely. Some folk loyalists felt betrayed. Its standing has never really wavered; the debate is whether it or 'Blonde on Blonde' is the summit of the 1965 to 1966 run, and whether its surreal imagery is profound or brilliantly arbitrary.","Take side one straight through, then sit still for 'Desolation Row' as a single long journey. Notice how the band plays like a Chicago blues outfit that has read too many French poets. Follow with Robert Johnson's 'Cross Road Blues' and Charley Patton's 'High Water Everywhere' to hear the highway the album is named for. The mono mix is punchier."],"bob-dylan-blonde-on-blonde":["A double album of fourteen songs, softer and stranger than its predecessor. Organ and electric guitar swirl together into a silvery, nocturnal glow. 'Visions of Johanna' drifts through a sleepless night; 'I Want You' and 'Just Like a Woman' are sharp pop; 'Rainy Day Women #12 & 35' is a raucous, laughing brass-band romp; and the whole fourth side is given to 'Sad Eyed Lady of the Lowlands', an eleven-minute slow hymn to one woman.","Sessions began in New York in January 1966 with the Hawks, the group later known as the Band, and stalled. Producer Bob Johnston moved them to Nashville, where Dylan wrote in the studio while crack country session players such as Charlie McCoy, Kenny Buttrey and Wayne Moss waited, sometimes until dawn, playing cards. Robbie Robertson and Al Kooper came along from New York. It appeared in June 1966, among the first double albums in rock.","'4th Time Around' is a sly answer to the Beatles' 'Norwegian Wood', which had borrowed Dylan's own manner; John Lennon admitted feeling uneasy about it. Kenny Buttrey's drumming on 'Sad Eyed Lady' swells in near-panic because nobody knew how long the song would go on. Dylan later described the sound he was chasing as thin and wild and mercurial, which is exactly what the organ and harmonica do together.","Hailed at once and still widely ranked his finest record. Its reputation also made Nashville respectable to rock musicians, opening the road to country rock. Arguments continue about how much of the imagery is private, how much Sara Lownds, whom he had just married, lies behind 'Sad Eyed Lady', and whether the rambling side four is devotion or indulgence.","Start with 'Visions of Johanna' in the dark, then 'I Want You' for the bright pop Dylan. Listen for Garth Hudson-style organ and McCoy's bass harmonica in the corners. Play the Beatles' 'Rubber Soul' beside it to hear two camps trading ideas in real time. The 2010s mono reissues are worth seeking out."],"bob-dylan-live-1966":["A whole concert on two discs. The first half is Dylan alone with acoustic guitar and harmonica, playing long, hushed songs such as 'Visions of Johanna' and 'Desolation Row' to an attentive, reverent hall. The second half brings on the Hawks, loud and ferocious, for 'Tell Me, Momma', 'Ballad of a Thin Man' and more, while the audience slow-handclaps and heckles. It ends with a snarling 'Like a Rolling Stone'.","Recorded at the Free Trade Hall, Manchester, on 17 May 1966, during a British tour on which Dylan was booed nightly by folk audiences who felt he had sold out. The Hawks were Robbie Robertson, Rick Danko, Richard Manuel and Garth Hudson, with Mickey Jones on drums. A tape circulated for three decades as one of rock's most famous bootlegs, wrongly labelled as the Royal Albert Hall show; Columbia finally issued it officially in 1998, keeping the wrong name in quotation marks.","Just before the last song a voice from the audience shouts 'Judas!'. Dylan answers that he doesn't believe him and calls him a liar, then turns and tells the band, profanely, to play loud. At least two men have since claimed to be the heckler. Listen too to the acoustic half: the silence of the crowd there is part of the drama, because it is the same people.","Long before release, critics treated the bootleg as a founding document of rock's live power and of the folk-rock schism. Its official issue confirmed it; many rank it the greatest live rock recording. The debate now is about whose side history takes: the hecklers are usually cast as philistines, though some writers argue they were defending a real community Dylan had left behind.","Play one acoustic song, then jump to the electric 'Ballad of a Thin Man', and feel the shock the audience felt. Then save the last track for the end. Compare with Muddy Waters at Newport in 1960, and with Neil Young's 'Rust Never Sleeps', which stages the same acoustic-then-electric split as a choice rather than a fight."],"bob-dylan-blood-on-the-tracks":["Ten songs of love gone wrong, mostly built on Dylan's acoustic guitar with a light band. 'Tangled Up in Blue' opens, a story that jumps between times and pronouns; 'Simple Twist of Fate' is quiet and rueful; 'Idiot Wind' is a long, organ-driven storm of accusation; 'Shelter from the Storm' is a patient, circling ballad; 'Buckets of Rain' closes almost in a whisper. The long narrative 'Lily, Rosemary and the Jack of Hearts' is a Western told in fifteen verses.","Dylan recorded the album in New York in September 1974, then, dissatisfied, re-cut five songs in Minneapolis that December with local players gathered by his brother. His marriage to Sara was coming apart. He had also spent weeks that year in a painting class with Norman Raeben, who, he said, taught him to see past, present and future at once, which he credited for the album's shifting sense of time.","Test pressings of the New York version reached reviewers before the Minneapolis re-recordings replaced half of it, and the full September sessions were released in 2018 as 'More Blood, More Tracks'. Compare the two 'Idiot Wind's: in New York it is weary and hushed, in Minneapolis it is furious. Dylan has denied the songs are autobiography and pointed to Chekhov's short stories instead.","Initial reviews were mixed, some finding it slight, but within a few years it was ranked among his best, and it is often called the great breakup album. The autobiography question remains open: his son Jakob said it sounded like his parents talking, while Dylan insists on fiction. Fans still argue whether the New York takes were the better record.","Start with 'Tangled Up in Blue' and track who 'I' and 'she' are in each verse; the confusion is the point. Then hear 'Idiot Wind' and 'You're Gonna Make Me Lonesome When You Go' back to back: rage and tenderness a track apart. Schubert's 'Winterreise' is a surprisingly close cousin, a cycle of a jilted man walking through his memories."],"bob-dylan-time-out-of-mind":["Eleven songs in a murky, reverberant sound: organ, twanging guitars, doubled drums and a voice now cracked and worn, pushed through echo as if recorded in a swamp. The songs are slow blues and ballads about walking, waiting and ageing: 'Love Sick' creeps in over a ticking organ; 'Not Dark Yet' is a stately meditation on fading; 'Make You Feel My Love' is a plain ballad; and 'Highlands' closes the album with sixteen shambling minutes of wry monologue.","Recorded in early 1997 at Criteria Studios in Miami with producer Daniel Lanois, who had made Dylan's 'Oh Mercy' in 1989, and a crowded room of musicians. Dylan had issued no original songs for seven years. Just before release he was hospitalised with a serious heart infection, and the album's preoccupation with mortality was read through that, though it was finished beforehand. It won the Grammy for Album of the Year.","Dylan was frustrated by the sessions and later said he wanted a sound closer to the old records he loved; the album is full of borrowed phrases and melodies from pre-war blues and country, a practice that grew on his later records. 'Make You Feel My Love' became a standard through covers by Billy Joel, Garth Brooks and Adele, the rare Dylan song others sing more than he does.","It was hailed as a comeback, the start of a late period many now rank alongside the 1960s. Lanois's atmospheric production divides listeners: for some it is the album's soul, for others a fog. Dylan went on to produce himself, under the alias Jack Frost. The borrowings, later more explicit, opened a lasting argument over his methods.","Begin with 'Not Dark Yet' at night. Then try 'Highlands' as you would a long short story. Listen for the old blues shapes under the fog; Muddy Waters and Charley Patton are close by. Schubert's 'Winterreise', a wanderer's cycle in a cold landscape, is an unexpected but apt companion."],"neil-young-after-the-gold-rush":["Eleven short songs alternating between fragile piano-and-voice ballads and loose, loud electric rock. Young's thin, high tenor is the signature. The title track is only piano, voice and a closing flugelhorn solo, a dream of knights, a burning world and silver spaceships. 'Southern Man' is a furious electric guitar assault on racism; 'Only Love Can Break Your Heart' is a gentle waltz; 'Don't Let It Bring You Down' is bleak and consoling at once.","Young made it in 1970, partly in a basement studio at his house in Topanga Canyon outside Los Angeles, between tours with Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young, whose 'Déjà Vu' had just been a huge success. He used members of Crazy Horse and an eighteen-year-old Nils Lofgren, a guitarist, whom he put on piano. Several songs grew from a screenplay by the actor Dean Stockwell for a film that was never made.","Lofgren's piano is simple because he had barely played the instrument; its plainness is part of the charm. 'Southern Man' drew a reply four years later from Lynyrd Skynyrd's 'Sweet Home Alabama', which names Young directly; he took it good-naturedly. The album also includes a cover, Don Gibson's country hit 'Oh, Lonesome Me', slowed into a dirge.","Rolling Stone's first review was cool, calling it uneven, but the album sold well and its reputation climbed steadily. It is now considered one of the defining records of the Laurel Canyon era. The title song's cryptic ecological vision has been widely covered, notably by Dolly Parton, Linda Ronstadt and Emmylou Harris on 'Trio II'.","Play the title track first with nothing else going on, then 'Southern Man' straight after to hear Young's two voices, the choirboy and the wrecker. Compare Joni Mitchell's 'Blue' from the next year for the same canyon, a different temperament. Then hear 'Tonight's the Night' to see where this fragility went."],"neil-young-tonights-the-night":["A wake on record. Twelve songs played loose and ragged, slurred and off-key by design, with piano, guitars and Ben Keith's crying pedal steel. The title song opens and closes the album in two versions, an unsettling chant naming a dead friend. Between them come shambling barroom ballads such as 'Mellow My Mind', 'Tired Eyes' and 'Albuquerque', with voices cracking and tempos dragging throughout. It sounds like a late night that never quite ended.","Crazy Horse guitarist Danny Whitten died of an overdose in November 1972, days after Young had sent him home from rehearsals; roadie Bruce Berry died the same way the following year. In August and September 1973 Young gathered a band he called the Santa Monica Flyers at a rehearsal studio in Los Angeles and recorded late at night, often after heavy drinking. Reprise shelved the album; it came out only in June 1975.","One track, 'Come On Baby Let's Go Downtown', is a 1970 live recording from the Fillmore East with Whitten himself singing lead; the dead man is on his own memorial. Young kept the flubbed notes and cracked high notes on purpose. It belongs to what fans call the 'ditch trilogy', with 'Time Fades Away' and 'On the Beach', his deliberate swerve away from the success of 'Harvest'.","Critics were divided on release; some heard sloppiness, others unbearable honesty. Its reputation grew steadily, and it is now often called his best album and a precursor to punk's and grunge's distrust of polish. Young himself said it was the closest he came to art. The argument over whether rawness is a style or a truth starts here.","Listen straight through, late, in one sitting, as one long wake. Hear 'Tired Eyes', half-spoken, and notice how the pedal steel does the weeping the voice will not. Compare Hank Williams, whose broken-hearted country it drinks from, and the grief of Nick Cave's 'Skeleton Tree' forty years later."],"neil-young-rust-never-sleeps":["An album of two halves. Side one is Young alone with acoustic guitar and harmonica: 'Thrasher', about leaving old companions, and 'Pocahontas', a dreamy history of dispossession. Side two brings on Crazy Horse at deafening volume: 'Powderfinger', a doomed young man defending his family's river, 'Welfare Mothers', 'Sedan Delivery'. The same song bookends it, as the acoustic 'My My, Hey Hey (Out of the Blue)' and the distorted, grinding 'Hey Hey, My My (Into the Black)'.","Most of it was recorded live on Young's 1978 tour with Crazy Horse, with the audience largely removed and some overdubs added. Punk had just arrived, and many 1960s stars felt pushed aside; Young instead embraced it, naming Johnny Rotten of the Sex Pistols in the bookend song. The title came from the band Devo, who adapted it from an advertising slogan for a rust-proofing paint.","The bookend songs argue that it is better to burn out than to fade away, an idea taken up by a whole generation; Kurt Cobain borrowed a line from it in his suicide note in 1994, which haunted Young. Listen to how the feedback on 'Into the Black' is played almost melodically: Crazy Horse's sludge is the sound grunge would later adopt as its own.","Hailed on release, it topped the Village Voice critics' poll for 1979, and a concert film of the tour followed. It is regularly cited as a bridge between 1960s rock and punk, and Young as the 'godfather of grunge'. Debate lingers over whether the 'burn out' ethic is inspiring or dangerous, especially after Cobain.","Play the first track, then skip to the last, and hear one song change from a campfire tune to a wall of noise. Then give 'Powderfinger' full attention as a short story. Compare Dylan's 1966 Manchester concert, which stages the same acoustic-then-electric split under protest."],"joni-mitchell-blue":["Ten songs, spare and close-miked, sung in a voice that leaps without warning from low murmur to high clear octave. Mitchell accompanies herself on Appalachian dulcimer on 'All I Want', 'Carey', 'California' and 'A Case of You', on piano for the title song, 'River' and 'The Last Time I Saw Richard', and on open-tuned guitar elsewhere. The sound is intimate and unguarded; the songs are about travel, love affairs and a longing for home.","Recorded at A&M Studios in Hollywood in early 1971 and released that June, it followed a European journey in 1970, including a stay among hippies in the caves at Matala on Crete, the setting of 'Carey'. Mitchell was living in Laurel Canyon, the Los Angeles hillside community of songwriters, and had just ended relationships with Graham Nash and James Taylor, who plays guitar on three songs; Stephen Stills and pedal steel player Sneaky Pete Kleinow also appear.","'Little Green' is about the daughter Mitchell gave up for adoption in 1965, a secret that became public only in the 1990s, before they were reunited. 'River' opens and closes with the piano playing 'Jingle Bells' in a minor, melancholy key. Her open tunings, partly a way round the weakness polio left in her left hand, create chords no standard-tuned guitarist would find.","Reviews were warm but some were uneasy at its nakedness; Mitchell herself said she had no defences left while making it. Its standing only grew, and in 2020 Rolling Stone ranked it third among all albums. It is now the model of confessional songwriting, though Mitchell has resisted the word 'confessional' and insisted on craft over diary.","Start with 'A Case of You' and listen to the dulcimer's bright drone against her low voice. Then 'River' at Christmas time. Hear it beside Nick Drake's 'Pink Moon' for the other great solitary record of the moment, and beside Schumann's 'Dichterliebe', a song cycle of love recalled and lost, for its oldest ancestor."],"joni-mitchell-court-and-spark":["Eleven songs, most of them with a full band of jazz players, polished and sophisticated, with woodwinds, electric piano and supple drums. The title song opens on solo piano; 'Help Me' is a lithe pop hit with jazz harmonies; 'Free Man in Paris' is a breezy portrait of a music-business friend longing to escape; 'People's Parties' and 'Down to You' are introspective; the record ends, unexpectedly, with a jazz vocalese cover, 'Twisted'.","Released in January 1974 on David Geffen's Asylum label, it was made with Tom Scott's L.A. Express, a group of Los Angeles jazz and studio musicians. It became the best-selling album of Mitchell's career, and 'Help Me' was her only top ten single in America. 'Free Man in Paris' is widely understood to be about Geffen himself. Mitchell was at the peak of her commercial power.","'Twisted' was written in the early 1950s by the jazz singer Annie Ross, setting words to a saxophone solo by Wardell Gray; Mitchell's version even includes a comic cameo from the duo Cheech and Chong. The arrangements' jazz harmonies, not the pop hooks, are the real signal: from here on she would move steadily into jazz, working with Jaco Pastorius, Wayne Shorter and eventually Charles Mingus.","Warmly received and commercially enormous, it topped the Village Voice critics' poll for 1974. Some early fans missed the stark dulcimer of 'Blue'. It is now regarded as a model of how a songwriter can move into jazz-inflected pop without losing edge, and its polish now sounds less like compromise than like a new kind of precision.","Listen first to 'Help Me' for the way the melody leaps unexpectedly between registers, then to 'Down to You' for the orchestral side. Then hear 'Twisted' and seek out the Lambert, Hendricks and Ross version. Follow with 'Hejira' to see where the jazz leads."],"joni-mitchell-hejira":["Nine long, wandering songs of the road, built on Mitchell's chiming open-tuned guitar with very little else. On four of them Jaco Pastorius's fretless bass sings like a second voice, sliding, harmonising, chiming harmonics. There are no choruses in the usual sense; the melodies flow like speech over steady rolling guitar. 'Coyote' opens with a portrait of a restless man; 'Amelia' addresses the lost aviator Amelia Earhart; 'Refuge of the Roads' ends it.","Mitchell wrote most of the songs in 1976 while driving alone across America, from Maine back to Los Angeles, after touring with Bob Dylan's Rolling Thunder Revue. 'Coyote' is usually read as a portrait of the playwright Sam Shepard, also on that tour. 'Hejira' is the Arabic word for the Prophet Muhammad's flight from Mecca to Medina; she chose it for its sense of honourable escape.","'Furry Sings the Blues' is about visiting the aged Memphis blues man Furry Lewis on Beale Street, then being demolished; Neil Young plays harmonica on it. Lewis was reportedly unhappy with the song. Listen to Pastorius on 'Hejira' itself: he recorded his parts afterward, overdubbing melody lines that treat the bass as a lead instrument, a first in her work.","Received respectfully but with less excitement than its predecessors, it sold modestly. Over time it has become a musicians' favourite and for many critics her finest record, the point where her folk roots and jazz ambitions fully merge. Some still find its long, hookless songs monotonous; its admirers call that monotony the hypnotic hum of a highway.","Play 'Coyote' then 'Amelia' on a long drive. Focus on Pastorius's bass as a second singer. To hear the jazz he brought, try Miles Davis's 'Bitches Brew'; for the blues she was visiting, Mississippi John Hurt's 'Avalon Blues' is close in spirit to Furry Lewis."],"leonard-cohen-songs-of-leonard-cohen":["Ten songs sung in a low, level, almost unmusical voice over steady nylon-string fingerpicking, with discreet strings, soft female backing voices and odd touches such as a jaw harp. 'Suzanne', a slow, circling portrait of a woman by the Montreal waterfront, opens; 'Sisters of Mercy', 'So Long, Marianne' and 'Hey, That's No Way to Say Goodbye' follow. The songs are long, literary and patient; the voice never raises itself, and that restraint is the spell.","Cohen was thirty-three and already a known Canadian poet and novelist, whose 'Beautiful Losers' appeared in 1966. He turned to songs partly because books did not pay. Judy Collins recorded 'Suzanne' first, in 1966, and John Hammond signed him to Columbia. Producer John Simon completed the album in New York in 1967, over Cohen's objections to some of his arrangements. 'So Long, Marianne' is for Marianne Ihlen, with whom he had lived on the Greek island of Hydra.","The rolling guitar pattern owes a debt to flamenco: Cohen said a young Spanish guitarist in Montreal taught him a few chords before dying, not long after. Listen to how the melodies stay within a narrow range, like chanted verse, and how the backing voices sometimes carry the melody while Cohen intones below it, an arrangement idea he kept for fifty years.","It sold modestly in America and better in Britain and Europe, where Cohen quickly became a cult figure. Early critics found his voice dreary; later listeners heard gravity. Robert Altman's film 'McCabe & Mrs. Miller' (1971) used three of its songs. It is now regarded as a founding document of the literary singer-songwriter, and its songs are sung widely.","Hear 'Suzanne' first, paying attention to the guitar more than the words, then 'Master Song', and notice how the voice never hurries even when the song runs past five minutes. Compare Nick Drake's 'Five Leaves Left', also dark and hushed, and Hugo Wolf's Mörike songs, where the poem similarly governs the music."],"leonard-cohen-hallelujah":["A slow song in a rocking triple time, built on simple chords and a chorus of one word repeated. In Cohen's own recording, his deep, weathered voice is set against a synthesiser, a plodding rhythm and a gospel-tinged choir. The verses move between biblical stories (David the harpist, Samson and Delilah) and a love affair, mixing reverence and sensuality, with praise and doubt sitting side by side.","It first appeared on the album 'Various Positions' in 1984, produced by John Lissauer. Columbia's chief declined to release the album in America, and it came out there through a small label. Cohen said he wrote dozens of verses over several years, and he changed the verses he sang in concert, moving it from sacred to more worldly. He was fifty, his career in the doldrums, his record company unsure what to do with him.","The first verse describes its own harmony: as the words name the chord changes, the music makes them. John Cale's 1991 recording for a tribute album chose different verses and put it on piano; Jeff Buckley's 1994 version was based on Cale's, and it was Cale's version that played in the film 'Shrek' in 2001. The song everyone knows is a collaboration across three recordings.","Hardly noticed in 1984, it became over the next three decades one of the most covered songs in the world, sung at funerals, in talent shows and at Christmas. That ubiquity has its critics, including, at times, Cohen, who suggested it should be rested. The debate over which verses make the true song is unanswerable, since Cohen himself never fixed it.","Hear Cohen's original first, with its slightly dated synthesiser, then Cale's, then Buckley's, and notice how the meaning shifts with the verses chosen. Compare Mahalia Jackson's gospel for the tradition of praise it borrows from. Then return to the start and hear the song describe its own chords."],"leonard-cohen-im-your-man":["Eight songs framed in cheap synthesisers and drum machines, with Cohen's voice now a deep, dry bass murmur. 'First We Take Manhattan' is a menacing synth-pop march; 'Ain't No Cure for Love' a sly ballad; 'Everybody Knows' a grim catalogue of the world's rigged game; 'Take This Waltz' a Viennese waltz with violin; 'Tower of Song' a wry self-portrait of the songwriter's trade. Female backing voices, especially Jennifer Warnes and Anjani Thomas, carry the tunes.","Released in early 1988, it followed Jennifer Warnes's 1986 album of Cohen songs, 'Famous Blue Raincoat', which revived interest in him. Cohen co-produced with several collaborators, and co-wrote 'Everybody Knows' with his longtime singer Sharon Robinson. He was in his fifties and embracing irony, sharp suits and cheap keyboards, the opposite of his 1960s image as a mournful troubadour, and his voice had dropped to a register that made every line sound like a verdict.","'Take This Waltz' adapts a poem by Federico García Lorca, the poet after whom Cohen named his daughter; it first appeared on a Spanish tribute album for the fiftieth anniversary of Lorca's death. In 'Tower of Song' he imagines Hank Williams in the same building, a hundred floors above: a songwriter's lineage placed in a joke. The cheap keyboard solo there is played by Cohen himself, deliberately clumsy.","A critical and commercial comeback, especially in Europe and Canada. Some old fans found the synthesisers a betrayal; others heard wit and menace they had missed. Now it is often ranked among his best, and its songs, especially 'Everybody Knows' and 'Tower of Song', are cited as late-career models of irony and gravity combined.","Start with 'Tower of Song' and hear the jokes; then 'Everybody Knows' for the dread. Notice how the women's voices sing the melody while Cohen speaks under it. Then hear Hank Williams's 'I'm So Lonesome I Could Cry' to meet the man upstairs."],"van-morrison-astral-weeks":["Eight long songs that feel like one improvised dream. Morrison's acoustic guitar and voice are surrounded by jazz players: Richard Davis's double bass wanders freely, Connie Kay's drums whisper, vibraphone and flute drift in, strings float on top. There are no hooks and few choruses; songs like 'Madame George' and 'Cyprus Avenue' stretch to nine or ten minutes, circling, repeating a phrase until it becomes an incantation. The voice stammers, soars and scats.","Morrison, a young singer from Belfast who had fronted the band Them and had a hit with 'Brown Eyed Girl', was stuck in a contract dispute after his producer Bert Berns died in 1967. Living near Boston, he signed with Warner Bros. and recorded the album in three sessions in New York in autumn 1968 with producer Lewis Merenstein and jazz musicians he had just met. Strings and horns were added afterward.","The musicians say Morrison sang and played in a vocal booth, barely speaking, and they simply followed; most songs were done in one or two takes, so what you hear is them discovering the song. The streets are real: Cyprus Avenue is a leafy road in east Belfast. Listen for Davis's bass: he played as freely as in a jazz trio, and the whole record hangs on him.","It sold poorly at first and reviews were few, but it became a cult treasure. Lester Bangs's 1979 essay made the case that it had saved him. Now it routinely appears near the top of all-time album lists, though Morrison has been dismissive of the record's mystique and of the way it was made.","Hear it whole, in one sitting, perhaps on a long walk. Start with 'Sweet Thing' for joy, 'Madame George' for longing. Follow the bass rather than the voice. Compare Miles Davis's 'In a Silent Way', from months later, and Joni Mitchell's 'Hejira' for other open-ended musical journeys."],"van-morrison-moondance":["Ten tight, warm songs, all under five minutes, with horns, piano and organ, a joyful soul-and-rhythm-and-blues record. 'And It Stoned Me' recalls a childhood day of rain; the title song swings like a jazz standard with walking bass and flute; 'Crazy Love' is a falsetto ballad; 'Caravan' is a radio-worship stomp; 'Into the Mystic' is a quietly ecstatic song about sailing home. The mood is unforced celebration.","After 'Astral Weeks' failed commercially, Morrison produced this himself in New York in late 1969, with a regular band and a horn section. He was living in Woodstock, in upstate New York, where Dylan and the Band were neighbours. Released early in 1970, it was his first big album success and set the pattern for his long career. Where the earlier album had been improvised by strangers, this one was rehearsed and arranged, with Morrison firmly in charge.","Morrison has said he wanted to sound like the rhythm-and-blues and soul singers he grew up with in Belfast, Ray Charles especially, and listen to how he bends phrases like a soul shouter. The swinging title song, now a jazz-club standard, was originally conceived around a saxophone riff; Morrison was a saxophonist before he was a singer.","Well reviewed and popular, it has always stood slightly in the shadow of 'Astral Weeks' for critics, while being the better loved for many listeners. It shows that Morrison could be both mystic and hit-maker. Its classic mixture of soul, jazz and Irish folk was later called 'Celtic soul', and 'Into the Mystic' has become one of his most covered songs.","Put on 'Into the Mystic' first, then 'Caravan', loud. Notice how the horns answer his voice the way a gospel choir answers a preacher. Hear Otis Redding's 'Otis Blue' and Sam Cooke's 'Live at the Harlem Square Club' for his models."],"bruce-springsteen-born-to-run":["Eight songs, layered and huge, full of piano, glockenspiel, surging guitars and Clarence Clemons's saxophone. 'Thunder Road' opens on harmonica and piano, a young man urging a girl into his car; 'Backstreets' is operatic betrayal; the title song is a wall of sound with a four-count countdown; 'Jungleland' closes with nine minutes of street drama and a long saxophone solo. The tone is romantic, cinematic, desperate: escape from small-town New Jersey at any cost.","Springsteen's first two albums had sold poorly and Columbia was losing patience. The title song alone took months to record in 1974, at a studio in Blauvelt, New York, as he tried to pack in the dense Phil Spector sound of his youth. Critic Jon Landau, who had written that he had seen rock and roll's future in Springsteen, came in as co-producer. In October 1975 Springsteen was on the covers of Time and Newsweek the same week.","The title song has dozens of overdubbed guitars, several keyboards and strings stacked into a single roar, with the glockenspiel cutting through at the top; Springsteen has said he wanted Roy Orbison's voice and Dylan's words over Spector's sound. On the cover photograph he leans on Clemons: the image of a Black and white partnership was part of the message.","It was a hit and a critical triumph, though the media hype prompted a backlash and doubts about whether he was a record-company creation. Its reputation has never slipped; it is regularly ranked among rock's greatest albums. Some critics find its romanticism overwrought; others hear it as the last flourish of 1950s rock's teenage dream.","Play 'Thunder Road' first; then 'Jungleland', listening for Clemons's solo as the emotional climax. Follow how Roy Bittan's piano carries the melodic line. Compare Dylan's 'Like a Rolling Stone' and Elvis's 'Mystery Train' for the lineage, then his own stark 'Nebraska' for the opposite response to the same streets."],"bruce-springsteen-nebraska":["Ten songs recorded alone on a four-track cassette machine: voice, acoustic guitar, harmonica, a bit of mandolin and glockenspiel, all bathed in tape hiss and echo. The title song is told by a young killer on death row, calm and unrepentant; 'Atlantic City' is a doomed man's plea; 'Highway Patrolman' a brother torn between duty and blood; 'Reason to Believe' ends the album with a bleak half-smile. The sound is lonely, stark, American gothic.","Springsteen recorded these songs at home in Colts Neck, New Jersey, in early January 1982, as demos for the E Street Band. The band versions felt wrong, so he released the cassette itself, after struggling to master it for vinyl. He was reading Flannery O'Connor and had seen Terrence Malick's film 'Badlands', about the 1958 killings by Charles Starkweather, the subject of the title song. Reagan's America was in recession.","Some songs recorded at the same full-band sessions became the album 'Born in the U.S.A.', whose title song began life in this acoustic set. 'Mansion on the Hill' borrows a Hank Williams title. Warren Zanes's 2023 book 'Deliver Me from Nowhere' describes how dark Springsteen's state of mind was during the recording.","Critics were impressed but puzzled; it sold modestly without a tour or single. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now seen as a founding document of lo-fi home recording and of the modern Americana revival. Johnny Cash recorded two of its songs within a year. Some prefer the bright E Street Springsteen; many now think this is his best.","Hear the title song alone, late at night. Notice how the hiss and echo make the room part of the song. Then hear Woody Guthrie's 'Dust Bowl Ballads', its ancestor, and Johnny Cash's 'American Recordings', its descendant. Play 'Born to Run' straight after to hear the same highways without hope."],"tom-waits-swordfishtrombones":["Fifteen short songs for a bizarre junkyard band: marimba, pump organ, bass, bagpipes, clanging metal, muted brass and a double bass slapped like a drum. Waits growls, croons and barks. 'Underground' is a stomping march of subterranean creatures; 'In the Neighborhood' is a funeral brass-band hymn to his street; 'Frank's Wild Years' is a deadpan spoken story over organ; 'Soldier's Things' is a tender piano ballad of army junk for sale.","Waits had spent the 1970s as a jazzy, beatnik piano balladeer on Asylum Records. In 1980 he married Kathleen Brennan, who introduced him to Captain Beefheart and to the instrument-builder Harry Partch. Asylum reportedly would not release this new direction; Island Records did, in 1983. Waits produced it himself, a first, and wrote many of the arrangements around sounds he found rather than chords he knew.","Harry Partch's home-built instruments and hobo songs are the key: Waits hunted down odd percussion and chromelodeon-like organs to get that sound. 'Johnsburg, Illinois' is a tiny love song for Brennan's hometown. The album is the first of a trilogy about the character Frank, continued in 'Rain Dogs' and 'Franks Wild Years'.","Critics hailed a reinvention; some old fans were bewildered. It is now regarded as the turn that made Waits one of the most original voices in rock, and its 'junkyard orchestra' was widely imitated. Few argue now about its importance; the question is whether it or 'Rain Dogs' is the peak.","Start with 'In the Neighborhood' for the brass, then 'Underground' for the percussion. Listen for what is being hit. Then hear Harry Partch's 'Barstow' and Captain Beefheart's 'Trout Mask Replica' for the sources, and Kurt Weill's 'Threepenny Opera' for the cabaret in his voice."]});